window.COURSE_DATA = {"phases":[{"id":0,"name":"Foundations","short":"Excel basics","done_when":"You built the two-week downtime summary with SUMIFS formulas that reconcile to 124 events / 1,931 minutes, plus a decluttered chart."},{"id":1,"name":"Advanced Excel & Power Query","short":"Excel & PQ","done_when":"You drop a new export into the folder, press Refresh All, and every table and chart updates with zero manual edits."},{"id":2,"name":"Data modelling & SQL","short":"SQL","done_when":"You can answer 'which 10 assets lost the most hours to reactive work last quarter, and were their PMs on time?' in one query."},{"id":3,"name":"Power BI","short":"Power BI","done_when":"The Model Line team could use the dashboard in a weekly review without you explaining it."},{"id":4,"name":"Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau","short":"OEE & Tableau","done_when":"You can rebuild line OEE from raw shift data to match the dashboard, and show in Tableau what share of lost time is maintenance-related and which assets drive it."},{"id":5,"name":"Python for data","short":"Python","done_when":"A task that took you an hour a week in Excel now runs from a single command."},{"id":6,"name":"Statistics & reliability analytics","short":"Reliability","done_when":"You can defend a PM interval change with numbers in front of an engineering manager."},{"id":7,"name":"Machine learning & condition monitoring","short":"ML & CM","done_when":"You can explain to a non-specialist why your model's alerts are worth acting on, and how many false alarms to expect."}],"weeks":[{"week":1,"phase":0,"title":"Excel basics, your first look at downtime data","path":"course/phase-0-foundations/week-01-excel-basics.md","ticks":["Saved the CSV as `.xlsx` with frozen headers and filters","Used the status bar to check a total","Wrote `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `IF`","Used `$` to lock a reference and know why","Answered all six handover questions"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 0 · Foundations** · Mon 2 Nov – Sun 8 Nov 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nYou're starting from scratch, so this week is about getting comfortable: opening a data file, moving around it quickly, and getting Excel to do sums for you. Nothing here is hard, but everything later is built on it.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- open a CSV file and save it as a proper Excel workbook\n- sort, filter and freeze headers so a big list is easy to read\n- write formulas with `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT` and `IF`\n- explain the difference between `A1`, `$A$1` and `A$1`, and why it matters\n\n**You need:** Excel (Microsoft 365) and the file `data/week01/shift_downtime_log.csv` from this repo. To get the files, open the repo on GitHub, click the green **Code** button, then **Download ZIP**, and unzip it somewhere like `Documents\\Learning-Data`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-find-your-way-around","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Find your way around","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Double-click `shift_downtime_log.csv`. It opens in Excel. This is two weeks of downtime from a fictional cereal line, the same kind of log a shift team fills in.\n2. **Save it as a workbook straight away:** File → Save As → choose *Excel Workbook (\\*.xlsx)* → name it `week01_downtime.xlsx`. A CSV can't hold formulas, formatting or extra sheets, so always save a working copy as `.xlsx`.\n3. **Make it readable.**\n   - Select all (`Ctrl+A`), then on the Home tab choose Format → AutoFit Column Width.\n   - Click cell `A2`, then View → Freeze Panes → *Freeze Panes*. Now the headers stay put when you scroll.\n   - Make row 1 bold (`Ctrl+B`).\n4. **Learn to move fast.** Try each of these and watch where the cursor lands:\n   - `Ctrl+↓` jumps to the bottom of the data, `Ctrl+↑` jumps back up.\n   - `Ctrl+Home` goes to A1, and `Ctrl+End` goes to the last used cell.\n   - `Ctrl+Shift+↓` selects from where you are down to the bottom.\n5. **Use the status bar.** Click the column letter **I** (Minutes). Look at the bottom-right of the window: Excel shows Average, Count and Sum. Right-click the status bar and tick Minimum and Maximum as well.\n   - You should see **Sum 1931**, **Average ≈ 15.6**, **Count 125** (124 rows plus the header), **Min 2**, **Max 177**.\n6. **Sort.** Click any cell in the Minutes column, then go to Data → Sort Largest to Smallest. The top row is the worst stop: a vacuum pump bearing in Forming, 177 minutes.\n7. **Filter.** Data → Filter. Little arrows appear in the headers. Filter **Category** to *Breakdown* only. Select column I again and check the status bar: **14 breakdowns, 976 minutes.** Clear the filter afterwards (Data → Clear).\n\n### Why it works\n\nExcel stores data in a **grid of cells**: columns are letters, rows are numbers, so `I2` is column I, row 2. A data file like this is a **list**: one header row, then one row per event, with no gaps. Sort, filter and the status bar all work by detecting that list, so **a blank row or column in the middle breaks them.** Keep lists solid.\n\nThe status bar is the quickest sanity check in Excel. Before trusting any formula, select the column and see if the sum looks right.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-formulas-and-cell-references","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Formulas and cell references","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Go to the first empty cell under the Minutes column (`I126`, since the data ends on row 125). Type `=SUM(I2:I125)` and press Enter. You get **1931**.\n2. In the cells below, add:\n\n   | Cell | Type this | Result |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | H127 | `Average` | |\n   | I127 | `=AVERAGE(I2:I125)` | 15.57… |\n   | H128 | `Longest` | |\n   | I128 | `=MAX(I2:I125)` | 177 |\n   | H129 | `Shortest` | |\n   | I129 | `=MIN(I2:I125)` | 2 |\n   | H130 | `Events` | |\n   | I130 | `=COUNT(I2:I125)` | 124 |\n\n3. **Add a calculated column.** In `J1` type `Hours`. In `J2` type `=I2/60` and press Enter. Now copy it down: select `J2`, then double-click the tiny square at the bottom-right corner of the cell (the *fill handle*). Excel fills the formula to the bottom of the data.\n4. Click `J3` and look at the formula bar. It says `=I3/60`, not `=I2/60`. Excel moved the reference down one row for you. That's a **relative reference**.\n5. **Percent of total.** In `K1` type `% of total`. In `K2` type `=I2/I126` and fill down. The results go wrong straight away: `K3` divides by `I127`, `K4` by `I128`… The total moved too.\n   - Fix it: in `K2` type `=I2/$I$126` and fill down again. The `$` signs lock the reference. Now every row divides by the total.\n   - Format column K as a percentage: Home → `%` button.\n6. **Your first decision formula.** In `L1` type `Long stop?`. In `L2` type:\n\n   ```\n   =IF(I2>=30,\"Long\",\"Short\")\n   ```\n\n   Fill it down. Filter column L to *Long*: you should get **12 rows**.\n\n### Why it works\n\nEvery formula starts with `=`. When you copy a formula, Excel shifts its references by the same amount you moved, which is why copying down \"just works\" most of the time.\n\n| Reference | Copy down | Copy across | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `I2` | changes row | changes column | row-by-row calculations |\n| `$I$126` | stays | stays | a single fixed cell like a total or a target |\n| `I$126` | stays | changes column | a header row you look across |\n| `$I2` | changes row | stays | a column you look down |\n\nPress `F4` while your cursor is on a reference to cycle through these four.\n\n`IF` reads like a PLC rung: *if* the condition is true, output this, *else* output that. `IF(I2>=30,\"Long\",\"Short\")` is the formula version of a comparator feeding a coil.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-shift-handover-questions","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Shift handover questions","kind":"hands","md":"Add a new sheet (the `+` at the bottom), name it `Answers`, and use sort, filter, the status bar and formulas to answer these. Write the answer **and how you got it** next to each one.\n\n1. Which zone had the most events? (Filter Zone one at a time, or sort by Zone and count.)\n2. Which zone lost the most minutes?\n3. How many minor stops were there, and how many minutes did they cost in total?\n4. Were Days or Nights worse, by minutes?\n5. Which single asset appears most often?\n6. What percentage of total minutes came from breakdowns?\n\nThen tidy the sheet: bold headers, a fill colour on the header row, borders around the data, and numbers with sensible decimals (Home → Increase/Decrease Decimal).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"Watch these **after** you've tried the tasks. They'll make more sense once you've hit the problems yourself.\n\n- **Kevin Stratvert** (YouTube): search *\"Kevin Stratvert Excel tutorial for beginners\"*. A complete beginner walkthrough, about an hour. Watch at 1.25× speed.\n- **Leila Gharani** (YouTube): search *\"Leila Gharani absolute relative references\"*. A short, clear explanation of the `$` signs.\n- **ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/shortcuts](https://exceljet.net/shortcuts) lists the shortcuts. Learn five a week."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. You type `=B2*C2` in `D2` and copy it to `D5`. What formula is in `D5`?\n2. You have a target downtime in cell `F1`. Write a formula for `G2` that you can fill down to show `\"Over\"` if `I2` is above the target, otherwise `\"OK\"`.\n3. Why does a blank row in the middle of your data cause problems?\n4. You select a column and the status bar shows *Count* but no *Sum*. What does that tell you about the data?\n5. Write a formula that converts the minutes in `I2` to hours and rounds to 1 decimal place. (Hint: look up `ROUND`.)\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong> (try first)<\/summary>\n\n1. `=B5*C5`. Both references are relative, so both move down 3 rows.\n2. `=IF(I2>$F$1,\"Over\",\"OK\")`. The target must be locked with `$`, or it'll slide down as you fill.\n3. Excel treats the blank row as the end of the list. Sort, filter, the fill-handle double-click and later Tables and PivotTables all stop at the gap, so part of your data silently gets left out.\n4. The cells contain **text**, not numbers. They might look like numbers, but they're stored as text, often because of a stray space or because the data came from an export. You'll learn to fix this in Week 4.\n5. `=ROUND(I2/60,1)`\n\n**Weekend answers:** 1) Primary Packing, 49 events · 2) Primary Packing, 660 min · 3) 110 minor stops, 955 min · 4) Nights, 1080 min vs Days 851 · 5) LD-EOL-016, 5 times · 6) 976 / 1931 = **50.5 %**. Half the time lost came from 14 breakdowns, and the other half from 110 small stops. That's a good thing to notice: the minor stops add up.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 2, 3, 5 and 7 (save as `.xlsx`, freeze headers, check the status bar, filter to breakdowns) and Session 2 steps 1, 3, 5 and 6 (`SUM`, the fill handle, the `$I$126` fix and the `IF` column). Then answer the six weekend handover questions, even if roughly.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani video on absolute and relative references, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and go through the Check yourself questions in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The hour-long Kevin Stratvert walkthrough, the ExcelJet shortcuts list and the weekend tidy-up (fill colours, borders, decimals) can all wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Saved the CSV as `.xlsx` with frozen headers and filters\n- [ ] Used the status bar to check a total\n- [ ] Wrote `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `IF`\n- [ ] Used `$` to lock a reference and know why\n- [ ] Answered all six handover questions"}],"quiz":[{"id":"1-1","n":1,"q":"You type `=B2*C2` in `D2` and copy it to `D5`. What formula is in `D5`?","a":"`=B5*C5`. Both references are relative, so both move down 3 rows."},{"id":"1-2","n":2,"q":"You have a target downtime in cell `F1`. Write a formula for `G2` that you can fill down to show `\"Over\"` if `I2` is above the target, otherwise `\"OK\"`.","a":"`=IF(I2>$F$1,\"Over\",\"OK\")`. The target must be locked with `$`, or it'll slide down as you fill."},{"id":"1-3","n":3,"q":"Why does a blank row in the middle of your data cause problems?","a":"Excel treats the blank row as the end of the list. Sort, filter, the fill-handle double-click and later Tables and PivotTables all stop at the gap, so part of your data silently gets left out."},{"id":"1-4","n":4,"q":"You select a column and the status bar shows *Count* but no *Sum*. What does that tell you about the data?","a":"The cells contain **text**, not numbers. They might look like numbers, but they're stored as text, often because of a stray space or because the data came from an export. You'll learn to fix this in Week 4."},{"id":"1-5","n":5,"q":"Write a formula that converts the minutes in `I2` to hours and rounds to 1 decimal place. (Hint: look up `ROUND`.)","a":"`=ROUND(I2/60,1)`"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Kevin Stratvert** (YouTube): search *\"Kevin Stratvert Excel tutorial for beginners\"*. A complete beginner walkthrough, about an hour. Watch at 1.25× speed.","searches":["Kevin Stratvert Excel tutorial for beginners"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Leila Gharani** (YouTube): search *\"Leila Gharani absolute relative references\"*. A short, clear explanation of the `$` signs.","searches":["Leila Gharani absolute relative references"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/shortcuts](https://exceljet.net/shortcuts) lists the shortcuts. Learn five a week.","searches":[],"urls":["https://exceljet.net/shortcuts"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 2, 3, 5 and 7 (save as `.xlsx`, freeze headers, check the status bar, filter to breakdowns) and Session 2 steps 1, 3, 5 and 6 (`SUM`, the fill handle, the `$I$126` fix and the `IF` column). Then answer the six weekend handover questions, even if roughly."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani video on absolute and relative references, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and go through the Check yourself questions in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The hour-long Kevin Stratvert walkthrough, the ExcelJet shortcuts list and the weekend tidy-up (fill colours, borders, decimals) can all wait."}}},{"week":2,"phase":0,"title":"Summarising and charting","path":"course/phase-0-foundations/week-02-excel-summaries-charts.md","ticks":["Zone summary built with `COUNTIF`/`SUMIFS`, totals reconcile to 124 / 1931","Extracted zone code with `MID` and day name with `TEXT`","Drop-down that drives a formula","One clean, titled, decluttered chart","Phase 0 done. Next week you start the real course."],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 0 · Foundations** · Mon 9 Nov – Sun 15 Nov 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nLast week you answered questions by filtering and reading the status bar. That works once, but next week you'd have to do it all again. This week you build formulas that do the summarising for you, so new data means new answers automatically.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- summarise a list with `COUNTIF(S)`, `SUMIF(S)` and `AVERAGEIFS`\n- pull pieces out of text and dates (`LEFT`, `MID`, `TEXT`, `WEEKDAY`)\n- add a drop-down list with Data Validation\n- build and format a clear column chart\n\n**You need:** your `week01_downtime.xlsx` from last week. If anything went wrong, start again from `data/week01/shift_downtime_log.csv`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-conditional-sums-and-counts","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Conditional sums and counts","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Add a new sheet called `Summary`. In `A1` type `Zone`, in `B1` `Events`, in `C1` `Minutes`, in `D1` `Avg mins`.\n2. In `A2:A8` type the seven zones **exactly** as they appear in the data: Cooking, Drying, Flaking, Forming, Baking, Primary Packing, End of Line.\n3. In `B2` type:\n\n   ```\n   =COUNTIF(Sheet1!$D:$D,A2)\n   ```\n\n   (If your data sheet has a different name, use that instead of `Sheet1`. The easiest way is to type `=COUNTIF(`, click across to the data sheet, click column D, and Excel writes the reference for you.)\n4. In `C2`: `=SUMIF(Sheet1!$D:$D,A2,Sheet1!$I:$I)`\n5. In `D2`: `=AVERAGEIF(Sheet1!$D:$D,A2,Sheet1!$I:$I)`\n6. Select `B2:D2` and fill down to row 8. Add totals in row 9 with `SUM`. Check that **total Events = 124** and **total Minutes = 1931**. If they don't match, a zone name is misspelt.\n7. **Two conditions.** Add `E1` = `Breakdown mins` and `F1` = `Minor stop mins`. In `E2`:\n\n   ```\n   =SUMIFS(Sheet1!$I:$I,Sheet1!$D:$D,$A2,Sheet1!$G:$G,\"Breakdown\")\n   ```\n\n   In `F2` use the same formula with `\"Minor stop\"`. Fill down.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`COUNTIF(range, criteria)` counts cells that match. `SUMIF(range, criteria, sum_range)` adds up the numbers alongside the matches. The **S** versions (`COUNTIFS`, `SUMIFS`, `AVERAGEIFS`) take as many condition pairs as you like, all of which must be true (AND logic).\n\nWatch the argument order: **`SUMIFS` puts the sum range first**, but `SUMIF` puts it last. Microsoft made that choice years ago and it trips everyone up. Just use `SUMIFS` everywhere; it works with one condition too.\n\nCriteria can be comparisons in quotes: `COUNTIF(Sheet1!I:I,\">=30\")` counts stops of 30 minutes or more.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-text-dates-and-drop-downs","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Text, dates and drop-downs","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Back on the data sheet, add a column `Zone Code` with `=MID(E2,4,3)`. The asset `LD-PPK-021` becomes `PPK`. `MID(text, start, length)` takes 3 characters starting at character 4.\n2. Add `Day` with `=TEXT(A2,\"ddd\")`, which gives `Mon`, `Tue` and so on.\n   - If you get `#VALUE!`, your dates were read as text. Check that they're right-aligned in the cell; real dates are numbers, and numbers align right. Excel set to UK regional settings reads `01/06/2026` as 1 June. If yours didn't, see Check yourself, Q5.\n3. Add `Weekend?` with `=IF(WEEKDAY(A2,2)>5,\"Weekend\",\"Weekday\")`. `WEEKDAY(...,2)` numbers Monday as 1 and Sunday as 7.\n4. Add `Label` with `=E2&\" - \"&F2`, which joins the asset code and description with `&`.\n5. **Drop-down filter on your Summary sheet:**\n   - In `H1` type `Pick a shift:`. Select `I1`, go to Data → Data Validation → Allow: *List*, Source: `Days,Nights`.\n   - In `H3` type `Minutes for that shift`, and in `I3`: `=SUMIFS(Sheet1!$I:$I,Sheet1!$C:$C,I1)`.\n   - Change the drop-down and watch the number change. This is the seed of every dashboard you'll ever build.\n\n### Why it works\n\nDates in Excel are **numbers**: 1 June 2026 is 46174, which is the number of days since 1 January 1900. Formatting just changes how that number is displayed. That's why you can subtract one date from another to get the days between them, and why `TEXT(A2,\"ddd\")` can turn it into a day name.\n\nText functions (`LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `MID`, `LEN`, `TRIM`, `UPPER`) let you slice up codes. Asset numbering schemes almost always have structure, like site-zone-number here, and pulling that structure out is a daily task in maintenance data.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-two-week-downtime-summary","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Two-week downtime summary","kind":"hands","md":"On the `Summary` sheet:\n\n1. Sort your zone table by Minutes, largest first (select `A1:F8`, then Data → Sort by Minutes, Largest to Smallest).\n2. **Insert a chart:** select the Zone and Minutes columns (hold `Ctrl` to select non-adjacent columns), then Insert → Clustered Column.\n3. **Format it properly**, because this is what separates a chart people read from one they ignore:\n   - Title that says the finding, not the topic: *\"Primary Packing lost the most time (1–14 June)\"*, not *\"Minutes by Zone\"*.\n   - Delete the gridlines and the legend (you only have one series).\n   - Add data labels (the `+` next to the chart → Data Labels).\n   - Make all the bars grey except the top one, which gets a strong colour: click once to select all bars, then click again to select only one bar.\n4. Add a second chart: a **stacked column** of Breakdown mins vs Minor stop mins by zone.\n5. Write two sentences under the charts: what you'd tell the shift manager.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: search *\"Leila Gharani SUMIFS COUNTIFS\"*.\n- **Excel Campus (Jon Acampora)**: search *\"Excel Campus data validation drop down list\"*.\n- **Storytelling with Data (Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic)**: search *\"storytelling with data declutter chart\"*. A 10-minute talk on why the grey-bars-plus-one-colour trick works. You'll use this idea for the rest of the course.\n\nFunction reference when you get stuck: [exceljet.net/functions](https://exceljet.net/functions)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Count the stops in Primary Packing that lasted 10 minutes or more.\n2. Total minutes for **Breakdowns on Nights**.\n3. From `LD-EOL-016`, extract just the number `016` as text.\n4. What does `=DATE(2026,6,14)-DATE(2026,6,1)` return, and why?\n5. Your dates came in as text like `01/06/2026` and are left-aligned. Give one way to fix them.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `=COUNTIFS(Sheet1!D:D,\"Primary Packing\",Sheet1!I:I,\">=10\")`\n2. `=SUMIFS(Sheet1!I:I,Sheet1!G:G,\"Breakdown\",Sheet1!C:C,\"Nights\")`\n3. `=RIGHT(E2,3)`, or `=MID(E2,8,3)`\n4. `13`: dates are day numbers, so subtracting gives the days between them.\n5. Select the column, go to Data → Text to Columns → Next → Next → choose *Date: DMY* → Finish. (Or use `=DATEVALUE(A2)` in a helper column. In Week 7 Power Query will do this properly every time.)\n\n**Reference numbers** so you can check your Summary sheet:\n\n| Zone | Events | Minutes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary Packing | 49 | 660 |\n| End of Line | 32 | 467 |\n| Forming | 22 | 420 |\n| Drying | 3 | 148 |\n| Flaking | 7 | 145 |\n| Baking | 5 | 58 |\n| Cooking | 6 | 33 |\n| **Total** | **124** | **1931** |\n\nShifts: Days 851 min, Nights 1080 min. The worst day of the week was Tuesday (392 min).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 0 done-when, so the tick-off list is not skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 7 with totals reconciling to 124 / 1931, Session 2 steps 1, 2 and 5 (`MID`, `TEXT`, the drop-down driving `SUMIFS`), and weekend steps 1 to 3 for one clean, titled chart.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani `SUMIFS` video and the Storytelling with Data declutter talk, read the \"Why it works\" parts on argument order and dates as numbers, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The second chart (weekend step 4, the stacked column), the `Weekend?` and `Label` columns (Session 2 steps 3 and 4) and the two written sentences are polish you can come back to."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Zone summary built with `COUNTIF`/`SUMIFS`, totals reconcile to 124 / 1931\n- [ ] Extracted zone code with `MID` and day name with `TEXT`\n- [ ] Drop-down that drives a formula\n- [ ] One clean, titled, decluttered chart\n- [ ] Phase 0 done. Next week you start the real course."}],"quiz":[{"id":"2-1","n":1,"q":"Count the stops in Primary Packing that lasted 10 minutes or more.","a":"`=COUNTIFS(Sheet1!D:D,\"Primary Packing\",Sheet1!I:I,\">=10\")`"},{"id":"2-2","n":2,"q":"Total minutes for **Breakdowns on Nights**.","a":"`=SUMIFS(Sheet1!I:I,Sheet1!G:G,\"Breakdown\",Sheet1!C:C,\"Nights\")`"},{"id":"2-3","n":3,"q":"From `LD-EOL-016`, extract just the number `016` as text.","a":"`=RIGHT(E2,3)`, or `=MID(E2,8,3)`"},{"id":"2-4","n":4,"q":"What does `=DATE(2026,6,14)-DATE(2026,6,1)` return, and why?","a":"`13`: dates are day numbers, so subtracting gives the days between them."},{"id":"2-5","n":5,"q":"Your dates came in as text like `01/06/2026` and are left-aligned. Give one way to fix them.","a":"Select the column, go to Data → Text to Columns → Next → Next → choose *Date: DMY* → Finish. (Or use `=DATEVALUE(A2)` in a helper column. In Week 7 Power Query will do this properly every time.)"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: search *\"Leila Gharani SUMIFS COUNTIFS\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani SUMIFS COUNTIFS"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Excel Campus (Jon Acampora)**: search *\"Excel Campus data validation drop down list\"*.","searches":["Excel Campus data validation drop down list"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Storytelling with Data (Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic)**: search *\"storytelling with data declutter chart\"*. A 10-minute talk on why the grey-bars-plus-one-colour trick works. You'll use this idea for the rest of the course.","searches":["storytelling with data declutter chart"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 0 done-when, so the tick-off list is not skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 7 with totals reconciling to 124 / 1931, Session 2 steps 1, 2 and 5 (`MID`, `TEXT`, the drop-down driving `SUMIFS`), and weekend steps 1 to 3 for one clean, titled chart."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani `SUMIFS` video and the Storytelling with Data declutter talk, read the \"Why it works\" parts on argument order and dates as numbers, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The second chart (weekend step 4, the stacked column), the `Weekend?` and `Label` columns (Session 2 steps 3 and 4) and the two written sentences are polish you can come back to."}}},{"week":3,"phase":1,"title":"Structured Tables and XLOOKUP","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-03-tables-xlookup.md","ticks":["Three named Tables: `tblWO`, `tblAssets`, `tblPM`","XLOOKUP columns for manufacturer, install date and PM frequency, with no `#N/A`","Days Late and Asset Age calculated","Summary sheet survives the \"paste more rows\" test"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 16 Nov – Sun 22 Nov 2026 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 1 goal:** stop hand-editing spreadsheets. By Week 8 you'll drop a new export into a folder, press *Refresh All*, and the whole PM compliance workbook updates itself.\n\nThis week you meet the two features that make that possible: **Tables**, which grow automatically, and **XLOOKUP**, which joins information from one list onto another. Every CMMS export is missing something you need, like asset criticality or PM frequency, and XLOOKUP is how you bring it in.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- turn a range into a named Table and use structured references like `[@Minutes]`\n- use `XLOOKUP` to pull asset and PM details into a work order list\n- handle \"not found\" results cleanly\n- calculate days late and asset age with date arithmetic\n\n**Files:** `data/exports/work_orders/WO_Export_2026-06.csv` (one month of work orders, 394 rows), `data/csv/assets.csv` (292 assets) and `data/csv/pm_tasks.csv` (475 PM routines).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-tables","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Tables","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Open a new blank workbook and save it as `week03_lookups.xlsx`.\n2. Open `WO_Export_2026-06.csv`, select all, copy it, and paste it into `A1` of a sheet called `WOs`. Do the same for `assets.csv` → sheet `Assets` and `pm_tasks.csv` → sheet `PMs`.\n3. On the `WOs` sheet, click any cell in the data and press **`Ctrl+T`** → tick *My table has headers* → OK.\n4. **Name the Table** (this matters): with a cell selected, go to Table Design → Table Name and type `tblWO`. Do the same for the other two sheets: `tblAssets` and `tblPM`.\n5. Try out what a Table gives you:\n   - Scroll down. The column letters are replaced by your headers.\n   - Table Design → tick **Total Row**. In the total under *Labour Hrs*, pick *Sum* from the drop-down: **316.16** hours in June.\n   - Type anything in the first empty row below the Table. The Table grows to include it. Press `Ctrl+Z` to undo.\n6. **Structured references.** In the first empty column to the right of the Table, type the header `Labour Mins`. In the cell below it, type `=` and then click the *Labour Hrs* cell in the same row. Excel writes `=[@[Labour Hrs]]`. Finish it as `=[@[Labour Hrs]]*60` and press Enter. The formula fills the whole column by itself.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA Table is a list that Excel **knows** is a list. It knows where the list starts and ends, and what each column is called. So:\n\n- formulas that refer to `tblWO[Labour Hrs]` always cover the whole column, however many rows you add, so you never need to update `I2:I125` again\n- calculated columns fill automatically and stay consistent\n- PivotTables, charts and Power Query that point at the Table grow with it\n\n`[@Column]` means \"this row's value in Column\". `tblWO[Column]` means \"the whole column\". Everything from here on uses Tables.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-xlookup","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): XLOOKUP","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nThe export has the asset code but not the manufacturer, install date or PM frequency. You'll bring those in.\n\n1. Add a column to `tblWO` called `Manufacturer`:\n\n   ```\n   =XLOOKUP([@Asset], tblAssets[asset_id], tblAssets[manufacturer])\n   ```\n\n   Read it as: *look for this row's Asset in the asset_id column, and return the matching manufacturer.*\n2. Add `Install Date` the same way, returning `tblAssets[install_date]`. Format the column as a date. (If it shows text like `2018-12-01`, that's fine for now. Week 4 deals with it.)\n3. Add `PM Frequency (days)`:\n\n   ```\n   =XLOOKUP([@[PM Ref]], tblPM[pm_id], tblPM[frequency_days], \"\")\n   ```\n\n   The fourth argument is what to return **if not found**. Reactive jobs have no PM Ref, so without it you'd get a column full of `#N/A`.\n4. Add `Days Late` for PM rows:\n\n   ```\n   =IF(OR([@[WO Type]]<>\"PM\", [@Completed]=\"\"), \"\", [@Completed]-[@Due])\n   ```\n\n   A positive number means late, and zero or negative means on time.\n5. Add `Asset Age (yrs)`: `=ROUND((DATE(2026,6,30)-[@[Install Date]])/365.25,1)`.\n6. **Look something up the other way.** On a new sheet, type `WO-111700` in `A1`. In `B1`, return the asset description for that work order. In `C1`, return its failure code. (Hint: XLOOKUP can return from any column; it doesn't care which side of the lookup column it's on.)\n\n### Why it works\n\n`XLOOKUP(lookup_value, lookup_array, return_array, [if_not_found], [match_mode], [search_mode])`\n\nIt's the modern replacement for `VLOOKUP`. You'll still see VLOOKUP in old spreadsheets at work, so here's why XLOOKUP is better:\n\n| | VLOOKUP | XLOOKUP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Return column | counted by number (breaks if you insert a column) | points at the actual column |\n| Look left | no | yes |\n| Exact match by default | no (you must add `FALSE`) | yes |\n| Not-found value | wrap in `IFERROR` | built in |\n\nThe idea that matters more than the function is the **key**. `asset_id` uniquely identifies an asset, so it can link the work order list to the asset list. Phase 2 is all about keys, and this is your first taste.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-june-work-order-fact-sheet","title":"Weekend build (2 h): June work order fact sheet","kind":"hands","md":"Build a `Summary` sheet that uses formulas pointing at the Table columns (`tblWO[...]`) to answer:\n\n1. How many work orders of each type (PM, Reactive, Corrective)?\n2. Total labour hours by zone for Reactive work only.\n3. How many PMs were completed late, and what was the average days late of those?\n4. Which manufacturer's assets had the most work orders?\n5. How many June work orders were on assets installed after 1 July 2024 (new kit)?\n\nThen **test that it's robust**: copy 10 rows from `WO_Export_2026-05.csv` and paste them directly under the last row of `tblWO`. The Table should grow, the calculated columns should fill, and your Summary totals should all change. If any didn't, that formula was pointing at a fixed range. Fix it, then undo the paste.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Excel tables\"* and *\"Leila Gharani XLOOKUP\"*.\n- **Excel Campus**: *\"Excel Campus structured references\"*.\n- **Microsoft Support**: search *\"XLOOKUP function Microsoft Support\"* for the official page with examples."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Write a formula that returns the zone **code** (e.g. `PPK`) for an asset, using `tblAssets`.\n2. Your XLOOKUP returns `#N/A` for asset `ld-ppk-005`, but `LD-PPK-005` exists. Why, and what's the quick fix?\n3. What's the difference between `tblWO[Labour Hrs]` and `[@[Labour Hrs]]`?\n4. Write a single formula that counts PM work orders in `tblWO` that were completed late. (Use your `Days Late` column.)\n5. Why is a Table better than `A1:P395` as the source for a chart you'll update monthly?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `=XLOOKUP([@Asset], tblAssets[asset_id], tblAssets[zone_code])`\n2. Actually, XLOOKUP isn't case-sensitive, so case alone won't cause it. The real culprit is usually **hidden spaces** (`\" LD-PPK-005\"`) or different separators (`LDPPK005`). Use `=XLOOKUP(TRIM([@Asset]), …)` for spaces. Week 4 covers cleaning properly.\n3. `tblWO[Labour Hrs]` is the whole column (use it in `SUM`, `COUNTIFS` and so on). `[@[Labour Hrs]]` is the value on the current row (use it in calculated columns).\n4. `=COUNTIFS(tblWO[WO Type],\"PM\",tblWO[Days Late],\">0\")`\n5. The Table grows with new rows, so the chart picks up new data automatically. A fixed range has to be edited by hand every month, and someone always forgets.\n\n**Weekend reference numbers (June 2026):**\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| WO types | PM 336 · Reactive 47 · Corrective 11 (total 394) |\n| Reactive labour by zone | add up `Labour Hrs` with `SUMIFS`; the total is 86.44 h |\n| Late PMs | 30, averaging 5.6 days late (worst 18 days) |\n| Most WOs by manufacturer | Northgate (69) |\n| New-kit WOs | 19 |\n\nFor `WO-111700`: *End of Line Case packer 3*, failure code *Operational - jam*.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 4 and 6 (three named Tables and one structured reference), Session 2 steps 1 to 5 (the XLOOKUP columns, Days Late, Asset Age), and the weekend Summary sheet for questions 1 to 3 followed by the \"paste more rows\" test.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani Excel tables and XLOOKUP videos, read the VLOOKUP versus XLOOKUP table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself questions 2, 3 and 5 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 step 6 (the `WO-111700` lookup), Session 1 step 5 (trying out the Total Row) and weekend questions 4 and 5 can be skipped; the Tables and XLOOKUP pattern are what later weeks need."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Three named Tables: `tblWO`, `tblAssets`, `tblPM`\n- [ ] XLOOKUP columns for manufacturer, install date and PM frequency, with no `#N/A`\n- [ ] Days Late and Asset Age calculated\n- [ ] Summary sheet survives the \"paste more rows\" test"}],"quiz":[{"id":"3-1","n":1,"q":"Write a formula that returns the zone **code** (e.g. `PPK`) for an asset, using `tblAssets`.","a":"`=XLOOKUP([@Asset], tblAssets[asset_id], tblAssets[zone_code])`"},{"id":"3-2","n":2,"q":"Your XLOOKUP returns `#N/A` for asset `ld-ppk-005`, but `LD-PPK-005` exists. Why, and what's the quick fix?","a":"Actually, XLOOKUP isn't case-sensitive, so case alone won't cause it. The real culprit is usually **hidden spaces** (`\" LD-PPK-005\"`) or different separators (`LDPPK005`). Use `=XLOOKUP(TRIM([@Asset]), …)` for spaces. Week 4 covers cleaning properly."},{"id":"3-3","n":3,"q":"What's the difference between `tblWO[Labour Hrs]` and `[@[Labour Hrs]]`?","a":"`tblWO[Labour Hrs]` is the whole column (use it in `SUM`, `COUNTIFS` and so on). `[@[Labour Hrs]]` is the value on the current row (use it in calculated columns)."},{"id":"3-4","n":4,"q":"Write a single formula that counts PM work orders in `tblWO` that were completed late. (Use your `Days Late` column.)","a":"`=COUNTIFS(tblWO[WO Type],\"PM\",tblWO[Days Late],\">0\")`"},{"id":"3-5","n":5,"q":"Why is a Table better than `A1:P395` as the source for a chart you'll update monthly?","a":"The Table grows with new rows, so the chart picks up new data automatically. A fixed range has to be edited by hand every month, and someone always forgets."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Excel tables\"* and *\"Leila Gharani XLOOKUP\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani Excel tables","Leila Gharani XLOOKUP"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Excel Campus**: *\"Excel Campus structured references\"*.","searches":["Excel Campus structured references"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Support**: search *\"XLOOKUP function Microsoft Support\"* for the official page with examples.","searches":["XLOOKUP function Microsoft Support"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 4 and 6 (three named Tables and one structured reference), Session 2 steps 1 to 5 (the XLOOKUP columns, Days Late, Asset Age), and the weekend Summary sheet for questions 1 to 3 followed by the \"paste more rows\" test."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani Excel tables and XLOOKUP videos, read the VLOOKUP versus XLOOKUP table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself questions 2, 3 and 5 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 step 6 (the `WO-111700` lookup), Session 1 step 5 (trying out the Total Row) and weekend questions 4 and 5 can be skipped; the Tables and XLOOKUP pattern are what later weeks need."}}},{"week":4,"phase":1,"title":"Cleaning messy data and formula-based conditional formatting","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-04-cleaning-conditional-formatting.md","ticks":["Profiled the raw file: rows, uniques, spellings, text-numbers","Asset, zone and labour cleaned with formulas, zero NOT FOUND","Duplicates removed (1,470 rows), labour reconciled, and the gap explained","Row-highlighting conditional formatting with `$`-locked formulas"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 23 Nov – Sun 29 Nov 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nReal exports are messy. Someone types `ld-ppk-005`, another person types `LD PPK 005`, a zone gets abbreviated, labour gets entered as `1.5h`. Every one of those breaks a lookup or a total without warning you. This week you learn to **find** mess, **fix** it with formulas, and **highlight** problems automatically.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- spot dirty data quickly (filters, `COUNTIF` checks, `ISNUMBER`)\n- clean text with `TRIM`, `UPPER`, `SUBSTITUTE`, `CLEAN` and `VALUE`\n- remove duplicates safely\n- write conditional formatting rules **with formulas**, such as highlighting a whole row if a PM is overdue\n\n**File:** `data/dirty/wo_export_q1_2026_dirty.csv`. This is January to March 2026, deliberately broken. The clean version is the three monthly exports `WO_Export_2026-01/02/03.csv`, so you can check your work against them.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-find-the-mess","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Find the mess","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Open the dirty file, copy everything into a new workbook `week04_cleaning.xlsx` on a sheet `Raw`, and make it a Table called `tblRaw`.\n2. **Profile it before touching anything.** Add a sheet `Checks` and work out:\n   - Row count: `=ROWS(tblRaw)` gives **1,499**.\n   - Unique WO numbers: `=COUNTA(UNIQUE(tblRaw[WO Number]))` gives **1,470**. So there are 29 duplicate rows.\n   - Zone spellings: `=UNIQUE(tblRaw[Zone])`. This *spills* a list of every distinct value. You'll see *Prim Pack*, *EOL*, *end of line* and *Primary Packing* with a trailing space…\n3. **Use filters to look.** Turn on the filter on the Asset column and scroll the drop-down list. You'll see lower-case codes, codes with spaces, and codes with no dashes.\n4. **Find numbers stored as text.** Add a column `Labour OK?` with `=ISNUMBER([@[Labour Hrs]])`, then filter it to FALSE. You'll find `1.5h`-style entries and blanks.\n5. **Find impossible combinations.** Add `Status Check`:\n\n   ```\n   =IF(AND([@Status]=\"Completed\",[@Completed]=\"\"),\"Completed but no date\",\"\")\n   ```\n\n   Count them: **44**.\n\n### Why it works\n\nMessy data rarely throws an error. It just gives you **wrong totals that look right**. `SUMIFS` by zone will quietly skip the ~70 rows spelt \"Prim Pack\" or \"primary packing\", and the ~50 \"EOL\" ones, and your packing figures will be too low. So the habit to build is: **profile first, count everything, and reconcile** (does my cleaned total match the source total?).\n\n`UNIQUE` is a *dynamic array* function: one formula that returns many cells, which \"spill\" down the sheet. You'll use more of these next week.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-fix-it-with-formulas","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Fix it with formulas","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nAdd these cleaning columns to `tblRaw`. Never overwrite the raw column: keep raw, add clean. That way you can always see what you changed.\n\n1. **Asset (clean)**:\n\n   ```\n   =LET(\n     a, UPPER(TRIM([@Asset])),\n     b, SUBSTITUTE(a,\" \",\"-\"),\n     IF(MID(b,3,1)=\"-\", b, LEFT(b,2)&\"-\"&MID(b,3,3)&\"-\"&RIGHT(b,3))\n   )\n   ```\n\n   `LET` lets you name steps inside a formula (`a`, `b`). Read it top to bottom: trim the spaces and upper-case, swap spaces for dashes, and if there are no dashes at all, rebuild the code as `LD-XXX-NNN`.\n2. **Check it worked**: add a column with `=XLOOKUP([@[Asset (clean)]],tblAssets[asset_id],tblAssets[asset_id],\"NOT FOUND\")`. (Copy `assets.csv` into the workbook as `tblAssets` first.) Filter for NOT FOUND. You want zero.\n3. **Zone (clean)**: the best fix isn't a clever formula, it's **looking it up from the asset**, which is the trusted source:\n\n   ```\n   =XLOOKUP([@[Asset (clean)]],tblAssets[asset_id],tblAssets[zone])\n   ```\n\n4. **Labour (clean)**: `=IFERROR(VALUE(SUBSTITUTE([@[Labour Hrs]],\"h\",\"\")),\"\")`\n5. **Remove duplicates**: copy the whole Table to a new sheet `Clean` (paste as values: `Ctrl+Alt+V` → Values). Then Data → Remove Duplicates → tick all columns. Excel should report 29 removed, leaving **1,470**.\n6. **Reconcile**: total `Labour (clean)` on the Clean sheet. Then open the three clean monthly exports and total their Labour Hrs (**1,142.17**). Yours will be about **1,124**. The ~18 h gap is the blank labour entries, which can't be recovered. Write that down: knowing what you *couldn't* fix is part of the job.\n\n### Why it works\n\nThe order of operations matters: **trim → standardise case → standardise separators → rebuild structure**. And whenever there's a trusted master list (the asset register), *look values up from it* rather than trying to repair free text. That's the same principle as a CMMS using a pick-list instead of free typing, and it's why data migration projects spend so much time on master data.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-conditional-formatting-wit","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Conditional formatting with formulas","kind":"hands","md":"Use the clean June export from Week 3 (`week03_lookups.xlsx`), or reload `WO_Export_2026-06.csv` into a Table.\n\n1. **Highlight whole rows for late PMs.** Select the whole Table body (click the top-left corner of the Table, avoiding the header). Go to Home → Conditional Formatting → New Rule → *Use a formula to determine which cells to format*:\n\n   ```\n   =AND($F2=\"PM\",$L2<>\"\",$L2>$K2)\n   ```\n\n   (F = WO Type, K = Due, L = Completed. Adjust the letters to match your sheet.) Choose a light red fill. **The `$` before the column letter is the trick**: it locks the column but lets the row move, so every cell in a row checks the same columns.\n2. **Highlight PMs not done at all**: `=AND($F2=\"PM\",$M2<>\"Completed\")` with an amber fill. Put this rule *below* the red one in Manage Rules.\n3. **Data bars on Downtime Mins**: select the column → Conditional Formatting → Data Bars.\n4. **Icon sets for criticality**: not every rule needs a formula. Try Conditional Formatting → Highlight Cells Rules → *Text that Contains* \"A\" on the Criticality column.\n5. **One more formula rule**: highlight any asset whose code appears more than twice in the month: `=COUNTIF($B:$B,$B2)>2`. These are your repeat offenders.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani conditional formatting formula entire row\"*.\n- **MyOnlineTrainingHub (Mynda Treacy)**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub clean data Excel\"*.\n- **ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/functions/let-function](https://exceljet.net/functions/let-function)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why should you keep the raw column and add a clean column, rather than fixing data in place?\n2. A conditional formatting rule `=$L2>$K2` highlights the wrong rows: it's off by one. What's the likely cause?\n3. Write a formula that returns TRUE if an asset code is in the correct `LD-XXX-NNN` format. (Hint: check `LEN`, and check the dashes are at positions 3 and 7.)\n4. After cleaning, your Primary Packing reactive count goes **up** from 25 to 31. Is that good or bad news?\n5. How many distinct assets appear in the Q1 data after cleaning?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. You can audit what changed, re-run the cleaning if the rules were wrong, and prove to someone else that your numbers come from the source. Fixing in place destroys the evidence.\n2. The rule was written for row 2, but you selected the range starting at row 1 (the header), or the other way round. Excel's formula is relative to the **active cell** of your selection. Select from the first data row and write the formula for that row.\n3. `=AND(LEN(A2)=10, MID(A2,3,1)=\"-\", MID(A2,7,1)=\"-\", LEFT(A2,2)=\"LD\")`\n4. Good news for the data, bad news for packing. The extra jobs were always there but hidden by bad zone spellings. Any report built on the dirty data was **under-reporting** packing problems.\n5. **278**. (Before cleaning, the same assets appear under several spellings, so a naive `UNIQUE` count is much higher.)\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1, 2, 4 and 5 (profile the rows, uniques, spellings and text-numbers), Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (clean asset, zone and labour, remove duplicates to 1,470, reconcile and write down the gap), and weekend step 1, the whole-row late PM rule. That is a full 90 minutes, so don't stop to polish.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani video on conditional formatting for an entire row, read both \"Why it works\" parts (profile first and reconcile, then trim, case, separators, structure), and answer Check yourself questions 1, 2 and 4 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 1 step 3 (scrolling the filter drop-downs) and weekend steps 3 to 5 (data bars, the \"Text that Contains\" rule and the repeat offender rule) can be dropped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Profiled the raw file: rows, uniques, spellings, text-numbers\n- [ ] Asset, zone and labour cleaned with formulas, zero NOT FOUND\n- [ ] Duplicates removed (1,470 rows), labour reconciled, and the gap explained\n- [ ] Row-highlighting conditional formatting with `$`-locked formulas"}],"quiz":[{"id":"4-1","n":1,"q":"Why should you keep the raw column and add a clean column, rather than fixing data in place?","a":"You can audit what changed, re-run the cleaning if the rules were wrong, and prove to someone else that your numbers come from the source. Fixing in place destroys the evidence."},{"id":"4-2","n":2,"q":"A conditional formatting rule `=$L2>$K2` highlights the wrong rows: it's off by one. What's the likely cause?","a":"The rule was written for row 2, but you selected the range starting at row 1 (the header), or the other way round. Excel's formula is relative to the **active cell** of your selection. Select from the first data row and write the formula for that row."},{"id":"4-3","n":3,"q":"Write a formula that returns TRUE if an asset code is in the correct `LD-XXX-NNN` format. (Hint: check `LEN`, and check the dashes are at positions 3 and 7.)","a":"`=AND(LEN(A2)=10, MID(A2,3,1)=\"-\", MID(A2,7,1)=\"-\", LEFT(A2,2)=\"LD\")`"},{"id":"4-4","n":4,"q":"After cleaning, your Primary Packing reactive count goes **up** from 25 to 31. Is that good or bad news?","a":"Good news for the data, bad news for packing. The extra jobs were always there but hidden by bad zone spellings. Any report built on the dirty data was **under-reporting** packing problems."},{"id":"4-5","n":5,"q":"How many distinct assets appear in the Q1 data after cleaning?","a":"**278**. (Before cleaning, the same assets appear under several spellings, so a naive `UNIQUE` count is much higher.)"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani conditional formatting formula entire row\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani conditional formatting formula entire row"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**MyOnlineTrainingHub (Mynda Treacy)**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub clean data Excel\"*.","searches":["MyOnlineTrainingHub clean data Excel"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/functions/let-function](https://exceljet.net/functions/let-function).","searches":[],"urls":["https://exceljet.net/functions/let-function"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1, 2, 4 and 5 (profile the rows, uniques, spellings and text-numbers), Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (clean asset, zone and labour, remove duplicates to 1,470, reconcile and write down the gap), and weekend step 1, the whole-row late PM rule. That is a full 90 minutes, so don't stop to polish."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani video on conditional formatting for an entire row, read both \"Why it works\" parts (profile first and reconcile, then trim, case, separators, structure), and answer Check yourself questions 1, 2 and 4 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 1 step 3 (scrolling the filter drop-downs) and weekend steps 3 to 5 (data bars, the \"Text that Contains\" rule and the repeat offender rule) can be dropped this week."}}},{"week":5,"phase":1,"title":"Dynamic arrays, LET and LAMBDA","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-05-dynamic-arrays-lambda.md","ticks":["FILTER with a zone drop-down and an \"if empty\" message","Top-5 list with TAKE + SORTBY","SEQUENCE-driven June calendar with a chart","Compliance calculated with LET","At least three LAMBDA functions in Name Manager"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 30 Nov – Sun 6 Dec 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nOld Excel worked one cell at a time. Modern Excel can return a whole list from **one formula**: an overdue list, a top-10 or a calendar, and it updates the moment the data changes. This is how you build \"live\" report sheets without PivotTables or copy-pasting.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use `FILTER`, `UNIQUE`, `SORT`, `SORTBY`, `TAKE` and `SEQUENCE`\n- reference a spilled range with `#`\n- tidy long formulas with `LET`\n- write your own reusable function with `LAMBDA`\n\n**File:** your `week03_lookups.xlsx` with `tblWO` (June 2026). If you don't have it, load `WO_Export_2026-06.csv` into a Table called `tblWO`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-filter-unique-sort","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): FILTER, UNIQUE, SORT","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nMake a new sheet `Live`.\n\n1. **Every PM not completed in June**: in `A3`:\n\n   ```\n   =FILTER(tblWO[[WO Number]:[Asset Description]], (tblWO[WO Type]=\"PM\")*(tblWO[Status]<>\"Completed\"))\n   ```\n\n   **37 rows** spill out. You typed one formula and got an entire report.\n2. **Filter to one zone with a drop-down.** In `B1`, add a Data Validation list with source `=UNIQUE(tblWO[Zone])`. (Validation can't point at a spill directly, so first put `=SORT(UNIQUE(tblWO[Zone]))` in a spare cell, say `Z1`, and use `=$Z$1#` as the list source.) Now change the formula in `A3` to add a third condition:\n\n   ```\n   =FILTER(tblWO[[WO Number]:[Asset Description]],\n     (tblWO[WO Type]=\"PM\")*(tblWO[Status]<>\"Completed\")*(tblWO[Zone]=B1),\n     \"Nothing outstanding\")\n   ```\n\n   Pick *Primary Packing*: **9 rows**.\n3. **Top 5 downtime jobs**: in a new area:\n\n   ```\n   =TAKE(SORTBY(tblWO[[WO Number]:[Asset Description]], tblWO[Downtime Mins], -1), 5)\n   ```\n\n   The top row should be `LD-EOL-016`, End of Line Conveyor 6, with 253 minutes.\n4. **The `#` reference**: if your UNIQUE zone list spilled from `Z1`, then `=ROWS(Z1#)` returns 7. `Z1#` means \"the whole spill, however big it is today\".\n\n### Why it works\n\n`FILTER(array, include, [if_empty])`: the *include* part is a column of TRUE/FALSE values. **Multiplying** conditions means AND (TRUE×TRUE = 1, anything×FALSE = 0). **Adding** them means OR. That's the same Boolean logic as series and parallel contacts on a PLC rung.\n\nIf the spill area isn't empty, you get `#SPILL!`. Clear the cells in the way.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-let-sequence-and-a-live-calend","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): LET, SEQUENCE and a live calendar","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **SEQUENCE** builds lists of numbers. Try `=SEQUENCE(10)`, then `=SEQUENCE(1,7)`, then `=SEQUENCE(5,7,1,1)`.\n2. **A June calendar of PMs due per day**: in a new sheet:\n   - `A2`: `=SEQUENCE(30,1,DATE(2026,6,1),1)`, formatted as dates. That's 30 days.\n   - `B2`: `=COUNTIFS(tblWO[WO Type],\"PM\",tblWO[Due],A2#)`\n\n     Because `A2#` is a list, `COUNTIFS` returns a list too: one count per day. This is called **lifting**.\n   - `C2`: `=COUNTIFS(tblWO[WO Type],\"PM\",tblWO[Due],A2#,tblWO[Status],\"Completed\")`\n   - Add a line chart of B and C.\n3. **LET for readable formulas.** This calculates on-time PM compliance for June:\n\n   ```\n   =LET(\n     isPM,   tblWO[WO Type]=\"PM\",\n     due,    SUM(--isPM),\n     ontime, SUM(isPM*(tblWO[Completed]<>\"\")*(tblWO[Completed]<=tblWO[Due])),\n     ontime/due\n   )\n   ```\n\n   Each name is defined once and used later. The `--` converts TRUE/FALSE to 1/0. Format as %. You should get about **80 %**.\n\n> Note: this counts PMs **raised** in June (that's what the export contains). Proper compliance counts PMs **due** in the period, which needs several months of data combined. You'll do that properly with Power Query in Week 7.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`LET(name1, value1, name2, value2, …, result)` works like declaring tags before the logic. It makes long formulas readable, and faster too, because each step is calculated once.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-your-own-functions-with-la","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Your own functions with LAMBDA","kind":"hands","md":"1. Go to Formulas → Name Manager → New.\n   - Name: `ZONECODE`\n   - Refers to: `=LAMBDA(asset, MID(asset,4,3))`\n2. Now `=ZONECODE(\"LD-PPK-005\")` returns `PPK` anywhere in the workbook.\n3. Create `DAYSLATE`:\n\n   ```\n   =LAMBDA(due, done, IF(OR(due=\"\",done=\"\"), \"\", done-due))\n   ```\n\n4. Create `ONTIME`, which returns TRUE if done is on or before due:\n\n   ```\n   =LAMBDA(due, done, AND(done<>\"\", due<>\"\", done<=due))\n   ```\n\n5. Create a `CLEANASSET` function from last week's asset-cleaning `LET` formula. Test it on `\"ld ppk 005\"`, `\" LD-PPK-005 \"` and `\"LDPPK005\"`.\n6. **Build a \"Live\" summary panel** on one sheet using only dynamic array formulas:\n   - outstanding PMs for the zone picked in a drop-down (FILTER)\n   - the top 5 downtime jobs (TAKE + SORTBY)\n   - the list of assets that had a breakdown this month: `=SORT(UNIQUE(FILTER(tblWO[Asset],tblWO[WO Type]=\"Reactive\")))` should give **44 assets**\n\n**Bonus:** if your Excel has `GROUPBY` (newer Microsoft 365 builds do), try `=GROUPBY(tblWO[Zone], tblWO[Downtime Mins], SUM)`. It's a PivotTable in one formula.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani FILTER function\"* and *\"Leila Gharani LAMBDA\"*.\n- **ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/articles/dynamic-array-formulas-in-excel](https://exceljet.net/articles/dynamic-array-formulas-in-excel).\n- **Excel Off The Grid**: *\"Excel Off The Grid LET function\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Write a FILTER that returns Reactive work orders in June with more than 60 downtime minutes.\n2. How do you write OR logic inside FILTER? Give the include argument for \"Reactive OR Corrective\".\n3. What causes `#SPILL!` and how do you fix it?\n4. Write a formula that returns the **3** most frequent failure codes in June. (Hint: `UNIQUE`, then `COUNTIF` against it, then `SORTBY` and `TAKE`.)\n5. Why is a LAMBDA in Name Manager better than copying the same long formula into 20 sheets?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `=FILTER(tblWO, (tblWO[WO Type]=\"Reactive\")*(tblWO[Downtime Mins]>60))`\n2. `(tblWO[WO Type]=\"Reactive\")+(tblWO[WO Type]=\"Corrective\")`. Use `+` for OR.\n3. Something is already in the cells the result wants to spill into, or the result is inside a Table (spills aren't allowed in Tables). Clear the cells or move the formula outside the Table.\n4. ```\n   =LET(fc, UNIQUE(FILTER(tblWO[Failure Code], tblWO[Failure Code]<>\"\")),\n        n,  COUNTIF(tblWO[Failure Code], fc),\n        TAKE(SORTBY(HSTACK(fc,n), n, -1), 3))\n   ```\n   Top three in June: *Mechanical - belt/chain* (13), *Electrical - sensor* (9), *Mechanical - bearing* (8). These counts include Corrective rows as well as Reactive, because both carry a failure code. Filter to Reactive only and belt/chain drops to 9.\n5. One definition, one place to fix. If you change the rule (say, on time now means within 2 days of due), you edit it once and every sheet updates. It's the same reason you'd use a subroutine or an AOI in a PLC instead of copying the same logic onto 20 rungs.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 3 (FILTER with the zone drop-down and \"Nothing outstanding\", then the top 5 with TAKE and SORTBY), Session 2 steps 2 and 3 (the SEQUENCE calendar with its chart and the LET compliance formula), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for three LAMBDA functions.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani FILTER and LAMBDA videos, read the \"Why it works\" note on multiplying for AND and adding for OR, and answer Check yourself questions 2, 3 and 5 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The Session 2 step 1 SEQUENCE warm-ups, weekend step 5 (`CLEANASSET`), the step 6 Live summary panel and the `GROUPBY` bonus can all be left for another week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] FILTER with a zone drop-down and an \"if empty\" message\n- [ ] Top-5 list with TAKE + SORTBY\n- [ ] SEQUENCE-driven June calendar with a chart\n- [ ] Compliance calculated with LET\n- [ ] At least three LAMBDA functions in Name Manager"}],"quiz":[{"id":"5-1","n":1,"q":"Write a FILTER that returns Reactive work orders in June with more than 60 downtime minutes.","a":"`=FILTER(tblWO, (tblWO[WO Type]=\"Reactive\")*(tblWO[Downtime Mins]>60))`"},{"id":"5-2","n":2,"q":"How do you write OR logic inside FILTER? Give the include argument for \"Reactive OR Corrective\".","a":"`(tblWO[WO Type]=\"Reactive\")+(tblWO[WO Type]=\"Corrective\")`. Use `+` for OR."},{"id":"5-3","n":3,"q":"What causes `#SPILL!` and how do you fix it?","a":"Something is already in the cells the result wants to spill into, or the result is inside a Table (spills aren't allowed in Tables). Clear the cells or move the formula outside the Table."},{"id":"5-4","n":4,"q":"Write a formula that returns the **3** most frequent failure codes in June. (Hint: `UNIQUE`, then `COUNTIF` against it, then `SORTBY` and `TAKE`.)","a":"```\n=LET(fc, UNIQUE(FILTER(tblWO[Failure Code], tblWO[Failure Code]<>\"\")),\n     n,  COUNTIF(tblWO[Failure Code], fc),\n     TAKE(SORTBY(HSTACK(fc,n), n, -1), 3))\n```\nTop three in June: *Mechanical - belt/chain* (13), *Electrical - sensor* (9), *Mechanical - bearing* (8). These counts include Corrective rows as well as Reactive, because both carry a failure code. Filter to Reactive only and belt/chain drops to 9."},{"id":"5-5","n":5,"q":"Why is a LAMBDA in Name Manager better than copying the same long formula into 20 sheets?","a":"One definition, one place to fix. If you change the rule (say, on time now means within 2 days of due), you edit it once and every sheet updates. It's the same reason you'd use a subroutine or an AOI in a PLC instead of copying the same logic onto 20 rungs."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani FILTER function\"* and *\"Leila Gharani LAMBDA\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani FILTER function","Leila Gharani LAMBDA"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**ExcelJet**: [exceljet.net/articles/dynamic-array-formulas-in-excel](https://exceljet.net/articles/dynamic-array-formulas-in-excel).","searches":[],"urls":["https://exceljet.net/articles/dynamic-array-formulas-in-excel"]},{"i":2,"md":"**Excel Off The Grid**: *\"Excel Off The Grid LET function\"*.","searches":["Excel Off The Grid LET function"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 3 (FILTER with the zone drop-down and \"Nothing outstanding\", then the top 5 with TAKE and SORTBY), Session 2 steps 2 and 3 (the SEQUENCE calendar with its chart and the LET compliance formula), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for three LAMBDA functions."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani FILTER and LAMBDA videos, read the \"Why it works\" note on multiplying for AND and adding for OR, and answer Check yourself questions 2, 3 and 5 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The Session 2 step 1 SEQUENCE warm-ups, weekend step 5 (`CLEANASSET`), the step 6 Live summary panel and the `GROUPBY` bonus can all be left for another week."}}},{"week":6,"phase":1,"title":"PivotTables, PivotCharts, slicers and a downtime Pareto","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-06-pivottables-pareto.md","ticks":["Count and sum pivots with Show Values As","Dates grouped by month","Slicers and a timeline connected to several pivots","Top-10 downtime Pareto combo chart, matching the reference","Three sentences on what you'd investigate"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 7 Dec – Sun 13 Dec 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nA PivotTable answers \"how much, by what\" in seconds: labour by zone, downtime by asset, jobs by month. You drag fields instead of writing formulas. This week you'll build the core of a maintenance review pack: a **downtime Pareto**, which is the chart every reliability meeting starts with.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build PivotTables with rows, columns, values and filters\n- group dates by month or quarter, and show values as % of total or running total\n- add slicers and timelines that control several pivots at once\n- build a proper Pareto chart (bars plus a cumulative % line)\n\n**Files:** the three Q2 2026 exports `WO_Export_2026-04.csv`, `-05.csv` and `-06.csv`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-your-first-pivottables","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Your first PivotTables","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Combine three months, the old way.** Open the April export, copy all the rows into a new workbook `week06_pivots.xlsx`, then paste May's and June's rows underneath, **without** their header rows. Make it a Table `tblQ2`. You should have **1,391 rows**.\n\n   > Notice how tedious and error-prone that was. Next week Power Query does it in one click, forever. Hold on to that feeling.\n\n2. Click in the Table → Insert → PivotTable → New Worksheet.\n3. Drag **Zone** to *Rows*, **WO Type** to *Columns*, and **WO Number** to *Values*. Excel counts the work orders. Check: Primary Packing has **38 reactive** jobs, and the grand total is **1,391**.\n4. Make a second pivot: **Zone** in *Rows*, **Downtime Mins** in *Values* (Sum), with a filter on **WO Type** = Reactive. Sort largest to smallest (right-click a value → Sort).\n5. **Show values as**: right-click a value → *Show Values As* → *% of Grand Total*. Then try *% of Row Total* on the first pivot: what percentage of each zone's jobs were reactive?\n6. **Group dates**: in a new pivot, drag **Raised** to *Rows*. Excel usually groups by month automatically. If it doesn't, right-click a date → Group → Months. Add Downtime Mins and filter to Reactive. Totals: April **2,516**, May **3,402**, June **2,675** minutes.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA PivotTable is a **summary engine**: it scans every row and groups by whatever you put in Rows/Columns. It works on a *cache*, a copy of the data, so **when the source changes you must Refresh** (right-click → Refresh, or Data → Refresh All). Forgetting to refresh is the number one pivot mistake.\n\nValues can be *Sum*, *Count*, *Average*, *Max* and so on (right-click → Summarize Values By). Count of WO Number counts rows. Sum of WO Number would be nonsense, but Excel won't stop you.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-slicers-timelines-and-pivotcha","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Slicers, timelines and PivotCharts","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Click in any pivot → PivotTable Analyze → **Insert Slicer** → tick *Zone*, *Criticality* and *WO Type*.\n2. **Insert Timeline** → *Raised*. Drag the timeline to pick a single month.\n3. **Connect one slicer to all pivots**: right-click the Zone slicer → *Report Connections* → tick every pivot. Now one click filters the lot.\n4. **PivotChart**: click your Zone/Downtime pivot → PivotTable Analyze → PivotChart → Clustered Bar. Apply the Week 2 formatting rules: a title that states the finding, no gridlines, data labels, and one highlight colour.\n5. **Field buttons**: right-click a button on the chart → *Hide All Field Buttons* for a clean look.\n6. Put the slicers, timeline and two charts on one sheet called `Dashboard`. Set View → untick Gridlines. You've built your first interactive dashboard.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-build-downtime-pareto-q2-2026","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Downtime Pareto, Q2 2026","kind":"hands","md":"A **Pareto** sorts causes from biggest to smallest and adds a cumulative % line. It shows the \"vital few\": usually a small number of assets cause most of the loss.\n\n1. New pivot: **Asset** and **Asset Description** in *Rows* (PivotTable Design → Report Layout → *Show in Tabular Form*, and *Repeat All Item Labels*). Set **WO Type** = Reactive as a filter. Put **Downtime Mins** in *Values* twice.\n2. Sort by Downtime Mins, largest first.\n3. On the second Downtime Mins: right-click → Show Values As → **% Running Total In** → Asset. That's your cumulative %.\n4. Filter to the top 10: click the Rows drop-down → Value Filters → **Top 10** → by Sum of Downtime Mins.\n5. Insert a **Combo chart** from the pivot: Change Chart Type → Combo → Downtime = Clustered Column, Cumulative % = Line on the **secondary axis**. Set the secondary axis maximum to 1 (100 %).\n6. Title it with the finding. Check it against the answers below.\n\n**Then ask the question that matters:** the top 10 is only about 39 % of the downtime here, so the losses are spread out. Two flaking rolls lead, and flow wrappers show up twice. What would you investigate first, and why? Write three sentences. That's the habit of turning a chart into a decision.\n\n> Also try Insert → Chart → **Histogram → Pareto** on a plain (non-pivot) two-column table of asset and downtime. Excel 365 has a built-in Pareto chart type. It's quick but less flexible than the combo version.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani pivot tables beginners\"*.\n- **Excel Campus**: *\"Excel Campus slicers multiple pivot tables\"*.\n- **MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub Pareto chart pivot table\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. You add 50 new rows to `tblQ2` but the pivot doesn't change. Why?\n2. How would you show reactive **labour hours** by technician, for Primary Packing only?\n3. What's the difference between *% of Grand Total* and *% Running Total In*?\n4. Why does a Pareto use a secondary axis?\n5. In Q2, which failure code caused the most reactive downtime minutes?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Pivots don't update live. Right-click → **Refresh**. Because the source is a Table, the new rows are included once you refresh. With a fixed range they wouldn't be, even after a refresh.\n2. Technician in *Rows*, Labour Hrs in *Values* (Sum), and filters (or slicers) of WO Type = Reactive and Zone = Primary Packing.\n3. % of Grand Total is each item's share of the total. % Running Total is the cumulative share as you go down the sorted list, which is what makes the Pareto line.\n4. The bars are minutes (hundreds) and the line is a percentage (0–100 %). On one axis the line would be flat along the bottom.\n5. **Mechanical - bearing**, 2,328 minutes, more than double the next one (*Electrical - sensor*, 1,095).\n\n**Pareto reference (Q2 2026, reactive downtime, 8,593 min total):**\n\n| # | Asset | Description | Mins | Cum % |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | LD-FLK-010 | Flaking roll 2 | 575 | 6.7 % |\n| 2 | LD-FLK-018 | Flaking roll 10 | 513 | 12.7 % |\n| 3 | LD-PPK-030 | Primary Packing Servo drive 3 | 402 | 17.3 % |\n| 4 | LD-PPK-003 | Primary Packing Flow wrapper 3 | 322 | 21.1 % |\n| 5 | LD-PPK-002 | Primary Packing Flow wrapper 2 | 280 | 24.3 % |\n| 6 | LD-EOL-008 | End of Line Label printer applicator 1 | 271 | 27.5 % |\n| 7 | LD-FLK-006 | Flaking Drive motor 6 | 269 | 30.6 % |\n| 8 | LD-EOL-028 | End of Line Drive motor 4 | 261 | 33.7 % |\n| 9 | LD-EOL-016 | End of Line Conveyor 6 | 253 | 36.6 % |\n| 10 | LD-EOL-002 | End of Line Case packer 2 | 242 | 39.4 % |\n\nNote that Pareto by **asset** gives a flat picture here, but Pareto by **failure code** is much sharper: bearings alone are 27 %. Always try more than one cut.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (the 1,391-row `tblQ2`, count and sum pivots, Show Values As, dates grouped by month), Session 2 steps 1 to 3 (slicers and a timeline connected to every pivot), and the full weekend Pareto plus your three sentences. Week 8 reuses this Pareto, so build it properly.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani pivot tables video and the MyOnlineTrainingHub Pareto video, read the \"Why it works\" note on the pivot cache and Refresh, and answer Check yourself questions 1, 3 and 4 in your head. Once the Pareto is built, think through your three sentences on the bus.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 steps 4 to 6 (the formatted PivotChart and the `Dashboard` sheet) and the built-in Histogram Pareto chart can be skipped; the combo Pareto is the one you need."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Count and sum pivots with Show Values As\n- [ ] Dates grouped by month\n- [ ] Slicers and a timeline connected to several pivots\n- [ ] Top-10 downtime Pareto combo chart, matching the reference\n- [ ] Three sentences on what you'd investigate"}],"quiz":[{"id":"6-1","n":1,"q":"You add 50 new rows to `tblQ2` but the pivot doesn't change. Why?","a":"Pivots don't update live. Right-click → **Refresh**. Because the source is a Table, the new rows are included once you refresh. With a fixed range they wouldn't be, even after a refresh."},{"id":"6-2","n":2,"q":"How would you show reactive **labour hours** by technician, for Primary Packing only?","a":"Technician in *Rows*, Labour Hrs in *Values* (Sum), and filters (or slicers) of WO Type = Reactive and Zone = Primary Packing."},{"id":"6-3","n":3,"q":"What's the difference between *% of Grand Total* and *% Running Total In*?","a":"% of Grand Total is each item's share of the total. % Running Total is the cumulative share as you go down the sorted list, which is what makes the Pareto line."},{"id":"6-4","n":4,"q":"Why does a Pareto use a secondary axis?","a":"The bars are minutes (hundreds) and the line is a percentage (0–100 %). On one axis the line would be flat along the bottom."},{"id":"6-5","n":5,"q":"In Q2, which failure code caused the most reactive downtime minutes?","a":"**Mechanical - bearing**, 2,328 minutes, more than double the next one (*Electrical - sensor*, 1,095)."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani pivot tables beginners\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani pivot tables beginners"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Excel Campus**: *\"Excel Campus slicers multiple pivot tables\"*.","searches":["Excel Campus slicers multiple pivot tables"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub Pareto chart pivot table\"*.","searches":["MyOnlineTrainingHub Pareto chart pivot table"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (the 1,391-row `tblQ2`, count and sum pivots, Show Values As, dates grouped by month), Session 2 steps 1 to 3 (slicers and a timeline connected to every pivot), and the full weekend Pareto plus your three sentences. Week 8 reuses this Pareto, so build it properly."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani pivot tables video and the MyOnlineTrainingHub Pareto video, read the \"Why it works\" note on the pivot cache and Refresh, and answer Check yourself questions 1, 3 and 4 in your head. Once the Pareto is built, think through your three sentences on the bus."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 steps 4 to 6 (the formatted PivotChart and the `Dashboard` sheet) and the built-in Histogram Pareto chart can be skipped; the combo Pareto is the one you need."}}},{"week":7,"phase":1,"title":"Power Query, import, clean and combine automatically","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-07-power-query.md","ticks":["All 24 files combined from a folder, 11,902 rows, correct UK dates","PM frequency and asset details merged in, with the row count unchanged","Wide sheet unpivoted to 182 rows","`FolderPath` and `ReportDate` parameters working","\"Drop a file in, Refresh All\" test passed"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 14 Dec – Sun 20 Dec 2026 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is the most important week of Phase 1. **Power Query** records your cleaning steps once and replays them on every refresh. Last week you copied and pasted three files together. This week you'll combine all 24 monthly exports in one go, and next month's file will be picked up just by dropping it in the folder.\n\nThe same tool is built into Power BI, so everything you learn here carries straight into Phase 3.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- import a whole folder of CSVs as one table\n- set data types correctly, including UK dates\n- clean columns (trim, replace, split, conditional columns)\n- **merge** (look up) and **append** (stack) queries\n- **unpivot** a wide sheet into a proper list\n- use a **parameter** so the folder path or report date lives in one place\n\n**Files:** the whole folder `data/exports/work_orders/` (24 files, July 2024 to June 2026), `data/csv/assets.csv`, `data/csv/pm_tasks.csv` and `data/week07/zone_downtime_by_week_wide.csv`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-import-from-folder","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Import from Folder","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. New workbook `week07_powerquery.xlsx`. Go to Data → Get Data → From File → **From Folder** → browse to `data\\exports\\work_orders` → Open.\n2. Excel shows the file list. Click **Combine → Combine & Transform Data**, pick the first file as the sample → OK. The **Power Query Editor** opens.\n3. Look around the editor:\n   - **Queries** pane on the left\n   - the data preview in the middle\n   - **Applied Steps** on the right. Every click you make becomes a step here. Click an earlier step to see the data at that point.\n4. You'll see a `Source.Name` column (the filename). Keep it for now; it's handy for checking.\n5. **Set data types properly:**\n   - Select `Raised` → right-click → Change Type → **Using Locale…** → *Date/Time*, *English (United Kingdom)*. Do the same for `Due` and `Completed` with type *Date*.\n   - `Labour Hrs` → Decimal Number. `Downtime Mins` → Whole Number.\n   - **Delete** any automatic *Changed Type* step that ran before yours if it turned dates into errors (it can happen when the sample file guessed US dates).\n6. Rename the query (right pane, Properties) to `WorkOrders`.\n7. **Home → Close & Load**. A green Table appears with **11,902 rows**. Check the row count in the Queries & Connections pane.\n\n### Why it works\n\nPower Query separates **what you want** (the steps) from **the data** (the files). Refreshing re-runs every step on whatever files are in the folder *now*. Under the hood each step is a line of the **M language**. View → Advanced Editor shows it. You don't need to write M, but it's worth seeing that it's just a recipe.\n\n**Locale matters.** `03/02/2026` is 3 February in the UK and 2 March in the US. Setting the type *using locale* removes the ambiguity regardless of whose PC refreshes the file.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-clean-merge-add-columns","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Clean, merge, add columns","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nOpen the editor again (Data → Queries & Connections → double-click `WorkOrders`).\n\n1. **Add a custom column** `Is PM`: Add Column → Conditional Column → if `WO Type` equals `PM` then `1` else `0`.\n2. **Add a column** `On Time` (Add Column → Custom Column):\n\n   ```\n   if [WO Type] = \"PM\" and [Completed] <> null and [Completed] <= [Due] then 1 else 0\n   ```\n\n   (M is case-sensitive: `null`, `and`, `if … then … else`.)\n3. **Due Month**: select `Due` → Add Column → Date → Month → **Start of Month**. Rename it `Due Month`.\n4. **Import the asset register**: Data → Get Data → From Text/CSV → `assets.csv` → **Transform Data**. Name it `Assets`. Then Close & Load To… → **Only Create Connection** (you don't need it on a sheet).\n5. Do the same for `pm_tasks.csv` → query `PMTasks`, connection only.\n6. **Merge** (this is XLOOKUP in Power Query): in `WorkOrders`, go to Home → Merge Queries → choose `PMTasks` → click `PM Ref` in the top table and `pm_id` in the bottom → Join Kind **Left Outer** → OK. A new column of nested tables appears. Click its expand icon (↔) → tick only `frequency_days` and `task_type` → untick *Use original column name as prefix*.\n7. Merge `Assets` on `Asset` = `asset_id` and bring in `manufacturer`, `asset_type` and `install_date`.\n8. Close & Load. Still **11,902 rows**. (If the count went up, your lookup table had duplicate keys. Always check the row count after a merge.)\n\n### Why it works\n\n**Merge** puts tables side by side by matching a key, which is the same idea as XLOOKUP but done once for the whole table, and it can bring back several columns. **Append** stacks tables with the same columns on top of each other. From Folder is really an automatic append of every file.\n\nJoin kinds are a big idea that comes back in SQL (Phase 2):\n\n| Join | Keeps |\n|---|---|\n| Left Outer | all rows from the first table, and matches from the second if they exist |\n| Inner | only rows that match in both |\n| Left Anti | rows in the first table with **no** match, which is great for finding orphans |","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-unpivot-and-parameters","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Unpivot and parameters","kind":"hands","md":"### Unpivot\n\n1. Get Data → From Text/CSV → `zone_downtime_by_week_wide.csv` → Transform. Look at it: one row per zone, and **a column per week**. It looks fine to a person, but it's terrible for analysis. You can't easily filter by date, and next week's data adds a column, not a row.\n2. Select the `Zone` column → right-click → **Unpivot Other Columns**. You get `Attribute` (the week header) and `Value`.\n3. Rename them `Week` and `Downtime Mins`. Clean `Week`: Transform → Replace Values `w/c ` with nothing → Change Type Using Locale → Date (UK).\n4. Load it. You should have **182 rows** (7 zones × 26 weeks), and the minutes should total **26,837**. Make a PivotChart: line chart of downtime by week, one line per zone.\n\n### Parameters\n\n1. In the editor: Home → Manage Parameters → New → Name `FolderPath`, Type *Text*, Current Value = your full folder path, e.g. `C:\\Users\\Josh\\Documents\\Learning-Data\\data\\exports\\work_orders`.\n2. Open the `WorkOrders` query's **Source** step (the gear icon or the formula bar) and replace the hard-coded path with `FolderPath`. In the formula bar it becomes `= Folder.Files(FolderPath)`.\n3. Create a second parameter `ReportDate`, type *Date*, value `30/06/2026`. Add a custom column `Overdue` to `WorkOrders`:\n\n   ```\n   if [WO Type] = \"PM\" and [Completed] = null and [Due] < ReportDate then 1 else 0\n   ```\n\n4. Now if you move the folder, or want to report \"as at\" another date, you change one value.\n\n**Final test:** copy `WO_Export_2026-06.csv`, rename the copy `WO_Export_TEST.csv`, put it in the folder and Refresh All. The row count should jump by 394. Delete the test file and refresh again to get back to 11,902. **That's the whole point of this week.**","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Power Query beginner\"* and *\"Leila Gharani combine files from folder\"*.\n- **MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub unpivot Power Query\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Power Query documentation Microsoft Learn\"* for the official reference.\n- Book (optional): *M Is for (Data) Monkey* by Ken Puls and Miguel Escobar, the classic Power Query book."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Your refreshed query shows errors in the `Due` column for some rows. What's the likely cause, and how do you investigate it?\n2. When would you use **Append** instead of **Merge**?\n3. You merge `WorkOrders` with `Assets` and the row count rises from 11,902 to 12,050. What happened?\n4. How would you find work orders whose asset doesn't exist in the asset register?\n5. Why is a \"one column per week\" layout bad for analysis?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. A US-locale type change was applied to UK dates, so 13/01/2026 can't be month 13. Click the column's error count, or use Keep Rows → Keep Errors to see the bad rows, then fix the Change Type step to use the UK locale.\n2. **Append** when the tables have the same columns and you want more rows (January + February). **Merge** when you want more columns from a related table (work orders + asset details).\n3. The `Assets` table has duplicate `asset_id` values, so some work orders matched twice. Remove duplicates on the key in `Assets` first, then merge again.\n4. Merge `WorkOrders` with `Assets` using **Left Anti** join. The result is only the orphans. (With this data you should get zero, but try it on last week's dirty file.)\n5. Every new week adds a column, so formulas and charts must be edited; you can't filter or group by date; and tools like PivotTables, Power BI and SQL all expect **one row per observation**. That idea is called *tidy data*.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 7 (From Folder, UK locale types, 11,902 rows), Session 2 steps 2 and 4 to 8 (the `On Time` column, the Assets and PMTasks merges with the row count unchanged), then the weekend unpivot to 182 rows, both parameters and the drop-a-file test. That is more than 90 minutes, so split it across two sittings if you need to, but don't drop any of it: Week 8 is built on this.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Leila Gharani Power Query beginner and combine-from-folder videos, read the \"Why it works\" parts on locale and on join kinds, and answer all five Check yourself questions in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 steps 1 and 3 (the `Is PM` and `Due Month` columns), the PivotChart line chart after the unpivot and the *M Is for (Data) Monkey* book can all wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] All 24 files combined from a folder, 11,902 rows, correct UK dates\n- [ ] PM frequency and asset details merged in, with the row count unchanged\n- [ ] Wide sheet unpivoted to 182 rows\n- [ ] `FolderPath` and `ReportDate` parameters working\n- [ ] \"Drop a file in, Refresh All\" test passed"}],"quiz":[{"id":"7-1","n":1,"q":"Your refreshed query shows errors in the `Due` column for some rows. What's the likely cause, and how do you investigate it?","a":"A US-locale type change was applied to UK dates, so 13/01/2026 can't be month 13. Click the column's error count, or use Keep Rows → Keep Errors to see the bad rows, then fix the Change Type step to use the UK locale."},{"id":"7-2","n":2,"q":"When would you use **Append** instead of **Merge**?","a":"**Append** when the tables have the same columns and you want more rows (January + February). **Merge** when you want more columns from a related table (work orders + asset details)."},{"id":"7-3","n":3,"q":"You merge `WorkOrders` with `Assets` and the row count rises from 11,902 to 12,050. What happened?","a":"The `Assets` table has duplicate `asset_id` values, so some work orders matched twice. Remove duplicates on the key in `Assets` first, then merge again."},{"id":"7-4","n":4,"q":"How would you find work orders whose asset doesn't exist in the asset register?","a":"Merge `WorkOrders` with `Assets` using **Left Anti** join. The result is only the orphans. (With this data you should get zero, but try it on last week's dirty file.)"},{"id":"7-5","n":5,"q":"Why is a \"one column per week\" layout bad for analysis?","a":"Every new week adds a column, so formulas and charts must be edited; you can't filter or group by date; and tools like PivotTables, Power BI and SQL all expect **one row per observation**. That idea is called *tidy data*."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Power Query beginner\"* and *\"Leila Gharani combine files from folder\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani Power Query beginner","Leila Gharani combine files from folder"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub unpivot Power Query\"*.","searches":["MyOnlineTrainingHub unpivot Power Query"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Power Query documentation Microsoft Learn\"* for the official reference.","searches":["Power Query documentation Microsoft Learn"],"urls":[]},{"i":3,"md":"Book (optional): *M Is for (Data) Monkey* by Ken Puls and Miguel Escobar, the classic Power Query book.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 7 (From Folder, UK locale types, 11,902 rows), Session 2 steps 2 and 4 to 8 (the `On Time` column, the Assets and PMTasks merges with the row count unchanged), then the weekend unpivot to 182 rows, both parameters and the drop-a-file test. That is more than 90 minutes, so split it across two sittings if you need to, but don't drop any of it: Week 8 is built on this."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Leila Gharani Power Query beginner and combine-from-folder videos, read the \"Why it works\" parts on locale and on join kinds, and answer all five Check yourself questions in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 steps 1 and 3 (the `Is PM` and `Due Month` columns), the PivotChart line chart after the unpivot and the *M Is for (Data) Monkey* book can all wait."}}},{"week":8,"phase":1,"title":"Power Pivot, first DAX, and the Phase 1 build","path":"course/phase-1-excel-power-query/week-08-power-pivot-phase1-build.md","ticks":["Calendar table and relationships in the Data Model","Five DAX measures working in a Data Model pivot","PM Compliance Workbook: Dashboard, Overdue, Pareto and About sheets","Phase 1 done-when test passed (ticked in the tracker)"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 1 · Advanced Excel & Power Query** · Mon 21 Dec – Sun 27 Dec 2026 · about 5 hours (it's Christmas week, so spread it out or roll some into next week)\n\nThis week you bring it all together into the **PM Compliance Workbook**, the Phase 1 portfolio piece. You'll also take your first step into the Data Model and DAX, the engine behind Power BI, so Phase 3 won't feel foreign.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- load Power Query tables into the **Data Model** and relate them\n- build a **Calendar** table\n- write simple **DAX measures**: `COUNTROWS`, `SUM`, `CALCULATE`, `DIVIDE`\n- pass the Phase 1 *done when* test\n\n**Files:** your `week07_powerquery.xlsx` (or start a fresh workbook `PM_Compliance.xlsx` and rebuild the queries, which is good practice).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-data-model","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The Data Model","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. In Queries & Connections, right-click `WorkOrders` → Load To… → tick **Add this data to the Data Model**. Do the same for `Assets`.\n2. **Make a Calendar table in Power Query**: Get Data → From Other Sources → **Blank Query**. Open the Advanced Editor and paste:\n\n   ```\n   let\n       Start = #date(2024, 7, 1),\n       End   = #date(2026, 12, 31),\n       Dates = List.Dates(Start, Duration.Days(End - Start) + 1, #duration(1,0,0,0)),\n       T     = Table.FromList(Dates, Splitter.SplitByNothing(), {\"Date\"}),\n       Typed = Table.TransformColumnTypes(T, {{\"Date\", type date}}),\n       Month = Table.AddColumn(Typed, \"Month\", each Date.StartOfMonth([Date]), type date),\n       MName = Table.AddColumn(Month, \"Month Name\", each Date.ToText([Date], \"MMM yyyy\"), type text),\n       Qtr   = Table.AddColumn(MName, \"Quarter\", each \"Q\" & Text.From(Date.QuarterOfYear([Date])) & \" \" & Text.From(Date.Year([Date])), type text),\n       Week  = Table.AddColumn(Qtr, \"Week Commencing\", each Date.StartOfWeek([Date], Day.Monday), type date)\n   in\n       Week\n   ```\n\n   Name it `Calendar` and load it to the Data Model only.\n3. Data → **Manage Data Model** (the Power Pivot window opens). Switch to **Diagram View** (top right).\n4. Drag to create relationships:\n   - `WorkOrders[Asset]` → `Assets[asset_id]`\n   - `WorkOrders[Due]` → `Calendar[Date]`\n5. Notice the **1** and **\\*** ends: one asset, many work orders. That's a *one-to-many* relationship, and you'll see it again in Phase 2.\n\n### Why it works\n\nThe Data Model holds tables **separately but related**, instead of one giant merged sheet. A pivot built on it can use fields from any related table: Zone from Assets, Month from Calendar, values from WorkOrders. It also handles millions of rows that would choke a normal sheet. Power BI uses exactly this engine.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-your-first-dax-measures","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Your first DAX measures","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nIn the Power Pivot window, click the `WorkOrders` table (Data View). In the **calculation area** (the grid under the data), click a cell and type each of these measures. Or, from Excel, go to Power Pivot tab → Measures → New Measure.\n\n```\nPMs Due := CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"PM\" )\n\nPMs On Time := SUM ( WorkOrders[On Time] )\n\nPM Compliance % := DIVIDE ( [PMs On Time], [PMs Due] )\n\nReactive Jobs := CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" )\n\nDowntime Hrs := DIVIDE ( SUM ( WorkOrders[Downtime Mins] ), 60 )\n```\n\nFormat `PM Compliance %` as a percentage.\n\nNow Insert → PivotTable → **From Data Model**. Put `Assets[zone]` in Rows, `Calendar[Month]` in Columns, and `PM Compliance %` in Values.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA **measure** is a calculation that runs **in whatever context the pivot gives it**. Put Zone on rows and the same `PM Compliance %` formula calculates per zone. Add Month to columns and it calculates per zone per month. You write it once and it's right everywhere. Compare that with Week 5, where you needed a separate formula for every cut.\n\n`CALCULATE` changes the filter, as in \"count rows, *but only where* WO Type is PM\". `DIVIDE` is safe division that returns blank instead of an error when there's nothing to divide by. These two functions are 50 % of real-world DAX. Phase 3 goes much deeper.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-build-pm-compliance-workbook","title":"Weekend build (2 h): PM Compliance Workbook","kind":"hands","md":"Build `PM_Compliance.xlsx` with these sheets. Everything must come from queries and the Data Model, with **no typed-in numbers.**\n\n| Sheet | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| **Dashboard** | Slicers for Zone and Criticality, and a timeline on Calendar[Date]. KPI cells for PM Compliance %, PMs Due, Reactive Jobs and Downtime Hrs (use `=GETPIVOTDATA` or small pivots). Chart: compliance by zone. Chart: compliance trend by month. |\n| **Overdue** | PMs not completed with Due before `ReportDate`. Build it as a Power Query table (reference `WorkOrders`, filter `WO Type = PM`, `Completed` is null, `Status = Open`, `Due < ReportDate`), sorted oldest first. |\n| **Pareto** | Top 10 assets by reactive downtime hours, with the combo chart from Week 6, driven by the timeline. |\n| **About** | Data source, refresh instructions, definitions (*on time = completed on or before due date*), and the report date. |\n\nFormatting rules: one colour for \"bad\" (overdue, low compliance) used consistently, titles that state findings, no gridlines, and the slicers lined up neatly.\n\n### Phase 1 *done when* test\n\n1. Move `WO_Export_2026-06.csv` **out** of the folder → **Refresh All**. The dashboard should now end at May.\n2. Put it **back** → **Refresh All**. June reappears.\n3. If every table and chart updated with **zero manual edits**, you've passed Phase 1. 🎉\n\nTick it off in the tracker app, then commit the workbook to your portfolio (see `portfolio/README.md`).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub Power Pivot tutorial\"*.\n- **Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Excel data model relationships\"*.\n- **SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI CALCULATE introduction\"*. It's a little ahead of you, but watch it for the idea of filter context."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between a calculated column (like `On Time` in Power Query) and a measure (like `PM Compliance %`)?\n2. Why does `PM Compliance %` give the right answer for each zone without being told about zones?\n3. Why do you need a Calendar table instead of just using the `Due` column's dates?\n4. Your Data Model pivot shows the same total for every zone. What's wrong?\n5. What was PM compliance for **Primary Packing**, Jan–Jun 2026?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. A **column** is calculated once per row and stored (on time: yes or no for each WO). A **measure** is calculated on the fly over whatever rows are in the current filter (the % for this zone and this month). Rule of thumb: if you'd want to filter or slice *by* it, make it a column. If you'd want to *see the total* of it, make it a measure.\n2. The pivot puts each zone into the **filter context**. The filter flows from `Assets` across the relationship to `WorkOrders`, so the measure only \"sees\" that zone's rows.\n3. The Calendar gives you every date, including days with no work orders, plus months, quarters and weeks to group by. It also lets several tables (work orders, downtime) share the same date filter. Power BI time intelligence needs one.\n4. There's no relationship (or a wrong one) between `Assets` and `WorkOrders`, so the zone filter can't reach the work orders. Check the Diagram View.\n5. **64.7 %** (356 on time out of 550 due). That's the lowest of any zone. End of Line is next lowest at 74.8 %. The line overall is 79.5 % for the same period.\n\n**More reference numbers:**\n\n- Over all 24 months: 10,502 PMs, overall compliance **79.5 %**. Monthly figures range between about 76 % and 83 %.\n- Overdue as at 30/06/2026 (status Open, due before the report date): **17 PMs**. The oldest is `WO-111320`, *Regrind / swap flaking roll* on LD-FLK-013, due 27/05/2026.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 1 done-when, so none of it is skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (Calendar and relationships), the five Session 2 measures, the four weekend sheets (Dashboard, Overdue, Pareto, About) and the move-June-out, Refresh All test. It won't fit in 90 minutes, so spread it over several sittings across the holiday week.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the MyOnlineTrainingHub Power Pivot tutorial and the SQLBI CALCULATE introduction, read both \"Why it works\" parts on the Data Model and measures, answer Check yourself questions 1 to 4 in your head, and draft the About sheet definitions on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only the polish can wait: lining up the slicers, one consistent \"bad\" colour and finding-style chart titles. The four sheets and the done-when test cannot."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Calendar table and relationships in the Data Model\n- [ ] Five DAX measures working in a Data Model pivot\n- [ ] PM Compliance Workbook: Dashboard, Overdue, Pareto and About sheets\n- [ ] **Phase 1 done-when test passed** (ticked in the tracker)"}],"quiz":[{"id":"8-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between a calculated column (like `On Time` in Power Query) and a measure (like `PM Compliance %`)?","a":"A **column** is calculated once per row and stored (on time: yes or no for each WO). A **measure** is calculated on the fly over whatever rows are in the current filter (the % for this zone and this month). Rule of thumb: if you'd want to filter or slice *by* it, make it a column. If you'd want to *see the total* of it, make it a measure."},{"id":"8-2","n":2,"q":"Why does `PM Compliance %` give the right answer for each zone without being told about zones?","a":"The pivot puts each zone into the **filter context**. The filter flows from `Assets` across the relationship to `WorkOrders`, so the measure only \"sees\" that zone's rows."},{"id":"8-3","n":3,"q":"Why do you need a Calendar table instead of just using the `Due` column's dates?","a":"The Calendar gives you every date, including days with no work orders, plus months, quarters and weeks to group by. It also lets several tables (work orders, downtime) share the same date filter. Power BI time intelligence needs one."},{"id":"8-4","n":4,"q":"Your Data Model pivot shows the same total for every zone. What's wrong?","a":"There's no relationship (or a wrong one) between `Assets` and `WorkOrders`, so the zone filter can't reach the work orders. Check the Diagram View."},{"id":"8-5","n":5,"q":"What was PM compliance for **Primary Packing**, Jan–Jun 2026?","a":"**64.7 %** (356 on time out of 550 due). That's the lowest of any zone. End of Line is next lowest at 74.8 %. The line overall is 79.5 % for the same period."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**MyOnlineTrainingHub**: *\"MyOnlineTrainingHub Power Pivot tutorial\"*.","searches":["MyOnlineTrainingHub Power Pivot tutorial"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Leila Gharani**: *\"Leila Gharani Excel data model relationships\"*.","searches":["Leila Gharani Excel data model relationships"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI CALCULATE introduction\"*. It's a little ahead of you, but watch it for the idea of filter context.","searches":["SQLBI CALCULATE introduction"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 1 done-when, so none of it is skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (Calendar and relationships), the five Session 2 measures, the four weekend sheets (Dashboard, Overdue, Pareto, About) and the move-June-out, Refresh All test. It won't fit in 90 minutes, so spread it over several sittings across the holiday week."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the MyOnlineTrainingHub Power Pivot tutorial and the SQLBI CALCULATE introduction, read both \"Why it works\" parts on the Data Model and measures, answer Check yourself questions 1 to 4 in your head, and draft the About sheet definitions on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only the polish can wait: lining up the slicers, one consistent \"bad\" colour and finding-style chart titles. The four sheets and the done-when test cannot."}}},{"week":9,"phase":2,"title":"Data modelling, how a CMMS stores its data","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-09-data-modelling.md","ticks":["DB Browser installed, `line_d.db` explored","Grain, PK and FK identified for all five tables","Built `my_cmms.db` from CSVs, with matching row counts","Star schema drawn, including Calendar","Designed `sensor_readings` and `technicians`"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 28 Dec 2026 – Sun 3 Jan 2027 · about 5 hours (New Year week, so go easy)\n\n**Phase 2 goal:** answer *\"which 10 assets lost the most hours to reactive work last quarter, and were their PMs on time?\"* in **one query**.\n\nMost self-taught analysts skip this phase, and it's why their dashboards break. Every maintenance system (and nearly every business system) stores data in **related tables**, not one big spreadsheet. Once you understand how, you'll understand your CMMS, you'll be useful on any system migration, and Power BI will make far more sense.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain *grain*, *primary key*, *foreign key*, *fact* and *dimension*\n- draw the Line D data model as a star schema\n- create tables with keys in SQLite and import CSV data into them\n- open the database in DB Browser and browse it\n\n**Install:** DB Browser for SQLite from [sqlitebrowser.org](https://sqlitebrowser.org/dl/). Pick the Windows *Standard installer* (64-bit). It's free and needs no server.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-explore-a-real-model","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Explore a real model","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Open DB Browser → **Open Database** → `data/line_d.db`.\n2. **Database Structure** tab: expand each table and look at its columns. There are five tables: `zones`, `assets`, `pm_tasks`, `work_orders` and `downtime_events`.\n3. **Browse Data** tab: look through each table. For every one, answer on paper:\n   - What does **one row** represent? (This is the *grain*.)\n   - Which column **uniquely identifies** a row? (This is the *primary key*.)\n   - Which columns **point at another table**? (These are *foreign keys*.)\n4. Draw the model on paper: a box per table, and lines between the key columns. Mark each line `1` at one end and `*` (many) at the other.\n5. Open the **Execute SQL** tab, type the line below, and press `Ctrl+Return`:\n\n   ```sql\n   SELECT * FROM assets LIMIT 10;\n   ```\n\n   That's your first SQL query. You'll learn it properly next week.\n\n### Why it works\n\n| Table | Grain (one row is…) | Primary key | Foreign keys | Type |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `zones` | one zone | `zone_code` | | dimension |\n| `assets` | one asset | `asset_id` | `zone_code` → zones | dimension |\n| `pm_tasks` | one PM routine on one asset | `pm_id` | `asset_id` → assets | dimension |\n| `work_orders` | one work order | `wo_id` | `asset_id` → assets, `pm_id` → pm_tasks | **fact** |\n| `downtime_events` | one stoppage | `event_id` | `asset_id` → assets, `wo_id` → work_orders | **fact** |\n\n- **Facts** are things that *happen*: jobs, stops, readings. They're long (thousands of rows) and have numbers you add up (hours, minutes).\n- **Dimensions** *describe* things: assets, zones, dates. They're short and have text you group by.\n- A **star schema** puts facts in the middle with dimensions around them. Power BI is designed for exactly this shape.\n\nWhy not one big table? If every work order row carried the asset's zone, name and criticality, then changing an asset's criticality would mean editing hundreds of rows, and one missed row means a wrong report. Storing each fact once and *relating* it is called **normalisation**.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-build-the-database-yourself","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Build the database yourself","kind":"hands","md":"You'll rebuild `line_d.db` from the CSVs, so you understand every part of it.\n\n### Do it\n\n1. DB Browser → **New Database** → save as `my_cmms.db` (in your own folder, not `data/`).\n2. Close the table-creation dialog that pops up, then go to **Execute SQL** and run:\n\n   ```sql\n   CREATE TABLE zones (\n       zone_code TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n       zone      TEXT NOT NULL\n   );\n\n   CREATE TABLE assets (\n       asset_id     TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n       asset_name   TEXT NOT NULL,\n       zone_code    TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES zones(zone_code),\n       asset_type   TEXT,\n       criticality  TEXT CHECK (criticality IN ('A','B','C')),\n       manufacturer TEXT,\n       install_date TEXT\n   );\n\n   CREATE TABLE pm_tasks (\n       pm_id            TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n       asset_id         TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES assets(asset_id),\n       task_description TEXT,\n       task_type        TEXT,\n       frequency_days   INTEGER,\n       est_minutes      INTEGER\n   );\n   ```\n\n3. Write the `CREATE TABLE` for `work_orders` and `downtime_events` **yourself**, using the column list in `data/README.md` and the keys in the table above. Then check your version against the answer below.\n4. **Import the data**: File → Import → Table from CSV file → `data/csv/zones.csv`. Tick *Column names in first line*, set the table name to `zones` (the existing one), and confirm that you want to add to the existing table. Repeat for each CSV **in this order**: zones, assets, pm_tasks, work_orders, downtime_events.\n5. **Write Changes** (`Ctrl+S`). SQLite doesn't save until you do.\n6. Check the counts:\n\n   ```sql\n   SELECT 'assets', COUNT(*) FROM assets\n   UNION ALL SELECT 'work_orders', COUNT(*) FROM work_orders\n   UNION ALL SELECT 'downtime_events', COUNT(*) FROM downtime_events;\n   ```\n\n   You should get **292**, **11,902** and **6,177**.\n\n### Why the import order matters\n\nA foreign key says *\"this value must exist in that table\"*. You can't record a work order against an asset that doesn't exist yet, so parents (zones, assets) go in before children (work orders). That's also how a CMMS stops you raising a job on a non-existent asset.\n\n> **Blank vs NULL.** CSV imports bring empty cells in as empty text `''`, not `NULL`. The provided `line_d.db` uses proper `NULL`s. To fix yours, run `UPDATE work_orders SET completed_date = NULL WHERE completed_date = '';` and the same for `pm_id`, `due_date`, `failure_code`, `technician` and `shift`. Then use `line_d.db` for the rest of the phase so your answers match the lessons.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-task-model-your-own-world","title":"Weekend task (2 h): Model your own world","kind":"hands","md":"1. In `data/README.md`, read the data dictionary for every table.\n2. Draw the **star schema** neatly (paper, PowerPoint or [draw.io](https://app.diagrams.net) are all fine): facts in the middle, dimensions around them, `1` and `*` on every line. Add a **Calendar** dimension. Which columns in the facts would connect to it?\n3. **Design exercise.** Line D is getting vibration sensors. Design a table `sensor_readings` for them: decide its grain, primary key, foreign keys and columns, and write its `CREATE TABLE`. Then design `technicians` (a dimension) and work out what changes in `work_orders`.\n4. Think about your real CMMS at work (don't export anything). What do you think its asset, work order and PM tables look like? Which fields are free text that *should* be pick-lists?","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL basics\"*. Start this series now; it carries into next week.\n- **Lucidchart**: *\"Lucidchart what is an entity relationship diagram\"*, a clear ERD explainer.\n- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube star schema\"*. It's the Power BI view of the same idea, and you'll meet it again in Phase 3."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the grain of `downtime_events`?\n2. Why is `wo_id` sometimes empty in `downtime_events`?\n3. Can one asset have many PM tasks? Can one PM task belong to many assets? What does that make the relationship?\n4. Is `labour_hours` a fact or a dimension attribute? What about `criticality`?\n5. Write the `CREATE TABLE` for `work_orders`.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. One stoppage of one asset (start, end and duration).\n2. Minor stops are cleared by operators and don't get a work order. Breakdowns do. That makes it an **optional** foreign key, and it's why you'll need a `LEFT JOIN` to keep minor stops in your results.\n3. Yes, and no (in this model each routine is for one asset). So it's **one-to-many**: assets 1 → * pm_tasks.\n4. `labour_hours` is a **fact** measure: it lives on the work order and you add it up. `criticality` is a **dimension** attribute: it describes the asset, and you group or filter by it.\n5. ```sql\n   CREATE TABLE work_orders (\n       wo_id           TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n       asset_id        TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES assets(asset_id),\n       pm_id           TEXT REFERENCES pm_tasks(pm_id),\n       wo_type         TEXT,\n       priority        INTEGER,\n       description     TEXT,\n       failure_code    TEXT,\n       raised_datetime TEXT,\n       due_date        TEXT,\n       completed_date  TEXT,\n       status          TEXT,\n       labour_hours    REAL,\n       technician      TEXT,\n       shift           TEXT\n   );\n\n   CREATE TABLE downtime_events (\n       event_id       TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n       asset_id       TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES assets(asset_id),\n       wo_id          TEXT REFERENCES work_orders(wo_id),\n       start_datetime TEXT,\n       end_datetime   TEXT,\n       duration_min   INTEGER,\n       category       TEXT,\n       cause          TEXT\n   );\n   ```\n   SQLite stores dates as text in `YYYY-MM-DD` format. That sorts and compares correctly, which is why ISO format is used everywhere.\n\n**Design exercise (one good answer):** `sensor_readings(reading_id PK, sensor_id FK → sensors, reading_datetime, rms_velocity_mm_s, peak_accel_g, temp_c)` plus a `sensors(sensor_id PK, asset_id FK → assets, location, sensor_type)` dimension. The grain is one reading from one sensor at one time.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 3 (grain, primary key and foreign keys for all five tables, on paper) and Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (build `my_cmms.db`, import in parent-first order, Write Changes, check 292 / 11,902 / 6,177), then run the NULL fix and switch to `line_d.db`.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Lucidchart ERD explainer and the Guy in a Cube star schema video, read the grain table and \"Why the import order matters\", and sketch the star schema with a Calendar plus your `sensor_readings` and `technicians` designs on paper. Weekend step 4, thinking about your real CMMS, fits a break at work.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Writing the `work_orders` and `downtime_events` CREATE TABLE fully from scratch (Session 2 step 3, copy it from the answer if you're tired) and a neat draw.io version of the star schema can be skipped; a paper sketch is fine."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] DB Browser installed, `line_d.db` explored\n- [ ] Grain, PK and FK identified for all five tables\n- [ ] Built `my_cmms.db` from CSVs, with matching row counts\n- [ ] Star schema drawn, including Calendar\n- [ ] Designed `sensor_readings` and `technicians`"}],"quiz":[{"id":"9-1","n":1,"q":"What's the grain of `downtime_events`?","a":"One stoppage of one asset (start, end and duration)."},{"id":"9-2","n":2,"q":"Why is `wo_id` sometimes empty in `downtime_events`?","a":"Minor stops are cleared by operators and don't get a work order. Breakdowns do. That makes it an **optional** foreign key, and it's why you'll need a `LEFT JOIN` to keep minor stops in your results."},{"id":"9-3","n":3,"q":"Can one asset have many PM tasks? Can one PM task belong to many assets? What does that make the relationship?","a":"Yes, and no (in this model each routine is for one asset). So it's **one-to-many**: assets 1 → * pm_tasks."},{"id":"9-4","n":4,"q":"Is `labour_hours` a fact or a dimension attribute? What about `criticality`?","a":"`labour_hours` is a **fact** measure: it lives on the work order and you add it up. `criticality` is a **dimension** attribute: it describes the asset, and you group or filter by it."},{"id":"9-5","n":5,"q":"Write the `CREATE TABLE` for `work_orders`.","a":"```sql\nCREATE TABLE work_orders (\n    wo_id           TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n    asset_id        TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES assets(asset_id),\n    pm_id           TEXT REFERENCES pm_tasks(pm_id),\n    wo_type         TEXT,\n    priority        INTEGER,\n    description     TEXT,\n    failure_code    TEXT,\n    raised_datetime TEXT,\n    due_date        TEXT,\n    completed_date  TEXT,\n    status          TEXT,\n    labour_hours    REAL,\n    technician      TEXT,\n    shift           TEXT\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE downtime_events (\n    event_id       TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n    asset_id       TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES assets(asset_id),\n    wo_id          TEXT REFERENCES work_orders(wo_id),\n    start_datetime TEXT,\n    end_datetime   TEXT,\n    duration_min   INTEGER,\n    category       TEXT,\n    cause          TEXT\n);\n```\nSQLite stores dates as text in `YYYY-MM-DD` format. That sorts and compares correctly, which is why ISO format is used everywhere."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL basics\"*. Start this series now; it carries into next week.","searches":["Alex the Analyst SQL basics"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Lucidchart**: *\"Lucidchart what is an entity relationship diagram\"*, a clear ERD explainer.","searches":["Lucidchart what is an entity relationship diagram"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube star schema\"*. It's the Power BI view of the same idea, and you'll meet it again in Phase 3.","searches":["Guy in a Cube star schema"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 3 (grain, primary key and foreign keys for all five tables, on paper) and Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (build `my_cmms.db`, import in parent-first order, Write Changes, check 292 / 11,902 / 6,177), then run the NULL fix and switch to `line_d.db`."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Lucidchart ERD explainer and the Guy in a Cube star schema video, read the grain table and \"Why the import order matters\", and sketch the star schema with a Calendar plus your `sensor_readings` and `technicians` designs on paper. Weekend step 4, thinking about your real CMMS, fits a break at work."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Writing the `work_orders` and `downtime_events` CREATE TABLE fully from scratch (Session 2 step 3, copy it from the answer if you're tired) and a neat draw.io version of the star schema can be skipped; a paper sketch is fine."}}},{"week":10,"phase":2,"title":"SQL core, SELECT, WHERE and ORDER BY","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-10-sql-select-where.md","ticks":["SQLBolt lessons 1–4 done","Can filter with `IN`, `LIKE`, `BETWEEN` and `IS NULL`","Understand why brackets matter with `AND`/`OR`","`week10.sql` saved with all 7 weekend queries"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 4 Jan – Sun 10 Jan 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nSQL (*Structured Query Language*) is how you ask a database questions. It's 50 years old, every serious system speaks it, and it reads almost like English. This week covers the part you'll use in every single query: picking columns, filtering rows and sorting.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- write `SELECT … FROM … WHERE … ORDER BY … LIMIT`\n- filter with `=`, `<>`, `>`, `BETWEEN`, `IN`, `LIKE` and `IS NULL`\n- combine conditions with `AND` and `OR`, and use brackets properly\n- use `DISTINCT`, `COUNT(*)`, and aliases with `AS`\n\n**Tools:** DB Browser for SQLite with `data/line_d.db` open, on the **Execute SQL** tab. Run the whole editor with `Ctrl+Return`, or just the current statement with `Shift+F5`.\n\n**Practice site:** [sqlbolt.com](https://sqlbolt.com). Lessons 1–4 match this week exactly, and you type answers right in the browser.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-select-and-where","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): SELECT and WHERE","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nType each query (don't paste; typing builds memory), run it, and look at the result.\n\n```sql\n-- Every column, first 10 assets\nSELECT * FROM assets LIMIT 10;\n\n-- Just the columns you want\nSELECT asset_id, asset_name, criticality FROM assets;\n\n-- Filter: only Primary Packing\nSELECT asset_id, asset_name, criticality\nFROM assets\nWHERE zone_code = 'PPK';\n\n-- Two conditions, both must be true\nSELECT asset_id, asset_name, asset_type\nFROM assets\nWHERE zone_code = 'PPK' AND criticality = 'A'\nORDER BY asset_id;\n```\n\nThe last query should return **12 assets**, starting with `LD-PPK-007`, Primary Packing Carton erector 1.\n\nNow try each of these filters on `work_orders`:\n\n```sql\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive'\nWHERE wo_type <> 'PM'\nWHERE labour_hours > 4\nWHERE labour_hours BETWEEN 1 AND 2\nWHERE wo_type IN ('Reactive', 'Corrective')\nWHERE description LIKE '%bearing%'\nWHERE completed_date IS NULL\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\nThe order you **write** a query in:\n\n```\nSELECT   columns\nFROM     table\nWHERE    row filter\nORDER BY sort\nLIMIT    how many\n```\n\nThe order the database **runs** it in is different: `FROM` → `WHERE` → `SELECT` → `ORDER BY` → `LIMIT`. Knowing that explains a lot of errors later. For example, you can't use a `SELECT` alias inside `WHERE`, because `WHERE` runs first.\n\n- Text goes in **single quotes**: `'PPK'`. Numbers don't.\n- `LIKE '%bearing%'` means \"contains bearing\", because `%` stands for any characters. In SQLite `LIKE` isn't case-sensitive.\n- `NULL` means **unknown or empty**, and it isn't equal to anything, not even itself. So `= NULL` never matches. Always use `IS NULL` or `IS NOT NULL`.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-sorting-distinct-dates-and-bra","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Sorting, DISTINCT, dates and brackets","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```sql\n-- The 10 longest stoppages ever\nSELECT event_id, asset_id, start_datetime, duration_min, cause\nFROM downtime_events\nORDER BY duration_min DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nThe top one should be `DT-004654`: LD-FLK-013, a bearing, 1,375 minutes on New Year's Eve 2025. Ouch.\n\n```sql\n-- Every failure code that's ever been used on a breakdown\nSELECT DISTINCT failure_code\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive'\nORDER BY failure_code;\n```\n\nThat gives **28 codes**.\n\n```sql\n-- Dates are text in YYYY-MM-DD format, so they compare correctly as text\nSELECT wo_id, asset_id, description, labour_hours\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive'\n  AND raised_datetime >= '2026-06-01'\n  AND raised_datetime <  '2026-07-01'\n  AND labour_hours > 4\nORDER BY labour_hours DESC;\n```\n\nThat's **2 jobs** in June over 4 hours.\n\n**Brackets matter.** Compare:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive' OR wo_type = 'Corrective' AND labour_hours > 3;\n\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM work_orders\nWHERE (wo_type = 'Reactive' OR wo_type = 'Corrective') AND labour_hours > 3;\n```\n\nThey give **1,071** and **280**. The first one is really \"all Reactive jobs, plus Corrective jobs over 3 hours\", because `AND` binds tighter than `OR`, just like × before + in maths. When you mix them, always use brackets.\n\n**Aliases:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT asset_id AS asset, ROUND(labour_hours * 60) AS labour_mins\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive'\nORDER BY labour_mins DESC\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\n### Why `>= '2026-06-01' AND < '2026-07-01'`?\n\n`raised_datetime` includes a time (`2026-06-30 14:22`). If you wrote `BETWEEN '2026-06-01' AND '2026-06-30'`, anything after midnight on the 30th would be missed, because `'2026-06-30 14:22'` is \"bigger\" than `'2026-06-30'`. A half-open range (`>=` start, `<` next start) is always safe.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Finish **SQLBolt lessons 1–4** (and their exercises).\n2. Write queries for each question below. Save them all in one file `week10.sql` (DB Browser → Save SQL file). Keep every SQL file you write; they're portfolio material.\n\n   1. All assets made by *Kestrel Drives*, sorted by install date (oldest first).\n   2. All PM work orders for asset `LD-PPK-005` that weren't completed.\n   3. The 5 reactive jobs with the most labour hours, ever.\n   4. All assets with \"Burner\" in the name.\n   5. How many work orders have no completed date?\n   6. Every breakdown raised on Christmas Day 2025. (Hint: `LIKE '2025-12-25%'`.)\n   7. Every *Replace sealing jaws* PM that wasn't completed.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL SELECT WHERE\"*.\n- **Programming with Mosh**: *\"Mosh SQL tutorial for beginners\"*. It's a longer, very clear full course. Watch the first hour.\n- Reference: [sqlite.org/lang_select.html](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html), the official (dense) docs."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does `WHERE completed_date = NULL` return nothing?\n2. Write a query for A-critical assets in either Baking or Drying.\n3. What's wrong with `SELECT asset_id, ROUND(labour_hours) AS hrs FROM work_orders WHERE hrs > 5;`?\n4. How many assets are there of each criticality? (Hint: try `GROUP BY criticality` with `COUNT(*)`. That's next week, but have a go.)\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `NULL` isn't a value, it's \"unknown\", and comparing anything to unknown gives unknown (not TRUE). Use `IS NULL`.\n2. ```sql\n   SELECT asset_id, asset_name\n   FROM assets\n   WHERE criticality = 'A' AND zone_code IN ('BAK', 'DRY');\n   ```\n3. `WHERE` runs **before** `SELECT`, so the alias `hrs` doesn't exist yet. Use `WHERE ROUND(labour_hours) > 5` (or a CTE, Week 12).\n4. ```sql\n   SELECT criticality, COUNT(*) FROM assets GROUP BY criticality;\n   ```\n   A = 86, B = 129, C = 77.\n\nWeekend answers are in [`solutions/phase-2-sql/w10_select.sql`](../../solutions/phase-2-sql/w10_select.sql). Q5 = **534** work orders with no completed date. Q7 = 2 (WO-109531 cancelled, WO-111369 still open).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Type Session 2's brackets comparison and half-open date range query, then write and save all seven weekend queries in `week10.sql`, and get through SQLBolt lessons 1 to 4 at the PC.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Alex The Analyst SELECT WHERE video, read the \"Why it works\" parts on written versus run order and on NULL, plus the note on why `>=` and `<` beats `BETWEEN` for dates, and answer Check yourself questions 1 and 3 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The first hour of the Mosh course, the sqlite.org docs and typing every one of the seven Session 1 filter lines separately can go, because the weekend queries practise the same filters."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] SQLBolt lessons 1–4 done\n- [ ] Can filter with `IN`, `LIKE`, `BETWEEN` and `IS NULL`\n- [ ] Understand why brackets matter with `AND`/`OR`\n- [ ] `week10.sql` saved with all 7 weekend queries"}],"quiz":[{"id":"10-1","n":1,"q":"Why does `WHERE completed_date = NULL` return nothing?","a":"`NULL` isn't a value, it's \"unknown\", and comparing anything to unknown gives unknown (not TRUE). Use `IS NULL`."},{"id":"10-2","n":2,"q":"Write a query for A-critical assets in either Baking or Drying.","a":"```sql\nSELECT asset_id, asset_name\nFROM assets\nWHERE criticality = 'A' AND zone_code IN ('BAK', 'DRY');\n```"},{"id":"10-3","n":3,"q":"What's wrong with `SELECT asset_id, ROUND(labour_hours) AS hrs FROM work_orders WHERE hrs > 5;`?","a":"`WHERE` runs **before** `SELECT`, so the alias `hrs` doesn't exist yet. Use `WHERE ROUND(labour_hours) > 5` (or a CTE, Week 12)."},{"id":"10-4","n":4,"q":"How many assets are there of each criticality? (Hint: try `GROUP BY criticality` with `COUNT(*)`. That's next week, but have a go.)","a":"```sql\nSELECT criticality, COUNT(*) FROM assets GROUP BY criticality;\n```\nA = 86, B = 129, C = 77."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL SELECT WHERE\"*.","searches":["Alex the Analyst SQL SELECT WHERE"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Programming with Mosh**: *\"Mosh SQL tutorial for beginners\"*. It's a longer, very clear full course. Watch the first hour.","searches":["Mosh SQL tutorial for beginners"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Reference: [sqlite.org/lang_select.html](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html), the official (dense) docs.","searches":[],"urls":["https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Type Session 2's brackets comparison and half-open date range query, then write and save all seven weekend queries in `week10.sql`, and get through SQLBolt lessons 1 to 4 at the PC."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Alex The Analyst SELECT WHERE video, read the \"Why it works\" parts on written versus run order and on NULL, plus the note on why `>=` and `<` beats `BETWEEN` for dates, and answer Check yourself questions 1 and 3 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The first hour of the Mosh course, the sqlite.org docs and typing every one of the seven Session 1 filter lines separately can go, because the weekend queries practise the same filters."}}},{"week":11,"phase":2,"title":"JOINs, GROUP BY and HAVING","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-11-joins-groupby.md","ticks":["Three-table JOIN written from memory","Can explain why the INNER join lost 5,467 rows","`LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` used to find assets that never failed","GROUP BY + HAVING for repeat offenders","`week11.sql` saved"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 11 Jan – Sun 17 Jan 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is where SQL gets powerful. **JOIN** puts related tables back together (work orders + assets + zones), and **GROUP BY** summarises them (jobs per zone, hours per asset). Together they're 80 % of all real-world SQL.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- `JOIN` two or three tables on their keys\n- explain `INNER` vs `LEFT` join, and use `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` to find missing matches\n- aggregate with `COUNT`, `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN` and `MAX`\n- group with `GROUP BY` and filter groups with `HAVING`","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-joins","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): JOINs","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```sql\n-- Work orders with the asset's name and zone\nSELECT w.wo_id, w.asset_id, a.asset_name, a.zone_code, w.wo_type\nFROM work_orders AS w\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = w.asset_id\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\n`w` and `a` are **table aliases**, short names so you don't have to type `work_orders.asset_id` every time.\n\nAdd a third table:\n\n```sql\nSELECT w.wo_id, a.asset_name, z.zone, w.wo_type, w.labour_hours\nFROM work_orders AS w\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id  = w.asset_id\nJOIN zones  AS z ON z.zone_code = a.zone_code\nWHERE w.wo_type = 'Reactive'\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n**INNER vs LEFT.** Downtime events don't all have a work order. Compare:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM downtime_events AS d\nJOIN work_orders AS w ON w.wo_id = d.wo_id;          -- 710\n\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM downtime_events AS d\nLEFT JOIN work_orders AS w ON w.wo_id = d.wo_id;     -- 6,177\n```\n\nThe `JOIN` (inner) **silently dropped** 5,467 minor stops because they had no matching work order. That's one of the commonest ways reports go wrong.\n\n**Find things with no match.** These are assets that have *never* broken down:\n\n```sql\nSELECT a.asset_id, a.asset_name\nFROM assets AS a\nLEFT JOIN work_orders AS w\n       ON w.asset_id = a.asset_id AND w.wo_type = 'Reactive'\nWHERE w.wo_id IS NULL;\n```\n\nThat gives **32 assets**. (Notice that the `wo_type` condition sits in the `ON`, not the `WHERE`. Think about why. The answer is in Check yourself, below.)\n\n### Why it works\n\n```\nINNER JOIN: only rows that match in both tables\nLEFT JOIN:  every row from the left table, plus matches from the right (NULLs where there's none)\n```\n\nIt's exactly the Power Query Merge from Week 7: Inner, Left Outer, and Left Anti (`LEFT JOIN … WHERE right.key IS NULL`).\n\nAlways join **on keys**: a foreign key to its primary key. Joining on names or descriptions breaks as soon as someone renames something.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-group-by-and-having","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): GROUP BY and HAVING","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```sql\n-- How many of each WO type?\nSELECT wo_type, COUNT(*) AS jobs\nFROM work_orders\nGROUP BY wo_type\nORDER BY jobs DESC;\n```\n\nThat's PM 10,502 · Reactive 1,027 · Corrective 373.\n\n```sql\n-- Reactive jobs and labour by zone\nSELECT z.zone, COUNT(*) AS reactive_jobs, ROUND(SUM(w.labour_hours), 1) AS labour_hrs\nFROM work_orders AS w\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id  = w.asset_id\nJOIN zones  AS z ON z.zone_code = a.zone_code\nWHERE w.wo_type = 'Reactive'\nGROUP BY z.zone\nORDER BY reactive_jobs DESC;\n```\n\nPrimary Packing has the most jobs (292), but **Flaking has the most labour** (519.7 h), because flaking rolls take a long time to fix.\n\n```sql\n-- Repeat offenders: assets with more than 8 breakdowns\nSELECT a.asset_id, a.asset_name, COUNT(*) AS breakdowns\nFROM work_orders AS w\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = w.asset_id\nWHERE w.wo_type = 'Reactive'\nGROUP BY a.asset_id, a.asset_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 8\nORDER BY breakdowns DESC;\n```\n\nThat returns 20 assets. Flow wrappers dominate: LD-PPK-005 has had **37 breakdowns** in two years.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`GROUP BY` collapses rows into one per group, and aggregate functions summarise each group. The rule: **every column in `SELECT` must either be in `GROUP BY` or inside an aggregate.** (SQLite is lenient and won't always complain, but other databases will, and the results can be nonsense.)\n\n`WHERE` filters **rows before** grouping. `HAVING` filters **groups after** grouping. The full run order is now:\n\n```\nFROM → JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT\n```","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **SQLBolt lessons 6–12** (joins, nulls, expressions, aggregates).\n2. Write these in `week11.sql`:\n   1. Downtime hours by zone and category (Breakdown/Minor stop). Which zone loses most to minor stops?\n   2. How many downtime events have a work order vs not? (Hint: `CASE WHEN wo_id IS NULL …`; you can borrow it from Week 12.)\n   3. Average repair labour by asset type, only for types with at least 20 breakdowns.\n   4. For each technician, the number of reactive jobs and their total labour hours.\n   5. Number of PM tasks by `task_type` and `frequency_days`.\n   6. Which **manufacturer**'s assets have the most breakdowns per asset? (Count breakdowns ÷ count of distinct assets: `COUNT(DISTINCT a.asset_id)`.)","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL joins\"* and *\"Alex the Analyst GROUP BY HAVING\"*.\n- **Data with Baraa**: *\"Data with Baraa SQL joins\"*. It has excellent join diagrams.\n- The famous visual: search *\"SQL joins Venn diagram\"*. It's a useful mental picture, but remember that joins really match *rows on keys*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. In the \"never broken down\" query, what goes wrong if you move `w.wo_type = 'Reactive'` from `ON` into `WHERE`?\n2. Why is `HAVING COUNT(*) > 8` correct but `WHERE COUNT(*) > 8` an error?\n3. You join `work_orders` to `downtime_events` on `asset_id` (not `wo_id`). What happens to the row count, and why?\n4. What's the average breakdown duration in minutes, by zone? Write the query.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The `WHERE` runs after the join. For assets with no reactive jobs, the left join still brings in their *PM* rows, so `w.wo_type` is `'PM'`. The `WHERE` then throws those rows away, and `IS NULL` never matches. You'd get 0 instead of 32. **Conditions on the right-hand table of a LEFT JOIN belong in the `ON`.**\n2. `WHERE` runs before grouping, so there's no count yet. `HAVING` runs after.\n3. It **explodes**: every work order on an asset matches every downtime event on that asset, so 10 WOs × 20 events = 200 rows for one asset. This is called *fan-out*, and it's why you join on the true key. If totals ever look too big after a join, suspect fan-out.\n4. ```sql\n   SELECT a.zone_code, ROUND(AVG(d.duration_min), 1) AS avg_breakdown_min\n   FROM downtime_events AS d\n   JOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = d.asset_id\n   WHERE d.category = 'Breakdown'\n   GROUP BY a.zone_code\n   ORDER BY avg_breakdown_min DESC;\n   ```\n   Flaking is highest, because roll and bearing jobs are long.\n\nFull answers are in [`solutions/phase-2-sql/w11_joins_groupby.sql`](../../solutions/phase-2-sql/w11_joins_groupby.sql). Minor-stop hours: Primary Packing is worst (312 h), then End of Line (206 h).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's three-table JOIN, the INNER versus LEFT counts (710 against 6,177) and the `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` query for the 32 assets that never failed, then Session 2's repeat-offenders query with `HAVING`, and weekend queries 1 to 4 saved in `week11.sql`.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Alex The Analyst joins and GROUP BY HAVING videos and the Data with Baraa join diagrams, read both \"Why it works\" parts (the full run order especially), and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** SQLBolt lessons 6 to 12, weekend queries 5 and 6 (PM tasks by type, breakdowns per asset by manufacturer) and the Venn diagram search can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Three-table JOIN written from memory\n- [ ] Can explain why the INNER join lost 5,467 rows\n- [ ] `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` used to find assets that never failed\n- [ ] GROUP BY + HAVING for repeat offenders\n- [ ] `week11.sql` saved"}],"quiz":[{"id":"11-1","n":1,"q":"In the \"never broken down\" query, what goes wrong if you move `w.wo_type = 'Reactive'` from `ON` into `WHERE`?","a":"The `WHERE` runs after the join. For assets with no reactive jobs, the left join still brings in their *PM* rows, so `w.wo_type` is `'PM'`. The `WHERE` then throws those rows away, and `IS NULL` never matches. You'd get 0 instead of 32. **Conditions on the right-hand table of a LEFT JOIN belong in the `ON`.**"},{"id":"11-2","n":2,"q":"Why is `HAVING COUNT(*) > 8` correct but `WHERE COUNT(*) > 8` an error?","a":"`WHERE` runs before grouping, so there's no count yet. `HAVING` runs after."},{"id":"11-3","n":3,"q":"You join `work_orders` to `downtime_events` on `asset_id` (not `wo_id`). What happens to the row count, and why?","a":"It **explodes**: every work order on an asset matches every downtime event on that asset, so 10 WOs × 20 events = 200 rows for one asset. This is called *fan-out*, and it's why you join on the true key. If totals ever look too big after a join, suspect fan-out."},{"id":"11-4","n":4,"q":"What's the average breakdown duration in minutes, by zone? Write the query.","a":"```sql\nSELECT a.zone_code, ROUND(AVG(d.duration_min), 1) AS avg_breakdown_min\nFROM downtime_events AS d\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = d.asset_id\nWHERE d.category = 'Breakdown'\nGROUP BY a.zone_code\nORDER BY avg_breakdown_min DESC;\n```\nFlaking is highest, because roll and bearing jobs are long."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst SQL joins\"* and *\"Alex the Analyst GROUP BY HAVING\"*.","searches":["Alex the Analyst SQL joins","Alex the Analyst GROUP BY HAVING"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Data with Baraa**: *\"Data with Baraa SQL joins\"*. It has excellent join diagrams.","searches":["Data with Baraa SQL joins"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"The famous visual: search *\"SQL joins Venn diagram\"*. It's a useful mental picture, but remember that joins really match *rows on keys*.","searches":["SQL joins Venn diagram"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's three-table JOIN, the INNER versus LEFT counts (710 against 6,177) and the `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` query for the 32 assets that never failed, then Session 2's repeat-offenders query with `HAVING`, and weekend queries 1 to 4 saved in `week11.sql`."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Alex The Analyst joins and GROUP BY HAVING videos and the Data with Baraa join diagrams, read both \"Why it works\" parts (the full run order especially), and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"SQLBolt lessons 6 to 12, weekend queries 5 and 6 (PM tasks by type, breakdowns per asset by manufacturer) and the Venn diagram search can wait."}}},{"week":12,"phase":2,"title":"CASE, CTEs and dates","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-12-case-cte-dates.md","ticks":["CASE labels and conditional SUM for compliance (79.5 %)","Compliance by month with `strftime`","Days late with `julianday`","A multi-step CTE query","`week12.sql` saved"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 18 Jan – Sun 24 Jan 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nReal questions need logic (\"was this PM on time?\"), steps (\"first work out compliance per zone, *then* keep the bad ones\") and dates (\"by month\", \"days late\"). This week gives you all three, and you'll calculate PM compliance properly in SQL.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- write `CASE WHEN … THEN … ELSE … END` for labels and conditional counting\n- break a query into steps with `WITH` (Common Table Expressions)\n- use SQLite date functions: `date()`, `strftime()` and `julianday()`\n- calculate PM compliance by month and zone","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-case","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): CASE","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```sql\nSELECT wo_id, due_date, completed_date,\n       CASE\n         WHEN status = 'Open'            THEN 'Open'\n         WHEN completed_date IS NULL     THEN 'Missed'\n         WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 'On time'\n         ELSE 'Late'\n       END AS pm_result\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM'\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nNow **group by the CASE**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT CASE\n         WHEN status = 'Open'            THEN 'Open'\n         WHEN completed_date IS NULL     THEN 'Missed'\n         WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 'On time'\n         ELSE 'Late'\n       END AS pm_result,\n       COUNT(*) AS pms\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM'\nGROUP BY pm_result\nORDER BY pms DESC;\n```\n\nYou should get On time 8,349 · Late 1,619 · Missed 513 · Open 21.\n\n**Conditional counting** is the most useful trick in SQL. Here's compliance in one line:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS pms_due,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS on_time,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*), 1) AS compliance_pct\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM';\n```\n\nThat gives **79.5 %**, which matches your Excel workbook from Week 8. Two tools, same answer: that's how you know both are right.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`CASE` is checked **top to bottom**, and the first true `WHEN` wins. Order matters: if `completed_date <= due_date` came first, an Open PM (NULL completed date) would fall through to `ELSE 'Late'`.\n\n`100.0 *` forces decimal division. In SQL, `8349 / 10502` with whole numbers gives `0`. That's integer division, and it catches everyone at least once.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-dates-and-ctes","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Dates and CTEs","kind":"hands","md":"### Dates in SQLite\n\n| Function | Example | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `date(x)` | `date('2026-06-14 09:30')` | `2026-06-14` |\n| `strftime('%Y-%m', x)` | year-month | `2026-06` |\n| `strftime('%H', x)` | hour | `09` |\n| `strftime('%w', x)` | weekday (0 = Sunday) | `0` |\n| `julianday(x)` | day number | `2461205.89…` |\n| `julianday(a) - julianday(b)` | days between | e.g. `12.5` |\n| `date(x, '+28 days')` | add days | |\n\n```sql\n-- PM compliance by month due\nSELECT strftime('%Y-%m', due_date) AS due_month,\n       COUNT(*) AS pms_due,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*), 1) AS compliance_pct\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM'\nGROUP BY due_month\nORDER BY due_month;\n\n-- Most-late PMs\nSELECT wo_id, asset_id, due_date, completed_date,\n       CAST(julianday(completed_date) - julianday(due_date) AS INTEGER) AS days_late\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM' AND completed_date > due_date\nORDER BY days_late DESC\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\n### CTEs: queries in steps\n\n```sql\nWITH pm AS (                       -- step 1: one row per PM, flagged\n    SELECT a.zone_code,\n           CASE WHEN w.completed_date <= w.due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS on_time\n    FROM work_orders AS w\n    JOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = w.asset_id\n    WHERE w.wo_type = 'PM'\n      AND w.due_date BETWEEN '2026-01-01' AND '2026-06-30'\n),\nby_zone AS (                       -- step 2: summarise per zone\n    SELECT zone_code, COUNT(*) AS due, SUM(on_time) AS on_time,\n           ROUND(100.0 * SUM(on_time) / COUNT(*), 1) AS compliance_pct\n    FROM pm\n    GROUP BY zone_code\n)\nSELECT *                           -- step 3: keep the problem zones\nFROM by_zone\nWHERE compliance_pct < 80\nORDER BY compliance_pct;\n```\n\nThat returns **PPK 64.7 %** and **EOL 74.8 %**.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA CTE is a **named temporary result** you can query like a table. It's the SQL version of `LET`, or of helper columns in Excel. Use one whenever a query has more than one \"and then…\". Each step can be tested on its own: just `SELECT * FROM pm LIMIT 10` while you're building.\n\n(`due_date` has no time part, so `BETWEEN` is safe here, unlike `raised_datetime` last week.)","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"Write in `week12.sql`:\n\n1. **Planned vs reactive by quarter**: % of work orders that weren't Reactive, per quarter. (Build the quarter label with `strftime('%Y', …) || '-Q' || ((CAST(strftime('%m', …) AS INTEGER) + 2) / 3)`.)\n2. **When do breakdowns happen?** Count of reactive jobs by hour of day. Is there a pattern?\n3. **Breakdowns by day of week.**\n4. **Average days late** of late PMs, by zone.\n5. **A \"PM health\" label per asset**: in a CTE, calculate each asset's PM compliance; then label it `'Good'` (≥ 90 %), `'Watch'` (75–90 %) or `'Poor'` (< 75 %), and count assets per label per zone.\n6. **Stretch:** for each zone, compliance in H2 2025 vs H1 2026 side by side, plus the change. (Hint: two conditional `SUM`s in one query.)","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst CASE statement\"* and *\"Alex the Analyst CTE\"*.\n- **Mode SQL Tutorial**: [mode.com/sql-tutorial](https://mode.com/sql-tutorial/). Read the *SQL CASE* and *SQL Date Format* pages.\n- SQLite date reference: [sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does `SELECT 8349 / 10502` give 0?\n2. Rewrite this to avoid repeating the CASE three times: compliance = on_time / due, using a CTE.\n3. What's the difference between a CTE and saving a query result as a new table?\n4. What was the worst month for PM compliance in the data?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Both are integers, so SQL does integer division and drops the remainder. Write `100.0 * 8349 / 10502` or `CAST(8349 AS REAL) / 10502`.\n2. ```sql\n   WITH f AS (\n     SELECT CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS on_time\n     FROM work_orders WHERE wo_type = 'PM'\n   )\n   SELECT COUNT(*) AS due, SUM(on_time) AS on_time,\n          ROUND(100.0 * SUM(on_time) / COUNT(*), 1) AS pct\n   FROM f;\n   ```\n3. A CTE exists only while that query runs, so it's always up to date and leaves no clutter. A saved table is a snapshot that goes stale when the data changes.\n4. **March 2025 (76.4 %)**, closely followed by December 2024 (76.9 %).\n\nPlanned share is about 90–92 % in every quarter. That looks great, but remember it's a **count** of jobs. By **hours**, reactive work is a much bigger slice. Try it.\n\nAnswers are in [`solutions/phase-2-sql/w12_case_cte_dates.sql`](../../solutions/phase-2-sql/w12_case_cte_dates.sql).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's CASE grouped into On time, Late, Missed and Open plus the conditional `SUM` for 79.5 %, Session 2's compliance by month, the `julianday` days-late query and the three-step CTE (PPK 64.7 %, EOL 74.8 %), then weekend queries 1, 4 and 5 saved in `week12.sql`.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Alex The Analyst CASE and CTE videos, read the Mode SQL Tutorial CASE and Date Format pages and the \"Why it works\" notes on CASE order and integer division, and answer Check yourself questions 1 and 3 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend queries 2 and 3 (breakdowns by hour and by weekday), the stretch query 6 (H2 2025 against H1 2026) and the SQLite date reference page can be left for later."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] CASE labels and conditional SUM for compliance (79.5 %)\n- [ ] Compliance by month with `strftime`\n- [ ] Days late with `julianday`\n- [ ] A multi-step CTE query\n- [ ] `week12.sql` saved"}],"quiz":[{"id":"12-1","n":1,"q":"Why does `SELECT 8349 / 10502` give 0?","a":"Both are integers, so SQL does integer division and drops the remainder. Write `100.0 * 8349 / 10502` or `CAST(8349 AS REAL) / 10502`."},{"id":"12-2","n":2,"q":"Rewrite this to avoid repeating the CASE three times: compliance = on_time / due, using a CTE.","a":"```sql\nWITH f AS (\n  SELECT CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS on_time\n  FROM work_orders WHERE wo_type = 'PM'\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS due, SUM(on_time) AS on_time,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(on_time) / COUNT(*), 1) AS pct\nFROM f;\n```"},{"id":"12-3","n":3,"q":"What's the difference between a CTE and saving a query result as a new table?","a":"A CTE exists only while that query runs, so it's always up to date and leaves no clutter. A saved table is a snapshot that goes stale when the data changes."},{"id":"12-4","n":4,"q":"What was the worst month for PM compliance in the data?","a":"**March 2025 (76.4 %)**, closely followed by December 2024 (76.9 %)."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst CASE statement\"* and *\"Alex the Analyst CTE\"*.","searches":["Alex the Analyst CASE statement","Alex the Analyst CTE"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Mode SQL Tutorial**: [mode.com/sql-tutorial](https://mode.com/sql-tutorial/). Read the *SQL CASE* and *SQL Date Format* pages.","searches":[],"urls":["https://mode.com/sql-tutorial/"]},{"i":2,"md":"SQLite date reference: [sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html).","searches":[],"urls":["https://www.sqlite.org/lang_datefunc.html"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's CASE grouped into On time, Late, Missed and Open plus the conditional `SUM` for 79.5 %, Session 2's compliance by month, the `julianday` days-late query and the three-step CTE (PPK 64.7 %, EOL 74.8 %), then weekend queries 1, 4 and 5 saved in `week12.sql`."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Alex The Analyst CASE and CTE videos, read the Mode SQL Tutorial CASE and Date Format pages and the \"Why it works\" notes on CASE order and integer division, and answer Check yourself questions 1 and 3 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend queries 2 and 3 (breakdowns by hour and by weekday), the stretch query 6 (H2 2025 against H1 2026) and the SQLite date reference page can be left for later."}}},{"week":13,"phase":2,"title":"Window functions, MTBF and MTTR","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-13-window-functions-mtbf.md","ticks":["LAG days-between-failures partitioned by asset","Running total with `SUM() OVER`","MTBF/MTTR/availability per asset, with LD-PPK-005 worst","Can explain why 99.8 % asset availability doesn't mean a good line","`week13.sql` saved"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 25 Jan – Sun 31 Jan 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n`GROUP BY` collapses rows. **Window functions** calculate across rows *without* collapsing them: \"days since this asset's last breakdown\", \"running total\", \"rank within zone\". They're how you get time between failures, which is the foundation of the reliability engineering in Phase 6.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use `ROW_NUMBER`, `RANK`, `LAG`, `LEAD` and `SUM() OVER`\n- explain `PARTITION BY` and `ORDER BY` inside `OVER (...)`\n- calculate **MTBF**, **MTTR** and **availability** per asset\n- calculate time between failures and spot repeat failures","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-window-function-basics","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Window function basics","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```sql\n-- Number each breakdown on LD-PPK-005 in order\nSELECT asset_id, wo_id, raised_datetime, failure_code,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY asset_id ORDER BY raised_datetime) AS failure_no\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive' AND asset_id = 'LD-PPK-005';\n```\n\nNow the important one, **LAG**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT asset_id, wo_id, raised_datetime, failure_code,\n       LAG(raised_datetime) OVER (PARTITION BY asset_id ORDER BY raised_datetime) AS previous_failure,\n       ROUND(julianday(raised_datetime)\n           - julianday(LAG(raised_datetime) OVER (PARTITION BY asset_id ORDER BY raised_datetime)), 1) AS days_since_last\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'Reactive' AND asset_id = 'LD-PPK-005';\n```\n\nThe first row has no previous failure (NULL). The second is **4.0 days** later, and the third is 13.3 days after that.\n\nRemove the `asset_id = 'LD-PPK-005'` filter and run it again. `PARTITION BY asset_id` restarts the LAG for every asset, so you never compare one asset's failure with another's.\n\n**Running total:**\n\n```sql\nWITH daily AS (\n    SELECT date(d.start_datetime) AS day, SUM(d.duration_min) / 60.0 AS hrs\n    FROM downtime_events AS d\n    JOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = d.asset_id\n    WHERE a.zone_code = 'PPK' AND d.category = 'Breakdown' AND d.start_datetime >= '2026-01-01'\n    GROUP BY day\n)\nSELECT day, ROUND(hrs, 2) AS hrs, ROUND(SUM(hrs) OVER (ORDER BY day), 2) AS running_hrs\nFROM daily\nORDER BY day;\n```\n\nBy the end of June, packing has lost **56.75 hours** to breakdowns in 2026.\n\n### Why it works\n\n```\nfunction() OVER (PARTITION BY group_column ORDER BY sort_column)\n```\n\n- **PARTITION BY** sets the groups (like GROUP BY), but every row is kept.\n- **ORDER BY** sets the sequence inside each group.\n- `LAG(x)` is the previous row's x, `LEAD(x)` is the next row's x, `ROW_NUMBER()` gives 1, 2, 3…, `RANK()` is like ROW_NUMBER but ties share a rank, and `SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY …)` is a running total.\n\nThink of it like a shift register on a PLC: LAG looks back one position in the sequence.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-reliability-metrics","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Reliability metrics","kind":"hands","md":"### The definitions\n\n| Metric | Meaning | Formula used in this course |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MTBF**, mean time between failures | how long it usually runs before breaking | operating time ÷ number of failures |\n| **MTTR**, mean time to repair | how long it's usually down when it breaks | total breakdown downtime ÷ number of breakdowns |\n| **Availability** (inherent) | share of time it's able to run | MTBF ÷ (MTBF + MTTR) |\n| **Failure rate (λ)** | failures per hour | 1 ÷ MTBF |\n\nOur window is **1 July 2024 – 30 June 2026 = 730 days = 17,520 hours**, assuming the line runs 24/7. (At work you'd use actual scheduled run hours. Ask your planner where those come from.)\n\n### Do it\n\n```sql\nWITH failures AS (\n    SELECT asset_id, COUNT(*) AS n_failures\n    FROM work_orders\n    WHERE wo_type = 'Reactive'\n    GROUP BY asset_id\n),\nrepairs AS (\n    SELECT asset_id, AVG(duration_min) / 60.0 AS mttr_hrs\n    FROM downtime_events\n    WHERE category = 'Breakdown'\n    GROUP BY asset_id\n)\nSELECT a.asset_id, a.asset_name, f.n_failures,\n       ROUND(17520.0 / f.n_failures, 0) AS mtbf_hrs,\n       ROUND(r.mttr_hrs, 2)             AS mttr_hrs,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (17520.0 / f.n_failures) / ((17520.0 / f.n_failures) + r.mttr_hrs), 2) AS availability_pct\nFROM failures AS f\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = f.asset_id\nLEFT JOIN repairs AS r ON r.asset_id = f.asset_id\nORDER BY mtbf_hrs\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nThe worst is **LD-PPK-005, Flow wrapper 5**: 37 failures, MTBF ≈ **474 h** (about 20 days), MTTR ≈ 0.76 h. The top four are all flow wrappers.\n\n### Why it works (and its limits)\n\nAvailability comes out at about 99.8 % even for the worst asset. That looks great, so why does packing feel so bad on the floor? Because:\n\n1. **These are single assets.** The line is a chain: if *any* of ~40 packing assets stops, packing stops. Line availability ≈ the product of every asset's availability in series.\n2. **Minor stops aren't included.** They're a separate loss (OEE *performance*), and in packing they cost more hours than breakdowns (Week 11).\n3. **MTBF hides the pattern.** 474 h is an average. Are the failures random, or do they bunch up as the jaws wear? That question is what **Weibull analysis** answers in Phase 6, and the `days_since_last` column you made with LAG is its raw input.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"Write in `week13.sql`:\n\n1. **MTBF and MTTR by asset type** (not per asset). Which type has the worst MTBF?\n2. **Mean days between failures by asset type** using LAG gaps, for types with ≥ 10 gaps.\n3. **Repeat failures**: count breakdowns that happened within **7 days** of the previous breakdown on the same asset. These usually point to a bad fix or a root cause that wasn't found.\n4. Same again, but **same asset and same failure code**. Show the top 5 assets.\n5. **Rank zones** by breakdown downtime hours within each quarter of 2026 (`RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY quarter ORDER BY hrs DESC)`).\n6. **LEAD**: for each PM completion on a flow wrapper, find the date of the next breakdown on that asset. How many days of \"breakdown-free\" running did each PM buy?","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst window functions\"*.\n- **Data with Baraa**: *\"Data with Baraa SQL window functions\"*. It's long but excellent.\n- **Reliability**: search *\"MTBF MTTR explained maintenance\"*. Pick a short video from a maintenance or reliability channel, not an IT one.\n- Docs: [sqlite.org/windowfunctions.html](https://www.sqlite.org/windowfunctions.html)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between `ROW_NUMBER()` and `RANK()` when two values tie?\n2. Why must LAG be partitioned by asset when calculating days between failures?\n3. An asset had 0 breakdowns. What's its MTBF in our formula, and how should you report it?\n4. Why is the window function version of \"days since last failure\" better than doing it in Excel?\n5. How many breakdowns happened within 7 days of the previous one on the same asset?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. ROW_NUMBER always gives 1, 2, 3, 4. RANK gives 1, 2, 2, 4 (ties share a rank and the next one is skipped). `DENSE_RANK` gives 1, 2, 2, 3.\n2. Otherwise LAG would compare the first failure of asset B with the last failure of asset A, which gives a meaningless gap.\n3. Division by zero, so it's undefined. Report it as \"> 17,520 h (no failures in window)\". Never report 0 or blank, because 0 would look like the *worst* asset.\n4. It runs over all 1,027 breakdowns at once, re-runs instantly when the data changes, can't be broken by someone sorting the sheet, and lives in a reusable query.\n5. **68**.\n\nMean days between failures, shortest first: Flow wrapper 26.6, Case packer 44.4, Carton erector 48.2, Label printer applicator 53.8.\n\nAnswers are in [`solutions/phase-2-sql/w13_window_reliability.sql`](../../solutions/phase-2-sql/w13_window_reliability.sql).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's LAG days-between-failures partitioned by asset and the `SUM() OVER` running total, Session 2's MTBF, MTTR and availability query (LD-PPK-005 worst), then weekend queries 1 to 3 saved in `week13.sql`. Phase 6 Weibull work uses these LAG gaps.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Alex The Analyst window functions video and a short \"MTBF MTTR explained\" video from a maintenance channel, read \"Why it works (and its limits)\" on why 99.8 % asset availability isn't a good line, and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head. At work, ask your planner where scheduled run hours come from.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend queries 4 to 6 (same failure code repeats, ranking zones by quarter, the LEAD query on flow wrapper PMs), the long Data with Baraa video and the SQLite window functions docs can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] LAG days-between-failures partitioned by asset\n- [ ] Running total with `SUM() OVER`\n- [ ] MTBF/MTTR/availability per asset, with LD-PPK-005 worst\n- [ ] Can explain why 99.8 % asset availability doesn't mean a good line\n- [ ] `week13.sql` saved"}],"quiz":[{"id":"13-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between `ROW_NUMBER()` and `RANK()` when two values tie?","a":"ROW_NUMBER always gives 1, 2, 3, 4. RANK gives 1, 2, 2, 4 (ties share a rank and the next one is skipped). `DENSE_RANK` gives 1, 2, 2, 3."},{"id":"13-2","n":2,"q":"Why must LAG be partitioned by asset when calculating days between failures?","a":"Otherwise LAG would compare the first failure of asset B with the last failure of asset A, which gives a meaningless gap."},{"id":"13-3","n":3,"q":"An asset had 0 breakdowns. What's its MTBF in our formula, and how should you report it?","a":"Division by zero, so it's undefined. Report it as \"> 17,520 h (no failures in window)\". Never report 0 or blank, because 0 would look like the *worst* asset."},{"id":"13-4","n":4,"q":"Why is the window function version of \"days since last failure\" better than doing it in Excel?","a":"It runs over all 1,027 breakdowns at once, re-runs instantly when the data changes, can't be broken by someone sorting the sheet, and lives in a reusable query."},{"id":"13-5","n":5,"q":"How many breakdowns happened within 7 days of the previous one on the same asset?","a":"**68**."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst window functions\"*.","searches":["Alex the Analyst window functions"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Data with Baraa**: *\"Data with Baraa SQL window functions\"*. It's long but excellent.","searches":["Data with Baraa SQL window functions"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Reliability**: search *\"MTBF MTTR explained maintenance\"*. Pick a short video from a maintenance or reliability channel, not an IT one.","searches":["MTBF MTTR explained maintenance"],"urls":[]},{"i":3,"md":"Docs: [sqlite.org/windowfunctions.html](https://www.sqlite.org/windowfunctions.html).","searches":[],"urls":["https://www.sqlite.org/windowfunctions.html"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's LAG days-between-failures partitioned by asset and the `SUM() OVER` running total, Session 2's MTBF, MTTR and availability query (LD-PPK-005 worst), then weekend queries 1 to 3 saved in `week13.sql`. Phase 6 Weibull work uses these LAG gaps."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Alex The Analyst window functions video and a short \"MTBF MTTR explained\" video from a maintenance channel, read \"Why it works (and its limits)\" on why 99.8 % asset availability isn't a good line, and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head. At work, ask your planner where scheduled run hours come from."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend queries 4 to 6 (same failure code repeats, ranking zones by quarter, the LEAD query on flow wrapper PMs), the long Data with Baraa video and the SQLite window functions docs can wait."}}},{"week":14,"phase":2,"title":"Data quality, and the Phase 2 build","path":"course/phase-2-sql/week-14-data-quality-phase2-build.md","ticks":["Seven data quality checks written and understood","`v_pm_results` view created","Done-when query written, matching the Week 6 Pareto","`portfolio/02-mini-cmms-sql/` committed, with README and findings"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 2 · Data modelling & SQL** · Mon 1 Feb – Sun 7 Feb 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nBefore you trust any analysis, you **test the data**. This week you build a set of data quality checks you can run against any CMMS export. Then you write the Phase 2 *done when* query and package your SQL work as a portfolio piece.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- check for duplicates, orphans, nulls, impossible values and outliers in SQL\n- check that codes follow a pattern with `GLOB`\n- create a reusable **view**\n- answer the Phase 2 question in one query","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-data-quality-checks","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Data quality checks","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nWrite each check and run it against `line_d.db`. A good check returns **nothing** (or 0) when the data is healthy.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Duplicate primary keys\nSELECT wo_id, COUNT(*) FROM work_orders GROUP BY wo_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n\n-- 2. Orphans: work orders on assets that don't exist\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS orphan_wos\nFROM work_orders AS w\nLEFT JOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = w.asset_id\nWHERE a.asset_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 3. Nulls in important columns (in SQLite a TRUE condition counts as 1)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS pm_rows,\n       SUM(due_date IS NULL)       AS null_due,\n       SUM(completed_date IS NULL) AS null_completed,\n       SUM(technician IS NULL)     AS null_technician\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE wo_type = 'PM';\n\n-- 4. Impossible sequence: completed before raised\nSELECT wo_id, raised_datetime, due_date, completed_date\nFROM work_orders\nWHERE completed_date < date(raised_datetime);\n\n-- 5. Status vs date consistency\nSELECT status,\n       SUM(completed_date IS NULL)     AS no_date,\n       SUM(completed_date IS NOT NULL) AS has_date\nFROM work_orders\nGROUP BY status;\n\n-- 6. Outliers: breakdowns over 12 hours\nSELECT event_id, asset_id, start_datetime, duration_min, cause\nFROM downtime_events\nWHERE category = 'Breakdown' AND duration_min > 720\nORDER BY duration_min DESC;\n\n-- 7. Codes that break the LD-XXX-NNN pattern\nSELECT asset_id\nFROM assets\nWHERE asset_id NOT GLOB 'LD-[A-Z][A-Z][A-Z]-[0-9][0-9][0-9]';\n```\n\n**What you should find:**\n\n- Checks 1, 2 and 7: clean.\n- Check 3: 534 null completions, which matches Missed + Open from Week 12. That's consistent.\n- Check 4: **10 rows**, PMs completed a day or two *before* the work order was raised. In real life this happens when the PM gets done early and the paperwork is backfilled. It isn't wrong data, but you need to know about it. If you calculated \"response time\" on these, you'd get negative numbers.\n- Check 6: **4 events over 12 hours**, the longest 1,375 min. Are those real (a big bearing job over New Year), or did someone forget to close the stop? At work, you'd ask. Never silently delete outliers.\n\n### Why it works\n\nEvery check is something you already know (GROUP BY, LEFT JOIN, CASE), pointed at the data instead of the business question. A professional analyst runs checks like these **every time** new data arrives, before building anything on it.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-views-and-the-done-when-query","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Views and the done-when query","kind":"hands","md":"### Views\n\nA **view** is a saved query that behaves like a table. Make one for the logic you keep repeating:\n\n```sql\nCREATE VIEW v_pm_results AS\nSELECT w.wo_id, w.asset_id, a.zone_code, a.criticality, w.due_date, w.completed_date,\n       CASE\n         WHEN w.status = 'Open'              THEN 'Open'\n         WHEN w.completed_date IS NULL       THEN 'Missed'\n         WHEN w.completed_date <= w.due_date THEN 'On time'\n         ELSE 'Late'\n       END AS pm_result,\n       CASE WHEN w.completed_date <= w.due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS on_time\nFROM work_orders AS w\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = w.asset_id\nWHERE w.wo_type = 'PM';\n```\n\nNow `SELECT zone_code, AVG(on_time) FROM v_pm_results GROUP BY zone_code;` is one line. (Remember **Write Changes**, `Ctrl+S`, after creating a view.)\n\n> Do this in your own copy of the database, or keep a backup of `line_d.db`. You can always regenerate it by running `python data/generate_data.py` (from Phase 5 onwards).\n\n### Phase 2 *done when*\n\n> **Which 10 assets lost the most hours to reactive work last quarter, and were their PMs on time?**\n\n\"Last quarter\" is **Q2 2026** (1 April – 30 June), the latest full quarter in the data.\n\nTry it yourself first. You need:\n\n1. a CTE of breakdown downtime hours per asset in Q2\n2. a CTE of PMs due and PMs on time per asset in Q2\n3. a join of both to `assets`, sorted by hours, top 10\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Show the solution<\/strong> (only after a real attempt)<\/summary>\n\n```sql\nWITH loss AS (\n    SELECT d.asset_id, SUM(d.duration_min) / 60.0 AS reactive_hrs, COUNT(*) AS breakdowns\n    FROM downtime_events AS d\n    WHERE d.category = 'Breakdown'\n      AND d.start_datetime >= '2026-04-01' AND d.start_datetime < '2026-07-01'\n    GROUP BY d.asset_id\n),\npm AS (\n    SELECT asset_id,\n           COUNT(*) AS pms_due,\n           SUM(CASE WHEN completed_date <= due_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS pms_on_time\n    FROM work_orders\n    WHERE wo_type = 'PM' AND due_date BETWEEN '2026-04-01' AND '2026-06-30'\n    GROUP BY asset_id\n)\nSELECT a.asset_id, a.asset_name, a.criticality,\n       l.breakdowns,\n       ROUND(l.reactive_hrs, 1) AS reactive_hrs,\n       p.pms_due, p.pms_on_time,\n       ROUND(100.0 * p.pms_on_time / p.pms_due, 0) AS pm_on_time_pct\nFROM loss AS l\nJOIN assets AS a ON a.asset_id = l.asset_id\nLEFT JOIN pm AS p ON p.asset_id = l.asset_id\nORDER BY l.reactive_hrs DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n| Asset | Name | Breakdowns | Reactive hrs | PM on time |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LD-FLK-010 | Flaking roll 2 | 1 | 9.6 | 6/8 (75 %) |\n| LD-FLK-018 | Flaking roll 10 | 1 | 8.6 | 8/8 (100 %) |\n| LD-PPK-030 | Primary Packing Servo drive 3 | 1 | 6.7 | 1/1 (100 %) |\n| LD-PPK-003 | Primary Packing Flow wrapper 3 | 3 | 5.4 | 9/14 (64 %) |\n| LD-PPK-002 | Primary Packing Flow wrapper 2 | 4 | 4.7 | 5/14 (36 %) |\n| LD-EOL-008 | End of Line Label printer applicator 1 | 3 | 4.5 | 9/13 (69 %) |\n| LD-FLK-006 | Flaking Drive motor 6 | 1 | 4.5 | 4/4 (100 %) |\n| LD-EOL-028 | End of Line Drive motor 4 | 1 | 4.3 | 4/4 (100 %) |\n| LD-EOL-016 | End of Line Conveyor 6 | 1 | 4.2 | 4/6 (67 %) |\n| LD-EOL-002 | End of Line Case packer 2 | 1 | 4.0 | 7/7 (100 %) |\n\nIt matches the Week 6 Excel Pareto: same assets, same order. **That cross-check is the real done-when.**\n\n**Reading it:** the top two are single long flaking-roll jobs, so they're about severity. Flow wrappers 2 and 3 are about **frequency** and have the worst PM compliance. Those are two different problems with two different fixes, and that's exactly the kind of distinction an engineering manager wants to hear.\n\n<\/details>","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-build-mini-cmms-portfolio-piece","title":"Weekend build (2 h): Mini CMMS portfolio piece","kind":"hands","md":"Create a folder `portfolio/02-mini-cmms-sql/` in your GitHub repo (see `portfolio/README.md`) containing:\n\n1. `schema.sql`: your `CREATE TABLE` statements from Week 9, plus the `v_pm_results` view.\n2. `quality_checks.sql`: this week's checks, each with a comment explaining what \"good\" looks like.\n3. `analysis.sql`: your best queries, covering PM compliance by month and zone, MTBF/MTTR per asset, time between failures with LAG, repeat failures, and the done-when query.\n4. `README.md`: one paragraph on what the database is, an ERD image of your star schema, and 3–5 findings written in plain English, like *\"Flow wrappers account for 4 of the 5 lowest-MTBF assets; LD-PPK-005 fails every ~20 days.\"*\n\nTick off **Phase 2** in the tracker. 🎉","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst data cleaning SQL\"*.\n- **SQLBolt**: finish any lessons you skipped (13–18 cover creating and changing tables).\n- Book (dip in, don't read cover to cover): *SQL for Data Analysis* by Cathy Tanimura, chapters 2 (data preparation) and 3 (time series)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Which data quality check would catch the \"Prim Pack\" problem from Week 4, if the zone were stored as free text on the work order?\n2. Why are views better than copying the same CASE logic into every query?\n3. Why should you check your SQL done-when answer against the Excel Pareto?\n4. Write a check that finds PM work orders whose `pm_id` doesn't exist in `pm_tasks`.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `SELECT zone, COUNT(*) FROM work_orders GROUP BY zone;`, which lists the distinct values, or an orphan check (`LEFT JOIN zones … IS NULL`) against the trusted zones table.\n2. One definition of \"on time\", so every query agrees and you only fix it once. It's the same reason as LAMBDA in Week 5.\n3. Two independent methods giving the same answer is the strongest evidence that both are right. If they differ, one has a bug, and finding it teaches you something.\n4. ```sql\n   SELECT w.wo_id, w.pm_id\n   FROM work_orders AS w\n   LEFT JOIN pm_tasks AS p ON p.pm_id = w.pm_id\n   WHERE w.pm_id IS NOT NULL AND p.pm_id IS NULL;\n   ```\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 2 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the seven Session 1 checks, the `v_pm_results` view, the done-when query matching the Week 6 Pareto, and the `portfolio/02-mini-cmms-sql/` folder with all four files. That is more than 90 minutes, so give the portfolio folder its own sitting.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Alex The Analyst data cleaning SQL video, read the \"What you should find\" notes and the \"Reading it\" paragraph under the solution, and draft your 3 to 5 plain-English README findings on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only the extras can wait: SQLBolt lessons 13 to 18, the Cathy Tanimura book chapters and a fresh ERD drawing (reuse your Week 9 star schema for the README image)."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Seven data quality checks written and understood\n- [ ] `v_pm_results` view created\n- [ ] **Done-when query** written, matching the Week 6 Pareto\n- [ ] `portfolio/02-mini-cmms-sql/` committed, with README and findings"}],"quiz":[{"id":"14-1","n":1,"q":"Which data quality check would catch the \"Prim Pack\" problem from Week 4, if the zone were stored as free text on the work order?","a":"`SELECT zone, COUNT(*) FROM work_orders GROUP BY zone;`, which lists the distinct values, or an orphan check (`LEFT JOIN zones … IS NULL`) against the trusted zones table."},{"id":"14-2","n":2,"q":"Why are views better than copying the same CASE logic into every query?","a":"One definition of \"on time\", so every query agrees and you only fix it once. It's the same reason as LAMBDA in Week 5."},{"id":"14-3","n":3,"q":"Why should you check your SQL done-when answer against the Excel Pareto?","a":"Two independent methods giving the same answer is the strongest evidence that both are right. If they differ, one has a bug, and finding it teaches you something."},{"id":"14-4","n":4,"q":"Write a check that finds PM work orders whose `pm_id` doesn't exist in `pm_tasks`.","a":"```sql\nSELECT w.wo_id, w.pm_id\nFROM work_orders AS w\nLEFT JOIN pm_tasks AS p ON p.pm_id = w.pm_id\nWHERE w.pm_id IS NOT NULL AND p.pm_id IS NULL;\n```"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Alex The Analyst**: *\"Alex the Analyst data cleaning SQL\"*.","searches":["Alex the Analyst data cleaning SQL"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**SQLBolt**: finish any lessons you skipped (13–18 cover creating and changing tables).","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book (dip in, don't read cover to cover): *SQL for Data Analysis* by Cathy Tanimura, chapters 2 (data preparation) and 3 (time series).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 2 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the seven Session 1 checks, the `v_pm_results` view, the done-when query matching the Week 6 Pareto, and the `portfolio/02-mini-cmms-sql/` folder with all four files. That is more than 90 minutes, so give the portfolio folder its own sitting."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Alex The Analyst data cleaning SQL video, read the \"What you should find\" notes and the \"Reading it\" paragraph under the solution, and draft your 3 to 5 plain-English README findings on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only the extras can wait: SQLBolt lessons 13 to 18, the Cathy Tanimura book chapters and a fresh ERD drawing (reuse your Week 9 star schema for the README image)."}}},{"week":15,"phase":3,"title":"Power BI Desktop and getting data in","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-15-power-bi-desktop-get-data.md","ticks":["Power BI Desktop installed","Six tables plus Calendar loaded with correct types and row counts","`PM Result`, `Is Planned`, `Raised Date` and `Duration Hrs` columns added in Power Query","First card, bar, table and slicer on the canvas","`LineD_Maintenance.pbix` saved"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 8 Feb – Sun 14 Feb 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 3 goal:** a Line D maintenance dashboard that the team could use in a weekly review **without you explaining it**. This is the longest phase (10 weeks), because DAX is where most people stall, and the payoff at work is huge.\n\nGood news: Power BI Desktop *is* Power Query + the Data Model + charts. You already know two of those three.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- find your way around Power BI Desktop's three views (Report, Table, Model)\n- load all the Line D tables from CSV with correct types\n- reuse your Power Query skills in Power BI\n- build your first visuals and save a `.pbix`\n\n**Install:** Power BI Desktop is free from the **Microsoft Store** (search *Power BI Desktop*). The Store version updates itself. It runs on Windows only.\n\n**Before you start at work:** check with IT whether Power BI Desktop is allowed on your work laptop, and whether the site has Power BI *Pro* licences for sharing (Week 23). For now, everything happens on your home PC with practice data.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-tour-and-first-load","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Tour and first load","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Open Power BI Desktop → **Blank report**. Look at the left-hand bar: **Report** view (canvas), **Table** view (data grid) and **Model** view (relationships).\n2. Home → Get Data → **Text/CSV** → `data/csv/assets.csv` → **Transform Data**. The Power Query editor opens, the same one as in Excel.\n3. Check the types. `install_date` should be *Date* and `criticality` *Text*. Name the query `Assets`.\n4. Load the other tables the same way, and rename each query:\n\n   | File | Query name |\n   |---|---|\n   | `work_orders.csv` | `WorkOrders` |\n   | `downtime_events.csv` | `Downtime` |\n   | `pm_tasks.csv` | `PMTasks` |\n   | `zones.csv` | `Zones` |\n   | `sensor_rollout.csv` | `SensorRollout` |\n\n5. Type checks in `WorkOrders`:\n   - `raised_datetime` → **Date/Time**\n   - `due_date` and `completed_date` → **Date** (blanks become `null`, which is correct)\n   - `labour_hours` → Decimal, and `priority` → Whole Number\n6. In `Downtime`, set `start_datetime`/`end_datetime` → Date/Time and `duration_min` → Whole Number.\n7. **Close & Apply.** Go to Table view and click each table. Check the row count at the bottom: WorkOrders **11,902**, Downtime **6,177**, Assets **292**.\n8. Save as `LineD_Maintenance.pbix`.\n\n### Why it works\n\nThese CSVs use ISO dates (`2026-06-30`), so there's no UK/US ambiguity and Power BI types them correctly. That's another reason ISO is the standard for data files.\n\nA `.pbix` stores the queries, the compressed data, the model and the report all in one file. When the CSVs change, **Home → Refresh** re-runs the queries.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-shape-data-in-power-query-powe","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Shape data in Power Query (Power BI edition)","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nOpen **Transform data**. In `WorkOrders`:\n\n1. Add Column → Custom Column `Raised Date` = `DateTime.Date([raised_datetime])`, type Date. You'll need a date-only column to relate to a calendar.\n2. Add a conditional column `Is Planned`: if `wo_type` equals `Reactive` then `0` else `1`.\n3. Add `PM Result` (Custom Column):\n\n   ```\n   if [wo_type] <> \"PM\" then null\n   else if [status] = \"Open\" then \"Open\"\n   else if [completed_date] = null then \"Missed\"\n   else if [completed_date] <= [due_date] then \"On time\"\n   else \"Late\"\n   ```\n\n   It's the same logic as your SQL CASE in Week 12. Set its type to Text.\n4. In `Downtime`, add `Start Date` = `DateTime.Date([start_datetime])`, and `Duration Hrs` = `[duration_min] / 60`.\n5. **Build a Calendar in Power Query.** New Source → Blank Query → Advanced Editor → paste the M code from Week 8 (1 July 2024 – 31 Dec 2026), and add a `Year` column at the end:\n\n   ```\n   Year = Table.AddColumn(Week, \"Year\", each Date.Year([Date]), Int64.Type)\n   ```\n\n   (Remember to change the final `in` line to `Year`.) Name it `Calendar`.\n6. Close & Apply.\n\n### Why it works\n\nThe rule from Phase 1 still holds: **do row-level shaping in Power Query** (types, flags, labels, date-only columns), and **do aggregation in DAX measures** (next weeks). Power Query results are stored and compressed; measures are calculated on demand.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-task-first-visuals","title":"Weekend task (2 h): First visuals","kind":"hands","md":"On the Report canvas:\n\n1. **Card**: drag `WorkOrders[wo_id]` onto the canvas → change the visual to *Card* → it shows Count of wo_id = **11,902**.\n2. **Clustered bar**: `Assets[zone]` on the Y-axis and `WorkOrders[wo_id]` (Count) on the X-axis. It won't split properly by zone yet. **Why not?** Write down your guess. (Next week's relationships fix it.)\n3. **Table** visual: `WorkOrders[wo_type]`, Count of wo_id, Sum of labour_hours.\n4. **Slicer**: `WorkOrders[wo_type]`. Click Reactive and watch the other visuals change.\n5. **Explore the formatting pane** (the paintbrush icon): titles, data labels, colours. Make the bar chart readable.\n6. Play. Drag fields about and try different visuals. Nothing you do here can break the data.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube Power BI for beginners\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: the free PL-300 learning path. Search *\"PL-300 Microsoft Learn study guide\"*, then do the module *Get data in Power BI*.\n- **Kevin Stratvert**: *\"Kevin Stratvert Power BI tutorial for beginners\"*, a full one-hour walkthrough."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Which view do you use to check a table's row count? Which do you use to set relationships?\n2. Why add a date-only `Raised Date` column when `raised_datetime` already exists?\n3. Where should \"is this PM on time?\" be calculated, in Power Query or as a DAX measure? Why?\n4. Your bar of work orders by zone shows the same number (11,902) for every zone. Why?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Table view for row counts. Model view for relationships.\n2. A calendar has one row per **date**. `2026-06-14 09:30` won't match `2026-06-14` in a relationship, so you need the date part on its own.\n3. Power Query (or a calculated column): it's a property of **each row**, and you'll want to filter and group by it. The *percentage* on time is the measure.\n4. There's no relationship between Assets and WorkOrders yet, so the zone filter can't reach the work orders, and every bar shows the grand total. (Power BI may have auto-detected the relationship. If so, your chart is already right. Check Model view.)\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 2 to 8 (six tables with correct types, row counts 11,902 / 6,177 / 292, saved as `LineD_Maintenance.pbix`), Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (`Raised Date`, `Is Planned`, `PM Result`, `Start Date`, `Duration Hrs` and the Calendar), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for the first card, bar, table and slicer.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Guy in a Cube Power BI for beginners video, read the PL-300 *Get data in Power BI* module and both \"Why it works\" parts, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask IT whether Power BI Desktop is allowed and whether the site has Pro licences.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend steps 5 and 6 (the formatting pane and free play) and the hour-long Kevin Stratvert walkthrough can be skipped."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Power BI Desktop installed\n- [ ] Six tables plus Calendar loaded with correct types and row counts\n- [ ] `PM Result`, `Is Planned`, `Raised Date` and `Duration Hrs` columns added in Power Query\n- [ ] First card, bar, table and slicer on the canvas\n- [ ] `LineD_Maintenance.pbix` saved"}],"quiz":[{"id":"15-1","n":1,"q":"Which view do you use to check a table's row count? Which do you use to set relationships?","a":"Table view for row counts. Model view for relationships."},{"id":"15-2","n":2,"q":"Why add a date-only `Raised Date` column when `raised_datetime` already exists?","a":"A calendar has one row per **date**. `2026-06-14 09:30` won't match `2026-06-14` in a relationship, so you need the date part on its own."},{"id":"15-3","n":3,"q":"Where should \"is this PM on time?\" be calculated, in Power Query or as a DAX measure? Why?","a":"Power Query (or a calculated column): it's a property of **each row**, and you'll want to filter and group by it. The *percentage* on time is the measure."},{"id":"15-4","n":4,"q":"Your bar of work orders by zone shows the same number (11,902) for every zone. Why?","a":"There's no relationship between Assets and WorkOrders yet, so the zone filter can't reach the work orders, and every bar shows the grand total. (Power BI may have auto-detected the relationship. If so, your chart is already right. Check Model view.)"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube Power BI for beginners\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube Power BI for beginners"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: the free PL-300 learning path. Search *\"PL-300 Microsoft Learn study guide\"*, then do the module *Get data in Power BI*.","searches":["PL-300 Microsoft Learn study guide"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Kevin Stratvert**: *\"Kevin Stratvert Power BI tutorial for beginners\"*, a full one-hour walkthrough.","searches":["Kevin Stratvert Power BI tutorial for beginners"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 2 to 8 (six tables with correct types, row counts 11,902 / 6,177 / 292, saved as `LineD_Maintenance.pbix`), Session 2 steps 1 to 6 (`Raised Date`, `Is Planned`, `PM Result`, `Start Date`, `Duration Hrs` and the Calendar), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for the first card, bar, table and slicer."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Guy in a Cube Power BI for beginners video, read the PL-300 *Get data in Power BI* module and both \"Why it works\" parts, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask IT whether Power BI Desktop is allowed and whether the site has Pro licences."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend steps 5 and 6 (the formatting pane and free play) and the hour-long Kevin Stratvert walkthrough can be skipped."}}},{"week":16,"phase":3,"title":"Modelling, star schema, relationships and the date table","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-16-modelling-star-schema.md","ticks":["Star schema built: 7 active relationships plus 1 inactive (due date)","Auto date/time off, Calendar marked as date table, months sort correctly","Model tested with two facts sliced by shared dimensions","Columns renamed, formatted and foldered; keys hidden","`model.png` saved"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 15 Feb – Sun 21 Feb 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThe model is the foundation. Get it right and DAX is easy. Get it wrong and every measure turns into a fight. You designed this star schema on paper in Week 9; now you build it.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- create one-to-many relationships with single-direction filtering\n- explain **cardinality** and **cross-filter direction**\n- mark a proper **date table**\n- handle **two dates on one fact** (raised vs due) with an inactive relationship\n- hide technical columns and organise the field list","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-build-the-star","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Build the star","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Go to **Model view**. If Power BI auto-created relationships, **delete them all**. You'll build them deliberately. (Also turn off File → Options → Current File → Data Load → *Autodetect new relationships* and *Auto date/time*.)\n2. Drag to create these relationships, **dimension → fact**:\n\n   | From (1 side) | To (* side) |\n   |---|---|\n   | `Zones[zone_code]` | `Assets[zone_code]` |\n   | `Assets[asset_id]` | `WorkOrders[asset_id]` |\n   | `Assets[asset_id]` | `Downtime[asset_id]` |\n   | `Assets[asset_id]` | `PMTasks[asset_id]` |\n   | `Assets[asset_id]` | `SensorRollout[asset_id]` |\n   | `Calendar[Date]` | `WorkOrders[Raised Date]` |\n   | `Calendar[Date]` | `Downtime[Start Date]` |\n\n3. Double-click each relationship and check it's *Many to one (\\*:1)* with cross-filter direction **Single**.\n4. **Second date relationship**: create `Calendar[Date]` → `WorkOrders[due_date]`. Power BI makes it **inactive** (a dotted line), because there can only be one active path between two tables. That's what you want: you'll switch it on in DAX for PM compliance (Week 19).\n5. **Mark as date table**: select `Calendar` in Table view → Table tools → *Mark as date table* → choose `Date`.\n6. Tidy the field list:\n   - Hide the key columns on the fact tables (right-click → *Hide in report view*): `WorkOrders[asset_id]`, `Downtime[asset_id]` and so on. Users should pick asset fields from `Assets`, not from the facts.\n   - Hide `raised_datetime` and `start_datetime` if you don't need them on visuals.\n   - Set `Calendar[Month Name]` → *Sort by column* → `Month`, so months sort Jan, Feb, Mar instead of alphabetically.\n7. Now re-check last week's zone bar chart. Each zone now shows its own count. 🎉\n\n### Why it works\n\nFilters flow **from the 1 side to the many side**, down the arrow. Click \"Primary Packing\" in a slicer and:\n\n```\nZones (PPK) → Assets (PPK assets) → WorkOrders (their jobs)\n                                   → Downtime (their stops)\n```\n\nFacts never filter dimensions (with Single direction), and facts never filter each other directly. That's deliberate: it stops ambiguous paths and wrong totals.\n\n**Why not just merge everything into one big table?** You could, but then downtime and work orders (two facts at different grains) would have to be squashed together, and you'd get fan-out (Week 11). A star keeps each fact at its natural grain and lets the dimensions slice both.\n\n**Why turn off Auto date/time?** It silently creates a hidden calendar for *every* date column. That bloats the file and doesn't work with proper time intelligence. One explicit Calendar is the professional way.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-test-the-model","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Test the model","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nBuild a **Matrix** visual: `Zones[zone]` on Rows, `Calendar[Year]` on Columns, Count of `WorkOrders[wo_id]` in Values. Then test the model:\n\n1. Add `Downtime[duration_min]` (Sum). Both facts slice correctly by zone and year, because they share dimensions.\n2. Add a slicer on `Assets[criticality]`. Both facts respond.\n3. Try putting `Downtime[category]` in a slicer. Do work order counts change? (They shouldn't. Downtime can't filter WorkOrders.) That's correct behaviour, so make sure you understand why.\n4. Put `WorkOrders[wo_type]` on the rows of a new matrix with `Downtime` minutes. Every row shows the same total. Explain why to yourself.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-task-model-hygiene-and-documenta","title":"Weekend task (2 h): Model hygiene and documentation","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Rename for humans** (in the model, not in Power Query): `asset_name` → *Asset*, `zone` → *Zone*, `wo_type` → *WO Type*, `labour_hours` → *Labour Hrs*, and so on. Report users should never see underscores.\n2. **Set data categories and formats**: percentages, whole numbers, date formats `dd/mm/yyyy`.\n3. **Default summarisation**: set `priority`, `frequency_days` and `rollout_wave` to *Don't summarize*. Nobody wants a \"Sum of priority\".\n4. **Display folders**: in Model view, group `Assets` columns into folders like *Identity* and *Classification*.\n5. Take a screenshot of your Model view and save it as `model.png` for your portfolio.\n6. Read *\"Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI\"* on Microsoft Learn (search that title). It's the single best page on this topic.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube star schema Power BI\"*.\n- **SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI relationships Power BI\"*. It's precise and goes deeper.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *Design a semantic model in Power BI*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What does the arrow on a relationship line show?\n2. Why can't there be two active relationships between `Calendar` and `WorkOrders`?\n3. A slicer on `Downtime[category]` doesn't change your work order card. Is that a bug?\n4. When might you need a **bidirectional** filter, and why is it risky?\n5. Why hide `WorkOrders[asset_id]` but keep `Assets[asset_id]` visible?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The direction filters travel, from the \"1\" (dimension) to the \"many\" (fact).\n2. If both were active, a date filter would be ambiguous: does 1 June mean raised on 1 June or due on 1 June? Power BI forces one active path, and you activate the other one in specific measures with `USERELATIONSHIP`.\n3. No. Downtime is a fact, and facts don't filter other facts. If you need \"work orders on assets that had breakdown stops\", that's a measure-level question (Week 18).\n4. For example, if you want a slicer of *assets* to only list assets that have downtime in the selected period. Bidirectional filters can create ambiguous paths and slow, surprising results, so avoid them. If you must, use `CROSSFILTER` inside one measure.\n5. Users should always slice by the **dimension's** column, which filters every fact. The fact's copy only filters that one fact, which causes confusing results.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 7 (seven active relationships plus the inactive due date one, Auto date/time off, Calendar marked as date table, months sorted, keys hidden) and the four Session 2 model tests, then weekend step 1 (rename for humans) and step 5 (`model.png`).\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Guy in a Cube star schema video, read the \"Why it works\" part on filters flowing from the 1 side and on Auto date/time, read the Microsoft Learn page *Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI*, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend steps 2 to 4 (formats, default summarisation and display folders) are tidy-up you can do in a lighter week, and the SQLBI relationships video and PL-300 modelling module can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Star schema built: 7 active relationships plus 1 inactive (due date)\n- [ ] Auto date/time off, Calendar marked as date table, months sort correctly\n- [ ] Model tested with two facts sliced by shared dimensions\n- [ ] Columns renamed, formatted and foldered; keys hidden\n- [ ] `model.png` saved"}],"quiz":[{"id":"16-1","n":1,"q":"What does the arrow on a relationship line show?","a":"The direction filters travel, from the \"1\" (dimension) to the \"many\" (fact)."},{"id":"16-2","n":2,"q":"Why can't there be two active relationships between `Calendar` and `WorkOrders`?","a":"If both were active, a date filter would be ambiguous: does 1 June mean raised on 1 June or due on 1 June? Power BI forces one active path, and you activate the other one in specific measures with `USERELATIONSHIP`."},{"id":"16-3","n":3,"q":"A slicer on `Downtime[category]` doesn't change your work order card. Is that a bug?","a":"No. Downtime is a fact, and facts don't filter other facts. If you need \"work orders on assets that had breakdown stops\", that's a measure-level question (Week 18)."},{"id":"16-4","n":4,"q":"When might you need a **bidirectional** filter, and why is it risky?","a":"For example, if you want a slicer of *assets* to only list assets that have downtime in the selected period. Bidirectional filters can create ambiguous paths and slow, surprising results, so avoid them. If you must, use `CROSSFILTER` inside one measure."},{"id":"16-5","n":5,"q":"Why hide `WorkOrders[asset_id]` but keep `Assets[asset_id]` visible?","a":"Users should always slice by the **dimension's** column, which filters every fact. The fact's copy only filters that one fact, which causes confusing results."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube star schema Power BI\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube star schema Power BI"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI relationships Power BI\"*. It's precise and goes deeper.","searches":["SQLBI relationships Power BI"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *Design a semantic model in Power BI*.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 7 (seven active relationships plus the inactive due date one, Auto date/time off, Calendar marked as date table, months sorted, keys hidden) and the four Session 2 model tests, then weekend step 1 (rename for humans) and step 5 (`model.png`)."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Guy in a Cube star schema video, read the \"Why it works\" part on filters flowing from the 1 side and on Auto date/time, read the Microsoft Learn page *Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI*, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend steps 2 to 4 (formats, default summarisation and display folders) are tidy-up you can do in a lighter week, and the SQLBI relationships video and PL-300 modelling module can wait."}}},{"week":17,"phase":3,"title":"DAX 1, measures vs calculated columns","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-17-dax-measures-basics.md","ticks":["`_Measures` table with 12+ formatted measures, all matching the reference values","Age Band calculated column plus a chart","`MTTR Hrs` written with VAR/RETURN (1.86 h)","KPI page v1 with cards, charts and slicers","SQLBI *Introducing DAX*, modules 1–3"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 22 Feb – Sun 28 Feb 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nDAX (*Data Analysis Expressions*) is the formula language of Power BI. It looks like Excel, but it thinks in **tables and filters** rather than cells. This week you build the core KPI measures for the dashboard and learn the one distinction that matters most: **measure vs calculated column**.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- create measures with `SUM`, `COUNTROWS`, `DISTINCTCOUNT`, `AVERAGE` and `DIVIDE`\n- organise measures in a dedicated measures table, with formats\n- explain when to use a calculated column instead\n- use `VAR` / `RETURN` for readable measures","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-your-first-measures","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Your first measures","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Create a measures table**: Home → Enter Data → name it `_Measures`, then load it (it has one dummy column). Every measure goes here. After you add your first measure, you can hide the dummy column, and the table jumps to the top of the field list with a calculator icon.\n2. Select `_Measures` → **New measure**, and type each of these. (`Shift+Enter` gives a new line in the formula bar.)\n\n   ```dax\n   Total WOs = COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders )\n\n   Labour Hrs = SUM ( WorkOrders[Labour Hrs] )\n\n   Reactive Jobs =\n   CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" )\n\n   Reactive Labour Hrs =\n   CALCULATE ( [Labour Hrs], WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" )\n\n   Reactive Labour % = DIVIDE ( [Reactive Labour Hrs], [Labour Hrs] )\n\n   Planned % (count) = 1 - DIVIDE ( [Reactive Jobs], [Total WOs] )\n\n   Breakdown Hrs =\n   CALCULATE ( SUM ( Downtime[Duration Hrs] ), Downtime[category] = \"Breakdown\" )\n\n   Minor Stop Hrs =\n   CALCULATE ( SUM ( Downtime[Duration Hrs] ), Downtime[category] = \"Minor stop\" )\n\n   Assets With Breakdowns =\n   CALCULATE ( DISTINCTCOUNT ( WorkOrders[asset_id] ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" )\n   ```\n\n   (Use whatever names you gave the columns in Week 16, e.g. `WorkOrders[WO Type]` or `WorkOrders[wo_type]`.)\n3. **Format each one** (Measure tools ribbon): % for the percentages, 1 decimal for hours, and a thousands separator for counts.\n4. Put them all on **Cards** and check them against the reference values below.\n\n### Reference values (whole data set, no filters)\n\n| Measure | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Total WOs | 11,902 |\n| Labour Hrs | 9,654.9 |\n| Reactive Jobs | 1,027 |\n| Reactive Labour % | 24.5 % |\n| Planned % (count) | 91.4 % |\n| Breakdown Hrs | 1,321.4 |\n| Minor Stop Hrs | 776.6 |\n| Assets With Breakdowns | 260 |\n\nLook at **Planned % (91 %)** next to **Reactive Labour % (25 %)**. By count, reactive work looks small. By hours, it's a quarter of the workload. Choose your KPI definitions deliberately, and write them down.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-measures-vs-calculated-columns","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Measures vs calculated columns, and VAR","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Calculated column** (in the `Assets` table → New column):\n\n   ```dax\n   Asset Age Yrs = DATEDIFF ( Assets[install_date], DATE ( 2026, 6, 30 ), YEAR )\n   ```\n\n   And an age band:\n\n   ```dax\n   Age Band =\n   SWITCH (\n       TRUE (),\n       Assets[Asset Age Yrs] < 2, \"0-2 yrs\",\n       Assets[Asset Age Yrs] < 8, \"2-8 yrs\",\n       \"8+ yrs\"\n   )\n   ```\n\n2. Make a bar chart of `Reactive Jobs` by `Age Band`. Do old assets fail more? Be careful: the 8+ band holds about 200 of the 292 assets, so of course it has the most jobs. Compare **jobs per asset** instead (you'll build that measure this weekend). And remember that kit installed during the window hasn't been running for the full two years. Unfair comparisons like these are the most common analysis mistake.\n3. **The same thing as a measure doesn't work.** Try making `Asset Age Yrs` a measure with the same formula and you'll get an error. A measure has no \"current row\" for `Assets[install_date]` to come from.\n4. **VAR / RETURN** makes measures readable and efficient:\n\n   ```dax\n   MTTR Hrs =\n   VAR BreakdownHours = [Breakdown Hrs]\n   VAR Breakdowns =\n       CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Downtime ), Downtime[category] = \"Breakdown\" )\n   RETURN\n       DIVIDE ( BreakdownHours, Breakdowns )\n   ```\n\n   You should get **1.86 h** overall. Slice it by zone: Flaking has the longest repairs (3.88 h), and packing the shortest (1.17 h).\n\n### Why it works\n\n| | Calculated column | Measure |\n|---|---|---|\n| Calculated | at refresh, once per row | at query time, for each cell of a visual |\n| Stored | yes (uses memory) | no |\n| Has a \"current row\" | yes | no; it works over the filtered set of rows |\n| Use for | slicing, filtering, grouping (age band, flags) | numbers you aggregate (totals, %, ratios) |\n\n**Rule of thumb:** if it goes on an axis or in a slicer, it's a column (and better still done in Power Query). If it goes in *Values*, it's a measure.\n\n`VAR` works like `LET` in Excel. Each variable is calculated once, and `RETURN` gives the result.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-task-kpi-page-version-1","title":"Weekend task (2 h): KPI page, version 1","kind":"hands","md":"1. Add these measures:\n   - `PMs Due = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"PM\" )`, which gives 10,502\n   - `PMs On Time = CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[PM Result] = \"On time\" )`, which gives 8,349\n   - `PM Compliance % = DIVIDE ( [PMs On Time], [PMs Due] )`, which gives **79.5 %**\n   - `Corrective Jobs`, `Avg Labour per Reactive Job`, and `Breakdowns per Asset = DIVIDE ( [Reactive Jobs], [Assets With Breakdowns] )`\n2. Build a page with: a row of KPI cards across the top (PM Compliance %, Reactive Jobs, Breakdown Hrs, MTTR Hrs, Reactive Labour %), a bar chart of `PM Compliance %` by Zone, a column chart of `Breakdown Hrs` by `Calendar[Month]`, and slicers for Zone and Criticality.\n3. Check: slice to **Primary Packing** and the compliance card should drop to **66.4 %** over all two years. (If you also filter the Calendar, it filters on *raised* date for now. Week 19 switches PM compliance to the due-date relationship properly.)","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **SQLBI**: the free *Introducing DAX* video course. Register at [sqlbi.com/p/introducing-dax-video-course](https://www.sqlbi.com/p/introducing-dax-video-course/) and watch the first three modules this week.\n- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube measures vs calculated columns\"*.\n- Reference: [dax.guide](https://dax.guide) has every DAX function with examples. Bookmark it."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does `Asset Age Yrs` work as a column but not as a measure?\n2. Why use `DIVIDE(a, b)` instead of `a / b`?\n3. You need to slice by \"Has had a breakdown: Yes/No\". Column or measure?\n4. Your `PM Compliance %` card shows 79.5 %. You add a slicer on WO Type and pick *Reactive*. What happens, and why?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. A column is evaluated on each row of `Assets`, so `Assets[install_date]` has a value. A measure is evaluated over a *set* of rows (for example, all assets in a zone), so there's no single install date. You'd need an aggregate like `MIN` or `AVERAGE`.\n2. `DIVIDE` returns BLANK (or an alternate value you choose) when the denominator is 0 or blank, instead of an error. Cards and tables stay clean.\n3. A **column** on `Assets` (because you slice by it). For example: `Had Breakdown = IF ( CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" ) > 0, \"Yes\", \"No\" )`. Inside a calculated column, `CALCULATE` turns the current row into a filter. This is called *context transition*, and you'll see it next week.\n4. It goes **blank**. The slicer leaves only Reactive rows, and there are no PMs among them, so `PMs Due` = 0 and `DIVIDE` returns blank. A measure always respects the filters around it, which is usually what you want.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (the `_Measures` table and the nine measures, formatted and checked against the reference values), Session 2 steps 1 and 4 (`Age Band` and `MTTR Hrs` with VAR/RETURN at 1.86 h), and the weekend PM measures to 79.5 % on a basic KPI page with cards, two charts and slicers.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the first three modules of the SQLBI *Introducing DAX* course and the Guy in a Cube measures versus calculated columns video, read the column versus measure table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 step 3 (proving the age formula fails as a measure), the `Corrective Jobs` and `Avg Labour per Reactive Job` extras and fine-tuning the KPI page layout can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] `_Measures` table with 12+ formatted measures, all matching the reference values\n- [ ] Age Band calculated column plus a chart\n- [ ] `MTTR Hrs` written with VAR/RETURN (1.86 h)\n- [ ] KPI page v1 with cards, charts and slicers\n- [ ] SQLBI *Introducing DAX*, modules 1–3"}],"quiz":[{"id":"17-1","n":1,"q":"Why does `Asset Age Yrs` work as a column but not as a measure?","a":"A column is evaluated on each row of `Assets`, so `Assets[install_date]` has a value. A measure is evaluated over a *set* of rows (for example, all assets in a zone), so there's no single install date. You'd need an aggregate like `MIN` or `AVERAGE`."},{"id":"17-2","n":2,"q":"Why use `DIVIDE(a, b)` instead of `a / b`?","a":"`DIVIDE` returns BLANK (or an alternate value you choose) when the denominator is 0 or blank, instead of an error. Cards and tables stay clean."},{"id":"17-3","n":3,"q":"You need to slice by \"Has had a breakdown: Yes/No\". Column or measure?","a":"A **column** on `Assets` (because you slice by it). For example: `Had Breakdown = IF ( CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"Reactive\" ) > 0, \"Yes\", \"No\" )`. Inside a calculated column, `CALCULATE` turns the current row into a filter. This is called *context transition*, and you'll see it next week."},{"id":"17-4","n":4,"q":"Your `PM Compliance %` card shows 79.5 %. You add a slicer on WO Type and pick *Reactive*. What happens, and why?","a":"It goes **blank**. The slicer leaves only Reactive rows, and there are no PMs among them, so `PMs Due` = 0 and `DIVIDE` returns blank. A measure always respects the filters around it, which is usually what you want."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**SQLBI**: the free *Introducing DAX* video course. Register at [sqlbi.com/p/introducing-dax-video-course](https://www.sqlbi.com/p/introducing-dax-video-course/) and watch the first three modules this week.","searches":[],"urls":["https://www.sqlbi.com/p/introducing-dax-video-course/"]},{"i":1,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube measures vs calculated columns\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube measures vs calculated columns"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Reference: [dax.guide](https://dax.guide) has every DAX function with examples. Bookmark it.","searches":[],"urls":["https://dax.guide"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (the `_Measures` table and the nine measures, formatted and checked against the reference values), Session 2 steps 1 and 4 (`Age Band` and `MTTR Hrs` with VAR/RETURN at 1.86 h), and the weekend PM measures to 79.5 % on a basic KPI page with cards, two charts and slicers."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the first three modules of the SQLBI *Introducing DAX* course and the Guy in a Cube measures versus calculated columns video, read the column versus measure table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 step 3 (proving the age formula fails as a measure), the `Corrective Jobs` and `Avg Labour per Reactive Job` extras and fine-tuning the KPI page layout can wait."}}},{"week":18,"phase":3,"title":"DAX 2, CALCULATE and filter context","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-18-calculate-filter-context.md","ticks":["`% of Line` measures with REMOVEFILTERS","Understand why filters on one column replace and others stay","`SUMX` cost measure and `FILTER`-based count (39 assets)","MTBF measure that responds to Calendar and Zone","Comparison matrix with conditional formatting"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 1 Mar – Sun 7 Mar 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is the week DAX \"clicks\", or the week people give up. Take it slowly. Everything in DAX comes back to one idea: **every number is calculated inside a filter context**, and `CALCULATE` is how you change that context.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain filter context and row context in your own words\n- use `CALCULATE` with filters, `ALL`/`REMOVEFILTERS` and `KEEPFILTERS`\n- calculate % of total and \"zone vs line\" comparisons\n- use `FILTER` and iterators (`SUMX`, `AVERAGEX`) where you need row-by-row logic\n- build an **MTBF** measure that respects the selected time period","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-filter-context-by-experiment","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Filter context, by experiment","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Make a **Matrix**: Zones[Zone] on rows, `[Breakdown Hrs]` in values.\n2. Each cell shows a different number from the same measure. Ask yourself: *what filters are active in this cell?* For the Primary Packing row, it's `Zones[Zone] = \"Primary Packing\"`, plus anything from slicers. That set of filters is the **filter context**.\n3. Now add a measure that **ignores** the zone filter:\n\n   ```dax\n   Breakdown Hrs (All Zones) =\n   CALCULATE ( [Breakdown Hrs], REMOVEFILTERS ( Zones ) )\n   ```\n\n   It shows **1,321.4** on every row.\n4. **% of line**:\n\n   ```dax\n   Breakdown % of Line = DIVIDE ( [Breakdown Hrs], [Breakdown Hrs (All Zones)] )\n   ```\n\n   You should see Flaking **21.4 %**, Primary Packing **17.1 %**, Drying 13.4 %…\n5. Add a Criticality slicer and pick **A**. The % still adds up to 100 %, because `REMOVEFILTERS ( Zones )` only removes the zone filter; the criticality filter stays. Change it to `REMOVEFILTERS ( Zones, Assets )` and see what happens. Then change it back.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`CALCULATE ( expression, filter1, filter2, … )` does three things:\n\n1. takes the current filter context (rows, columns, slicers)\n2. **modifies** it with the filter arguments: adding, replacing or removing filters\n3. evaluates the expression in that new context\n\nA filter like `Downtime[category] = \"Breakdown\"` **replaces** any existing filter on that column. `REMOVEFILTERS ( Zones )` clears filters on the Zones table. `KEEPFILTERS ( … )` adds to the existing filter instead of replacing it. You'll need it less often, but know that it exists.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-iterators-filter-and-mtbf","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Iterators, FILTER and MTBF","kind":"hands","md":"### Iterators\n\n`SUM ( column )` adds up a column. **`SUMX ( table, expression )`** walks a table row by row, evaluates the expression on each row, and adds up the results. Use X functions when you need maths *per row* before aggregating.\n\n```dax\nDowntime Cost £ =\nSUMX (\n    FILTER ( Downtime, Downtime[category] = \"Breakdown\" ),\n    Downtime[Duration Hrs] * 2500          -- assume £2,500 per hour of lost production\n)\n```\n\n(That gives £3.3m over two years. Find out what your site's real cost per hour is; it's a powerful number in meetings.)\n\n### Counting with a measure condition\n\n\"How many assets had more than 5 breakdowns?\" You can't put a measure in a slicer, so iterate over assets:\n\n```dax\nAssets > 5 Breakdowns =\nCOUNTROWS (\n    FILTER ( Assets, [Reactive Jobs] > 5 )\n)\n```\n\nThat gives **39** (and 15 with more than 10). `FILTER` walks every asset, and for each one `[Reactive Jobs]` is evaluated **for that asset only**. That's *context transition*: a measure called inside a row context automatically filters to the current row.\n\n### MTBF that respects the date filter\n\n```dax\nPeriod Hours =\nVAR FirstDay = MIN ( Calendar[Date] )\nVAR LastDay  = MIN ( MAX ( Calendar[Date] ), DATE ( 2026, 6, 30 ) )   -- data ends 30 Jun 2026\nRETURN\n    ( LastDay - FirstDay + 1 ) * 24\n\nAsset Count = COUNTROWS ( Assets )\n\nMTBF Hrs (per asset) =\nDIVIDE ( [Period Hours] * [Asset Count], [Reactive Jobs] )\n```\n\nWith no filters, that's about **4,981 h** per asset. Slice by zone and Primary Packing drops to about **2,580 h**. Pick H1 2026 on the Calendar and the whole line is about 5,375 h.\n\n> **Why \"per asset\"?** \"Line MTBF\" can mean several things. Here it's *operating hours × number of assets ÷ failures*, which is the average asset's MTBF. Write the definition on the report's About page. Unlabelled reliability KPIs cause arguments.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-task-comparison-measures","title":"Weekend task (2 h): Comparison measures","kind":"hands","md":"Build these and put them in a matrix by Zone:\n\n1. `Reactive Jobs (Line)`: ignores the zone filter.\n2. `Reactive Jobs vs Line Avg`: the zone's jobs ÷ (line jobs ÷ 7).\n3. `PM Compliance % (Line)` and `PM Compliance Gap = [PM Compliance %] - [PM Compliance % (Line)]`. Use conditional formatting (red/green) on the gap.\n4. `Top Asset by Downtime`: the name of the worst asset in the current context:\n\n   ```dax\n   Top Asset by Downtime =\n   VAR t = TOPN ( 1, VALUES ( Assets[Asset] ), [Breakdown Hrs], DESC )\n   RETURN CONCATENATEX ( t, Assets[Asset], \", \" )\n   ```\n\n5. `Repeat Asset Count`: assets with 2 or more breakdowns in the selected period (`FILTER` + `COUNTROWS`).\n\nPut the matrix on a page with a Calendar slicer and test it on several periods.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **SQLBI**: *Introducing DAX*, the modules on CALCULATE and filter context. Watch them twice; it's normal.\n- **SQLBI article**: search *\"SQLBI filter context in DAX\"*.\n- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube CALCULATE explained\"*.\n- Book (when you're ready): *The Definitive Guide to DAX* by Russo & Ferrari. It's the bible, but heavy; dip in, don't read it cover to cover."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. In your own words, what is filter context?\n2. What's the difference between `CALCULATE ( [x], Assets[criticality] = \"A\" )` and `CALCULATE ( [x], KEEPFILTERS ( Assets[criticality] = \"A\" ) )` when the user has picked criticality **B** in a slicer?\n3. Why does `Assets > 5 Breakdowns` need `FILTER ( Assets, … )` and not just `[Reactive Jobs] > 5`?\n4. When should you use `SUMX` instead of `SUM`?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The set of filters active when a measure is calculated: from rows and columns of the visual, slicers, other visuals' cross-filtering, and page and report filters. The same measure gives different answers in different cells because each cell has a different filter context.\n2. The plain version **replaces** the slicer's B with A, so it shows A's numbers regardless. The KEEPFILTERS version **intersects** B and A, which is empty, so the result is blank.\n3. `[Reactive Jobs] > 5` on its own is a single TRUE/FALSE for the whole current context. You need to test each asset separately, so you iterate over the Assets table, and context transition evaluates the measure per asset.\n4. When the calculation needs a per-row step first: price × quantity, duration × cost rate, or anything where you'd otherwise need a calculated column just to add it up.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (`REMOVEFILTERS` and `Breakdown % of Line`), the three Session 2 measures (`Downtime Cost £`, `Assets > 5 Breakdowns` at 39, the MTBF set that responds to Calendar and Zone), and weekend items 1 to 3 in a matrix with red and green formatting on the gap.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the SQLBI *Introducing DAX* modules on CALCULATE and filter context (twice is normal) and the Guy in a Cube CALCULATE video, read the SQLBI filter context article and the \"Why it works\" list, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, find out your site's real cost per hour of lost production.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend items 4 and 5 (`Top Asset by Downtime` and `Repeat Asset Count`) and *The Definitive Guide to DAX* can be left for later."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] `% of Line` measures with REMOVEFILTERS\n- [ ] Understand why filters on one column replace and others stay\n- [ ] `SUMX` cost measure and `FILTER`-based count (39 assets)\n- [ ] MTBF measure that responds to Calendar and Zone\n- [ ] Comparison matrix with conditional formatting"}],"quiz":[{"id":"18-1","n":1,"q":"In your own words, what is filter context?","a":"The set of filters active when a measure is calculated: from rows and columns of the visual, slicers, other visuals' cross-filtering, and page and report filters. The same measure gives different answers in different cells because each cell has a different filter context."},{"id":"18-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between `CALCULATE ( [x], Assets[criticality] = \"A\" )` and `CALCULATE ( [x], KEEPFILTERS ( Assets[criticality] = \"A\" ) )` when the user has picked criticality **B** in a slicer?","a":"The plain version **replaces** the slicer's B with A, so it shows A's numbers regardless. The KEEPFILTERS version **intersects** B and A, which is empty, so the result is blank."},{"id":"18-3","n":3,"q":"Why does `Assets > 5 Breakdowns` need `FILTER ( Assets, … )` and not just `[Reactive Jobs] > 5`?","a":"`[Reactive Jobs] > 5` on its own is a single TRUE/FALSE for the whole current context. You need to test each asset separately, so you iterate over the Assets table, and context transition evaluates the measure per asset."},{"id":"18-4","n":4,"q":"When should you use `SUMX` instead of `SUM`?","a":"When the calculation needs a per-row step first: price × quantity, duration × cost rate, or anything where you'd otherwise need a calculated column just to add it up."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**SQLBI**: *Introducing DAX*, the modules on CALCULATE and filter context. Watch them twice; it's normal.","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**SQLBI article**: search *\"SQLBI filter context in DAX\"*.","searches":["SQLBI filter context in DAX"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube CALCULATE explained\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube CALCULATE explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":3,"md":"Book (when you're ready): *The Definitive Guide to DAX* by Russo & Ferrari. It's the bible, but heavy; dip in, don't read it cover to cover.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (`REMOVEFILTERS` and `Breakdown % of Line`), the three Session 2 measures (`Downtime Cost £`, `Assets > 5 Breakdowns` at 39, the MTBF set that responds to Calendar and Zone), and weekend items 1 to 3 in a matrix with red and green formatting on the gap."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the SQLBI *Introducing DAX* modules on CALCULATE and filter context (twice is normal) and the Guy in a Cube CALCULATE video, read the SQLBI filter context article and the \"Why it works\" list, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, find out your site's real cost per hour of lost production."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend items 4 and 5 (`Top Asset by Downtime` and `Repeat Asset Count`) and *The Definitive Guide to DAX* can be left for later."}}},{"week":19,"phase":3,"title":"DAX 3, time intelligence, variables and USERELATIONSHIP","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-19-time-intelligence.md","ticks":["YTD, PY, PY YTD and YoY measures, with June 2026 matching the reference","Rolling 13 weeks with the end-of-data cap","PM compliance by due date via USERELATIONSHIP (434 / 81.3 % for June 2026)","Trend page with target line and conditional colours"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 8 Mar – Sun 14 Mar 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nEvery maintenance review asks questions about time: *\"How are we doing year to date?\"*, *\"Better or worse than last year?\"*, *\"What's the rolling 13-week trend?\"* This week you build those measures, and you fix PM compliance so it counts PMs by the date they were **due**.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use `TOTALYTD`, `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR`, `DATEADD` and `DATESINPERIOD`\n- build rolling 13-week measures\n- activate the inactive due-date relationship with `USERELATIONSHIP`\n- stop time measures running past the end of the data\n\n**Needs:** the `Calendar` table marked as a date table, with a continuous date range (Week 16).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-ytd-and-last-year","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): YTD and last year","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```dax\nBreakdown Hrs YTD = TOTALYTD ( [Breakdown Hrs], Calendar[Date] )\n\nBreakdown Hrs PY = CALCULATE ( [Breakdown Hrs], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR ( Calendar[Date] ) )\n\nBreakdown Hrs PY YTD = CALCULATE ( [Breakdown Hrs YTD], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR ( Calendar[Date] ) )\n\nBreakdown Hrs YoY % = DIVIDE ( [Breakdown Hrs YTD] - [Breakdown Hrs PY YTD], [Breakdown Hrs PY YTD] )\n```\n\nBuild a **matrix** with `Calendar[Year]` and `Calendar[Month Name]` on the rows, and those four measures as values.\n\n**Check:** at **June 2026**, YTD = **266.9 h**, PY YTD = **310.4 h**, YoY = **−14.0 %**. Breakdown hours are down on last year. For single months, May 2026 was 56.7 h vs 51.4 h in May 2025 (worse), and June 2026 was 44.6 h vs 52.7 h (better).\n\n### Why it works\n\nTime intelligence functions **rewrite the date filter**. In the June 2026 cell, the filter is \"1–30 June 2026\". `TOTALYTD` replaces it with \"1 Jan – 30 Jun 2026\". `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` shifts it to \"1–30 June 2025\". That's why you need a proper Calendar table: the functions need every date to exist so they can shift and extend ranges.\n\n(If your year doesn't start in January, for example a site financial year starting in April, use `TOTALYTD ( [x], Calendar[Date], \"31/3\" )`.)","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-rolling-13-weeks-and-the-end-o","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Rolling 13 weeks and the end-of-data problem","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```dax\nBreakdown Hrs R13W =\nCALCULATE (\n    [Breakdown Hrs],\n    DATESINPERIOD ( Calendar[Date], MAX ( Calendar[Date] ), -91, DAY )\n)\n```\n\nPut `Calendar[Week Commencing]` on the X-axis of a line chart with `[Breakdown Hrs]` and `[Breakdown Hrs R13W]`. The weekly line is spiky, and the rolling line shows the real trend. For the week commencing **22/06/2026**, R13W = **139.4 h**.\n\n**The problem:** put `[Breakdown Hrs R13W]` on a card with no filters. `MAX ( Calendar[Date] )` is **31/12/2026**, because the Calendar runs to year end, but there's no data after June, so the card shows only part of a window (or blank). Fix it by capping at the last date with data:\n\n```dax\nLast Data Date = CALCULATE ( MAX ( Downtime[Start Date] ), REMOVEFILTERS ( Calendar ) )\n\nBreakdown Hrs R13W =\nVAR EndDate = MIN ( MAX ( Calendar[Date] ), [Last Data Date] )\nRETURN\n    CALCULATE (\n        [Breakdown Hrs],\n        DATESINPERIOD ( Calendar[Date], EndDate, -91, DAY )\n    )\n```\n\nAlso hide future periods on charts: add a calculated column `Calendar[Has Data] = Calendar[Date] <= DATE(2026,6,30)` and use it as a visual or page filter. (A better version looks up the max date from the data, but a fixed date is fine for practice.)\n\n### Why `VAR` matters here\n\n`EndDate` is calculated **once, in the original filter context**, before `CALCULATE` changes anything. That's what makes variables more than tidiness: they capture a value *before* the context shifts.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-task-pm-compliance-by-due-date","title":"Weekend task (2 h): PM compliance by due date","kind":"hands","md":"### USERELATIONSHIP\n\nRight now, the Calendar filters `WorkOrders` by **raised** date. For PM compliance, the question is \"of the PMs **due** in June, how many were done on time?\"\n\n```dax\nPMs Due (by Due Date) =\nCALCULATE ( [PMs Due], USERELATIONSHIP ( Calendar[Date], WorkOrders[due_date] ) )\n\nPMs On Time (by Due Date) =\nCALCULATE ( [PMs On Time], USERELATIONSHIP ( Calendar[Date], WorkOrders[due_date] ) )\n\nPM Compliance % (Due) = DIVIDE ( [PMs On Time (by Due Date)], [PMs Due (by Due Date)] )\n```\n\n**Check, June 2026:**\n\n| Measure | By raised date | By due date |\n|---|---|---|\n| PMs | 336 | **434** |\n| Compliance | 80.1 % | **81.3 %** |\n\nThe due-date version matches your SQL from Week 12 (`2026-06: 434 due, 81.3 %`). Three tools, one answer.\n\n### Then build:\n\n1. `PM Compliance % (Due) R13W`: rolling 13 weeks by due date. (Combine `USERELATIONSHIP` and `DATESINPERIOD` in one `CALCULATE`.)\n2. `Reactive Jobs R13W` and `Reactive Jobs PY`.\n3. A **trend page**: weekly line chart of R13W breakdown hours, monthly column chart of compliance (due) with a constant line at the 90 % target (Analytics pane → Constant line), and a YTD vs PY card pair.\n4. Add a measure `Target Compliance = 0.9` and a measure that returns a red/green colour for conditional formatting:\n\n   ```dax\n   Compliance Colour = IF ( [PM Compliance % (Due)] >= [Target Compliance], \"#2E7D32\", \"#C62828\" )\n   ```\n\n   Use it on the column chart: Format → Columns → Colour → *fx* → Field value → `Compliance Colour`.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI time intelligence\"* and *\"SQLBI USERELATIONSHIP\"*.\n- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube rolling average DAX\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *Use DAX time intelligence functions in Power BI Desktop models*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why do time intelligence functions need a marked date table with no gaps?\n2. What's the difference between `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` and `DATEADD ( Calendar[Date], -1, YEAR )`?\n3. Why might \"PM compliance by raised date\" and \"by due date\" disagree?\n4. Your R13W chart drops to almost zero in the last three points. What's going on?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. They work by shifting or extending date ranges in the Calendar. If dates are missing, the shifted range is incomplete and the results are wrong (often silently). Marking it as a date table also tells Power BI which column is the date key.\n2. For a whole year they're the same. `DATEADD` is more flexible: you can use -1 MONTH, -7 DAY, -1 QUARTER and so on. `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` is shorthand for `DATEADD ( …, -1, YEAR )`.\n3. A PM is raised up to a week before it's due, so PMs raised late in May can be due in June. The two months contain different sets of PMs. Compliance is about meeting the **due** date, so the due-date version is the correct KPI.\n4. The chart's axis runs past the end of the data (July onwards). Each window there contains fewer and fewer real days. Cap the end date, or filter the axis to dates with data.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** The four Session 1 measures in a matrix (check June 2026: 266.9 h, 310.4 h, −14.0 %), Session 2's R13W with the `Last Data Date` cap, and the weekend `USERELATIONSHIP` measures for compliance by due date (434 / 81.3 %), then a simple trend page with the 90 % constant line and `Compliance Colour`.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the SQLBI time intelligence and USERELATIONSHIP videos and the Guy in a Cube rolling average video, read \"Why it works\" and \"Why `VAR` matters here\", and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (compliance R13W by due date), the `Reactive Jobs R13W` and `Reactive Jobs PY` measures in item 2, the `Has Data` column and the PL-300 time intelligence module can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] YTD, PY, PY YTD and YoY measures, with June 2026 matching the reference\n- [ ] Rolling 13 weeks with the end-of-data cap\n- [ ] PM compliance by due date via USERELATIONSHIP (434 / 81.3 % for June 2026)\n- [ ] Trend page with target line and conditional colours"}],"quiz":[{"id":"19-1","n":1,"q":"Why do time intelligence functions need a marked date table with no gaps?","a":"They work by shifting or extending date ranges in the Calendar. If dates are missing, the shifted range is incomplete and the results are wrong (often silently). Marking it as a date table also tells Power BI which column is the date key."},{"id":"19-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` and `DATEADD ( Calendar[Date], -1, YEAR )`?","a":"For a whole year they're the same. `DATEADD` is more flexible: you can use -1 MONTH, -7 DAY, -1 QUARTER and so on. `SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR` is shorthand for `DATEADD ( …, -1, YEAR )`."},{"id":"19-3","n":3,"q":"Why might \"PM compliance by raised date\" and \"by due date\" disagree?","a":"A PM is raised up to a week before it's due, so PMs raised late in May can be due in June. The two months contain different sets of PMs. Compliance is about meeting the **due** date, so the due-date version is the correct KPI."},{"id":"19-4","n":4,"q":"Your R13W chart drops to almost zero in the last three points. What's going on?","a":"The chart's axis runs past the end of the data (July onwards). Each window there contains fewer and fewer real days. Cap the end date, or filter the axis to dates with data."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**SQLBI**: *\"SQLBI time intelligence\"* and *\"SQLBI USERELATIONSHIP\"*.","searches":["SQLBI time intelligence","SQLBI USERELATIONSHIP"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube rolling average DAX\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube rolling average DAX"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *Use DAX time intelligence functions in Power BI Desktop models*.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"The four Session 1 measures in a matrix (check June 2026: 266.9 h, 310.4 h, −14.0 %), Session 2's R13W with the `Last Data Date` cap, and the weekend `USERELATIONSHIP` measures for compliance by due date (434 / 81.3 %), then a simple trend page with the 90 % constant line and `Compliance Colour`."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the SQLBI time intelligence and USERELATIONSHIP videos and the Guy in a Cube rolling average video, read \"Why it works\" and \"Why `VAR` matters here\", and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (compliance R13W by due date), the `Reactive Jobs R13W` and `Reactive Jobs PY` measures in item 2, the `Has Data` column and the PL-300 time intelligence module can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":20,"phase":3,"title":"Visuals, KPI cards, Pareto and matrix formatting","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-20-visuals-kpis-pareto.md","ticks":["KPI cards with targets","Sorted zone bar chart with a dynamic finding-style title","DAX Pareto matching the Excel and SQL Q2 results","Heat-map matrix and icon formatting","Explored a decomposition tree down to failure code"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 15 Mar – Sun 21 Mar 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nYou have the numbers. Now make them **readable in five seconds**. A maintenance review has about 30 seconds per page before people start talking. This week is about choosing the right visual for each question and formatting it so the answer jumps out.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- pick the right chart for the question (the table below)\n- build KPI cards with targets and trends\n- build a DAX-driven Pareto chart\n- use conditional formatting (backgrounds, icons, data bars) in matrices\n- use the decomposition tree to explore causes","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-choosing-and-formatting-visual","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Choosing and formatting visuals","kind":"hands","md":"### Which visual?\n\n| Question | Visual |\n|---|---|\n| What's the number right now? | Card, with a target and a trend |\n| How do zones compare? | Bar chart (horizontal, sorted) |\n| How is it changing over time? | Line chart (continuous) or column chart (monthly totals) |\n| What causes most of the loss? | Pareto (sorted columns + cumulative line) |\n| Detail by two dimensions | Matrix with conditional formatting |\n| Part of a whole with 2–4 parts | 100 % stacked bar (avoid pies with more than 3 slices) |\n| Why is this number high? | Decomposition tree |\n\n### Do it\n\n1. **KPI cards**: use the new **Card** visual (Insert → Card). Put `PM Compliance % (Due)` in it, then add `Target Compliance` as a reference label. Do the same for `Breakdown Hrs YTD` vs `Breakdown Hrs PY YTD`.\n2. **Zone bar chart**: `PM Compliance % (Due)` by Zone, sorted ascending so the worst is at the top. Use the `Compliance Colour` measure from last week for the bar colours. Add a constant line at 90 %.\n3. **Remove clutter on every visual**: no gridlines unless you need them, axis titles off where the title explains it, and data labels on when there are fewer than ~12 bars.\n4. **Titles state findings.** Use *\"Packing is 15 points below line compliance\"*, not *\"PM Compliance by Zone\"*. You can make dynamic titles with a measure:\n\n   ```dax\n   Title Compliance =\n   VAR worst = TOPN ( 1, VALUES ( Zones[Zone] ), [PM Compliance % (Due)], ASC )\n   RETURN \"Lowest PM compliance: \" & CONCATENATEX ( worst, Zones[Zone] ) &\n          \" (\" & FORMAT ( MINX ( worst, [PM Compliance % (Due)] ), \"0%\" ) & \")\"\n   ```\n\n   Visual → Format → Title → *fx* → Field value → `Title Compliance`.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-a-dax-pareto","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): A DAX Pareto","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```dax\nBreakdown Hrs Cumulative % =\nVAR CurrentHrs = [Breakdown Hrs]\nVAR AllAssets =\n    ADDCOLUMNS ( ALLSELECTED ( Assets[Asset] ), \"@hrs\", [Breakdown Hrs] )\nVAR Cumulative =\n    SUMX ( FILTER ( AllAssets, [@hrs] >= CurrentHrs ), [@hrs] )\nVAR Total = SUMX ( AllAssets, [@hrs] )\nRETURN\n    DIVIDE ( Cumulative, Total )\n```\n\n1. Insert a **Line and clustered column chart**. X-axis = `Assets[Asset]`, column = `[Breakdown Hrs]`, line = `[Breakdown Hrs Cumulative %]`.\n2. Sort by Breakdown Hrs, descending.\n3. Use the Filters pane on the visual: `Assets[Asset]` → *Top N* → 15 by `Breakdown Hrs`.\n4. Set the secondary axis to 0–100 %.\n5. Slice to **Q2 2026** with a Calendar slicer and check it against your Week 6 Excel Pareto: LD-FLK-010 first (9.6 h ≈ 575 min), LD-FLK-018 second.\n\n### Why it works\n\nFor each asset on the axis, the measure builds a little table of every asset **visible in the current selection** (`ALLSELECTED`) with its hours. It then adds up the hours of all assets with **at least as many** hours as this one. That's the running total down the sorted list. `ALLSELECTED` (rather than `ALL`) means slicers still apply, so the Pareto respects the chosen period and zone.\n\n(Ties share a cumulative value. That's fine for a Pareto.)","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-task-matrix-and-decomposition-tr","title":"Weekend task (2 h): Matrix and decomposition tree","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Heat-map matrix**: rows = Zone, columns = `Calendar[Month Name]` (filtered to 2026), values = `PM Compliance % (Due)`. Format → Cell elements → **Background colour** → *Gradient*, red (low) to green (high). It shows instantly which zone and month went wrong.\n2. **Icons**: add `Breakdown Hrs YoY %` to a table by zone with icon rules: ▲ red if > 0 (more breakdowns), ▼ green if < 0.\n3. **Data bars** on `Reactive Jobs` in a top-20 assets table.\n4. **Decomposition tree** (Insert → Decomposition tree): Analyze = `Breakdown Hrs`, Explain by = Zone, Asset Type, Failure Code, Criticality, Asset. Click through: **Flaking → Flaking roll → Mechanical - roll wear / bearing**. Use the \"high value\" AI split (the lightbulb) to let Power BI pick the next split.\n5. **Small multiples**: a line chart of monthly `Reactive Jobs` with Zone in *Small multiples*. Seven mini charts on one shared scale.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube Power BI conditional formatting\"*.\n- **How to Power BI (Basim)**: *\"How to Power BI Pareto chart DAX\"*.\n- **Storytelling with Data**: revisit the declutter idea from Week 2. Book (strongly recommended, short): *Storytelling with Data* by Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why sort the compliance bar chart *ascending*?\n2. Why use `ALLSELECTED` and not `ALL` in the Pareto measure?\n3. When is a pie chart acceptable?\n4. Your heat-map has a white cell for Forming in March. What does that mean, and how should it look?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The question is \"where's the problem?\", so the worst zone should be the first thing the eye lands on (at the top).\n2. `ALL` ignores slicers, so the cumulative % would be calculated over all time and all zones while the bars show only the selection. `ALLSELECTED` keeps the user's slicer choices but ignores the asset on the current axis position.\n3. Rarely: 2–3 parts of one whole, where the exact values don't matter, like \"Planned vs Reactive\". A bar is almost always easier to read.\n4. The measure returned blank, so there were no PMs due (or a filter excludes them). Show it as a grey \"–\" or make the formatting treat blanks as neutral, so a blank isn't mistaken for \"good\" or \"bad\".\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (KPI cards with targets and the sorted zone bar with the `Title Compliance` dynamic title), the Session 2 DAX Pareto checked against Q2 2026, and weekend items 1, 2 and 4 (heat-map matrix, YoY icons, decomposition tree down to failure code).\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Guy in a Cube conditional formatting video and the How to Power BI Pareto video, read the \"Which visual?\" table and the Pareto \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 3 (data bars on a top-20 table), item 5 (small multiples) and the *Storytelling with Data* book can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] KPI cards with targets\n- [ ] Sorted zone bar chart with a dynamic finding-style title\n- [ ] DAX Pareto matching the Excel and SQL Q2 results\n- [ ] Heat-map matrix and icon formatting\n- [ ] Explored a decomposition tree down to failure code"}],"quiz":[{"id":"20-1","n":1,"q":"Why sort the compliance bar chart *ascending*?","a":"The question is \"where's the problem?\", so the worst zone should be the first thing the eye lands on (at the top)."},{"id":"20-2","n":2,"q":"Why use `ALLSELECTED` and not `ALL` in the Pareto measure?","a":"`ALL` ignores slicers, so the cumulative % would be calculated over all time and all zones while the bars show only the selection. `ALLSELECTED` keeps the user's slicer choices but ignores the asset on the current axis position."},{"id":"20-3","n":3,"q":"When is a pie chart acceptable?","a":"Rarely: 2–3 parts of one whole, where the exact values don't matter, like \"Planned vs Reactive\". A bar is almost always easier to read."},{"id":"20-4","n":4,"q":"Your heat-map has a white cell for Forming in March. What does that mean, and how should it look?","a":"The measure returned blank, so there were no PMs due (or a filter excludes them). Show it as a grey \"–\" or make the formatting treat blanks as neutral, so a blank isn't mistaken for \"good\" or \"bad\"."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube Power BI conditional formatting\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube Power BI conditional formatting"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**How to Power BI (Basim)**: *\"How to Power BI Pareto chart DAX\"*.","searches":["How to Power BI Pareto chart DAX"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Storytelling with Data**: revisit the declutter idea from Week 2. Book (strongly recommended, short): *Storytelling with Data* by Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 4 (KPI cards with targets and the sorted zone bar with the `Title Compliance` dynamic title), the Session 2 DAX Pareto checked against Q2 2026, and weekend items 1, 2 and 4 (heat-map matrix, YoY icons, decomposition tree down to failure code)."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Guy in a Cube conditional formatting video and the How to Power BI Pareto video, read the \"Which visual?\" table and the Pareto \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 3 (data bars on a top-20 table), item 5 (small multiples) and the *Storytelling with Data* book can wait."}}},{"week":21,"phase":3,"title":"Interactivity, drill-through, tooltips and bookmarks","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-21-drillthrough-tooltips-bookmarks.md","ticks":["Asset Detail drill-through page works from any asset visual","Report-page tooltip on the Pareto","Interactions edited and slicers synced","Chart/table bookmark toggle and a Reset button","Field parameter metric switcher"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 22 Mar – Sun 28 Mar 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nA good dashboard answers the first question on the page and lets people **click through to the next one** without you in the room. *\"Which asset is worst?\"* → click → *\"What's been happening on it?\"* That's drill-through. This week makes the report self-service.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build a **drill-through** page from any asset to its work order history\n- create **report page tooltips** (a mini page that appears on hover)\n- use **bookmarks** and buttons to toggle views and reset filters\n- control visual interactions and **sync slicers** across pages\n- use **field parameters** to let users switch the measure on a chart","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-drill-through-to-asset-history","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Drill-through to asset history","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Add a new page called **Asset Detail**.\n2. In the Visualizations pane → *Drill through* section, drag `Assets[asset_id]` into **Add drill-through fields here**. A back-arrow button appears automatically.\n3. Build the page:\n   - **Header cards**: Asset name (`SELECTEDVALUE ( Assets[Asset] )` in a measure), zone, criticality, type, install date.\n   - **KPI cards**: Reactive Jobs, Breakdown Hrs, MTTR Hrs, MTBF Hrs (per asset), PM Compliance % (Due).\n   - **Table**: work order history (`wo_id`, Raised Date, WO Type, description, failure code, labour hrs, status), sorted newest first.\n   - **Column chart**: breakdowns by month.\n   - **Bar chart**: breakdowns by failure code.\n4. Go back to your Pareto page, right-click **LD-PPK-005** → **Drill through** → Asset Detail. You should see 37 breakdowns, mostly *Operational - jam*, *Mechanical - jaws* and *Electrical - sensor*.\n5. Add a dynamic title measure:\n\n   ```dax\n   Asset Title = \"Asset history: \" & SELECTEDVALUE ( Assets[asset_id] ) & \" – \" & SELECTEDVALUE ( Assets[Asset] )\n   ```\n\n### Why it works\n\nDrill-through carries the clicked value (and by default all other filters, which you can switch off with *Keep all filters*) to the target page as a page filter. One detail page serves all 292 assets.\n\n`SELECTEDVALUE ( column, \"alternate\" )` returns the value when exactly one is selected, otherwise the alternate. It's the standard way to show \"what am I looking at?\".","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-tooltips-and-interactions","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Tooltips and interactions","kind":"hands","md":"### Report page tooltip\n\n1. New page **TT Asset**. Format → Page information → **Allow use as tooltip** = On. Canvas settings → Type = **Tooltip** (a small page).\n2. Put three cards on it (Reactive Jobs, Breakdown Hrs, MTTR Hrs) and a tiny column chart of breakdowns by month.\n3. Hide the page (right-click the tab → Hide).\n4. On the Pareto chart → Format → General → Tooltips → Type = **Report page** → Page = *TT Asset*.\n5. Hover over a bar. The mini page appears, filtered to that asset.\n\n### Visual interactions\n\nSelect a visual → Format ribbon → **Edit interactions**. Each other visual shows *Filter*, *Highlight* or *None* icons. Set KPI cards so clicking a bar *filters* them, and set the Pareto so clicking the zone chart *filters* (not highlights) it.\n\n### Sync slicers\n\nView → **Sync slicers**. Make the Zone and Date slicers sync across the Compliance, Trend and Pareto pages, so a choice on one page sticks when you move to the next.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-task-bookmarks-and-field-paramet","title":"Weekend task (2 h): Bookmarks and field parameters","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Chart/table toggle**:\n   - Put a chart and a table showing the same data in exactly the same position.\n   - View → Bookmarks pane and View → Selection pane.\n   - Hide the table and add a bookmark called *Show chart*. Then hide the chart, show the table, and add a bookmark called *Show table*.\n   - In each bookmark's options, untick **Data** so the toggle doesn't reset filters.\n   - Insert two buttons (\"Chart\", \"Table\") → Action → Bookmark.\n2. **Reset filters button**: clear all slicers, add a bookmark *Reset* (with Data ticked), and add a button \"Reset filters\" that uses it.\n3. **Field parameter**: Modeling → New parameter → **Fields** → add `Breakdown Hrs`, `Reactive Jobs`, `Minor Stop Hrs` and `Labour Hrs`. A slicer appears. Use the parameter as the column value in your zone bar chart. Now users pick the metric.\n4. **Navigation**: add a page navigator (Insert → Buttons → Navigator → Page navigator) to the top of every visible page.\n5. Test the whole report as a stranger would: start on page 1, click everything, and get lost on purpose. Fix anything confusing.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube drillthrough\"* and *\"Guy in a Cube report page tooltips\"*.\n- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube bookmarks\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Use field parameters to change dimensions Power BI\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between drill-*through* and drill-*down*?\n2. Why untick *Data* on a show/hide bookmark?\n3. Why hide tooltip pages?\n4. A manager says \"I clicked packing and the trend chart didn't change.\" What would you check?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. **Drill-down** moves down a hierarchy *within the same visual* (Year → Month → Day). **Drill-through** jumps to *another page* that's filtered to what you clicked.\n2. With Data ticked, the bookmark saves and restores filter and slicer states, so toggling the chart would reset whatever the user had selected. Untick it, so it only changes what's visible.\n3. They're fragments that make no sense on their own. Hiding them keeps the page tabs clean for users.\n4. Edit interactions (it may be set to *None*), whether the slicer is synced or applied to that page, and whether the chart's measure uses `REMOVEFILTERS` or `ALL` on Zones (like the \"line\" comparison measures).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (the Asset Detail drill-through, tested on LD-PPK-005), the Session 2 report page tooltip, edited interactions and synced slicers, and weekend items 1 to 3 (chart/table bookmark toggle, Reset button and the field parameter).\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the three Guy in a Cube videos on drillthrough, report page tooltips and bookmarks, read the \"Why it works\" note on `SELECTEDVALUE`, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 4 (the page navigator, which the Week 22 header adds anyway) and extra detail visuals on Asset Detail beyond the cards and history table can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Asset Detail drill-through page works from any asset visual\n- [ ] Report-page tooltip on the Pareto\n- [ ] Interactions edited and slicers synced\n- [ ] Chart/table bookmark toggle and a Reset button\n- [ ] Field parameter metric switcher"}],"quiz":[{"id":"21-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between drill-*through* and drill-*down*?","a":"**Drill-down** moves down a hierarchy *within the same visual* (Year → Month → Day). **Drill-through** jumps to *another page* that's filtered to what you clicked."},{"id":"21-2","n":2,"q":"Why untick *Data* on a show/hide bookmark?","a":"With Data ticked, the bookmark saves and restores filter and slicer states, so toggling the chart would reset whatever the user had selected. Untick it, so it only changes what's visible."},{"id":"21-3","n":3,"q":"Why hide tooltip pages?","a":"They're fragments that make no sense on their own. Hiding them keeps the page tabs clean for users."},{"id":"21-4","n":4,"q":"A manager says \"I clicked packing and the trend chart didn't change.\" What would you check?","a":"Edit interactions (it may be set to *None*), whether the slicer is synced or applied to that page, and whether the chart's measure uses `REMOVEFILTERS` or `ALL` on Zones (like the \"line\" comparison measures)."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube drillthrough\"* and *\"Guy in a Cube report page tooltips\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube drillthrough","Guy in a Cube report page tooltips"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube bookmarks\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube bookmarks"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Use field parameters to change dimensions Power BI\"*.","searches":["Use field parameters to change dimensions Power BI"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (the Asset Detail drill-through, tested on LD-PPK-005), the Session 2 report page tooltip, edited interactions and synced slicers, and weekend items 1 to 3 (chart/table bookmark toggle, Reset button and the field parameter)."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the three Guy in a Cube videos on drillthrough, report page tooltips and bookmarks, read the \"Why it works\" note on `SELECTEDVALUE`, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 4 (the page navigator, which the Week 22 header adds anyway) and extra detail visuals on Asset Detail beyond the cards and history table can wait."}}},{"week":22,"phase":3,"title":"Report design, and building the Model Line dashboard","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-22-report-design-build.md","ticks":["Theme JSON applied, colour rules written down","Layout grid and header consistent across pages","Pages 1–5 built","About page with KPI definitions"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 29 Mar – Sun 4 Apr 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nYou've learned the pieces. This week you design the real thing: the **Line D Maintenance Dashboard**, five pages, one question per page, in a consistent layout. Design is what decides whether people use it or politely ignore it.\n\n**By Sunday you'll have:**\n\n- a theme (colours, fonts) applied across the report\n- a layout grid used on every page\n- all five dashboard pages built, as below","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-design-system","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Design system","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **A theme file.** Save this as `lined_theme.json`, then go to View → Themes → Browse for themes:\n\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"name\": \"Line D\",\n     \"dataColors\": [\"#1F4E79\", \"#9E9E9E\", \"#C62828\", \"#2E7D32\", \"#F9A825\", \"#5C6BC0\", \"#8D6E63\", \"#26A69A\"],\n     \"background\": \"#FFFFFF\",\n     \"foreground\": \"#212121\",\n     \"tableAccent\": \"#1F4E79\",\n     \"good\": \"#2E7D32\",\n     \"bad\": \"#C62828\",\n     \"neutral\": \"#F9A825\",\n     \"textClasses\": {\n       \"title\":  { \"fontFace\": \"Segoe UI Semibold\", \"fontSize\": 14, \"color\": \"#212121\" },\n       \"label\":  { \"fontFace\": \"Segoe UI\", \"fontSize\": 10, \"color\": \"#424242\" },\n       \"callout\": { \"fontFace\": \"Segoe UI Semibold\", \"fontSize\": 28, \"color\": \"#1F4E79\" }\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **The colour rules** (write them on your About page):\n   - Navy = the thing you're looking at. Grey = context. **Red = bad, and only bad.** Green = on target.\n   - Never use red and green just for decoration. If a colour doesn't mean something, use grey.\n3. **The layout grid** (canvas 1280 × 720):\n   - Header band: 0–60 px (title, last refresh date, navigator)\n   - KPI row: 70–170 px (4–5 cards, equal widths)\n   - Main visual: left two-thirds. Supporting visual: right third.\n   - Slicers: one consistent place, either a left rail or the top right. Pick one and never move it.\n   - View → *Snap to grid* and *Gridlines* on while you build.\n4. Build the **header** once on a page, then copy it to every page so they all match.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nUsers learn a report's \"grammar\" on the first page. If the date slicer is top-right on page 1, they'll look for it there on page 4. Consistency is cheap, and it's the biggest single factor in whether people trust a report.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-and-weekend-build-the-five-pag","title":"Session 2 and weekend (3.5 h): Build the five pages","kind":"hands","md":"Each page answers **one question**. Write the question as a subtitle under the page title.\n\n### Page 1: PM Compliance\n*Are we doing our PMs on time, and where aren't we?*\n- KPI cards: PM Compliance % (Due) vs 90 % target, PMs Due, PMs Late, PMs Missed\n- Bar chart: compliance by zone, sorted worst first, with the target line\n- Heat-map matrix: zone × month\n- Table: the top 10 **overdue** PMs as at the last data date (Status = Open, due before the date), oldest first\n\n### Page 2: Planned vs Reactive\n*How much of our work is planned?*\n- KPI cards: Planned % (count), Reactive Labour %, Reactive Jobs R13W\n- 100 % stacked column: labour hours by WO Type by month\n- Bar chart: reactive labour % by zone\n- Line: Reactive Jobs vs PY\n\n### Page 3: Reliability (MTBF & MTTR)\n*Are assets getting more or less reliable?*\n- KPI cards: MTBF Hrs (per asset), MTTR Hrs, Breakdown Hrs YTD vs PY\n- Line chart: Breakdown Hrs R13W by week\n- Scatter: assets with x = MTBF, y = MTTR, size = Breakdown Hrs. Top-left is \"fails often, quick fix\" and bottom-right is \"rare but long\". Label the quadrants with text boxes.\n- Bar chart: MTBF by asset type (worst 10)\n\n### Page 4: Top-10 Loss Assets\n*Which assets cost us the most, and why?*\n- The Pareto from Week 20 (top 15, with cumulative line)\n- Table: top 10 with breakdowns, hours, MTTR and PM compliance (Due). This is your Phase 2 done-when query as a visual.\n- Right-click drill-through to **Asset Detail** (Week 21), and the report tooltip on hover\n\n### Page 5: Condition Monitoring Rollout\n*Where are we with the vibration sensor rollout?*\n\nMeasures (the `SensorRollout` table has 105 sensors):\n\n```dax\nSensors Planned = COUNTROWS ( SensorRollout )\nSensors Commissioned = CALCULATE ( [Sensors Planned], SensorRollout[status] = \"Commissioned\" )\nSensors Overdue = CALCULATE ( [Sensors Planned], SensorRollout[status] = \"Overdue\" )\nRollout % = DIVIDE ( [Sensors Commissioned], [Sensors Planned] )\n```\n\n- KPI cards: Planned **105**, Commissioned **52**, Overdue **28**, Rollout % **49.5 %**\n- Stacked bar: status by rollout wave (Wave 2 has the most overdue: 16)\n- Stacked bar: status by zone\n- Table: overdue sensors with asset, planned date and days overdue\n\n(Relate `SensorRollout[planned_date]` to the Calendar if you want a planned-vs-actual S-curve. Make it inactive and use USERELATIONSHIP, as in Week 19.)\n\n### About page (hidden from the navigator, linked by an ⓘ button)\nData sources, last refresh, every KPI definition (*on time*, *MTBF per asset*, *planned %*), and the colour rules.","mins":210},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube report design tips\"*.\n- **SQLBI / Tabular Editor blog**: search *\"Power BI report design best practices layout\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Tips for designing a great Power BI dashboard\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What should a page title say?\n2. Why must red only ever mean \"bad\"?\n3. Name two things that should be in the same place on every page.\n4. The rollout page shows 49.5 % commissioned. What extra visual would tell a manager whether that's good or bad?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The question the page answers, or better, the finding (\"Packing PM compliance is 15 points below the line\").\n2. So the eye can scan for red and trust that every red thing needs attention. Decorative red trains people to ignore it.\n3. Any of: the date slicer, other slicers, navigation, the last-refreshed date, KPI card positions, the title, and the ⓘ info button.\n4. **Plan vs actual over time** (an S-curve of planned and commissioned sensors cumulatively by week), or **commissioned vs plan to date**. 49.5 % of the total means nothing without knowing how many *should* be done by now. Wave 3 isn't due until later.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 (theme JSON applied, colour rules written, layout grid and one header copied to every page), then all five pages and the About page. This is the dashboard Week 24 tests, so none of the pages are optional, and it needs well over 90 minutes: give each page its own short sitting.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Guy in a Cube report design tips video, read the Microsoft Learn *Tips for designing a great Power BI dashboard* page and \"Why it matters\", and sketch the 1280 × 720 grid and each page's one-question subtitle on paper before you build.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The optional planned-versus-actual S-curve on page 5 and the quadrant text-box labels on the page 3 scatter are polish you can add later."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Theme JSON applied, colour rules written down\n- [ ] Layout grid and header consistent across pages\n- [ ] Pages 1–5 built\n- [ ] About page with KPI definitions"}],"quiz":[{"id":"22-1","n":1,"q":"What should a page title say?","a":"The question the page answers, or better, the finding (\"Packing PM compliance is 15 points below the line\")."},{"id":"22-2","n":2,"q":"Why must red only ever mean \"bad\"?","a":"So the eye can scan for red and trust that every red thing needs attention. Decorative red trains people to ignore it."},{"id":"22-3","n":3,"q":"Name two things that should be in the same place on every page.","a":"Any of: the date slicer, other slicers, navigation, the last-refreshed date, KPI card positions, the title, and the ⓘ info button."},{"id":"22-4","n":4,"q":"The rollout page shows 49.5 % commissioned. What extra visual would tell a manager whether that's good or bad?","a":"**Plan vs actual over time** (an S-curve of planned and commissioned sensors cumulatively by week), or **commissioned vs plan to date**. 49.5 % of the total means nothing without knowing how many *should* be done by now. Wave 3 isn't due until later."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube report design tips\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube report design tips"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**SQLBI / Tabular Editor blog**: search *\"Power BI report design best practices layout\"*.","searches":["Power BI report design best practices layout"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: search *\"Tips for designing a great Power BI dashboard\"*.","searches":["Tips for designing a great Power BI dashboard"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 (theme JSON applied, colour rules written, layout grid and one header copied to every page), then all five pages and the About page. This is the dashboard Week 24 tests, so none of the pages are optional, and it needs well over 90 minutes: give each page its own short sitting."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Guy in a Cube report design tips video, read the Microsoft Learn *Tips for designing a great Power BI dashboard* page and \"Why it matters\", and sketch the 1280 × 720 grid and each page's one-question subtitle on paper before you build."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The optional planned-versus-actual S-curve on page 5 and the quadrant text-box labels on the page 3 scatter are polish you can add later."}}},{"week":23,"phase":3,"title":"Power BI Service, sharing, refresh and row-level security","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-23-service-rls-power-platform.md","ticks":["Can explain workspace, semantic model, report and app","Know when a gateway is needed, and the SharePoint workaround","Static RLS roles tested with *View as*","Dynamic RLS with `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` tested","Power Automate flow and Power App designed on paper, plus a half-page proposal"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 5 Apr – Sun 11 Apr 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nA `.pbix` on your PC helps nobody else. The **Power BI Service** (app.powerbi.com) is where reports get published, refreshed and shared. This week you learn how that works, how **row-level security** keeps people to their own data, and where Power Automate and Power Apps fit.\n\n> **Important: account limits.** The Power BI Service needs a **work or school Microsoft account**. A personal Gmail or Outlook.com address can't sign up. So:\n> - Everything in Session 1 that needs the Service is **read and watch**, or do it later with your work account *if IT allows*.\n> - Row-level security can be built and **tested fully in Desktop** (Session 2), with no account needed.\n> - Never publish practice reports into your employer's tenant without asking.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain workspaces, semantic models, reports, dashboards and apps\n- describe scheduled refresh and when a **gateway** is needed\n- build and test **row-level security** (RLS) roles in Desktop\n- explain the licensing basics (Free, Pro, Premium Per User, Fabric capacity)\n- know what Power Automate and Power Apps could do for a maintenance team","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-how-the-service-works","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): How the Service works","kind":"hands","md":"### Learn\n\nWork through the Microsoft Learn PL-300 modules *\"Create and manage workspaces in Power BI\"* and *\"Manage semantic models in Power BI\"* (search those titles). Then write your own one-line definitions:\n\n| Item | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Workspace** | a shared folder for content, with roles (Admin, Member, Contributor, Viewer) |\n| **Semantic model** (was *dataset*) | the published model: tables, relationships, measures |\n| **Report** | the pages and visuals built on a semantic model |\n| **Dashboard** | a single canvas of pinned tiles from reports (less used now) |\n| **App** | a packaged, read-only bundle of reports for an audience, which is how you share with a team properly |\n\n### Refresh\n\n- Cloud sources (SharePoint, SQL in Azure) refresh directly.\n- **On-premises** sources (a file on a PC, a local SQL Server, a CMMS database on a plant server) need an **on-premises data gateway**: a small service on a machine that's always on, which the Service talks through. Your IT department owns this.\n- Pro licences allow 8 scheduled refreshes a day.\n\n**Practical tip:** if the source files live in a **SharePoint/OneDrive** folder (Get Data → SharePoint folder), no gateway is needed. That's often the easiest route at a manufacturing site.\n\n### Licensing (check current details on Microsoft's site; they change)\n\n- **Free**: you can build and publish to *My Workspace* only, with no sharing.\n- **Pro**: per user, needed to share and to consume shared content.\n- **Premium Per User / Fabric capacity**: larger models, more refreshes, and capacity-based sharing.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-row-level-security-in-desktop","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Row-level security in Desktop","kind":"hands","md":"The scenario: zone team leaders should only see their own zone.\n\n### Do it\n\n1. Modeling → **Manage roles** → New role `Zone Lead`.\n2. Select the `Zones` table and add a DAX filter:\n\n   ```dax\n   [zone] = \"Primary Packing\"\n   ```\n\n3. Make roles for two more zones the same way.\n4. Modeling → **View as** → tick `Zone Lead`. The whole report now shows only Primary Packing: Pareto, compliance, everything. The filter flows down the relationships, just as in Week 16.\n5. **Dynamic RLS** (the scalable version):\n   - Create a table `ZoneAccess` with columns `email` and `zone_code` (Enter Data), with rows like `lead.packing@example.com, PPK`.\n   - Relate `ZoneAccess[zone_code]` → `Zones[zone_code]`. (You may need the filter to flow *from* ZoneAccess to Zones; set that relationship's direction accordingly, and tick *Apply security filter in both directions* if required.)\n   - Role `By User`, with a filter on `ZoneAccess`: `[email] = USERPRINCIPALNAME()`.\n   - **View as** → `By User` + *Other user* `lead.packing@example.com`.\n\n### Why it works\n\nRLS adds a filter to the model **before any measure runs**, for that user. Measures don't need changing. Combined with relationships, one filter on a small dimension secures every fact. One report and one model then serve every team.\n\n**Caution:** RLS restricts *viewers*. Workspace Admins, Members and Contributors bypass it. Share with zone leads as *Viewers* or through an *App*.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-power-automate-and-power-apps","title":"Weekend (2 h): Power Automate and Power Apps","kind":"hands","md":"These are in the same Microsoft Power Platform family, and they're high value for a maintenance team. Like the Service, they need a work account, so this weekend is about **designing**, not building.\n\n1. Watch: **Reza Dorrani** (YouTube), *\"Reza Dorrani Power Apps beginner\"* and *\"Reza Dorrani Power Automate beginner\"*. He's the best teacher for these tools.\n2. **Design a Power Automate flow** on paper: *\"When the weekly PM export lands in SharePoint, refresh the Power BI semantic model, then email the zone leads a link plus their overdue PM count.\"* List the trigger, the actions, and which connectors would be needed.\n3. **Design a Power App** on paper: a phone form for an operator's daily check (metric for asset, OK/Not OK, comment, photo) that writes to a SharePoint list, which Power BI then reads. Sketch 3 screens.\n4. Write a half-page proposal: *\"What I'd automate first on Line D, and what it would save.\"* Keep it. It's a good conversation starter with your manager once you've finished Phase 3.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube row level security\"* and *\"Guy in a Cube on-premises data gateway\"*.\n- **Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *\"Implement row-level security\"* (search the title)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does a report sourced from CSVs on your PC need a gateway to refresh in the Service?\n2. Where do you put the RLS filter: on the fact table or on the dimension?\n3. A zone lead added as a workspace *Member* can see all zones. Why?\n4. What's the difference between sharing a report and publishing an app?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The Service runs in Microsoft's cloud and can't reach files on your PC. The gateway is the bridge. Moving the files to SharePoint/OneDrive avoids needing one.\n2. On the **dimension** (Zones), which is small and filters every related fact through the relationships. Filtering each fact separately is slower and easy to get wrong.\n3. RLS only applies to Viewers. Members, Contributors and Admins can edit the model, so security isn't enforced for them.\n4. Sharing gives access to one item. An **app** bundles several reports with navigation, has its own audience list, and lets you update content without disturbing users until you republish. It's the cleaner choice for a team.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 2 steps 1 to 5 in Desktop: static `Zone Lead` roles tested with *View as*, then dynamic RLS with `ZoneAccess` and `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` tested as `lead.packing@example.com`. Everything else this week works away from the PC.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Session 1 is reading: work through the two PL-300 workspace and semantic model modules and write your one-line definitions. Watch the Reza Dorrani Power Apps and Power Automate videos and the Guy in a Cube RLS video, then do the weekend flow design, the 3-screen app sketch and the half-page proposal on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The PL-300 *Implement row-level security* module and the Guy in a Cube gateway video can wait, and the licensing notes only need a quick skim."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Can explain workspace, semantic model, report and app\n- [ ] Know when a gateway is needed, and the SharePoint workaround\n- [ ] Static RLS roles tested with *View as*\n- [ ] Dynamic RLS with `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` tested\n- [ ] Power Automate flow and Power App designed on paper, plus a half-page proposal"}],"quiz":[{"id":"23-1","n":1,"q":"Why does a report sourced from CSVs on your PC need a gateway to refresh in the Service?","a":"The Service runs in Microsoft's cloud and can't reach files on your PC. The gateway is the bridge. Moving the files to SharePoint/OneDrive avoids needing one."},{"id":"23-2","n":2,"q":"Where do you put the RLS filter: on the fact table or on the dimension?","a":"On the **dimension** (Zones), which is small and filters every related fact through the relationships. Filtering each fact separately is slower and easy to get wrong."},{"id":"23-3","n":3,"q":"A zone lead added as a workspace *Member* can see all zones. Why?","a":"RLS only applies to Viewers. Members, Contributors and Admins can edit the model, so security isn't enforced for them."},{"id":"23-4","n":4,"q":"What's the difference between sharing a report and publishing an app?","a":"Sharing gives access to one item. An **app** bundles several reports with navigation, has its own audience list, and lets you update content without disturbing users until you republish. It's the cleaner choice for a team."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Guy in a Cube**: *\"Guy in a Cube row level security\"* and *\"Guy in a Cube on-premises data gateway\"*.","searches":["Guy in a Cube row level security","Guy in a Cube on-premises data gateway"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Microsoft Learn**: PL-300 module *\"Implement row-level security\"* (search the title).","searches":["Implement row-level security"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 2 steps 1 to 5 in Desktop: static `Zone Lead` roles tested with *View as*, then dynamic RLS with `ZoneAccess` and `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` tested as `lead.packing@example.com`. Everything else this week works away from the PC."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Session 1 is reading: work through the two PL-300 workspace and semantic model modules and write your one-line definitions. Watch the Reza Dorrani Power Apps and Power Automate videos and the Guy in a Cube RLS video, then do the weekend flow design, the 3-screen app sketch and the half-page proposal on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The PL-300 *Implement row-level security* module and the Guy in a Cube gateway video can wait, and the licensing notes only need a quick skim."}}},{"week":24,"phase":3,"title":"Finish the dashboard, done-when test, PL-300 plan","path":"course/phase-3-power-bi/week-24-phase3-done-when-pl300.md","ticks":["Performance Analyzer checked, model tidied, measure descriptions added","Reconciliation table: Power BI = SQL = Excel","Done-when test passed with an independent tester","`portfolio/03-lined-dashboard/` committed","PL-300 practice assessment taken, revision plan written"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 3 · Power BI** · Mon 12 Apr – Sun 18 Apr 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nLast week of Phase 3. Polish, test with a real person, package for your portfolio, and decide whether to sit **PL-300**.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-performance-and-quality-pass","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Performance and quality pass","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Performance Analyzer** (Optimize ribbon → Performance analyzer → Start recording → Refresh visuals). Any visual over ~1 second? Usually it's a complex measure inside a big table. Simplify it, or reduce the rows shown.\n2. **Model clean-up:**\n   - Remove unused columns in Power Query (fewer columns means a smaller, faster file).\n   - Check that every measure has a format and lives in `_Measures`, in display folders (*PM*, *Reliability*, *Downtime*, *Rollout*).\n   - Add a **description** to each key measure (Model view → Properties → Description). It shows up when users hover in the field list.\n3. **Reconciliation sheet**: on the About page (or in a separate check page), put a table of headline numbers next to their source:\n\n   | KPI | Power BI | SQL (Week 12–14) | Excel (Week 8) |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | PM compliance, all time | 79.5 % | 79.5 % | 79.5 % |\n   | PM compliance, June 2026 (due) | 81.3 % | 81.3 % | |\n   | Q2 2026 top loss asset | LD-FLK-010 | LD-FLK-010 | LD-FLK-010 |\n   | Total WOs | 11,902 | 11,902 | 11,902 |\n\n   If any of them disagree, find out why before you show anyone.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-the-done-when-test","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): The done-when test","kind":"hands","md":"> **Done when:** the team could use it in a weekly review **without you explaining it**.\n\n### Do it\n\n1. Find a tester: a colleague, friend or family member. Ideally someone who's never seen it.\n2. Give them **three questions** and say nothing else:\n   - \"Which zone is worst for PM compliance, and is it getting better?\"\n   - \"Which asset lost the most time last quarter? What keeps going wrong on it?\"\n   - \"How far behind is the sensor rollout?\"\n3. **Watch silently** and note every hesitation, wrong click and \"what does this mean?\".\n4. Fix each one: a clearer title, a tooltip, a definition on the About page, or a moved slicer.\n5. Repeat with a second person if you can. When someone answers all three in under five minutes without help, **you've passed Phase 3.** 🎉","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-portfolio-and-pl-300-plan","title":"Weekend (2 h): Portfolio and PL-300 plan","kind":"hands","md":"### Portfolio\n\nCreate `portfolio/03-lined-dashboard/` with:\n\n- `LineD_Maintenance.pbix` (it contains only synthetic data, so it's safe to share)\n- `screenshots/`: one PNG per page\n- `README.md` covering: the purpose, the five questions it answers, the model diagram (from Week 16), the KPI definitions, the three most interesting findings, and what you'd change with real data\n\n### PL-300 (Microsoft Power BI Data Analyst)\n\nPL-300 is the most recognised data certificate in UK manufacturing roles. It's optional, but you've now covered most of it.\n\n1. Download the **study guide** (search *\"PL-300 study guide\"* on Microsoft Learn). It lists the skills measured and their weightings: preparing data, modelling, visualising and analysing, and managing and securing.\n2. Do the free **Practice Assessment** on the PL-300 page on Microsoft Learn. Note your weak areas. For most people they're Service administration and RLS, which you couldn't practise fully at home.\n3. Plan **3–4 weeks of revision** around the next phases (an extra 1–2 hours a week), using the Microsoft Learn path for your weak areas.\n4. Check the **current exam price** on Microsoft's site, and ask your manager whether the training budget will fund it, since it maps directly onto the PM pillar work.\n5. The exam can be taken online at home (with proctoring) or at a test centre.","mins":120},{"id":"next-tableau-and-production-data","title":"Next: Tableau and production data","kind":"other","md":"Your production team's Redzone data lives in **Tableau**, so Phase 4 teaches it next, along with OEE and linking production downtime to CMMS work orders. Everything you've just learned transfers: the star schema becomes Tableau *relationships*, DAX measures become *calculated fields*, and filter context becomes Tableau's *order of operations*. Being fluent in both tools is a genuine strength: Power BI for maintenance reporting, Tableau where production already works.","readMins":2},{"id":"check-yourself-phase-3-review","title":"Check yourself: Phase 3 review","kind":"check","md":"Without looking anything up:\n\n1. Sketch the star schema with the relationship directions.\n2. Write `PM Compliance % (Due)` from memory.\n3. Explain filter context to someone who's never used Power BI.\n4. What's the difference between `ALL`, `ALLSELECTED` and `REMOVEFILTERS`?\n5. When does a report need a gateway?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Zones → Assets → (WorkOrders, Downtime, PMTasks, SensorRollout); Calendar → WorkOrders (raised, active), Calendar → WorkOrders (due, inactive), Calendar → Downtime. All single direction, one to many.\n2. ```dax\n   PM Compliance % (Due) =\n   CALCULATE (\n       DIVIDE (\n           CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[PM Result] = \"On time\" ),\n           CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"PM\" )\n       ),\n       USERELATIONSHIP ( Calendar[Date], WorkOrders[due_date] )\n   )\n   ```\n3. Every number on a report is calculated with a set of filters applied: whatever row, column, slicer and page you're on. Change the filters and the same formula gives a different answer. `CALCULATE` lets a measure change those filters.\n4. `ALL` removes filters and returns all rows (it can be used as a table). `REMOVEFILTERS` only removes filters, as a CALCULATE modifier, and is clearer to read. `ALLSELECTED` removes filters from inside the visual but keeps the outer slicer and page filters.\n5. When the Service needs to refresh from a source it can't reach from the cloud: files on a PC, or on-premises databases.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 3 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the Session 1 step 3 reconciliation table, the Session 2 test with an independent tester (fix what they trip on), the `portfolio/03-lined-dashboard/` folder with screenshots and README, and the PL-300 practice assessment. That won't fit in 90 minutes, so book the tester first and fit the rest around them.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Do the Phase 3 review in Check yourself in your head and sketch the star schema on paper, read the PL-300 study guide on Microsoft Learn, check the current exam price, and ask your manager at work whether the training budget will fund it.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only polish can wait: measure descriptions, display folders and removing unused columns (Session 1 step 2), a deep Performance Analyzer tidy-up and the second tester."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Performance Analyzer checked, model tidied, measure descriptions added\n- [ ] Reconciliation table: Power BI = SQL = Excel\n- [ ] **Done-when test passed** with an independent tester\n- [ ] `portfolio/03-lined-dashboard/` committed\n- [ ] PL-300 practice assessment taken, revision plan written"}],"quiz":[{"id":"24-1","n":1,"q":"Sketch the star schema with the relationship directions.","a":"Zones → Assets → (WorkOrders, Downtime, PMTasks, SensorRollout); Calendar → WorkOrders (raised, active), Calendar → WorkOrders (due, inactive), Calendar → Downtime. All single direction, one to many."},{"id":"24-2","n":2,"q":"Write `PM Compliance % (Due)` from memory.","a":"```dax\nPM Compliance % (Due) =\nCALCULATE (\n    DIVIDE (\n        CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[PM Result] = \"On time\" ),\n        CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( WorkOrders ), WorkOrders[WO Type] = \"PM\" )\n    ),\n    USERELATIONSHIP ( Calendar[Date], WorkOrders[due_date] )\n)\n```"},{"id":"24-3","n":3,"q":"Explain filter context to someone who's never used Power BI.","a":"Every number on a report is calculated with a set of filters applied: whatever row, column, slicer and page you're on. Change the filters and the same formula gives a different answer. `CALCULATE` lets a measure change those filters."},{"id":"24-4","n":4,"q":"What's the difference between `ALL`, `ALLSELECTED` and `REMOVEFILTERS`?","a":"`ALL` removes filters and returns all rows (it can be used as a table). `REMOVEFILTERS` only removes filters, as a CALCULATE modifier, and is clearer to read. `ALLSELECTED` removes filters from inside the visual but keeps the outer slicer and page filters."},{"id":"24-5","n":5,"q":"When does a report need a gateway?","a":"When the Service needs to refresh from a source it can't reach from the cloud: files on a PC, or on-premises databases."}],"watch":[],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 3 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the Session 1 step 3 reconciliation table, the Session 2 test with an independent tester (fix what they trip on), the `portfolio/03-lined-dashboard/` folder with screenshots and README, and the PL-300 practice assessment. That won't fit in 90 minutes, so book the tester first and fit the rest around them."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Do the Phase 3 review in Check yourself in your head and sketch the star schema on paper, read the PL-300 study guide on Microsoft Learn, check the current exam price, and ask your manager at work whether the training budget will fund it."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only polish can wait: measure descriptions, display folders and removing unused columns (Session 1 step 2), a deep Performance Analyzer tidy-up and the second tester."}}},{"week":25,"phase":4,"title":"OEE fundamentals, from shift data to one honest number","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-25-oee-fundamentals.md","ticks":["A, P, Q and OEE calculated for one shift by hand (73.4 %)","Line OEE calculated correctly from totals (70.3 % overall, 71.9 % H1 2026)","Can explain why averaging OEE percentages is wrong","H1 2026 minute bridge as a waterfall chart","One-page note of your site's OEE definitions"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 19 Apr – Sun 25 Apr 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 4 goal:** speak the production team's language. Your production team runs on **Redzone**: OEE, downtime and losses, viewed in **Tableau**. Maintenance runs on the CMMS (**Mainsaver**, moving to **Ultimo**). The person who can connect those two worlds, *\"this much of our lost OEE is maintenance, and it's these assets\"*, is rare and valuable. This phase makes you that person.\n\nThis week is the foundation: what OEE actually measures, how to calculate it correctly from raw shift data, and the aggregation mistake that makes most OEE spreadsheets wrong.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- define planned production time, availability, performance, quality and OEE\n- calculate OEE for one shift by hand, and for many shifts correctly (sum first, then divide)\n- build an OEE **minute bridge** (waterfall) from planned time down to fully productive time\n- write down your own site's OEE definitions, which is the most important thing to do this week\n\n**Data:** `data/production/shifts.csv`, 1,460 shifts of Line D (Days 06:00–18:00, Nights 18:00–06:00, crews A–D, four SKUs). It's synthetic, in the shape of what a production system like Redzone records per shift.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-one-shift-by-hand","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): One shift, by hand","kind":"hands","md":"### The definitions\n\n```\nShift time (720 min)\n └ minus planned stops (breaks, planned sanitation)  →  Planned production time\n     └ minus downtime (breakdowns, changeovers, minor stops, waiting) →  Run time            ← Availability = Run ÷ Planned\n         └ minus speed loss (running slower than ideal)            →  Net run time        ← Performance = (Total count × ideal cycle time) ÷ Run\n             └ minus quality loss (rejects, rework, start-up waste)  →  Fully productive time ← Quality = Good ÷ Total\n```\n\n**OEE = Availability × Performance × Quality = fully productive time ÷ planned production time.**\n\nThe second form, **good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned time**, is the most robust. It's one division, and it works even when different products have different ideal rates.\n\n### Do it\n\nOpen `shifts.csv` in Excel (save it as `week25_oee.xlsx` and make it a Table, `tblShifts`). Look at the first row, `S00001`:\n\n| planned_production_min | downtime_min | run_min | ideal_rate_ppm | total_count | good_count |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 690 | 47 | 643 | 75 | 38,451 | 37,981 |\n\nBy hand, or in cells:\n\n- Availability = 643 ÷ 690 = **93.2 %**\n- Performance = 38,451 ÷ (643 × 75) = **79.7 %**: it ran at about 80 % of ideal speed while running\n- Quality = 37,981 ÷ 38,451 = **98.8 %**\n- **OEE = 73.4 %**. Check it the other way: 37,981 ÷ 75 = 506.4 fully productive minutes, and 506.4 ÷ 690 = 73.4 %.\n\nAdd calculated columns to `tblShifts` for A, P, Q and OEE.\n\n### Why it works\n\nEach factor isolates one kind of loss, so you know **where** to look. Low A means stops. Low P means slow running or short stops that aren't being logged. Low Q means waste. One OEE number on its own tells you how much, not why.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-many-shifts-and-the-aggregatio","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Many shifts, and the aggregation trap","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **The wrong way:** `=AVERAGE(tblShifts[OEE])`, which gives **70.2 %** over two years.\n2. **The right way:** sum the minutes and counts first, then divide:\n\n   ```\n   =SUMPRODUCT(tblShifts[good_count]/tblShifts[ideal_rate_ppm]) / SUM(tblShifts[planned_production_min])\n   ```\n\n   That gives **70.3 %**. Then A = `SUM(run)/SUM(planned)` = **83.3 %**, P = **86.1 %**, Q = **98.1 %**.\n\nThe two answers are close here, because every shift is about the same length. Now see why it matters. Imagine two shifts:\n\n| Shift | Planned min | OEE |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full shift | 690 | 80 % |\n| Short Sunday shift after a deep clean | 60 | 10 % |\n\nThe average of the two OEEs is 45 %. The true OEE is (552 + 6) ÷ 750 = **74 %**. Averaging percentages gives a tiny, unusual period the same weight as a full one. **Always aggregate the underlying minutes and counts, never the percentages.** The same rule applies in pivots, Power BI and Tableau.\n\n3. **H1 2026** (filter `production_date` ≥ 1 Jan 2026): A **85.2 %**, P **86.0 %**, Q **98.1 %**, **OEE 71.9 %**.\n4. **By shift**: Days 71.8 %, Nights 72.0 %. **By SKU** (all data): from 69.8 % (Wheat Biscuits 24s) to 70.9 % (Wheat Biscuits 48s). The differences are small. Hold back from blaming a crew or a product on a 1-point gap; Phase 6 gives you the tests to check whether it's real.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-the-minute-bridge-and-your-site","title":"Weekend (2 h): The minute bridge, and your site's definitions","kind":"hands","md":"### Build the waterfall for H1 2026\n\n| Step | Minutes | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| Planned production time | **243,540** | `SUM(planned_production_min)` |\n| − Availability loss (downtime) | 36,146 | `SUM(downtime_min)` |\n| − Speed loss | 28,989 | `SUM(run_min − total_count ÷ ideal_rate)` |\n| − Quality loss | 3,350 | `SUM(reject_count ÷ ideal_rate)` |\n| = Fully productive time | **175,055** | 175,055 ÷ 243,540 = **71.9 %** ✅ |\n\nInsert → Chart → **Waterfall** in Excel. Title it with the finding. Notice that **speed loss (29k min) is nearly as big as all downtime (36k)**. It's invisible on a downtime Pareto, and it's often where the easiest gains are.\n\n### At work: write down your site's OEE rules (don't export anything yet)\n\nOpen your Redzone / Tableau OEE dashboard and find out, from the dashboard notes or by asking the CI or production lead:\n\n1. What counts as a **planned stop** (excluded from OEE)? Breaks? Cleans? Planned maintenance? Changeovers?\n2. What's the **ideal or target rate** for each SKU, and who sets it? (A lowered target hides speed loss.)\n3. Is there a **minimum stop length**? Short stops below it often get counted as speed loss instead of downtime.\n4. Where are **counts** taken: the wrapper, the case packer or the palletiser? Rejects from where?\n5. Is OEE shown per **line**, per **machine**, or both?\n\nWrite the answers in a one-page **\"OEE definitions\"** note. Without it, your numbers and Redzone's will never match, and everyone will argue about the numbers instead of the losses.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"OEE explained availability performance quality\"* and pick a 10–15 minute explainer.\n- Search *\"six big losses OEE\"* (next week's topic, so a head start).\n- Read [oee.com](https://www.oee.com), a clear free reference site on OEE calculation and the six big losses."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. A shift has 690 planned minutes, 90 minutes of downtime, ideal rate 100 ppm, 48,000 packs made, and 47,000 good. Work out A, P, Q and OEE.\n2. Why is \"good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned time\" the safest OEE formula?\n3. Your manager averages the seven daily OEE figures to get a weekly OEE. What's wrong, and when does it matter most?\n4. Why can lowering a product's \"target rate\" make OEE look better without anything improving?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Run = 600, so A = 600/690 = **87.0 %**. P = 48,000 / (600 × 100) = **80.0 %**. Q = 47,000/48,000 = **97.9 %**. OEE = **68.1 %**. Check: 47,000/100 = 470 min, and 470/690 = 68.1 %.\n2. It's one ratio of totals, with no averaging of percentages, and it handles mixed SKUs with different ideal rates correctly.\n3. Days with very different planned time get equal weight. A half-day Sunday counts as much as a full Monday. It matters most when periods differ a lot in length (weekends, short weeks, line trials). Sum the minutes and counts for the week instead.\n4. Performance = actual ÷ (run time × ideal rate). Lowering the \"ideal\" rate raises performance on paper. That's why the ideal rate should be the machine's design or demonstrated best rate, and why you need to know your site's definition.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's A, P, Q and OEE columns in `tblShifts` (73.4 % for `S00001`), Session 2 steps 1 to 3 (the wrong average against the right sum-then-divide, then H1 2026 at 71.9 %), and the weekend H1 2026 minute bridge as a waterfall chart.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch an \"OEE explained\" video, read oee.com and both \"Why it works\" parts, and work Check yourself question 1 on paper. At work, ask the five weekend questions about planned stops, ideal rates, minimum stop length, count points and line versus machine, and write your one-page OEE definitions note.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 step 4 (OEE by shift and by SKU) and the \"six big losses\" head-start video can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] A, P, Q and OEE calculated for one shift by hand (73.4 %)\n- [ ] Line OEE calculated correctly from totals (70.3 % overall, 71.9 % H1 2026)\n- [ ] Can explain why averaging OEE percentages is wrong\n- [ ] H1 2026 minute bridge as a waterfall chart\n- [ ] One-page note of your site's OEE definitions"}],"quiz":[{"id":"25-1","n":1,"q":"A shift has 690 planned minutes, 90 minutes of downtime, ideal rate 100 ppm, 48,000 packs made, and 47,000 good. Work out A, P, Q and OEE.","a":"Run = 600, so A = 600/690 = **87.0 %**. P = 48,000 / (600 × 100) = **80.0 %**. Q = 47,000/48,000 = **97.9 %**. OEE = **68.1 %**. Check: 47,000/100 = 470 min, and 470/690 = 68.1 %."},{"id":"25-2","n":2,"q":"Why is \"good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned time\" the safest OEE formula?","a":"It's one ratio of totals, with no averaging of percentages, and it handles mixed SKUs with different ideal rates correctly."},{"id":"25-3","n":3,"q":"Your manager averages the seven daily OEE figures to get a weekly OEE. What's wrong, and when does it matter most?","a":"Days with very different planned time get equal weight. A half-day Sunday counts as much as a full Monday. It matters most when periods differ a lot in length (weekends, short weeks, line trials). Sum the minutes and counts for the week instead."},{"id":"25-4","n":4,"q":"Why can lowering a product's \"target rate\" make OEE look better without anything improving?","a":"Performance = actual ÷ (run time × ideal rate). Lowering the \"ideal\" rate raises performance on paper. That's why the ideal rate should be the machine's design or demonstrated best rate, and why you need to know your site's definition."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"OEE explained availability performance quality\"* and pick a 10–15 minute explainer.","searches":["OEE explained availability performance quality"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"six big losses OEE\"* (next week's topic, so a head start).","searches":["six big losses OEE"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Read [oee.com](https://www.oee.com), a clear free reference site on OEE calculation and the six big losses.","searches":[],"urls":["https://www.oee.com"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's A, P, Q and OEE columns in `tblShifts` (73.4 % for `S00001`), Session 2 steps 1 to 3 (the wrong average against the right sum-then-divide, then H1 2026 at 71.9 %), and the weekend H1 2026 minute bridge as a waterfall chart."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch an \"OEE explained\" video, read oee.com and both \"Why it works\" parts, and work Check yourself question 1 on paper. At work, ask the five weekend questions about planned stops, ideal rates, minimum stop length, count points and line versus machine, and write your one-page OEE definitions note."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 step 4 (OEE by shift and by SKU) and the \"six big losses\" head-start video can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":26,"phase":4,"title":"Downtime reasons, the six big losses, and operator-coded data","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-26-downtime-reasons-six-big-losses.md","ticks":["Downtime mapped onto the six big losses","Category, reason and machine Paretos for H1 2026","Coding quality measured (unassigned 7.3 % of minutes), with miscoding examples found","Event minutes reconciled to shift minutes, and the gap explained","Half page: what our downtime data can and can't tell maintenance"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 26 Apr – Sun 2 May 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nIn Redzone, operators log each stop with a **machine** and a **reason**. That's a goldmine, and it's also the messiest data you'll work with: free choices made in a hurry by busy people. This week you analyse the downtime log the way a CI engineer would, and learn to judge how far you can trust it.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- map downtime reasons onto the **six big losses**\n- build loss Paretos by category, reason and machine\n- measure coding quality: unassigned stops, catch-all reasons, miscoding\n- reconcile event-level downtime with shift-level totals\n\n**Data:** `data/production/redzone_downtime.csv`, 6,725 stop events, each with production date, shift, crew, SKU, **machine**, start, end, minutes, **reason category**, **reason** and an optional **comment**. The same tables are in `data/line_d.db` (`redzone_downtime`, `shifts`).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-six-big-losses","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The six big losses","kind":"hands","md":"| OEE factor | Big loss | In this data |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Availability** | 1. Breakdowns (unplanned stops) | reason categories *Mechanical*, *Electrical* |\n| | 2. Setup and adjustments | *Changeover* |\n| **Performance** | 3. Small stops and idling | short *Operational* stops: jams, sensor misreads, guard doors |\n| | 4. Reduced speed | not in the downtime log at all. It's the speed loss from Week 25's bridge. |\n| **Quality** | 5. Start-up rejects | `reject_count` (shift table) |\n| | 6. Production rejects | `reject_count` (shift table) |\n\nPlus losses that are neither, which many sites log as their own categories: *Material* (waiting for film or cartons), *People* (no operator) and *Quality* (QA hold).\n\n> Note: this data set logs short stops as downtime events (so they reduce availability). Many sites, and many Redzone set-ups, treat stops under a few minutes as performance loss instead. That's one of the definitions you wrote down last week.\n\n### Do it\n\nIn DB Browser (or a pivot in Excel on the CSV):\n\n```sql\nSELECT reason_category, COUNT(*) AS events, SUM(duration_min) AS minutes\nFROM redzone_downtime\nWHERE production_date >= '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY reason_category\nORDER BY minutes DESC;\n```\n\n| Category (H1 2026) | Events | Minutes | % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mechanical | 96 | 11,358 | 31.1 |\n| Operational | 1,233 | 11,050 | 30.2 |\n| Electrical | 34 | 4,484 | 12.3 |\n| **Unassigned** | 155 | 3,670 | **10.0** |\n| Changeover | 88 | 3,522 | 9.6 |\n| Material | 24 | 1,572 | 4.3 |\n| Quality | 14 | 612 | 1.7 |\n| People | 5 | 281 | 0.8 |\n\nLook at the **shape**. Mechanical is about 100 long events. Operational is over 1,200 short ones. Same minutes, completely different problems and fixes: maintenance and reliability for one, minor-stop elimination for the other.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-drill-down-and-judge-the-codin","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Drill down, and judge the coding","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Top reasons within Mechanical + Electrical (H1 2026)**: Noise / bearing 2,796 min, Belt / chain off 2,514, Sensor fault 2,248, Jam 1,695, Drive fault 1,033.\n2. **Top Operational reasons**: Guard door opened 2,702, Sensor misread 2,557, Jam / blockage 1,931, Film / carton misfeed 1,541. Guard doors at the top is a classic finding. It's often a behaviour or guarding-design issue, not a maintenance one.\n3. **By machine**: Packing Conveyors 5,347 min, Wrapper 1 5,204, Flaker 4,493, Former 4,013, EOL Conveyors 3,353, Cooker 3,089.\n   - \"Packing Conveyors\" and \"Cooker\" are **groups**. Behind \"Cooker\" are ~43 CMMS assets. Operators pick the machine they can see, not the asset number. Week 30 deals with this.\n   - \"Wrapper 1\" carries every changeover, because that's where changeovers get logged. **Filter out changeovers before blaming a machine.**\n4. **Coding quality**:\n\n   ```sql\n   SELECT ROUND(100.0 * SUM(reason_category = 'Unassigned') / COUNT(*), 1) AS pct_events,\n          ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN reason_category = 'Unassigned' THEN duration_min END) / SUM(duration_min), 1) AS pct_minutes\n   FROM redzone_downtime;\n   ```\n\n   **8.8 % of events** and **7.3 % of minutes** have no reason. Also look for:\n   - \"Jam\" logged under *Mechanical* (an operator word for a maintenance failure), and *Operational* stops lasting over an hour (probably a breakdown coded as a jam)\n   - the `comment` field, where the real story often is (\"jaws not sealing, called eng\")\n5. **Reconcile**: the sum of event minutes in H1 2026 is **36,549**, but the shift table's downtime is **36,146**. Why the 403-minute gap? (Hint: stops that cross a shift boundary, and the worst shifts being capped. Look at shift `S01368` on 15 May 2026, Nights: 660 minutes down, OEE 3.7 %.) **Always reconcile two views of the same loss before presenting either.**","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-the-redzone-view-at-work","title":"Weekend (2 h): The Redzone view at work","kind":"hands","md":"Using **your site's** Redzone dashboards in Tableau (view and filter only, no downloads this week):\n\n1. Find the line's OEE for last month, and its A, P and Q. Does the dashboard show the minute bridge? If not, sketch it from what's shown.\n2. Find the top 5 downtime reasons for last month. How many are maintenance (mechanical/electrical) vs operational?\n3. Find the **unassigned / uncategorised** share, if the dashboard shows it. If it doesn't, that's a finding in itself.\n4. Ask a team leader: *\"When a machine stops, who enters the reason, and when?\"* The answer explains most coding problems.\n5. Write half a page: **\"What our downtime data can and can't tell maintenance.\"**","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"six big losses TPM explained\"*.\n- Search *\"SMED changeover reduction explained\"*. Changeovers are 10 % of the loss here.\n- Search *\"Redzone production system\"* to see the vendor's own overview, so you know what your production team sees."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Which big loss never appears in a downtime log, and how do you find it?\n2. Mechanical and Operational both lose about 11,000 minutes. Why do they need different improvement approaches?\n3. 10 % of minutes are Unassigned. How does that bias a Pareto?\n4. Why might Wrapper 1 top the machine Pareto even if it's reliable?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. **Reduced speed**. It's the gap between run time and (total count × ideal cycle time). Get it from the shift-level counts (Week 25's bridge), not from the stop log.\n2. Mechanical is about 100 long stops: root-cause each one, fix the failure modes, and improve PM and spares (your reliability toolkit). Operational is 1,200+ short stops: look for patterns (guard doors, sensor set-up, film splicing) and use small-stop elimination, standard work and SMED-style thinking.\n3. Every unassigned minute is missing from the real categories, so every bar is understated. If unassigned stops are mostly long breakdowns (which they often are, because nobody codes during a crisis), maintenance's share is under-reported. Check what the unassigned stops look like: duration, time of day, machine.\n4. Changeovers are logged against Wrapper 1, so it collects 3,500 minutes that aren't failures. Always separate planned or operational events from failures before ranking machines.\n\nWorked SQL is in [`solutions/phase-4-production/w25_w26_oee_losses.sql`](../../solutions/phase-4-production/w25_w26_oee_losses.sql).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** The Session 1 category query (or pivot) with the six big losses table, and Session 2 steps 1 to 5: top reasons, the machine Pareto, the unassigned share (8.8 % of events, 7.3 % of minutes) with a couple of miscoding examples, and the 36,549 against 36,146 reconciliation.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the six big losses and SMED videos, read the Session 1 note on short stops, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, look at your site's Redzone dashboards, ask a team leader who enters the stop reason and when, and draft your half page on what the downtime data can and can't tell maintenance.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The Redzone vendor overview video and weekend step 1 (sketching your site's minute bridge if the dashboard doesn't show it) can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Downtime mapped onto the six big losses\n- [ ] Category, reason and machine Paretos for H1 2026\n- [ ] Coding quality measured (unassigned 7.3 % of minutes), with miscoding examples found\n- [ ] Event minutes reconciled to shift minutes, and the gap explained\n- [ ] Half page: what our downtime data can and can't tell maintenance"}],"quiz":[{"id":"26-1","n":1,"q":"Which big loss never appears in a downtime log, and how do you find it?","a":"**Reduced speed**. It's the gap between run time and (total count × ideal cycle time). Get it from the shift-level counts (Week 25's bridge), not from the stop log."},{"id":"26-2","n":2,"q":"Mechanical and Operational both lose about 11,000 minutes. Why do they need different improvement approaches?","a":"Mechanical is about 100 long stops: root-cause each one, fix the failure modes, and improve PM and spares (your reliability toolkit). Operational is 1,200+ short stops: look for patterns (guard doors, sensor set-up, film splicing) and use small-stop elimination, standard work and SMED-style thinking."},{"id":"26-3","n":3,"q":"10 % of minutes are Unassigned. How does that bias a Pareto?","a":"Every unassigned minute is missing from the real categories, so every bar is understated. If unassigned stops are mostly long breakdowns (which they often are, because nobody codes during a crisis), maintenance's share is under-reported. Check what the unassigned stops look like: duration, time of day, machine."},{"id":"26-4","n":4,"q":"Why might Wrapper 1 top the machine Pareto even if it's reliable?","a":"Changeovers are logged against Wrapper 1, so it collects 3,500 minutes that aren't failures. Always separate planned or operational events from failures before ranking machines."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"six big losses TPM explained\"*.","searches":["six big losses TPM explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"SMED changeover reduction explained\"*. Changeovers are 10 % of the loss here.","searches":["SMED changeover reduction explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"Redzone production system\"* to see the vendor's own overview, so you know what your production team sees.","searches":["Redzone production system"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"The Session 1 category query (or pivot) with the six big losses table, and Session 2 steps 1 to 5: top reasons, the machine Pareto, the unassigned share (8.8 % of events, 7.3 % of minutes) with a couple of miscoding examples, and the 36,549 against 36,146 reconciliation."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the six big losses and SMED videos, read the Session 1 note on short stops, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, look at your site's Redzone dashboards, ask a team leader who enters the stop reason and when, and draft your half page on what the downtime data can and can't tell maintenance."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The Redzone vendor overview video and weekend step 1 (sketching your site's minute bridge if the dashboard doesn't show it) can wait."}}},{"week":27,"phase":4,"title":"Tableau 1, getting started with Tableau Public","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-27-tableau-basics.md","ticks":["Tableau Public installed, both CSVs connected with correct types","Can explain dimension/measure and discrete/continuous","Line, bar and table views built with Color, Label and filters","H1 2026 reason Pareto matches Week 26","Three Phase 3 visuals rebuilt in Tableau, with notes on the differences"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 3 May – Sun 9 May 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nYour production data lives in Tableau, so you need to be fluent in it, at least as a power user who can read, filter, download and rebuild views. You've already done Power BI, and the ideas transfer: a data model, measures, visuals, filters. Tableau's vocabulary and drag-and-drop logic are different, though. This week covers the basics.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- install Tableau Public and connect to CSV files\n- explain dimensions vs measures, and discrete (blue) vs continuous (green) fields\n- build bar, line and table views with the Marks card\n- use filters, sorting and *Show Me*\n\n**Install:** **Tableau Public** (free) from [public.tableau.com](https://public.tableau.com), then *Create → Download the app*. Its one limitation: you can only **save workbooks to your public Tableau Public profile**, not to your PC. That's fine for this course's synthetic data, but **never open work data in Tableau Public.**\n\n> **Tableau for Students:** if your HNC college counts as an accredited institution, you may qualify for a free Tableau Desktop licence, which saves locally. Search *\"Tableau for Students\"* and check eligibility.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-connect-and-first-views","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Connect and first views","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Open Tableau Public → **Connect → Text file** → `data/production/shifts.csv`.\n2. On the **Data Source** page, check the field types: `production_date` should be a **date** (calendar icon), and the counts should be **numbers** (#). Fix any that aren't by clicking the icon.\n3. Click **Sheet 1** at the bottom. Look at the Data pane: fields above the line are **dimensions** (things you slice by: shift, crew, SKU, date), and fields below are **measures** (things you add up: counts, minutes).\n4. **First view**: drag `SKU` to **Rows**, and `Good Count` to **Columns**. Tableau sums it automatically (`SUM(Good Count)`). Click the sort icon on the axis.\n5. **Over time**: new sheet. Drag `Production Date` to Columns. It shows as `YEAR(Production Date)`. Click the `+` on the pill to drill to quarter, then month. Drag `Downtime Min` to Rows. That's a line chart of downtime by month.\n6. Right-click the date pill and switch between **Month (discrete, blue)** and **Month (continuous, green)**. Watch the axis change from separate headers to a continuous timeline.\n\n### Why it works\n\n| | Blue (discrete) | Green (continuous) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Creates | headers / labels | an axis |\n| Typical | categories, date *parts* (May) | numbers, date *values* (May 2026) |\n\n**Every pill on Rows or Columns is aggregated** unless it's a dimension. Tableau decides \"SUM of what, grouped by what\" from what's on the shelves, much like a PivotTable. That's where the aggregation trap from Week 25 bites: putting a pre-calculated OEE % column on a shelf gives `SUM(OEE)` or `AVG(OEE)`, both wrong. Next week you'll build OEE as a proper calculated field.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-the-marks-card-filters-and-sho","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): The Marks card, filters and Show Me","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Marks card**: on the downtime-by-month line, drag `Shift` to **Color**. Now you have two lines, Days vs Nights. Drag `SUM(Downtime Min)` to **Label**.\n2. **Bar chart by crew and SKU**: `Crew` on Columns, `SKU` on Color, `Downtime Min` on Rows. Then try `SKU` on Columns too (nested headers).\n3. **Filters**: drag `Production Date` to the **Filters** shelf → *Range of dates* → 1 Jan – 30 Jun 2026. Right-click the filter → **Show Filter**, and a slider appears for the user.\n4. **Show Me** (top right): select `Production Date` and `Good Count`, then click different chart types to see what Tableau suggests and which fields each needs.\n5. **Connect the downtime log too**: Data → New Data Source → `redzone_downtime.csv`. Build a horizontal bar of `SUM(Duration Min)` by `Reason Category`, sorted, filtered to H1 2026. Check it against Week 26: Mechanical **11,358**, Operational **11,050**.\n6. **Save** to your Tableau Public profile as *Line D OEE Practice*. You can hide it from your public profile in its settings if you'd rather.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Do Tableau's free **official training videos**: search *\"Tableau free training videos getting started\"* and work through *Getting Started*, *Connecting to Data* and *Visual Analytics → Filtering and Sorting*.\n2. Rebuild **three** of your Power BI visuals from Phase 3 in Tableau, using `shifts.csv` and `redzone_downtime.csv`: a KPI-style big number, a bar chart sorted worst-first, and a monthly trend. Write down three things that are easier in Tableau and three that are easier in Power BI.\n3. **At work (view only):** open one Redzone dashboard and, for each chart, write down which fields are on Rows, Columns, Color and Filters. You'll start \"reading\" dashboards as structures, not pictures.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Tableau's official free training** (above). It's short and good.\n- **Andy Kriebel** (YouTube): *\"Andy Kriebel Tableau tip\"*. Hundreds of short, practical tips.\n- **Tableau Tim** (YouTube): *\"Tableau Tim beginner tutorial\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between a blue `MONTH(Date)` and a green `MONTH(Date)` pill?\n2. Why must you never open work data in Tableau Public?\n3. You drag a pre-calculated `OEE` column onto Rows and Tableau shows `SUM(OEE)` = 1,025. What's happened?\n4. In Power BI terms, what's the closest equivalent of a Tableau measure on Rows?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Blue is discrete. It treats each month as a separate header and groups by month name (or month-year, depending on the option). Green is continuous. It plots dates on a timeline axis.\n2. Tableau Public saves everything to a **public** website. Anything you save is published and searchable. Use it only for synthetic or public data.\n3. Tableau summed the per-shift OEE fractions across about 1,460 shifts. That's meaningless. OEE has to be calculated from summed minutes and counts (next week).\n4. An implicit measure, such as dragging a numeric column into Values (Sum). The DAX equivalent of a deliberate calculation is a Tableau **calculated field** with an aggregate inside it, like `SUM([Good Count]) / SUM([...])`.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (connect `shifts.csv` with correct types, first bar and line, blue against green dates), Session 2 steps 1, 3, 5 and 6 (Color and Label, a filter shown to the user, the reason category bar matching Week 26, saved to your profile), and weekend step 2, three Phase 3 visuals rebuilt with your notes.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch Tableau's free *Getting Started* training videos and a few Andy Kriebel tips, read the blue versus green table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, view one Redzone dashboard and note what's on Rows, Columns, Color and Filters for each chart.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 steps 2 and 4 (the nested crew and SKU bar, clicking through Show Me), the Tableau Tim tutorial and the *Filtering and Sorting* training module can be skipped."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Tableau Public installed, both CSVs connected with correct types\n- [ ] Can explain dimension/measure and discrete/continuous\n- [ ] Line, bar and table views built with Color, Label and filters\n- [ ] H1 2026 reason Pareto matches Week 26\n- [ ] Three Phase 3 visuals rebuilt in Tableau, with notes on the differences"}],"quiz":[{"id":"27-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between a blue `MONTH(Date)` and a green `MONTH(Date)` pill?","a":"Blue is discrete. It treats each month as a separate header and groups by month name (or month-year, depending on the option). Green is continuous. It plots dates on a timeline axis."},{"id":"27-2","n":2,"q":"Why must you never open work data in Tableau Public?","a":"Tableau Public saves everything to a **public** website. Anything you save is published and searchable. Use it only for synthetic or public data."},{"id":"27-3","n":3,"q":"You drag a pre-calculated `OEE` column onto Rows and Tableau shows `SUM(OEE)` = 1,025. What's happened?","a":"Tableau summed the per-shift OEE fractions across about 1,460 shifts. That's meaningless. OEE has to be calculated from summed minutes and counts (next week)."},{"id":"27-4","n":4,"q":"In Power BI terms, what's the closest equivalent of a Tableau measure on Rows?","a":"An implicit measure, such as dragging a numeric column into Values (Sum). The DAX equivalent of a deliberate calculation is a Tableau **calculated field** with an aggregate inside it, like `SUM([Good Count]) / SUM([...])`."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Tableau's official free training** (above). It's short and good.","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Andy Kriebel** (YouTube): *\"Andy Kriebel Tableau tip\"*. Hundreds of short, practical tips.","searches":["Andy Kriebel Tableau tip"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**Tableau Tim** (YouTube): *\"Tableau Tim beginner tutorial\"*.","searches":["Tableau Tim beginner tutorial"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (connect `shifts.csv` with correct types, first bar and line, blue against green dates), Session 2 steps 1, 3, 5 and 6 (Color and Label, a filter shown to the user, the reason category bar matching Week 26, saved to your profile), and weekend step 2, three Phase 3 visuals rebuilt with your notes."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch Tableau's free *Getting Started* training videos and a few Andy Kriebel tips, read the blue versus green table in \"Why it works\", and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, view one Redzone dashboard and note what's on Rows, Columns, Color and Filters for each chart."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 steps 2 and 4 (the nested crew and SKU bar, clicking through Show Me), the Tableau Tim tutorial and the *Filtering and Sorting* training module can be skipped."}}},{"week":28,"phase":4,"title":"Tableau 2, calculated fields, LOD expressions and table calculations","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-28-tableau-calculations-lod.md","ticks":["A, P, Q and OEE calculated fields, correct at every level (71.9 % H1 2026)","FIXED LOD for shift OEE; 50 shifts under 60 % found","Bins and histogram of shift OEE","Dual-axis Pareto with a running % of total","Moving average, with *Compute Using* understood"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 10 May – Sun 16 May 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is Tableau's \"DAX week\". You'll build OEE as a correct calculated field, learn **Level of Detail (LOD)** expressions (Tableau's answer to \"calculate at a different grain\"), and use **table calculations** for running totals, Paretos and moving averages.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- write row-level and aggregate calculated fields, and know the difference\n- calculate OEE, A, P and Q correctly at any level of the view\n- use `{FIXED …}` LOD expressions to calculate per shift, then summarise\n- use table calculations: running total, percent of total and moving average","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-oee-as-calculated-fields","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): OEE as calculated fields","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nUse `shifts.csv`. Analysis → **Create Calculated Field** for each of these:\n\n```\n// Availability\nSUM([Run Min]) / SUM([Planned Production Min])\n\n// Performance\nSUM([Total Count]) / SUM([Run Min] * [Ideal Rate Ppm])\n\n// Quality\nSUM([Good Count]) / SUM([Total Count])\n\n// OEE  (fully productive time ÷ planned time)\nSUM([Good Count] / [Ideal Rate Ppm]) / SUM([Planned Production Min])\n```\n\nFormat each one as a percentage (right-click → Default Properties → Number Format).\n\n1. Put `OEE` on Text with no dimensions. You should see **70.3 %** (all data). Filter to H1 2026 and it should read **71.9 %**.\n2. Drag `Shift` to Rows: Days 71.8 %, Nights 72.0 % (H1 2026). Then `SKU`, then `MONTH(Production Date)`: Jan 72.2 %, Feb 71.6 %, Mar 73.6 %, Apr 71.8 %, May 70.3 %, Jun 71.8 %.\n3. Because every calculation aggregates **inside** (SUM … / SUM …), the answer is right at **any** level: total, month, shift, crew. That's exactly the Week 25 rule, built into the field.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- `[Ideal Rate Ppm] * [Run Min]` with no aggregate is **row-level**: it's calculated per shift row, then you `SUM` it.\n- `SUM(...)/SUM(...)` is an **aggregate** calculation: it's calculated per mark in the view.\n- You can't mix them unaggregated: `SUM([Good Count]) / [Planned Production Min]` gives *\"cannot mix aggregate and non-aggregate\"*. Tableau is protecting you from the averaging trap.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-lod-expressions","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): LOD expressions","kind":"hands","md":"**Question:** *how many H1 2026 shifts had OEE below 60 %?* You need OEE **per shift**, and then a **count** of shifts. That's two levels of detail.\n\n```\n// Shift OEE: fixed at the shift level, whatever is in the view\n{ FIXED [Shift Id] : SUM([Good Count] / [Ideal Rate Ppm]) / SUM([Planned Production Min]) }\n\n// Low-OEE shift flag\nIF { FIXED [Shift Id] : SUM([Good Count] / [Ideal Rate Ppm]) / SUM([Planned Production Min]) } < 0.6\nTHEN 1 ELSE 0 END\n```\n\n1. Put `SUM(Low-OEE shift flag)` on Text, filtered to H1 2026: **50 of 362 shifts** were below 60 %.\n2. Create **bins** on the Shift OEE field (right-click → Create → Bins, size 0.1) and make a histogram. In H1 2026: 27 shifts below 50 %, 23 at 50–60 %, 59 at 60–70 %, 142 at 70–80 % and 111 above 80 %.\n3. Drag `Shift` to Color. Are low shifts more often Nights? (They aren't, here.)\n\n### The three LODs\n\n| Expression | Calculates at | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{FIXED [dim] : agg}` | exactly that dimension, ignoring the view (but not context filters) | per-shift or per-asset values, then count or bin them |\n| `{INCLUDE [dim] : agg}` | the view's level **plus** that dimension | average of per-shift values by month |\n| `{EXCLUDE [dim] : agg}` | the view's level **minus** that dimension | each zone vs the line total |\n\n**Filter gotcha:** normal dimension filters are applied **after** `FIXED`. If your FIXED calculation ignores a filter you expected it to respect, right-click the filter → **Add to Context**. This is Tableau's order of operations, which is worth searching for and bookmarking.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-table-calculations-a-pareto-and","title":"Weekend (2 h): Table calculations, a Pareto and a moving average","kind":"hands","md":"Using `redzone_downtime.csv`, filtered to H1 2026 and to reason category Mechanical or Electrical:\n\n1. **Pareto by reason**: `Reason` on Columns (sorted by `SUM(Duration Min)` descending), `SUM(Duration Min)` on Rows as bars.\n2. Drag `SUM(Duration Min)` onto Rows again. Right-click it → **Quick Table Calculation → Running Total**. Then **Edit Table Calculation** → tick *Add secondary calculation* → **Percent of Total**. That's your cumulative %.\n3. Right-click the second pill → **Dual Axis**, set its mark to Line, then right-click the right axis → *Edit axis* → 0 to 1 (100 %).\n4. **Moving average**: back on `shifts.csv`, plot `OEE` by **week** (continuous), then add a Quick Table Calculation **Moving Average** (edit: previous 3 values plus the current one). The weekly line is noisy, and the 4-week average shows the trend.\n5. **\"Compute using\"**: change it from *Table (across)* to *Pane*, and see what happens with Shift on Color. Table calculations always depend on *what they compute along*. That's the source of most Tableau confusion, so practise it.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Tableau free training: *Calculations → Getting Started with Calculations*, *LOD Expressions* and *Table Calculations*.\n- Search *\"Tableau LOD expressions FIXED INCLUDE EXCLUDE explained\"*.\n- Search *\"Tableau order of operations\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does `AVG([OEE column])` give a different answer from the OEE calculated field?\n2. Write a calculated field for **Speed loss (min)**.\n3. When would you use `FIXED` instead of a table calculation?\n4. Your running-total % restarts at 0 halfway along the chart. What's wrong?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. AVG weights every shift equally, whatever its planned time. The calculated field sums minutes and counts first, so a shift's influence matches its size (Week 25).\n2. `SUM([Run Min] - [Total Count] / [Ideal Rate Ppm])`. In H1 2026 that's **28,989 minutes**.\n3. Use FIXED when you need a value at a specific grain that you'll then filter, count or bin (per-shift OEE, per-asset failures). It's computed in the data. Use a table calculation when the result depends on the **order or layout of the view**: running totals, rank, % of total and moving averages.\n4. The table calculation's *Compute Using* partitions the data, for example by a dimension on Color or Rows. Change it to compute along the whole table, or along the dimension you intend.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's four calculated fields (70.3 % all data, 71.9 % H1 2026) checked by shift and month, Session 2 steps 1 and 2 (the FIXED shift OEE, 50 of 362 shifts below 60 %, and the binned histogram), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for the dual-axis Pareto and the 4-week moving average.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Tableau training modules on calculations, LOD expressions and table calculations, read the three LODs table and the filter gotcha, look up \"Tableau order of operations\", and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 step 3 (Shift on Color in the histogram) and weekend step 5 (the *Compute Using* experiment, though do read Check yourself question 4) can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] A, P, Q and OEE calculated fields, correct at every level (71.9 % H1 2026)\n- [ ] FIXED LOD for shift OEE; 50 shifts under 60 % found\n- [ ] Bins and histogram of shift OEE\n- [ ] Dual-axis Pareto with a running % of total\n- [ ] Moving average, with *Compute Using* understood"}],"quiz":[{"id":"28-1","n":1,"q":"Why does `AVG([OEE column])` give a different answer from the OEE calculated field?","a":"AVG weights every shift equally, whatever its planned time. The calculated field sums minutes and counts first, so a shift's influence matches its size (Week 25)."},{"id":"28-2","n":2,"q":"Write a calculated field for **Speed loss (min)**.","a":"`SUM([Run Min] - [Total Count] / [Ideal Rate Ppm])`. In H1 2026 that's **28,989 minutes**."},{"id":"28-3","n":3,"q":"When would you use `FIXED` instead of a table calculation?","a":"Use FIXED when you need a value at a specific grain that you'll then filter, count or bin (per-shift OEE, per-asset failures). It's computed in the data. Use a table calculation when the result depends on the **order or layout of the view**: running totals, rank, % of total and moving averages."},{"id":"28-4","n":4,"q":"Your running-total % restarts at 0 halfway along the chart. What's wrong?","a":"The table calculation's *Compute Using* partitions the data, for example by a dimension on Color or Rows. Change it to compute along the whole table, or along the dimension you intend."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Tableau free training: *Calculations → Getting Started with Calculations*, *LOD Expressions* and *Table Calculations*.","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"Tableau LOD expressions FIXED INCLUDE EXCLUDE explained\"*.","searches":["Tableau LOD expressions FIXED INCLUDE EXCLUDE explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"Tableau order of operations\"*.","searches":["Tableau order of operations"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's four calculated fields (70.3 % all data, 71.9 % H1 2026) checked by shift and month, Session 2 steps 1 and 2 (the FIXED shift OEE, 50 of 362 shifts below 60 %, and the binned histogram), and weekend steps 1 to 4 for the dual-axis Pareto and the 4-week moving average."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Tableau training modules on calculations, LOD expressions and table calculations, read the three LODs table and the filter gotcha, look up \"Tableau order of operations\", and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 step 3 (Shift on Color in the histogram) and weekend step 5 (the *Compute Using* experiment, though do read Check yourself question 4) can wait."}}},{"week":29,"phase":4,"title":"Tableau 3, dashboards, and getting data *out* of Tableau","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-29-tableau-dashboards-downloads.md","ticks":["Dashboard with KPI row, filter action, highlight action and target parameter","Can explain each Tableau download option and its traps","Crosstab cleaned in Power Query: total row removed, types fixed, partial week flagged","Downloaded total (71.9 %) matches my own calculation","Asked about (or done) a first real, permitted download"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 17 May – Sun 23 May 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nTwo skills this week. First, assembling sheets into an **interactive dashboard** with actions and parameters. Second, the one you'll use at work straight away: **downloading data from a Tableau dashboard and turning it into something you can analyse**, because what comes out is rarely analysis-ready.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build a dashboard with layout containers, filter actions and highlight actions\n- use a **parameter** to let users switch measures or set a target\n- explain the download options on Tableau Server/Cloud, and what each one really gives you\n- clean a Tableau crosstab download in Power Query","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-build-a-dashboard","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Build a dashboard","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\nUsing your Week 27–28 sheets (OEE by month, OEE by shift, the reason Pareto, the shift-OEE histogram):\n\n1. **Dashboard → New Dashboard**. Size: *Automatic*, or fixed 1366 × 768.\n2. Drag in a **Horizontal** container for a KPI row: four small sheets each showing one number (OEE, A, P, Q) as large text.\n3. Below it, OEE by month (left, wide) and the Pareto (right).\n4. **Filter action**: Dashboard → Actions → Add Action → *Filter*. Source = *OEE by month*, Target = *Pareto*, run on **Select**. Click a month, and the Pareto shows that month's reasons.\n5. **Highlight action** on Shift, so hovering over Days highlights Days everywhere.\n6. **Parameter: target OEE.** Create Parameter → *Target OEE*, float, current value 0.75 → Show Parameter. Then add a calculated field `IF [OEE] >= [Target OEE] THEN \"On target\" ELSE \"Below\" END`, put it on Color in the monthly chart, and add a **reference line** at the parameter value.\n7. Titles that state findings, consistent colours (red only for \"bad\"), and no unnecessary gridlines. The Phase 3 design rules all apply.\n8. Save to Tableau Public.\n\n### Why it works\n\nActions turn separate charts into one tool: *select here → filter there*. Parameters are user inputs that any calculation can use, much like Power BI's what-if parameters and field parameters.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-getting-data-out-of-tableau-at","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Getting data out of Tableau at work","kind":"hands","md":"On Tableau Server or Tableau Cloud, the **Download** button (top toolbar of a view) typically offers the options below. Which ones you see depends on the permissions the site admin has given you.\n\n| Option | What you get | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Image / PDF / PowerPoint** | a picture | no data |\n| **Data** | the *summary* data behind one sheet, and sometimes the *full* (underlying) rows | full data may be restricted; summary is already aggregated |\n| **Crosstab** | the sheet as a table (CSV or Excel), exactly as laid out | **pre-aggregated, formatted text** (`72.5%`, `1,304`), filters already applied, sometimes a **Grand Total** row |\n| **Tableau Workbook** | the .twb/.twbx | often disabled for viewers |\n\n**Golden rules for downloads:**\n\n1. Note **which filters were applied** when you downloaded. Write them in the file name, e.g. `OEE_weekly_LineD_2026H1_allshifts.csv`.\n2. Prefer the **lowest grain** you can get (shift or event rows, not weekly percentages), so you can re-aggregate correctly yourself.\n3. **Never average downloaded percentages.** If you only have percentages, ask for the underlying minutes and counts.\n4. Treat downloads as **company data**: keep them on work systems and follow the data policy.\n\n### Do it: clean a realistic crosstab download\n\n`data/production/tableau_crosstab_weekly_oee.csv` is shaped like a weekly OEE crosstab download for H1 2026. Open it in Notepad first and look at it:\n\n- dates as text: `5 January 2026`\n- percentages as text: `73.8%`\n- numbers with thousands commas, in quotes: `\"1,304\"`\n- a **Grand Total** row at the bottom\n- the last row before the total, **29 June 2026**, is a **partial week** (just 2 days: 525 minutes of downtime)\n\nIn Excel → **Power Query** (Data → From Text/CSV → Transform):\n\n1. Filter out `Grand Total` (Text Filters → does not equal).\n2. `Week of Production Date` → Change Type → **Using Locale** → Date, English (UK).\n3. For the % columns: Replace Values `%` → nothing, change the type to Decimal, then Transform → Standard → **Divide by 100**.\n4. `Downtime (min)` and `Good Packs`: Replace `,` → nothing, then change to Whole Number.\n5. Add a column `Partial week` that flags the last week. (Hint: weeks with much lower downtime and good packs. Better, compare with the number of shifts in `shifts.csv` for that week.)\n6. Load it and chart weekly OEE. Check that the Grand Total row you removed said **71.9 %**. That matches your own calculation from raw shifts, which proves the download and your method agree.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-your-first-real-download-with-pe","title":"Weekend (2 h): Your first real download (with permission)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Check your site's data policy, or ask your manager: *\"Can I download the weekly OEE and downtime data for Line D to analyse in Excel on my work laptop?\"*\n2. If yes: download the **lowest-grain** view you can find (shift-level or event-level if available), with a clear file name, and keep it on your work PC/OneDrive. Build the Week 25 minute bridge from it. Does your OEE match the dashboard's? If not, which definition differs (planned stops, target rates, short stops)?\n3. If not yet: repeat the Power Query cleaning on the synthetic crosstab until you can do it in under 10 minutes, and write your request to the CI/production lead for event-level access.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Tableau free training: *Dashboards and Stories → Getting Started with Dashboards*, *Dashboard Interactivity Using Actions*.\n- Search *\"Tableau download crosstab vs data\"* for a short explainer of the download options.\n- Search *\"Tableau parameters tutorial\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. You download a weekly crosstab with OEE % per week and average the 26 weeks. What two problems might you have?\n2. What's the difference between *Download → Data* and *Download → Crosstab*?\n3. Why put the filter settings in the file name?\n4. When would you use a highlight action instead of a filter action?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. (a) Averaging percentages gives equal weight to unequal weeks, and the partial final week (2 days) counts as a whole week. (b) The Grand Total row might be included in the average. Use the underlying minutes and counts instead, and exclude the total row.\n2. *Data* gives the data behind the marks: summary values, or with permission the underlying rows, as a clean table. *Crosstab* reproduces the visible table layout, often with formatted text values and totals. It's convenient for reading, but awkward for analysis.\n3. A downloaded file has no memory of the filters that produced it. Months later, nobody (including you) will know whether it was one line or all lines, or all shifts or Days only. Getting that wrong produces confidently wrong conclusions.\n4. Highlight when the user should see the selected item *in context* (all the other bars dimmed but visible). Filter when the other views should show *only* the selection, for example drilling from a month into that month's reasons.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 6 and 8 (KPI row, filter action, highlight action, the Target OEE parameter with its reference line, saved), and Session 2 steps 1 to 6 cleaning the crosstab in Power Query until your total matches the removed Grand Total of 71.9 %.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Tableau training modules on dashboards and actions, read the download options table and the four golden rules, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask your manager the weekend step 1 question about downloading Line D data.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 1 step 7 (finding-style titles and colour polish) and weekend step 3 (repeating the crosstab clean until it takes under 10 minutes) can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Dashboard with KPI row, filter action, highlight action and target parameter\n- [ ] Can explain each Tableau download option and its traps\n- [ ] Crosstab cleaned in Power Query: total row removed, types fixed, partial week flagged\n- [ ] Downloaded total (71.9 %) matches my own calculation\n- [ ] Asked about (or done) a first real, permitted download"}],"quiz":[{"id":"29-1","n":1,"q":"You download a weekly crosstab with OEE % per week and average the 26 weeks. What two problems might you have?","a":"(a) Averaging percentages gives equal weight to unequal weeks, and the partial final week (2 days) counts as a whole week. (b) The Grand Total row might be included in the average. Use the underlying minutes and counts instead, and exclude the total row."},{"id":"29-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between *Download → Data* and *Download → Crosstab*?","a":"*Data* gives the data behind the marks: summary values, or with permission the underlying rows, as a clean table. *Crosstab* reproduces the visible table layout, often with formatted text values and totals. It's convenient for reading, but awkward for analysis."},{"id":"29-3","n":3,"q":"Why put the filter settings in the file name?","a":"A downloaded file has no memory of the filters that produced it. Months later, nobody (including you) will know whether it was one line or all lines, or all shifts or Days only. Getting that wrong produces confidently wrong conclusions."},{"id":"29-4","n":4,"q":"When would you use a highlight action instead of a filter action?","a":"Highlight when the user should see the selected item *in context* (all the other bars dimmed but visible). Filter when the other views should show *only* the selection, for example drilling from a month into that month's reasons."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Tableau free training: *Dashboards and Stories → Getting Started with Dashboards*, *Dashboard Interactivity Using Actions*.","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"Tableau download crosstab vs data\"* for a short explainer of the download options.","searches":["Tableau download crosstab vs data"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"Tableau parameters tutorial\"*.","searches":["Tableau parameters tutorial"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 6 and 8 (KPI row, filter action, highlight action, the Target OEE parameter with its reference line, saved), and Session 2 steps 1 to 6 cleaning the crosstab in Power Query until your total matches the removed Grand Total of 71.9 %."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Tableau training modules on dashboards and actions, read the download options table and the four golden rules, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask your manager the weekend step 1 question about downloading Line D data."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 1 step 7 (finding-style titles and colour polish) and weekend step 3 (repeating the crosstab clean until it takes under 10 minutes) can wait."}}},{"week":30,"phase":4,"title":"Linking production downtime to maintenance work orders","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-30-link-production-maintenance.md","ticks":["Machine → asset mapping understood (one-to-one vs one-to-many)","Stops matched to WOs with a time window, chosen with evidence","Match rates in both directions, with the gaps explained","Maintenance share of lost time (~46 %) and top assets calculated","`matched_stops.csv` exported for Tableau"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 24 May – Sun 30 May 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is the most valuable week of the phase. Production sees stops in Redzone. Maintenance sees work orders in the CMMS. **Neither system knows about the other.** Joining them answers questions nobody can answer today:\n\n- *How much of our lost OEE is maintenance-related?*\n- *Which **assets** (not \"Cooker\") cause the downtime?*\n- *Which breakdowns cost production time, and which didn't stop the line?*\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- map production \"machines\" to CMMS assets, and handle one-to-many mapping\n- match stops to work orders with a **time window**, and choose the window with evidence\n- measure match rates in both directions, and explain the gaps\n- calculate the maintenance share of lost time, by asset\n\n**Data:** in `data/line_d.db`: `redzone_downtime`, `machine_asset_map`, `work_orders` and `assets`. Solutions are in [`solutions/phase-4-production/w30_link_production_maintenance.sql`](../../solutions/phase-4-production/w30_link_production_maintenance.sql).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-mapping-problem","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The mapping problem","kind":"hands","md":"Operators pick the machine they can see. The CMMS records the exact asset. Look at the map:\n\n```sql\nSELECT machine, COUNT(*) AS assets\nFROM machine_asset_map\nGROUP BY machine\nORDER BY assets DESC;\n```\n\n- **One to one**: `Wrapper 3` → `LD-PPK-003`. Easy.\n- **One to many**: `Cooker` → ~43 assets (motors, pumps, gearboxes, conveyors, sensors…). The stop tells you *roughly where*, and only the work order tells you *what*.\n- In real life the map is **something you build**, with production and engineering together. It's often the first deliverable of a project like this. Your site may also have different machine names on different lines.\n\n### Matching rule (first version)\n\nA stop and a work order belong together if:\n\n1. the WO's asset is one of the stop's machine's assets, **and**\n2. the WO is **Reactive**, **and**\n3. the WO was **raised between the start of the stop and 60 minutes after it**. The operator stops, tries to fix it, then calls maintenance, who raise the job.\n\n```sql\nWITH stops AS (\n    SELECT * FROM redzone_downtime\n    WHERE production_date BETWEEN '2026-01-01' AND '2026-06-30'\n      AND duration_min >= 20\n)\nSELECT s.event_id, s.machine, s.start_time, s.duration_min, s.reason,\n       w.wo_id, w.asset_id, w.failure_code,\n       ROUND((julianday(w.raised_datetime) - julianday(s.start_time)) * 1440) AS mins_to_wo\nFROM stops AS s\nJOIN machine_asset_map AS m ON m.machine = s.machine\nJOIN work_orders AS w\n  ON w.asset_id = m.asset_id\n AND w.wo_type = 'Reactive'\n AND w.raised_datetime BETWEEN s.start_time AND datetime(s.start_time, '+60 minutes')\nORDER BY s.start_time;\n```\n\nLook at a few rows. *\"Belt / chain off\"* on *Packing Conveyors* matches `LD-PPK-020` with *Mechanical - belt/chain*, raised 13 minutes after the stop started. The production event and the maintenance job are now one record.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-choose-the-window-with-evidenc","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Choose the window with evidence, and check both directions","kind":"hands","md":"### Match rate vs window\n\nFor maintenance-coded stops (Mechanical or Electrical, ≥ 20 min, H1 2026, **128 stops**):\n\n| Window after stop start | Stops matched |\n|---|---|\n| 60 min | 116 (91 %) |\n| 180 min | 128 (100 %) |\n\nWider finds more, but it also finds **wrong** matches. A short stop on \"Cooker\" can \"match\" an unrelated job on any of 43 assets that happened to be raised in the next hour. With a 60-minute window, **10 short stops (< 20 min) in H1 2026** match a WO, and most of those are probably coincidences. Look at the `mins_to_wo` distribution and choose a window (say 120 minutes) that catches the slow call-outs without inviting coincidences. Write down why.\n\n### By reason category (stops ≥ 20 min, 60-minute window)\n\n| Category | Stops | With WO | % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mechanical | 94 | 88 | 94 |\n| Electrical | 34 | 28 | 82 |\n| **Unassigned** | 21 | 21 | **100** |\n| Operational | 8 | 7 | 88 |\n| Changeover / Material / Quality / People | 126 | 0 | 0 |\n\nTwo great findings:\n\n- **Every long Unassigned stop has a breakdown work order.** Uncoded downtime is mostly maintenance. Linking the systems **recovers** the reason the operator didn't enter.\n- **7 \"Operational\" long stops have breakdown WOs.** They're breakdowns coded as jams, so the Redzone Pareto under-counts maintenance.\n\n### The other direction: reactive WOs with a production stop (H1 2026)\n\n| Criticality | Reactive WOs | With a stop | % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 73 | 66 | 90 |\n| B | 104 | 64 | 62 |\n| C | 59 | 17 | 29 |\n\nC-critical breakdowns mostly **don't stop the line**, and that's what criticality is supposed to mean. If an A-critical asset's breakdowns often *don't* stop the line, or a C asset's often *do*, your **criticality ranking is wrong**. That's direct, evidence-based input to the PM pillar.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-maintenance-share-of-lost-time-b","title":"Weekend (2 h): Maintenance share of lost time, by asset","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Maintenance share**: minutes of stops with a matched WO ÷ all downtime minutes. For H1 2026, that's **16,677 of 36,549 minutes = 45.6 %**. (Use your chosen window; the answer will move a little.)\n2. **By asset**: for matched stops, sum the stop minutes by the WO's `asset_id`, then join `assets`. That's the top-10 list *production* cares about, in *maintenance's* language. Compare it with your Phase 2 done-when list (based on CMMS downtime). Same assets? Why might they differ?\n3. **Vocabulary map**: count matched pairs of `reason` (operator words) and `failure_code` (maintenance code). \"Sensor fault\" ↔ *Electrical - sensor* is consistent. \"Jam\" ↔ *Operational - jam* raised as a breakdown WO is worth a conversation. Is that a jam or a failure?\n4. **Export** the matched-stops result (DB Browser: run the query, then the \"save results\" button → CSV) as `matched_stops.csv`. You'll use it in Tableau next week and in the Phase 4 build.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"joining production and maintenance data OEE CMMS\"* for case studies.\n- Re-watch your Week 11 and Week 13 notes on joins, `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` and window functions. They're all used here."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why match on a time *window* rather than an exact timestamp?\n2. What's the risk of a very wide window, and which machines are most exposed to it?\n3. 100 % of long Unassigned stops have a breakdown WO. What should you recommend?\n4. A-critical WOs have a production stop 90 % of the time. Give one reason for the other 10 %.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The two systems record different moments: the operator logs the stop start, and maintenance raises the job later (minutes or hours later). Clocks and habits differ too, so exact timestamps almost never line up.\n2. False matches: unrelated jobs raised during the window get linked to a stop. Grouped machines with many assets (\"Cooker\" ~43, \"Packing Conveyors\") are most exposed, because there are more candidate WOs. Use the shortest window that catches genuine late call-outs, and check matches on short stops.\n3. Two things. (a) In reporting, reclassify long Unassigned stops that have a breakdown WO as maintenance, and say so. (b) In the process, make reason entry quick at the time of the stop, or let maintenance's WO update the stop reason. The data shows uncoded stops are breakdowns, which are exactly the stops that matter.\n4. The breakdown happened when the line wasn't running (a weekend, or a planned stop), the asset has redundancy or a buffer, the WO was raised long after the stop (outside the window), or it was a minor failure that didn't stop production. Each one is worth checking.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's mapping query and first matching query, Session 2's window comparison with your chosen window and the reason written down, the match rates by reason category and by criticality, then weekend steps 1, 2 and 4: the maintenance share (about 45.6 %), the top assets and `matched_stops.csv` exported. Weeks 31 and 32 need that file.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read the Session 1 notes on one-to-many mapping, the \"two great findings\" and the criticality paragraph in Session 2, look back at your Week 11 and Week 13 notes on joins and window functions, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 3 (the reason to failure code vocabulary map) and the case study search can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Machine → asset mapping understood (one-to-one vs one-to-many)\n- [ ] Stops matched to WOs with a time window, chosen with evidence\n- [ ] Match rates in both directions, with the gaps explained\n- [ ] Maintenance share of lost time (~46 %) and top assets calculated\n- [ ] `matched_stops.csv` exported for Tableau"}],"quiz":[{"id":"30-1","n":1,"q":"Why match on a time *window* rather than an exact timestamp?","a":"The two systems record different moments: the operator logs the stop start, and maintenance raises the job later (minutes or hours later). Clocks and habits differ too, so exact timestamps almost never line up."},{"id":"30-2","n":2,"q":"What's the risk of a very wide window, and which machines are most exposed to it?","a":"False matches: unrelated jobs raised during the window get linked to a stop. Grouped machines with many assets (\"Cooker\" ~43, \"Packing Conveyors\") are most exposed, because there are more candidate WOs. Use the shortest window that catches genuine late call-outs, and check matches on short stops."},{"id":"30-3","n":3,"q":"100 % of long Unassigned stops have a breakdown WO. What should you recommend?","a":"Two things. (a) In reporting, reclassify long Unassigned stops that have a breakdown WO as maintenance, and say so. (b) In the process, make reason entry quick at the time of the stop, or let maintenance's WO update the stop reason. The data shows uncoded stops are breakdowns, which are exactly the stops that matter."},{"id":"30-4","n":4,"q":"A-critical WOs have a production stop 90 % of the time. Give one reason for the other 10 %.","a":"The breakdown happened when the line wasn't running (a weekend, or a planned stop), the asset has redundancy or a buffer, the WO was raised long after the stop (outside the window), or it was a minor failure that didn't stop production. Each one is worth checking."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"joining production and maintenance data OEE CMMS\"* for case studies.","searches":["joining production and maintenance data OEE CMMS"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Re-watch your Week 11 and Week 13 notes on joins, `LEFT JOIN … IS NULL` and window functions. They're all used here.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's mapping query and first matching query, Session 2's window comparison with your chosen window and the reason written down, the match rates by reason category and by criticality, then weekend steps 1, 2 and 4: the maintenance share (about 45.6 %), the top assets and `matched_stops.csv` exported. Weeks 31 and 32 need that file."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read the Session 1 notes on one-to-many mapping, the \"two great findings\" and the criticality paragraph in Session 2, look back at your Week 11 and Week 13 notes on joins and window functions, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 3 (the reason to failure code vocabulary map) and the case study search can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":31,"phase":4,"title":"The CMMS from an end user's side, Mainsaver to Ultimo","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-31-cmms-mainsaver-to-ultimo.md","ticks":["Can explain functional location vs equipment, and why history needs both","Close-out checklist written (6 lines)","Migration checks run: 1 duplicate, 3 unmapped, 1 merge, 20 unmapped codes","KPI restated on new equipment IDs, with totals reconciled","Questions asked of the Ultimo project team, answers noted"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 31 May – Sun 6 Jun 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nEvery analysis in this course depends on what technicians type into the CMMS. Your site is moving from **Mainsaver** to **Ultimo**. As an **end user**, you'll be one of the people whose job close-outs become everyone else's data. This week is about doing that well, understanding what a migration does to history, and spotting migration problems before they bite your reports.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain the asset hierarchy (functional location vs equipment) and why it matters for history\n- describe what a \"good\" work order close-out contains, and why each field matters\n- run migration checks: unmapped, duplicated and merged assets, and failure-code mapping\n- keep KPIs continuous across a system change\n\n> **About terminology:** EAM systems use different words for the same ideas. Ultimo, for example, talks about *jobs* (work orders), *process functions* (functional locations, the positions in the plant) and *equipment* (the physical items). Your site's Ultimo training is the authority on the exact terms and screens. The concepts below are what matter.\n\n**Data:** `data/cmms_migration/` (also loaded into `data/line_d.db` as tables of the same names), a made-up example of migration files, shaped like a real project:\n\n- `ultimo_equipment.csv`: new equipment records with new IDs (`EQ-nnnnn`) and a process-function path\n- `asset_id_map.csv`: legacy asset ID → new equipment ID\n- `failure_code_map.csv`: legacy failure codes → a structured *object part / damage / cause* coding (in the style of the ISO 14224 standard)","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-hierarchy-and-good-close-outs","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Hierarchy and good close-outs","kind":"hands","md":"### Functional location vs equipment\n\n- A **functional location / process function** is a *position* in the plant: \"Line D › Primary Packing › Wrapper 3 position\". It never moves.\n- **Equipment** is the *physical thing* installed there: \"flow wrapper, serial 12345\". It can be removed, overhauled and reinstalled somewhere else.\n\nWhy you care: if a gearbox is swapped from position A to position B, do its failures follow the **gearbox** (equipment history: is this unit unreliable?) or stay with the **position** (location history: is this duty hard on gearboxes?). Good reliability analysis needs **both**, which is why the hierarchy is set up carefully during migration.\n\n### A good reactive job close-out\n\n| Field | Why it matters downstream |\n|---|---|\n| Correct **asset / equipment** (not the parent \"Line D\") | asset-level MTBF, Weibull, Paretos (Phases 2, 6) |\n| **Failure coding**: what part, what damage, what cause | Weibull per failure mode (Week 48); mixing modes ruins β |\n| **Actual start and finish times** | MTTR; linking to production stops (Week 30) |\n| **Did it stop production?** (Y/N) | criticality checks, maintenance share of OEE loss |\n| **Labour hours and parts used** | cost per failure; the C_CM in your optimal interval (Week 49) |\n| **Plain-English description** (\"bearing on drive end failed, replaced, shaft OK\") | the root-cause story; future technicians |\n\n### Do it\n\nUsing `data/dirty/wo_export_q1_2026_dirty.csv` and your Phase 1 skills, count the jobs that fail a \"good close-out\" check: blank completion, labour not numeric, reactive with no failure code. Write a 6-line **close-out checklist** you'd want every technician to follow in Ultimo.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-migration-checks","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Migration checks","kind":"hands","md":"The legacy history (all of `work_orders`) is keyed on legacy asset IDs (`LD-PPK-005`). After cutover, new jobs use new IDs (`EQ-…`). To keep **one continuous history**, every legacy ID must map cleanly to a new one.\n\n### Do it (SQL in DB Browser, or Power Query if you prefer)\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Duplicate rows in the map\nSELECT legacy_asset_id, equipment_id, COUNT(*) FROM asset_id_map\nGROUP BY legacy_asset_id, equipment_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n\n-- 2. Legacy assets with no new ID\nSELECT a.asset_id FROM assets a\nLEFT JOIN asset_id_map m ON m.legacy_asset_id = a.asset_id\nWHERE m.legacy_asset_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 3. New equipment that several legacy assets were merged into\nSELECT equipment_id, GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT legacy_asset_id) AS legacy_ids, COUNT(DISTINCT legacy_asset_id) AS n\nFROM asset_id_map GROUP BY equipment_id HAVING n > 1;\n\n-- 4. History at risk: legacy work orders on unmapped assets\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM work_orders w\nLEFT JOIN asset_id_map m ON m.legacy_asset_id = w.asset_id\nWHERE m.legacy_asset_id IS NULL;\n```\n\nFull answers are in [`solutions/phase-4-production/w31_migration_checks.sql`](../../solutions/phase-4-production/w31_migration_checks.sql).\n\n**Expected findings:**\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Map rows / new equipment records | 290 / 291 |\n| Duplicate map rows | **1** (LD-COK-011). It duplicates that asset's 32 jobs: a naive LEFT join turns 11,902 work orders into **11,934**, and an inner join gives 11,911, because the 23 orphaned jobs below also silently drop out. |\n| Legacy assets with no new ID | **3**: LD-COK-036, LD-DRY-032, LD-FLK-041 (sensors). Their **23** historical WOs would be orphaned. |\n| Merged equipment | **EQ-44667** ← LD-FRM-031 + LD-FRM-032. Two cylinders' histories are now combined, so their MTBF halves overnight. |\n| Failure codes | **28** legacy codes, only **8** mapped to the new structure. 20 still need a decision. |\n\n### Why it matters\n\nEach of these silently breaks a KPI after go-live: inflated counts (duplicates), lost history (unmapped), and fake reliability changes (merges). As an end user you can't fix the migration, but **you can spot these early and tell the project team, with evidence**. That's a very good way to get noticed.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-kpis-across-the-cutover","title":"Weekend (2 h): KPIs across the cutover","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Build the bridge table** that every post-cutover report needs: `legacy_asset_id → equipment_id`, de-duplicated, with the 3 gaps listed for the project team.\n2. **Restate a KPI on the new IDs**: reactive breakdowns per **equipment_id** over the two years of legacy history, using a de-duplicated map (`SELECT DISTINCT`). Reconcile: **1,016** of the 1,027 legacy breakdowns carry across, and the missing **11** are on the 3 unmapped sensors. The top equipment is the old LD-PPK-005, Flow wrapper 5, with 37.\n3. **Complete the failure-code map** for 5 more codes (e.g. *Mechanical - grippers* → Grippers / Wear / Normal wear). Notice how much judgement it takes. That's why failure coding should be decided once, centrally.\n4. **At work:** ask the Ultimo project lead or your supervisor:\n   - Will legacy work order history be migrated, or archived?\n   - Will failure codes be structured (part / damage / cause), and are they mandatory on close-out?\n   - Can end users export job lists to Excel, and which fields?\n   - Is there a plan for linking jobs to production stops (Redzone)?\n\n   Write down the answers. They decide what analysis will be possible after go-live.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"functional location vs equipment SAP PM explained\"*. It's the same concept in another EAM, and these videos explain it clearly.\n- Search *\"ISO 14224 failure mode taxonomy\"* for the standard behind structured failure coding.\n- Search *\"IFS Ultimo\"* for the vendor's own overview, so you know the product your site is moving to."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. A gearbox is swapped between two positions. Where should its failures be recorded, and why both?\n2. Why does a duplicate row in a mapping table inflate KPIs?\n3. Two legacy assets are merged into one new record. What happens to its MTBF after migration, and is the asset really less reliable?\n4. Why are structured failure codes better than free text for reliability analysis?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Against the **equipment** (that unit's history: is it a bad gearbox?) **and** the **functional location** (that position's history: is it a hard duty?). Recording both lets you tell a bad unit from a bad application.\n2. A join to a duplicated key matches every work order on that asset twice (fan-out, Week 11). Counts, hours and costs for that asset double, and the line totals rise with them.\n3. Its failure count roughly doubles in the same time window, so its MTBF roughly halves. **Nothing physical changed.** It's a data artefact, and anyone trending MTBF across the cutover would wrongly see a deterioration.\n4. You can count and group them reliably, which is essential for Weibull per failure mode, Paretos by mode, and FMEA occurrence scores. Free text varies with every writer (\"brg\", \"bearing gone\", \"noisy DE\"), so it can't be grouped without a lot of cleaning.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** The Session 1 count of jobs failing the close-out check on the dirty Q1 file, the four Session 2 migration checks (1 duplicate, 3 unmapped, 1 merge, 20 unmapped codes), and weekend steps 1 and 2: the de-duplicated bridge table and breakdowns restated on `equipment_id`, reconciled to 1,016 plus 11.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read the functional location versus equipment section and the close-out table, watch a \"functional location vs equipment\" video, and write your 6-line close-out checklist on paper. At work, ask the Ultimo project lead or your supervisor the four weekend step 4 questions and note the answers.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 3 (mapping 5 more failure codes) and the ISO 14224 and IFS Ultimo searches can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Can explain functional location vs equipment, and why history needs both\n- [ ] Close-out checklist written (6 lines)\n- [ ] Migration checks run: 1 duplicate, 3 unmapped, 1 merge, 20 unmapped codes\n- [ ] KPI restated on new equipment IDs, with totals reconciled\n- [ ] Questions asked of the Ultimo project team, answers noted"}],"quiz":[{"id":"31-1","n":1,"q":"A gearbox is swapped between two positions. Where should its failures be recorded, and why both?","a":"Against the **equipment** (that unit's history: is it a bad gearbox?) **and** the **functional location** (that position's history: is it a hard duty?). Recording both lets you tell a bad unit from a bad application."},{"id":"31-2","n":2,"q":"Why does a duplicate row in a mapping table inflate KPIs?","a":"A join to a duplicated key matches every work order on that asset twice (fan-out, Week 11). Counts, hours and costs for that asset double, and the line totals rise with them."},{"id":"31-3","n":3,"q":"Two legacy assets are merged into one new record. What happens to its MTBF after migration, and is the asset really less reliable?","a":"Its failure count roughly doubles in the same time window, so its MTBF roughly halves. **Nothing physical changed.** It's a data artefact, and anyone trending MTBF across the cutover would wrongly see a deterioration."},{"id":"31-4","n":4,"q":"Why are structured failure codes better than free text for reliability analysis?","a":"You can count and group them reliably, which is essential for Weibull per failure mode, Paretos by mode, and FMEA occurrence scores. Free text varies with every writer (\"brg\", \"bearing gone\", \"noisy DE\"), so it can't be grouped without a lot of cleaning."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"functional location vs equipment SAP PM explained\"*. It's the same concept in another EAM, and these videos explain it clearly.","searches":["functional location vs equipment SAP PM explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"ISO 14224 failure mode taxonomy\"* for the standard behind structured failure coding.","searches":["ISO 14224 failure mode taxonomy"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"IFS Ultimo\"* for the vendor's own overview, so you know the product your site is moving to.","searches":["IFS Ultimo"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"The Session 1 count of jobs failing the close-out check on the dirty Q1 file, the four Session 2 migration checks (1 duplicate, 3 unmapped, 1 merge, 20 unmapped codes), and weekend steps 1 and 2: the de-duplicated bridge table and breakdowns restated on `equipment_id`, reconciled to 1,016 plus 11."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read the functional location versus equipment section and the close-out table, watch a \"functional location vs equipment\" video, and write your 6-line close-out checklist on paper. At work, ask the Ultimo project lead or your supervisor the four weekend step 4 questions and note the answers."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 3 (mapping 5 more failure codes) and the ISO 14224 and IFS Ultimo searches can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":32,"phase":4,"title":"Phase 4 build, the OEE & maintenance impact dashboard","path":"course/phase-4-production-oee-tableau/week-32-oee-maintenance-dashboard-done-when.md","ticks":["Tableau model with relationships, OEE still 71.9 %","Bridge, maintenance share, top-10 assets and trend sheets","Dashboard with actions, an About box, and published to Tableau Public","Done-when passed: OEE reconciled three ways, and a colleague answered the questions unaided","`portfolio/04-oee-maintenance-impact/` committed"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 4 · Production data: OEE, Redzone & Tableau** · Mon 7 Jun – Sun 13 Jun 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n> **Done when:** you can rebuild the line's OEE from raw shift data so it **matches the dashboard figure**, and show in a **Tableau dashboard** what share of lost time is maintenance-related and **which assets** drive it.\n\nThis build joins the two worlds: production's OEE view and maintenance's asset view, in the tool your production team already uses.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-data-and-model-in-tableau","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Data and model in Tableau","kind":"hands","md":"### Sources\n\n| File | Grain | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `production/shifts.csv` | one row per shift | OEE inputs |\n| `production/redzone_downtime.csv` | one row per stop | reasons |\n| `matched_stops.csv` (yours, from Week 30) | one row per stop with a matched WO | `event_id`, `wo_id`, `asset_id`, `failure_code` |\n| `csv/assets.csv` | one row per asset | zone, type, criticality |\n\n### Do it\n\n1. Connect `shifts.csv`. Add `redzone_downtime.csv` and **relate** them (the \"noodle\", not a join) on `Production Date` = `Production Date` **and** `Shift` = `Shift`.\n2. Relate `matched_stops.csv` to `redzone_downtime` on `Event Id`, and `assets.csv` to `matched_stops` on `Asset Id`.\n\n### Why relationships, not joins\n\nA join of shifts (one row) to stops (many rows) would repeat each shift's planned minutes once per stop, and your OEE would be wrong (fan-out, Week 11). Tableau **relationships** keep each table at its own grain and only combine them at the level of the view, which is the same idea as a Power BI star schema. Test it: OEE for H1 2026 must still read **71.9 %** after adding the other tables.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-build-the-sheets","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Build the sheets","kind":"hands","md":"1. **KPI row**: OEE, Availability, Performance, Quality (H1 2026: 71.9 / 85.2 / 86.0 / 98.1 %).\n2. **Minute bridge**: planned 243,540 → downtime 36,146 → speed 28,989 → quality 3,350 → productive 175,055. Tableau has no built-in waterfall, so use a **Gantt bar** with a running total (search *\"Tableau waterfall chart\"*). It's a good table-calculation workout.\n3. **Downtime by reason category** with a calculated field `Maintenance?`:\n\n   ```\n   IF NOT ISNULL([Wo Id]) THEN \"Maintenance (has breakdown WO)\"\n   ELSEIF [Reason Category] IN (\"Mechanical\", \"Electrical\") THEN \"Maintenance-coded, no WO\"\n   ELSE \"Not maintenance\" END\n   ```\n\n   as a stacked bar. Show the maintenance share of lost time as a big number (about **46 %** with a 60-minute window).\n4. **Top 10 assets by matched stop minutes**: the asset ID, name and criticality, with the failure code on tooltips. Production's losses in maintenance's language.\n5. **OEE trend** by week with a 4-week moving average and a target parameter line.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-assemble-test-publish","title":"Weekend (2 h): Assemble, test, publish","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Dashboard**: KPI row at the top, bridge (left) plus maintenance share (right), top-10 assets, and the trend at the bottom. Add a filter action from the reason chart to the top-10 list, and a date-range filter applied to all sheets.\n2. **About** text box: OEE definitions (Week 25), the matching rule and window (Week 30), and \"synthetic data\" clearly stated.\n3. **The done-when test:**\n   - Rebuild H1 2026 OEE from raw shifts in Excel or SQL, without Tableau. It must equal the dashboard's **71.9 %**, and the downloaded crosstab's Grand Total.\n   - Show someone the dashboard and ask: *\"How much of our lost time is maintenance, and which three assets would you tackle first?\"* If they can answer from the dashboard alone, **Phase 4 is passed.** 🎉\n4. **Portfolio**: `portfolio/04-oee-maintenance-impact/` with a link to the Tableau Public dashboard, screenshots, your SQL from Week 30, and a README covering the problem (two systems that don't talk to each other), the method (mapping, time-window matching), the findings (≈ 46 % of lost time is maintenance, uncoded stops are mostly breakdowns, C-critical breakdowns rarely stop the line), and what you'd do with real data.","mins":120},{"id":"at-work-next-steps-with-permission","title":"At work, next steps (with permission)","kind":"other","md":"- Take your OEE definitions note and the matching method to your manager and the CI lead. Propose a small pilot on **one line, one month**: a permitted event-level Redzone download, plus a CMMS job export, matched with your Week 30 query.\n- After Ultimo goes live, include the **ID bridge table** (Week 31), so the legacy and new histories join up.\n- Suggest a simple process fix: maintenance adds the Redzone stop reference to the job, or production can see the job number. Once the two systems carry each other's IDs, matching becomes exact instead of time-window guesswork.","readMins":2},{"id":"check-yourself-phase-4-review","title":"Check yourself: Phase 4 review","kind":"check","md":"1. Define OEE in one sentence, and give the safest formula.\n2. Why use Tableau relationships rather than joins between shifts and stops?\n3. Name three things that make operator-coded downtime unreliable, and one way linking to the CMMS helps.\n4. What three migration problems can silently change a KPI after a CMMS change?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. OEE is the share of planned production time that was fully productive: running, at ideal speed, making good product. The safest formula is good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned production time.\n2. The tables have different grains. Joining repeats each shift row for every stop and inflates the minute and count totals. Relationships aggregate each table at its own level before combining.\n3. Any three of: unassigned stops; wrong categories (breakdowns coded as jams); grouped machines hiding the actual asset; reasons entered late from memory; changeovers logged against one machine. Linking to WOs recovers the true cause and the exact asset for maintenance-related stops.\n4. Duplicate map rows (inflated counts), unmapped assets (lost history) and merged assets (fake MTBF drops). Inconsistent failure-code mapping is a fourth.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 4 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the Session 1 relationships with OEE still at 71.9 %, the five Session 2 sheets, the weekend dashboard with About box published to Tableau Public, the three-way OEE reconciliation, a colleague answering the two questions unaided, and the portfolio folder. Plan on several sittings, not one.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read \"Why relationships, not joins\", do the Phase 4 review in Check yourself in your head, draft the About box text and the README findings on paper, and line up a colleague at work for the test.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only polish and follow-ups can wait: the moving average and target line on the trend sheet, extra formatting, and the \"At work, next steps\" pilot proposal, which comes after the phase."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Tableau model with relationships, OEE still 71.9 %\n- [ ] Bridge, maintenance share, top-10 assets and trend sheets\n- [ ] Dashboard with actions, an About box, and published to Tableau Public\n- [ ] **Done-when passed**: OEE reconciled three ways, and a colleague answered the questions unaided\n- [ ] `portfolio/04-oee-maintenance-impact/` committed"}],"quiz":[{"id":"32-1","n":1,"q":"Define OEE in one sentence, and give the safest formula.","a":"OEE is the share of planned production time that was fully productive: running, at ideal speed, making good product. The safest formula is good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned production time."},{"id":"32-2","n":2,"q":"Why use Tableau relationships rather than joins between shifts and stops?","a":"The tables have different grains. Joining repeats each shift row for every stop and inflates the minute and count totals. Relationships aggregate each table at its own level before combining."},{"id":"32-3","n":3,"q":"Name three things that make operator-coded downtime unreliable, and one way linking to the CMMS helps.","a":"Any three of: unassigned stops; wrong categories (breakdowns coded as jams); grouped machines hiding the actual asset; reasons entered late from memory; changeovers logged against one machine. Linking to WOs recovers the true cause and the exact asset for maintenance-related stops."},{"id":"32-4","n":4,"q":"What three migration problems can silently change a KPI after a CMMS change?","a":"Duplicate map rows (inflated counts), unmapped assets (lost history) and merged assets (fake MTBF drops). Inconsistent failure-code mapping is a fourth."}],"watch":[],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 4 done-when, so none of the core is skippable: the Session 1 relationships with OEE still at 71.9 %, the five Session 2 sheets, the weekend dashboard with About box published to Tableau Public, the three-way OEE reconciliation, a colleague answering the two questions unaided, and the portfolio folder. Plan on several sittings, not one."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read \"Why relationships, not joins\", do the Phase 4 review in Check yourself in your head, draft the About box text and the README findings on paper, and line up a colleague at work for the test."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only polish and follow-ups can wait: the moving average and target line on the trend sheet, extra formatting, and the \"At work, next steps\" pilot proposal, which comes after the phase."}}},{"week":33,"phase":5,"title":"Setup, Python, VS Code, virtual environments, Jupyter and Git","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-33-setup-python-vscode-git.md","ticks":["Python, VS Code (with Python and Jupyter extensions) and Git installed","Repo cloned, `.venv` created, requirements installed","`generate_data.py` runs and prints the expected counts","First script and first notebook run","Personal portfolio repo created on GitHub"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 14 Jun – Sun 20 Jun 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 5 goal:** a task that takes you an hour a week in Excel runs from a **single command**.\n\nPython is where you go when Excel and Power BI run out: big sensor files, automation, statistics, machine learning. It's free, and it's the most used language in data work. This week is all setup. It's a bit fiddly, but you only do it once.\n\n**By Sunday you'll have:**\n\n- Python 3.12+ and VS Code installed and working together\n- a **virtual environment** for this course with pandas and friends\n- run your first notebook and your first script\n- Git set up and this repo **cloned**, committing your own work","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-install","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Install","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Python**: download the latest 3.x Windows installer from [python.org/downloads](https://www.python.org/downloads/). On the first installer screen, **tick \"Add python.exe to PATH\"**, then Install Now.\n2. Open **PowerShell** (Start → type PowerShell) and check:\n\n   ```powershell\n   python --version\n   ```\n\n   You should see `Python 3.1x.x`. If Windows opens the Microsoft Store instead, go to Settings → Apps → Advanced app settings → App execution aliases, and turn off the two *python* aliases.\n3. **VS Code**: install from [code.visualstudio.com](https://code.visualstudio.com). In VS Code → Extensions (`Ctrl+Shift+X`), install **Python** and **Jupyter** (both by Microsoft).\n4. **Git**: install from [git-scm.com](https://git-scm.com/download/win), keeping the defaults. Then in PowerShell:\n\n   ```powershell\n   git config --global user.name \"Josh Wiley\"\n   git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\"     # the email on your GitHub account\n   ```\n\n5. **Clone this repo** (instead of the ZIP you used in Phase 1):\n\n   ```powershell\n   cd $HOME\\Documents\n   git clone https://github.com/griminal01/learning-data.git\n   cd learning-data\n   code .\n   ```\n\n   VS Code opens with the whole course in the sidebar.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-virtual-environment-and-first","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Virtual environment and first code","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. In VS Code: Terminal → New Terminal (it opens in the repo folder). Create and activate a virtual environment:\n\n   ```powershell\n   python -m venv .venv\n   .\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n   ```\n\n   If PowerShell blocks the script, run `Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned` once, then try again. Your prompt now starts with `(.venv)`.\n2. Install the course libraries:\n\n   ```powershell\n   pip install -r requirements.txt\n   ```\n\n3. **Regenerate the practice data** (checking that everything works):\n\n   ```powershell\n   python data\\generate_data.py\n   ```\n\n   It should print `assets=292 pm_tasks=475 work_orders=11902 downtime_events=6177 …`.\n4. **First script**: create `my_work\\hello.py`:\n\n   ```python\n   name = \"Line D\"\n   assets = 292\n   print(f\"{name} has {assets} assets\")\n   ```\n\n   Run it with the ▶ button at the top right, or `python my_work\\hello.py`.\n5. **First notebook**: create `my_work\\week25.ipynb`. VS Code opens the notebook editor. At the top right, **Select Kernel** → *Python Environments* → `.venv`. In the first cell:\n\n   ```python\n   import pandas as pd\n   wo = pd.read_csv(\"../data/csv/work_orders.csv\")\n   wo.head()\n   ```\n\n   Press `Shift+Enter`. A table of work orders appears. **That's pandas.** You'll spend Weeks 36–38 on it.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- A **virtual environment** is a private copy of Python for one project. Libraries installed in it don't clash with other projects, and `requirements.txt` lets anyone (or future you) rebuild it exactly.\n- **Scripts** (`.py`) are for things you'll run repeatedly: reports and automation. **Notebooks** (`.ipynb`) are for exploring: run a cell, look, adjust. You'll use both.\n- `.venv` is listed in `.gitignore`, so it never gets committed. Only `requirements.txt` does.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-git-workflow","title":"Weekend (2 h): Git workflow","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Make a branch for your own work so you never clash with course updates:\n\n   ```powershell\n   git checkout -b my-work\n   ```\n\n2. Commit your hello script and notebook:\n\n   ```powershell\n   git add my_work\n   git commit -m \"Week 33: setup, first script and notebook\"\n   ```\n\n3. **Your own portfolio repo**: on GitHub, create a new **public** repository called something like `maintenance-analytics-portfolio`. You'll copy your best work from each phase into it (Phase 1 workbook, Phase 2 SQL, Phase 3 PBIX) with a good README for each. See `portfolio/README.md` in this repo for the template.\n4. In VS Code, try the **Source Control** panel (`Ctrl+Shift+G`). It does the same as the commands above with buttons. Use whichever you prefer.\n5. Learn these 6 Git commands; they're 95 % of daily use:\n\n   | Command | Does |\n   |---|---|\n   | `git status` | what's changed |\n   | `git add <files>` | stage changes |\n   | `git commit -m \"msg\"` | save a snapshot |\n   | `git push` | upload to GitHub |\n   | `git pull` | download changes |\n   | `git log --oneline` | history |","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer** (YouTube): *\"Corey Schafer VS Code Python setup Windows\"*.\n- **Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python venv Windows\"*.\n- **freeCodeCamp**: *\"freeCodeCamp Git and GitHub for beginners crash course\"*.\n- **VS Code docs**: search *\"Python in Visual Studio Code tutorial\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why use a virtual environment instead of installing pandas for the whole PC?\n2. What's the difference between a `.py` script and a `.ipynb` notebook, and when would you use each?\n3. You open a new terminal and `import pandas` fails. What did you forget?\n4. Why shouldn't `.venv` be committed to Git?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Different projects need different library versions. A venv keeps each project's set separate and reproducible via `requirements.txt`, and it can't break anything else on the PC.\n2. A script runs top to bottom, for repeatable jobs and automation. A notebook runs cell by cell with results inline, for exploring and explaining. Explore in a notebook, then move the working code into a script.\n3. Activating the venv (`.\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1`), or selecting the `.venv` interpreter in VS Code (`Ctrl+Shift+P` → *Python: Select Interpreter*).\n4. It's large, Windows-specific, and rebuilt in one command from `requirements.txt`. Commit the recipe, not the kitchen.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (Python with PATH ticked, VS Code with both extensions, Git configured, repo cloned) and Session 2 steps 1 to 5 (`.venv`, `pip install -r requirements.txt`, `generate_data.py` printing the expected counts, first script, first notebook), then weekend steps 1 and 2 to branch and make your first commit.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Corey Schafer VS Code setup and venv videos and the freeCodeCamp Git crash course, read the \"Why it works\" notes on venvs, scripts and notebooks, and learn the six Git commands in the weekend table. Creating your public portfolio repository on GitHub (weekend step 3) also works in a phone browser.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 4 (trying the Source Control panel) and the VS Code Python tutorial can be skipped; the command line does the same job."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Python, VS Code (with Python and Jupyter extensions) and Git installed\n- [ ] Repo cloned, `.venv` created, requirements installed\n- [ ] `generate_data.py` runs and prints the expected counts\n- [ ] First script and first notebook run\n- [ ] Personal portfolio repo created on GitHub"}],"quiz":[{"id":"33-1","n":1,"q":"Why use a virtual environment instead of installing pandas for the whole PC?","a":"Different projects need different library versions. A venv keeps each project's set separate and reproducible via `requirements.txt`, and it can't break anything else on the PC."},{"id":"33-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between a `.py` script and a `.ipynb` notebook, and when would you use each?","a":"A script runs top to bottom, for repeatable jobs and automation. A notebook runs cell by cell with results inline, for exploring and explaining. Explore in a notebook, then move the working code into a script."},{"id":"33-3","n":3,"q":"You open a new terminal and `import pandas` fails. What did you forget?","a":"Activating the venv (`.\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1`), or selecting the `.venv` interpreter in VS Code (`Ctrl+Shift+P` → *Python: Select Interpreter*)."},{"id":"33-4","n":4,"q":"Why shouldn't `.venv` be committed to Git?","a":"It's large, Windows-specific, and rebuilt in one command from `requirements.txt`. Commit the recipe, not the kitchen."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer** (YouTube): *\"Corey Schafer VS Code Python setup Windows\"*.","searches":["Corey Schafer VS Code Python setup Windows"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python venv Windows\"*.","searches":["Corey Schafer Python venv Windows"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**freeCodeCamp**: *\"freeCodeCamp Git and GitHub for beginners crash course\"*.","searches":["freeCodeCamp Git and GitHub for beginners crash course"],"urls":[]},{"i":3,"md":"**VS Code docs**: search *\"Python in Visual Studio Code tutorial\"*.","searches":["Python in Visual Studio Code tutorial"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1 steps 1 to 5 (Python with PATH ticked, VS Code with both extensions, Git configured, repo cloned) and Session 2 steps 1 to 5 (`.venv`, `pip install -r requirements.txt`, `generate_data.py` printing the expected counts, first script, first notebook), then weekend steps 1 and 2 to branch and make your first commit."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Corey Schafer VS Code setup and venv videos and the freeCodeCamp Git crash course, read the \"Why it works\" notes on venvs, scripts and notebooks, and learn the six Git commands in the weekend table. Creating your public portfolio repository on GitHub (weekend step 3) also works in a phone browser."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 4 (trying the Source Control panel) and the VS Code Python tutorial can be skipped; the command line does the same job."}}},{"week":34,"phase":5,"title":"Core Python 1, values, lists, dictionaries, loops and decisions","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-34-core-python-1.md","ticks":["Comfortable with strings, f-strings, lists and dicts","`for` + `if/elif/else`, with correct indentation","List comprehension written","All six weekend exercises done","Kaggle Python lessons 1–3"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 21 Jun – Sun 27 Jun 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nBefore pandas, you need enough plain Python to read and write simple code with confidence. Good news: if you can follow ladder logic, you can follow Python. It's just written as text, top to bottom.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use numbers, strings, booleans and f-strings\n- store collections in **lists** and **dictionaries**\n- loop with `for`, decide with `if / elif / else`\n- write a list comprehension\n\n**Work in:** a notebook `my_work\\week26.ipynb`. Type everything; don't copy-paste.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-values-and-lists","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Values and lists","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\n# Numbers and strings\nasset = \"LD-PPK-005\"\nfailures = 37\noperating_hours = 17520\nmtbf = operating_hours / failures\nprint(asset, mtbf)\nprint(f\"{asset} MTBF is {mtbf:.0f} hours\")      # f-string, formatted to 0 decimals\n\n# Strings have methods\nprint(asset.split(\"-\"))        # ['LD', 'PPK', '005']\nzone_code = asset.split(\"-\")[1]\nprint(zone_code.lower(), len(asset), asset.startswith(\"LD\"))\n\n# Booleans\nis_critical = True\nprint(failures > 20 and is_critical)\n```\n\n```python\n# Lists: an ordered collection\ndowntime_mins = [12, 45, 3, 180, 7, 22, 60]\nprint(len(downtime_mins), sum(downtime_mins), max(downtime_mins))   # 7 329 180\nprint(downtime_mins[0], downtime_mins[-1])    # first and last\nprint(downtime_mins[1:3])                     # slice: items 1 and 2\ndowntime_mins.append(15)\ndowntime_mins.sort(reverse=True)\nprint(downtime_mins)\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n- `=` **assigns** (puts a value in a named box). `==` **compares**.\n- Python counts from **0**: `downtime_mins[0]` is the first item, and `[-1]` is the last.\n- Slices `[a:b]` include `a` but stop *before* `b`, the same \"half-open\" idea as the date ranges in SQL.\n- f-strings (`f\"...{value:.1f}...\"`) are the neatest way to build text. `:.0f` means 0 decimals, `:.1%` means a percentage with 1 decimal, and `:,` adds thousands commas.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-dictionaries-loops-and-if","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Dictionaries, loops and if","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\n# Dictionary: look up a value by a key (like XLOOKUP on a two-column table)\npm_frequency = {\n    \"Grease motor bearings\": 91,\n    \"Conveyor belt tension & tracking check\": 14,\n    \"Replace sealing jaws\": 182,\n}\nprint(pm_frequency[\"Replace sealing jaws\"])\npm_frequency[\"Pneumatic leak check\"] = 28        # add a new key\n\n# Loop over a dictionary\nfor task, days in pm_frequency.items():\n    if days <= 14:\n        label = \"weekly-ish\"\n    elif days <= 91:\n        label = \"monthly+\"\n    else:\n        label = \"6-monthly+\"\n    print(f\"{task:45} every {days:>3} days  ({label})\")\n```\n\n```python\n# Loop with a running total (like an accumulator rung)\ntotal = 0\nlong_stops = []\nfor m in [12, 45, 3, 180, 7, 22, 60]:\n    total += m\n    if m >= 30:\n        long_stops.append(m)\nprint(total, long_stops)     # 329 [45, 180, 60]\n\n# The same filter as a one-line list comprehension\nlong_stops = [m for m in [12, 45, 3, 180, 7, 22, 60] if m >= 30]\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n**Indentation is the syntax.** Everything indented under `for` or `if` belongs to it, so there are no `END IF`s. VS Code indents automatically after a `:`. Use 4 spaces.\n\nDictionaries are everywhere in Python data work: a row of data, a config file, a JSON response from an API. They're all key → value.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"In `week26.ipynb`, write code for each of these:\n\n1. Given `codes = [\"LD-PPK-005\", \"LD-EOL-001\", \"LD-FLK-010\", \"LD-PPK-003\"]`, print only the packing (`PPK`) assets.\n2. Count how many assets per zone code are in that list, using a dictionary. (Hint: `counts[z] = counts.get(z, 0) + 1`.)\n3. Given `due = 14` and `done = [10, 14, 15, 30, 13]` (days after the last PM), produce a list of `\"On time\"` / `\"Late\"` labels.\n4. Work out PM compliance % from question 3's labels.\n5. **FizzBuzz, maintenance edition:** loop over days 1 to 28. Print \"Weekly PM\" on multiples of 7, \"Fortnightly PM\" on multiples of 14 (instead of Weekly), and nothing otherwise.\n6. Write a dictionary describing one asset (`id`, `name`, `zone`, `criticality`, `failures`), then print a one-line summary of it with an f-string.\n\nAlso do the **Kaggle Learn: Python** course, lessons 1–3: [kaggle.com/learn/python](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/python). It's free and runs in the browser.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python tutorial for beginners\"*. Watch episodes 2–7 (strings, numbers, lists, dicts, conditionals, loops).\n- **Automate the Boring Stuff with Python** (free online): [automatetheboringstuff.com](https://automatetheboringstuff.com). Read chapters 1, 2 and 4."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What does `asset.split(\"-\")[2]` return for `\"LD-PPK-005\"`?\n2. What's the difference between a list and a dictionary?\n3. What's wrong here?\n   ```python\n   for m in downtime_mins:\n   if m > 30:\n       print(m)\n   ```\n4. Rewrite as a list comprehension: a list of every stop over 10 minutes, converted to hours.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `'005'`, as a string, not a number. `int(\"005\")` gives 5.\n2. A list is ordered and accessed by **position** (`x[0]`). A dictionary is accessed by **key** (`x[\"Replace sealing jaws\"]`). Use a dictionary when you look things up by name.\n3. The `if` must be indented under the `for`, and the `print` indented under the `if`. Python raises `IndentationError`.\n4. `hours = [m / 60 for m in downtime_mins if m > 10]`\n\n**Weekend answers:**\n\n1. `[c for c in codes if c.split(\"-\")[1] == \"PPK\"]`\n2. ```python\n   counts = {}\n   for c in codes:\n       z = c.split(\"-\")[1]\n       counts[z] = counts.get(z, 0) + 1\n   ```\n3. `[\"On time\" if d <= due else \"Late\" for d in done]`, which gives On time, On time, Late, Late, On time\n4. `labels.count(\"On time\") / len(labels)`, which gives 60 %\n5. ```python\n   for day in range(1, 29):\n       if day % 14 == 0:\n           print(day, \"Fortnightly PM\")\n       elif day % 7 == 0:\n           print(day, \"Weekly PM\")\n   ```\n   The fortnightly check must come first, because 14 is also a multiple of 7.\n\nMore worked examples are in [`solutions/phase-5-python/w34_w35_core_python.py`](../../solutions/phase-5-python/w34_w35_core_python.py).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Type the Session 1 and Session 2 code blocks into your notebook and run them, then do all six weekend exercises. Typing it yourself is the point, so don't paste.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch Corey Schafer's beginner episodes 2 to 7, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapters 1, 2 and 4 and both \"Why it works\" parts (indentation, counting from 0, half-open slices), and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Kaggle Learn Python lessons 1 to 3 can be pushed back a week if you're short on time, because the weekend exercises cover the same ground."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Comfortable with strings, f-strings, lists and dicts\n- [ ] `for` + `if/elif/else`, with correct indentation\n- [ ] List comprehension written\n- [ ] All six weekend exercises done\n- [ ] Kaggle Python lessons 1–3"}],"quiz":[{"id":"34-1","n":1,"q":"What does `asset.split(\"-\")[2]` return for `\"LD-PPK-005\"`?","a":"`'005'`, as a string, not a number. `int(\"005\")` gives 5."},{"id":"34-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between a list and a dictionary?","a":"A list is ordered and accessed by **position** (`x[0]`). A dictionary is accessed by **key** (`x[\"Replace sealing jaws\"]`). Use a dictionary when you look things up by name."},{"id":"34-3","n":3,"q":"What's wrong here?\n```python\nfor m in downtime_mins:\nif m > 30:\n    print(m)\n```","a":"The `if` must be indented under the `for`, and the `print` indented under the `if`. Python raises `IndentationError`."},{"id":"34-4","n":4,"q":"Rewrite as a list comprehension: a list of every stop over 10 minutes, converted to hours.","a":"`hours = [m / 60 for m in downtime_mins if m > 10]`"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python tutorial for beginners\"*. Watch episodes 2–7 (strings, numbers, lists, dicts, conditionals, loops).","searches":["Corey Schafer Python tutorial for beginners"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Automate the Boring Stuff with Python** (free online): [automatetheboringstuff.com](https://automatetheboringstuff.com). Read chapters 1, 2 and 4.","searches":[],"urls":["https://automatetheboringstuff.com"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Type the Session 1 and Session 2 code blocks into your notebook and run them, then do all six weekend exercises. Typing it yourself is the point, so don't paste."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch Corey Schafer's beginner episodes 2 to 7, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapters 1, 2 and 4 and both \"Why it works\" parts (indentation, counting from 0, half-open slices), and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Kaggle Learn Python lessons 1 to 3 can be pushed back a week if you're short on time, because the weekend exercises cover the same ground."}}},{"week":35,"phase":5,"title":"Core Python 2, functions, files, errors and modules","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-35-core-python-2.md","ticks":["Functions with docstrings, defaults and returns","Read and wrote CSVs with `pathlib` + `csv`","`try / except ValueError` used on messy values","`reliability.py` module imported into a notebook","Kaggle Python course finished"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 28 Jun – Sun 4 Jul 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nFunctions are the building blocks of every useful script. This week you write your own, read data files, handle bad data without crashing, and organise code into modules. It's the same idea as subroutines and AOIs on a PLC.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- write functions with arguments, defaults, return values and docstrings\n- read and write CSV files with `csv` and `pathlib`\n- handle errors with `try / except`\n- import from the standard library and from your own module","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-functions","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Functions","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\ndef mtbf_hours(operating_hours, failures):\n    \"\"\"Mean time between failures. Returns None when there were no failures.\"\"\"\n    if failures == 0:\n        return None\n    return operating_hours / failures\n\n\ndef availability(mtbf, mttr):\n    \"\"\"Inherent availability as a fraction (0-1).\"\"\"\n    return mtbf / (mtbf + mttr)\n\n\nprint(round(mtbf_hours(17520, 37)))           # 474\nprint(f\"{availability(474, 0.76):.2%}\")       # 99.84%\nprint(mtbf_hours(17520, 0))                   # None\n```\n\n**Defaults and keyword arguments:**\n\n```python\ndef compliance(on_time, due, as_percent=True):\n    if due == 0:\n        return None\n    value = on_time / due\n    return round(value * 100, 1) if as_percent else value\n\ncompliance(353, 434)                     # 81.3\ncompliance(353, 434, as_percent=False)   # 0.813...\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\nA function takes inputs, does one job, and **returns** an output. Name it after what it returns (`mtbf_hours`, not `do_calc`). The triple-quoted *docstring* appears when you hover over the function in VS Code, so write one for every function.\n\n**`print` vs `return`:** `print` shows something on screen. `return` hands a value back so other code can use it. A function that only prints is a dead end.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-files-and-errors","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Files and errors","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"          # adjust if your notebook lives elsewhere\n\ndef read_breakdowns(path):\n    \"\"\"Count reactive work orders per asset.\"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    with open(path, newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):           # each row is a dictionary\n            if row[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\":\n                counts[row[\"asset_id\"]] = counts.get(row[\"asset_id\"], 0) + 1\n    return counts\n\ncounts = read_breakdowns(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\")\nworst = sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1], reverse=True)[:5]\nprint(worst)\n```\n\nYou should get `LD-PPK-005` 37, `LD-PPK-003` 33, `LD-PPK-006` 30, `LD-PPK-004` 23, `LD-EOL-001` 20. That's the same as your SQL in Week 11.\n\n**Errors without crashing:**\n\n```python\nfor raw in [\"1.5\", \"2h\", \"\", \"0.75\"]:\n    try:\n        print(raw, \"->\", float(raw))\n    except ValueError:\n        print(raw, \"-> not a number, skipped\")\n```\n\n**Writing a file:**\n\n```python\nout = Path(\"worst_assets.csv\")\nwith open(out, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    w = csv.writer(f)\n    w.writerow([\"asset_id\", \"breakdowns\"])\n    w.writerows(worst)\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n- `with open(...) as f:` closes the file automatically, even if an error happens.\n- `pathlib.Path` builds file paths that work on any OS. `/` joins parts, so there's no fighting with backslashes.\n- `try / except` is a fault handler: catch the **specific** error you expect (`ValueError`), never a bare `except:` that hides real bugs.\n- `sorted(..., key=lambda kv: kv[1])` sorts by the second item. A `lambda` is a tiny one-line function.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-your-first-module","title":"Weekend (2 h): Your first module","kind":"hands","md":"1. Create `my_work\\reliability.py` containing `mtbf_hours`, `availability`, `compliance`, and a new function `failure_rate(mtbf)` that returns failures per hour.\n2. Add this at the bottom:\n\n   ```python\n   if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n       print(mtbf_hours(17520, 37))\n   ```\n\n   Now the file is both a module you can import *and* a script you can run.\n3. In a notebook in the same folder:\n\n   ```python\n   from reliability import mtbf_hours, availability\n   ```\n\n4. **Exercise:** write `read_pm_compliance(path, zone_code)`, which reads `work_orders.csv` and `assets.csv` with `csv.DictReader` and returns PM compliance % for one zone. Check: `PPK` gives about **66.4 %** for all time. (It's fiddly in plain Python. Next week pandas does it in three lines, and that's the point.)\n5. Kaggle Learn Python lessons 4–7 (functions, booleans, lists, loops, strings and dictionaries).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python functions\"*, *\"Corey Schafer Python file objects\"* and *\"Corey Schafer Python try except\"*.\n- **Automate the Boring Stuff**: chapters 3 (functions), 9 (files) and 16 (CSV)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What does a function return if it has no `return` statement?\n2. Why catch `ValueError` instead of using a bare `except:`?\n3. What does `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` do?\n4. Write a function `days_late(due, done)` that takes two `date` objects and returns the days late (0 if on time).\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `None`.\n2. A bare except also catches typos (`NameError`), Ctrl+C, and every other bug, so real problems disappear silently. Catch only what you expect, and let the rest crash loudly so you can fix them.\n3. The code under it runs only when the file is executed directly, not when it's imported as a module.\n4. ```python\n   from datetime import date\n   def days_late(due, done):\n       return max(0, (done - due).days)\n   days_late(date(2026, 6, 1), date(2026, 6, 4))   # 3\n   ```\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Type and run the Session 1 functions and the Session 2 file and `try / except` blocks (check the top five against Week 11), then weekend steps 1 to 3 to build `reliability.py` and import it into a notebook. Week 40 builds on scripts and functions like these.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the three Corey Schafer videos on functions, file objects and try except, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapter 3 and the \"Why it works\" notes on `print` versus `return` and on catching specific errors, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 4 (`read_pm_compliance` in plain Python, which pandas does in three lines next week) and the Kaggle lessons 4 to 7 can be deferred."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Functions with docstrings, defaults and returns\n- [ ] Read and wrote CSVs with `pathlib` + `csv`\n- [ ] `try / except ValueError` used on messy values\n- [ ] `reliability.py` module imported into a notebook\n- [ ] Kaggle Python course finished"}],"quiz":[{"id":"35-1","n":1,"q":"What does a function return if it has no `return` statement?","a":"`None`."},{"id":"35-2","n":2,"q":"Why catch `ValueError` instead of using a bare `except:`?","a":"A bare except also catches typos (`NameError`), Ctrl+C, and every other bug, so real problems disappear silently. Catch only what you expect, and let the rest crash loudly so you can fix them."},{"id":"35-3","n":3,"q":"What does `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` do?","a":"The code under it runs only when the file is executed directly, not when it's imported as a module."},{"id":"35-4","n":4,"q":"Write a function `days_late(due, done)` that takes two `date` objects and returns the days late (0 if on time).","a":"```python\nfrom datetime import date\ndef days_late(due, done):\n    return max(0, (done - due).days)\ndays_late(date(2026, 6, 1), date(2026, 6, 4))   # 3\n```"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python functions\"*, *\"Corey Schafer Python file objects\"* and *\"Corey Schafer Python try except\"*.","searches":["Corey Schafer Python functions","Corey Schafer Python file objects","Corey Schafer Python try except"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Automate the Boring Stuff**: chapters 3 (functions), 9 (files) and 16 (CSV).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Type and run the Session 1 functions and the Session 2 file and `try / except` blocks (check the top five against Week 11), then weekend steps 1 to 3 to build `reliability.py` and import it into a notebook. Week 40 builds on scripts and functions like these."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the three Corey Schafer videos on functions, file objects and try except, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapter 3 and the \"Why it works\" notes on `print` versus `return` and on catching specific errors, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 4 (`read_pm_compliance` in plain Python, which pandas does in three lines next week) and the Kaggle lessons 4 to 7 can be deferred."}}},{"week":36,"phase":5,"title":"pandas 1, reading, inspecting, selecting and filtering","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-36-pandas-1.md","ticks":["Read clean CSVs and the UK-dated export with correct types","`info`, `describe`, `value_counts` and `isna().sum()` on every table","Boolean filters with `&`, `|`, `isin`, `str.contains` and `between`","Weekend questions answered, top 10 written to Excel","Kaggle Pandas lessons 1–3"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 5 Jul – Sun 11 Jul 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**pandas** is Excel inside Python: tables (called *DataFrames*) that you filter, sort and summarise with code. Every step is recorded, it handles millions of rows, and the same script works next week on new data.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- read CSV and Excel files into DataFrames, with correct types and dates\n- inspect data with `head`, `info`, `describe` and `value_counts`\n- select columns and filter rows with boolean masks\n- sort, add columns and write results back to Excel\n\n**Work in:** `my_work\\week28.ipynb`, with the `.venv` kernel.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-read-and-inspect","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Read and inspect","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\",\n                 parse_dates=[\"raised_datetime\", \"due_date\", \"completed_date\"])\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\", parse_dates=[\"install_date\"])\n\nwo.shape            # (11902, 14)\nwo.head()           # first 5 rows\nwo.info()           # columns, types, non-null counts\nwo.describe()       # stats for numeric columns\nwo[\"wo_type\"].value_counts()          # PM 10502, Reactive 1027, Corrective 373\nwo[\"status\"].value_counts(normalize=True)\nwo.isna().sum()     # nulls per column\n```\n\n**The CMMS export format** (UK dates, spaces in the headers):\n\n```python\njune = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"exports\" / \"work_orders\" / \"WO_Export_2026-06.csv\")\njune[\"Raised\"] = pd.to_datetime(june[\"Raised\"], format=\"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\")\njune[\"Due\"] = pd.to_datetime(june[\"Due\"], format=\"%d/%m/%Y\")\njune[\"Completed\"] = pd.to_datetime(june[\"Completed\"], format=\"%d/%m/%Y\")\n```\n\nAlways give the `format`. Without it, pandas may guess month-first for ambiguous dates.\n\n### Why it works\n\n`info()` is your first move with any new file. It's the pandas version of the Week 4 profiling: how many rows, which columns have gaps, and whether the dates are really dates (`datetime64`) or still text (`object`).","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-select-filter-and-sort","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Select, filter and sort","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\n# Columns\nwo[\"asset_id\"]                                  # one column (a Series)\nwo[[\"wo_id\", \"asset_id\", \"labour_hours\"]]       # several columns (a DataFrame)\n\n# Rows: boolean mask, the pandas version of Excel's FILTER\nreactive = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"]\nlen(reactive)                                    # 1027\n\n# Several conditions: & (and), | (or), and brackets round each one\nbig = wo[(wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\") & (wo[\"labour_hours\"] > 6)]\nbig = big.sort_values(\"labour_hours\", ascending=False)\nbig[[\"wo_id\", \"asset_id\", \"description\", \"labour_hours\"]].head()\n```\n\nThe top one is **WO-100455**, a gearbox (gears) on LD-FLK-031: 36.2 labour hours.\n\n```python\n# Handy filters\nwo[wo[\"wo_type\"].isin([\"Reactive\", \"Corrective\"])]\nwo[wo[\"description\"].str.contains(\"bearing\", case=False)]\nwo[wo[\"completed_date\"].isna()]\nwo[wo[\"raised_datetime\"].between(\"2026-06-01\", \"2026-06-30 23:59\")]\nwo.query(\"wo_type == 'Reactive' and labour_hours > 6\")    # an alternative syntax\n\n# New columns (whole-column maths, no fill-down)\nwo[\"labour_mins\"] = wo[\"labour_hours\"] * 60\nwo[\"days_late\"] = (wo[\"completed_date\"] - wo[\"due_date\"]).dt.days\nwo[\"is_late\"] = wo[\"days_late\"] > 0\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n`wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"` produces a column of True/False, one per row. Putting that inside `wo[...]` keeps the True rows. It's the same Boolean multiplication as Excel's `FILTER`, but pandas uses `&` and `|`, **and each condition needs its own brackets**.\n\nOperations act on **whole columns at once**. There's no looping and no filling down. That's why pandas is fast.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Answer each of these in one or two lines of pandas:\n   1. How many A-critical assets are there in each zone?\n   2. The 10 longest downtime events (from `downtime_events.csv`).\n   3. All cancelled PMs for `LD-PPK-005`.\n   4. How many PMs were completed more than 14 days late?\n   5. Which `failure_code` appears most on reactive jobs?\n2. Save the answer to (b) to Excel: `top10.to_excel(\"top10_downtime.xlsx\", index=False)`.\n3. **Kaggle Learn: Pandas**, lessons 1–3: [kaggle.com/learn/pandas](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/pandas).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: the *\"Corey Schafer pandas tutorial\"* series, episodes 1–4.\n- **Keith Galli**: *\"Keith Galli complete pandas tutorial\"*.\n- Book: *Python for Data Analysis*, 3rd edition, by Wes McKinney (the creator of pandas). It's free at [wesmckinney.com/book](https://wesmckinney.com/book/). Read chapter 5 this week."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does `wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\" & wo[\"labour_hours\"] > 6]` raise an error?\n2. What's the difference between `wo[\"asset_id\"]` and `wo[[\"asset_id\"]]`?\n3. How do you check whether `due_date` was read as a date?\n4. How many PMs were completed more than 14 days late?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `&` binds tighter than `==`, so Python tries `\"Reactive\" & wo[\"labour_hours\"]` first. Put brackets round each condition.\n2. Single brackets return a **Series** (one column). Double brackets return a **DataFrame** with one column. Some functions want one or the other.\n3. `wo.dtypes` or `wo.info()`. It should say `datetime64[ns]`, not `object`.\n4. ```python\n   pm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"]\n   ((pm[\"completed_date\"] - pm[\"due_date\"]).dt.days > 14).sum()\n   ```\n   Run it and compare with SQL: `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM work_orders WHERE wo_type='PM' AND julianday(completed_date) - julianday(due_date) > 14;`. The two should match.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's read and inspect block (including the UK-dated export with `format`), Session 2's filters, sort and new columns, and the five weekend questions with the top 10 written to Excel.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch Corey Schafer's pandas episodes 1 to 4, read chapter 5 of Wes McKinney's free *Python for Data Analysis* and both \"Why it works\" parts on `info()` and boolean masks, and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Kaggle Pandas lessons 1 to 3, the long Keith Galli tutorial and the `query()` alternative syntax can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Read clean CSVs and the UK-dated export with correct types\n- [ ] `info`, `describe`, `value_counts` and `isna().sum()` on every table\n- [ ] Boolean filters with `&`, `|`, `isin`, `str.contains` and `between`\n- [ ] Weekend questions answered, top 10 written to Excel\n- [ ] Kaggle Pandas lessons 1–3"}],"quiz":[{"id":"36-1","n":1,"q":"Why does `wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\" & wo[\"labour_hours\"] > 6]` raise an error?","a":"`&` binds tighter than `==`, so Python tries `\"Reactive\" & wo[\"labour_hours\"]` first. Put brackets round each condition."},{"id":"36-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between `wo[\"asset_id\"]` and `wo[[\"asset_id\"]]`?","a":"Single brackets return a **Series** (one column). Double brackets return a **DataFrame** with one column. Some functions want one or the other."},{"id":"36-3","n":3,"q":"How do you check whether `due_date` was read as a date?","a":"`wo.dtypes` or `wo.info()`. It should say `datetime64[ns]`, not `object`."},{"id":"36-4","n":4,"q":"How many PMs were completed more than 14 days late?","a":"```python\npm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"]\n((pm[\"completed_date\"] - pm[\"due_date\"]).dt.days > 14).sum()\n```\nRun it and compare with SQL: `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM work_orders WHERE wo_type='PM' AND julianday(completed_date) - julianday(due_date) > 14;`. The two should match."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: the *\"Corey Schafer pandas tutorial\"* series, episodes 1–4.","searches":["Corey Schafer pandas tutorial"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Keith Galli**: *\"Keith Galli complete pandas tutorial\"*.","searches":["Keith Galli complete pandas tutorial"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book: *Python for Data Analysis*, 3rd edition, by Wes McKinney (the creator of pandas). It's free at [wesmckinney.com/book](https://wesmckinney.com/book/). Read chapter 5 this week.","searches":[],"urls":["https://wesmckinney.com/book/"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's read and inspect block (including the UK-dated export with `format`), Session 2's filters, sort and new columns, and the five weekend questions with the top 10 written to Excel."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch Corey Schafer's pandas episodes 1 to 4, read chapter 5 of Wes McKinney's free *Python for Data Analysis* and both \"Why it works\" parts on `info()` and boolean masks, and answer Check yourself questions 1 to 3 in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Kaggle Pandas lessons 1 to 3, the long Keith Galli tutorial and the `query()` alternative syntax can wait."}}},{"week":37,"phase":5,"title":"pandas 2, cleaning, merging, groupby and pivot","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-37-pandas-2.md","ticks":["Dirty export cleaned in pandas, matching the Excel result (1,470 rows, 1,124.02 h)","Merges with `validate=` on every one","groupby/agg summary and a styled pivot heat-map","Phase 2 done-when recreated in pandas","Repeat-failure count = 68"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 12 Jul – Sun 18 Jul 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis week you redo Phase 1 and 2 work in pandas: clean the dirty export, join tables, and summarise by group. You'll see why analysts move to Python. The Week 4 cleaning that took a whole evening of Excel formulas takes about ten lines here.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- clean text with `.str` methods and regular expressions\n- remove duplicates and convert messy numbers\n- `merge` DataFrames (left, inner) and validate the join\n- `groupby` + `agg` with named outputs\n- build `pivot_table`s","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-clean-the-dirty-export","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Clean the dirty export","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndirty = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"dirty\" / \"wo_export_q1_2026_dirty.csv\", dtype=str, keep_default_na=False)\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nprint(len(dirty), dirty[\"WO Number\"].nunique())        # 1499 1470\n\nclean = dirty.drop_duplicates().copy()\n\nclean[\"Asset\"] = (clean[\"Asset\"]\n    .str.strip()\n    .str.upper()\n    .str.replace(\" \", \"-\", regex=False)\n    .str.replace(r\"^LD-?([A-Z]{3})-?(\\d{3})$\", r\"LD-\\1-\\2\", regex=True))\n\nclean[\"Labour Hrs\"] = pd.to_numeric(clean[\"Labour Hrs\"].str.replace(\"h\", \"\", regex=False), errors=\"coerce\")\n\nclean = clean.merge(assets[[\"asset_id\", \"zone\"]], left_on=\"Asset\", right_on=\"asset_id\",\n                    how=\"left\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\n\nprint(len(clean), clean[\"asset_id\"].isna().sum(), round(clean[\"Labour Hrs\"].sum(), 2))\n# 1470 0 1124.02\n```\n\nThat matches your Week 4 Excel result exactly: 1,470 rows, every asset matched, 1,124.02 labour hours.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- `dtype=str, keep_default_na=False` reads everything as text with blanks as `\"\"`, so you see the raw mess and decide what to do with it.\n- `.str` gives you Excel-style text functions for a whole column at once.\n- The **regular expression** `^LD-?([A-Z]{3})-?(\\d{3})$` means: starts with LD, an optional dash, three capital letters (captured as group 1), an optional dash, three digits (group 2), end. `r\"LD-\\1-\\2\"` rebuilds it in the standard form. Regex is worth learning a little of; [regex101.com](https://regex101.com) lets you test patterns.\n- `errors=\"coerce\"` turns anything unconvertible into `NaN` instead of crashing.\n- `validate=\"many_to_one\"` makes pandas **raise an error** if the asset table has duplicate keys. That's the fan-out guard from Week 11, built in. Use it on every merge.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-groupby-and-pivot-table","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): groupby and pivot_table","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"raised_datetime\", \"due_date\", \"completed_date\"])\nwo = wo.merge(assets[[\"asset_id\", \"zone\", \"asset_type\", \"criticality\"]], on=\"asset_id\",\n              how=\"left\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\n\nby_zone = (wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"]\n           .groupby(\"zone\")\n           .agg(jobs=(\"wo_id\", \"count\"),\n                labour_hrs=(\"labour_hours\", \"sum\"),\n                failing_assets=(\"asset_id\", \"nunique\"))\n           .sort_values(\"jobs\", ascending=False))\nby_zone[\"jobs_per_failing_asset\"] = by_zone[\"jobs\"] / by_zone[\"failing_assets\"]\nby_zone.round(1)\n```\n\nPrimary Packing has 292 jobs over 39 failing assets (**7.5 per asset**). Flaking has the most labour (519.7 h).\n\n```python\n# PM compliance heat-map: zone × month, H1 2026\npm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"].copy()\npm[\"on_time\"] = pm[\"completed_date\"].le(pm[\"due_date\"])\npm[\"due_month\"] = pm[\"due_date\"].dt.to_period(\"M\")\n\npivot = pm[pm[\"due_date\"] >= \"2026-01-01\"].pivot_table(\n    index=\"zone\", columns=\"due_month\", values=\"on_time\", aggfunc=\"mean\")\n(pivot * 100).round(0)\n```\n\nPrimary Packing sits between **59 % and 70 %** every month. It's a consistent problem, not a blip.\n\n```python\n# Style it like conditional formatting (in a notebook)\n(pivot * 100).round(0).style.background_gradient(cmap=\"RdYlGn\", vmin=50, vmax=100)\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n`groupby` is SQL's `GROUP BY`, and `agg(name=(column, function))` is the `SELECT` list with aliases. `pivot_table` is an Excel PivotTable. **`completed_date.le(due_date)`** returns False when `completed_date` is missing (NaT), so missed PMs count as not on time, which matches the SQL CASE logic.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Recreate the Phase 2 done-when query in pandas**: the top 10 assets by Q2 2026 breakdown downtime hours, with their Q2 PM on-time %. Check it against the Week 14 table.\n2. **Planned vs reactive** labour hours by zone, as a pivot with zones as rows and WO types as columns, then add a \"% reactive\" column.\n3. **Repeat failures**: for each asset, sort reactive jobs by date, use `groupby(\"asset_id\")[\"raised_datetime\"].diff()` to get the gap, and count gaps under 7 days. The answer is **68**, the same as the SQL LAG in Week 13.\n4. Kaggle Learn Pandas, lessons 4–6.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: pandas episodes 5–8 (updating, adding/removing, sorting, grouping).\n- **Data School (Kevin Markham)**: *\"Data School pandas merge\"*.\n- McKinney's book, chapters 7 (cleaning), 8 (merge, reshape) and 10 (groupby)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What does `validate=\"many_to_one\"` protect you from?\n2. What's the difference between `.agg(jobs=(\"wo_id\", \"count\"))` and `.size()`?\n3. Why read the dirty file with `dtype=str`?\n4. Write the pandas for: count of PMs by `task_type`, from `pm_tasks.csv`.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Duplicate keys in the right-hand table, which would multiply rows (fan-out) and inflate totals. pandas raises a `MergeError` instead of silently producing wrong numbers.\n2. `count` counts non-null values in that column. `size()` counts rows, nulls included. For a key column like `wo_id` (never null) they're the same.\n3. So pandas doesn't guess. It might turn `005` into 5, or `1.5h` into NaN, before you've had a chance to see the problem. Read raw, inspect, then convert on purpose.\n4. `pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"pm_tasks.csv\")[\"task_type\"].value_counts()`\n\nFull worked code is in [`solutions/phase-5-python/w36_w38_pandas.py`](../../solutions/phase-5-python/w36_w38_pandas.py).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's clean of the dirty export (1,470 rows, 0 unmatched, 1,124.02 h) with `validate=` on the merge, Session 2's groupby summary and the zone by month compliance pivot, and weekend items 1 and 3: the Phase 2 done-when in pandas and the repeat-failure count of 68.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch Corey Schafer's pandas episodes 5 to 8 and the Data School merge video, read the \"Why it works\" bullets on regex, `errors=\"coerce\"` and `validate`, and answer Check yourself in your head. You can try the asset regex on regex101.com in a phone browser.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 2 (planned versus reactive pivot), the `.style` gradient, Kaggle Pandas lessons 4 to 6 and the McKinney chapters can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Dirty export cleaned in pandas, matching the Excel result (1,470 rows, 1,124.02 h)\n- [ ] Merges with `validate=` on every one\n- [ ] groupby/agg summary and a styled pivot heat-map\n- [ ] Phase 2 done-when recreated in pandas\n- [ ] Repeat-failure count = 68"}],"quiz":[{"id":"37-1","n":1,"q":"What does `validate=\"many_to_one\"` protect you from?","a":"Duplicate keys in the right-hand table, which would multiply rows (fan-out) and inflate totals. pandas raises a `MergeError` instead of silently producing wrong numbers."},{"id":"37-2","n":2,"q":"What's the difference between `.agg(jobs=(\"wo_id\", \"count\"))` and `.size()`?","a":"`count` counts non-null values in that column. `size()` counts rows, nulls included. For a key column like `wo_id` (never null) they're the same."},{"id":"37-3","n":3,"q":"Why read the dirty file with `dtype=str`?","a":"So pandas doesn't guess. It might turn `005` into 5, or `1.5h` into NaN, before you've had a chance to see the problem. Read raw, inspect, then convert on purpose."},{"id":"37-4","n":4,"q":"Write the pandas for: count of PMs by `task_type`, from `pm_tasks.csv`.","a":"`pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"pm_tasks.csv\")[\"task_type\"].value_counts()`"}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: pandas episodes 5–8 (updating, adding/removing, sorting, grouping).","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Data School (Kevin Markham)**: *\"Data School pandas merge\"*.","searches":["Data School pandas merge"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"McKinney's book, chapters 7 (cleaning), 8 (merge, reshape) and 10 (groupby).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's clean of the dirty export (1,470 rows, 0 unmatched, 1,124.02 h) with `validate=` on the merge, Session 2's groupby summary and the zone by month compliance pivot, and weekend items 1 and 3: the Phase 2 done-when in pandas and the repeat-failure count of 68."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch Corey Schafer's pandas episodes 5 to 8 and the Data School merge video, read the \"Why it works\" bullets on regex, `errors=\"coerce\"` and `validate`, and answer Check yourself in your head. You can try the asset regex on regex101.com in a phone browser."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 2 (planned versus reactive pivot), the `.style` gradient, Kaggle Pandas lessons 4 to 6 and the McKinney chapters can wait."}}},{"week":38,"phase":5,"title":"pandas 3, dates and time series","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-38-pandas-time-series.md","ticks":["Weekly/monthly resampling with empty periods kept","Rolling 13 weeks matches Power BI (139.4 h)","Hour, weekday and shift patterns","Days between failures with `groupby().diff()`","Kaggle Pandas course complete"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 19 Jul – Sun 25 Jul 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nMaintenance data is time data: when things fail, how long they're down, how a vibration reading drifts. pandas has the best time-series tools of anything you've used so far. This week: datetime indexes, resampling, rolling windows and year-on-year comparisons.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use the `.dt` accessor (hour, weekday, month, period)\n- set a **DatetimeIndex** and `resample` to days, weeks or months\n- calculate `rolling` windows and `pct_change`\n- find patterns by hour and weekday","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-resampling","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Resampling","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\", parse_dates=[\"start_datetime\", \"end_datetime\"])\nbd = (dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"]\n      .set_index(\"start_datetime\")\n      .sort_index())\n\nweekly = bd[\"duration_min\"].resample(\"W-SUN\").sum() / 60      # weeks ending Sunday\nmonthly = bd[\"duration_min\"].resample(\"MS\").sum() / 60        # MS = month start label\n\nprint(len(weekly), weekly.idxmax(), round(weekly.max(), 1))\nmonthly.tail(6).round(1)\n```\n\nThere are 105 weeks. The worst week ended **4 Jan 2026**, with 39.4 h (that's the New Year's Eve bearing job from Week 10). The 2026 months run Jan 49.4, Feb 43.0, Mar 31.3, Apr 41.9, May 56.7, Jun 44.6.\n\n```python\n# Rolling 13-week total: compare with Power BI R13W in Week 19\nrolling = weekly.rolling(13).sum()\nrolling.loc[\"2026-06-28\"]          # 139.4 h, the same answer as DAX\n\n# Year on year\nyoy = monthly.pct_change(12) * 100\nyoy.loc[\"2026-06-01\"]              # -15.5 % (June 2026 vs June 2025)\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n`resample` is `groupby` for time: it buckets rows into regular periods and aggregates them. **Weeks with no breakdowns still appear** (as 0), unlike a plain groupby. That's essential for honest trend charts. `\"W-SUN\"` means weeks ending Sunday, matching a Monday-start working week.\n\n`rolling(13)` looks back 13 rows. Because the rows are now evenly spaced weeks, that's exactly 13 weeks.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-patterns-in-time","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Patterns in time","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\ndt[\"hour\"] = dt[\"start_datetime\"].dt.hour\ndt[\"weekday\"] = dt[\"start_datetime\"].dt.day_name()\ndt[\"month\"] = dt[\"start_datetime\"].dt.to_period(\"M\")\n\n# Minor stops by hour\ndt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Minor stop\"].groupby(\"hour\").size()\n\n# Breakdown hours by weekday, in Mon-Sun order\norder = [\"Monday\", \"Tuesday\", \"Wednesday\", \"Thursday\", \"Friday\", \"Saturday\", \"Sunday\"]\n(dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"]\n   .groupby(\"weekday\")[\"duration_min\"].sum()\n   .reindex(order) / 60).round(1)\n\n# Shift view: days 06:00-18:00, nights otherwise\ndt[\"shift\"] = dt[\"hour\"].between(6, 17).map({True: \"Days\", False: \"Nights\"})\ndt.pivot_table(index=\"shift\", columns=\"category\", values=\"duration_min\", aggfunc=\"sum\") / 60\n```\n\n**Time between failures with `diff`:**\n\n```python\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"raised_datetime\"])\nr = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"].sort_values([\"asset_id\", \"raised_datetime\"])\nr[\"days_since_last\"] = r.groupby(\"asset_id\")[\"raised_datetime\"].diff().dt.total_seconds() / 86400\nr.loc[r[\"asset_id\"] == \"LD-PPK-005\", [\"raised_datetime\", \"failure_code\", \"days_since_last\"]].head()\n```\n\nThat's the SQL `LAG` from Week 13 in one line. Keep `days_since_last`, because Phase 6 fits a Weibull distribution to exactly this kind of column.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Weekly reactive jobs** and their 4-week rolling mean, for Primary Packing only.\n2. **PM compliance by due week** for 2026, then a 13-week rolling compliance (hint: resample the on-time *count* and the due *count* separately, roll both, then divide).\n3. **MTTR trend**: monthly mean breakdown duration in hours. Is it getting better?\n4. **Busiest hour** for minor stops. In this data, it's **21:00**. Is that real, or noise? Look at how much the counts per hour vary before you claim a pattern. (Phase 6 gives you the statistics to answer properly.)\n5. Kaggle Learn Pandas: finish the course.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer pandas datetime\"*.\n- **Data School**: *\"Data School pandas resample\"*.\n- McKinney's book, chapter 11 (time series)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why use `resample(\"W-SUN\")` rather than `groupby(dt.dt.isocalendar().week)`?\n2. What does `rolling(13).sum()` return for the first 12 weeks, and why?\n3. `diff()` on the raised dates gives a `Timedelta`. How do you turn it into days with decimals?\n4. Your weekly chart shows a huge spike. What's the first thing you check?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `resample` keeps the real dates, fills empty weeks with 0, and never mixes up week 1 of 2025 with week 1 of 2026. A week-number groupby loses the year and drops empty weeks.\n2. `NaN`, because there aren't 13 values yet. Use `rolling(13, min_periods=1)` if you want a partial window, but be clear on the chart that the early points are partial.\n3. `.dt.total_seconds() / 86400`. (`.dt.days` gives whole days only.)\n4. The raw events behind it: is it one huge event (a single outlier like the 1,375-min bearing job) or many? Check with `bd.loc[\"2025-12-29\":\"2026-01-04\"]`.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's weekly and monthly resample, the rolling 13 weeks (139.4 h) and year on year, then Session 2's hour, weekday and shift patterns and the `groupby().diff()` days between failures. Keep `days_since_last`: Phase 6 fits a Weibull to it.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Corey Schafer pandas datetime and Data School resample videos, read both \"Why it works\" parts on `resample` keeping empty weeks, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The weekend practice items 1 to 4, finishing the Kaggle Pandas course and McKinney chapter 11 can be deferred; the sessions cover what Week 39 needs."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Weekly/monthly resampling with empty periods kept\n- [ ] Rolling 13 weeks matches Power BI (139.4 h)\n- [ ] Hour, weekday and shift patterns\n- [ ] Days between failures with `groupby().diff()`\n- [ ] Kaggle Pandas course complete"}],"quiz":[{"id":"38-1","n":1,"q":"Why use `resample(\"W-SUN\")` rather than `groupby(dt.dt.isocalendar().week)`?","a":"`resample` keeps the real dates, fills empty weeks with 0, and never mixes up week 1 of 2025 with week 1 of 2026. A week-number groupby loses the year and drops empty weeks."},{"id":"38-2","n":2,"q":"What does `rolling(13).sum()` return for the first 12 weeks, and why?","a":"`NaN`, because there aren't 13 values yet. Use `rolling(13, min_periods=1)` if you want a partial window, but be clear on the chart that the early points are partial."},{"id":"38-3","n":3,"q":"`diff()` on the raised dates gives a `Timedelta`. How do you turn it into days with decimals?","a":"`.dt.total_seconds() / 86400`. (`.dt.days` gives whole days only.)"},{"id":"38-4","n":4,"q":"Your weekly chart shows a huge spike. What's the first thing you check?","a":"The raw events behind it: is it one huge event (a single outlier like the 1,375-min bearing job) or many? Check with `bd.loc[\"2025-12-29\":\"2026-01-04\"]`."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer pandas datetime\"*.","searches":["Corey Schafer pandas datetime"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Data School**: *\"Data School pandas resample\"*.","searches":["Data School pandas resample"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"McKinney's book, chapter 11 (time series).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's weekly and monthly resample, the rolling 13 weeks (139.4 h) and year on year, then Session 2's hour, weekday and shift patterns and the `groupby().diff()` days between failures. Keep `days_since_last`: Phase 6 fits a Weibull to it."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Corey Schafer pandas datetime and Data School resample videos, read both \"Why it works\" parts on `resample` keeping empty weeks, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The weekend practice items 1 to 4, finishing the Kaggle Pandas course and McKinney chapter 11 can be deferred; the sessions cover what Week 39 needs."}}},{"week":39,"phase":5,"title":"Plotting with matplotlib, seaborn and plotly","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-39-plotting.md","ticks":["`fig, ax` style with decluttered, finding-titled charts","seaborn box plot and histogram","Interactive plotly HTML","2×2 chart pack PNG","Kaggle Data Visualization course"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 26 Jul – Sun 1 Aug 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nPython charts are code, so a chart you build once regenerates every week with new data. That's what the automated report next week relies on. This week you learn matplotlib properly (it's the base for everything else), then the quicker seaborn and interactive plotly.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build line, bar, scatter and histogram charts with matplotlib's `fig, ax` style\n- apply the same declutter rules you used in Excel and Power BI\n- make statistical charts quickly with seaborn\n- make an interactive HTML chart with plotly\n- save charts as PNG for reports","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-matplotlib-the-proper-way","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): matplotlib, the proper way","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\", parse_dates=[\"start_datetime\"])\nweekly = (dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"].set_index(\"start_datetime\")[\"duration_min\"]\n          .resample(\"W-SUN\").sum() / 60)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 4))\nax.plot(weekly.index, weekly, color=\"#9E9E9E\", linewidth=1, label=\"Weekly\")\nax.plot(weekly.index, weekly.rolling(13).mean(), color=\"#1F4E79\", linewidth=2.5, label=\"13-week average\")\nax.set_title(\"Breakdown hours are flat at about 13 h a week\", loc=\"left\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"Hours\")\nax.legend(frameon=False)\nax.spines[[\"top\", \"right\"]].set_visible(False)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig(\"breakdown_trend.png\", dpi=150)\n```\n\n**A horizontal bar chart, worst first, with one highlight colour:**\n\n```python\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nz = (dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"].merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\")\n       .groupby(\"zone\")[\"duration_min\"].sum().div(60).sort_values())\ncolours = [\"#C62828\" if v == z.max() else \"#9E9E9E\" for v in z]\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.5))\nax.barh(z.index, z.values, color=colours)\nfor i, v in enumerate(z.values):\n    ax.text(v + 3, i, f\"{v:.0f} h\", va=\"center\")\nax.set_title(\"Flaking lost the most hours to breakdowns\", loc=\"left\")\nax.spines[[\"top\", \"right\", \"bottom\"]].set_visible(False)\nax.set_xticks([])\nfig.tight_layout()\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\nAlways use `fig, ax = plt.subplots()` and then call methods on `ax`. The quick `plt.plot()` style gets confusing as soon as there's more than one chart. `fig` is the whole picture (size, saving), and `ax` is one chart inside it (data, titles, axes).\n\nThe design rules are the same in every tool: a title that states the finding, grey for context, one strong colour for the point, and no chart junk.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-seaborn-and-plotly","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): seaborn and plotly","kind":"hands","md":"### seaborn: statistical charts in one line\n\n```python\nimport seaborn as sns\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\").merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\")\nr = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"]\n\nsns.boxplot(data=r, x=\"labour_hours\", y=\"zone\", color=\"#BBDEFB\")     # spread of repair effort\nsns.histplot(data=dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"], x=\"duration_min\", bins=40, log_scale=True)\n```\n\nThe histogram on a log scale shows breakdown durations are **right-skewed**: lots of short jobs and a long tail. Keep that in mind for Phase 6, because it's why averages can mislead.\n\n### plotly: interactive HTML\n\n```python\nimport plotly.express as px\nfig = px.line(weekly.reset_index(), x=\"start_datetime\", y=\"duration_min\",\n              title=\"Weekly breakdown hours (hover for values)\")\nfig.write_html(\"weekly_breakdowns.html\")\n```\n\nDouble-click the HTML file. You can zoom, hover and pan in any browser, and send it to someone without them needing Python.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-practice","title":"Weekend practice (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"Make a **one-page chart pack** (`fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 8))`), saved as a PNG:\n\n1. Top left: PM compliance % by zone (2026), worst highlighted, with a 90 % target line (`ax.axvline`).\n2. Top right: monthly reactive jobs, 2024–26, as a line.\n3. Bottom left: Pareto of Q2 2026 breakdown hours by asset (top 10 bars, plus `ax2 = ax.twinx()` for the cumulative % line).\n4. Bottom right: scatter of MTBF vs MTTR per asset, point size = breakdown hours.\n\nGive every chart a finding-style title. Then do **Kaggle Learn: Data Visualization** ([kaggle.com/learn/data-visualization](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/data-visualization)).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: the *\"Corey Schafer matplotlib tutorial\"* series, episodes 1–3.\n- **Seaborn docs**: [seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html).\n- **Plotly Express docs**: search *\"plotly express python\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between `fig` and `ax`?\n2. How do you put two y-axes on one chart (bars in hours plus a cumulative % line)?\n3. Why plot breakdown durations on a log scale?\n4. When would you choose plotly over matplotlib?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `fig` is the whole figure (size, layout, saving). `ax` is one set of axes, where the data and labels go. A figure can hold many axes.\n2. `ax2 = ax.twinx()`, then plot the line on `ax2` and set `ax2.set_ylim(0, 100)`.\n3. The values span 5 to 1,375 minutes. On a linear scale everything bunches up at the left and the long tail is invisible. A log scale spreads them out.\n4. When the audience will explore it (hover, zoom) in a browser, or for dashboards. Use matplotlib for static, carefully designed images in reports and documents.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's two matplotlib charts in `fig, ax` style (the trend saved as PNG and the worst-first bar), Session 2's seaborn box plot and log-scale histogram plus the plotly HTML, and the weekend 2×2 chart pack with finding-style titles. Week 40 reuses these chart functions.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch Corey Schafer's matplotlib episodes 1 to 3, read both \"Why it works\" parts on `fig` versus `ax` and the declutter rules, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The Kaggle Data Visualization course and the seaborn and Plotly Express docs can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] `fig, ax` style with decluttered, finding-titled charts\n- [ ] seaborn box plot and histogram\n- [ ] Interactive plotly HTML\n- [ ] 2×2 chart pack PNG\n- [ ] Kaggle Data Visualization course"}],"quiz":[{"id":"39-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between `fig` and `ax`?","a":"`fig` is the whole figure (size, layout, saving). `ax` is one set of axes, where the data and labels go. A figure can hold many axes."},{"id":"39-2","n":2,"q":"How do you put two y-axes on one chart (bars in hours plus a cumulative % line)?","a":"`ax2 = ax.twinx()`, then plot the line on `ax2` and set `ax2.set_ylim(0, 100)`."},{"id":"39-3","n":3,"q":"Why plot breakdown durations on a log scale?","a":"The values span 5 to 1,375 minutes. On a linear scale everything bunches up at the left and the long tail is invisible. A log scale spreads them out."},{"id":"39-4","n":4,"q":"When would you choose plotly over matplotlib?","a":"When the audience will explore it (hover, zoom) in a browser, or for dashboards. Use matplotlib for static, carefully designed images in reports and documents."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: the *\"Corey Schafer matplotlib tutorial\"* series, episodes 1–3.","searches":["Corey Schafer matplotlib tutorial"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Seaborn docs**: [seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html).","searches":[],"urls":["https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html"]},{"i":2,"md":"**Plotly Express docs**: search *\"plotly express python\"*.","searches":["plotly express python"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's two matplotlib charts in `fig, ax` style (the trend saved as PNG and the worst-first bar), Session 2's seaborn box plot and log-scale histogram plus the plotly HTML, and the weekend 2×2 chart pack with finding-style titles. Week 40 reuses these chart functions."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch Corey Schafer's matplotlib episodes 1 to 3, read both \"Why it works\" parts on `fig` versus `ax` and the declutter rules, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The Kaggle Data Visualization course and the seaborn and Plotly Express docs can wait."}}},{"week":40,"phase":5,"title":"Automation, the weekly PM report script","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-40-automation-weekly-report.md","ticks":["Spec written before code","Each function tested against the expected-values table","Formatted Excel output with charts embedded","argparse options and `--help`","Runs from Task Scheduler via a batch file, with a log"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 2 Aug – Sun 8 Aug 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThis is the payoff week of Phase 5. You'll build a script that reads every CMMS export in a folder and writes a finished **Excel PM report with charts**, from one command. Then you'll schedule it to run on its own.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- structure a script into small functions with a `main()`\n- take command-line options with `argparse`\n- write formatted multi-sheet Excel files with `xlsxwriter`, including images\n- schedule a script with Windows Task Scheduler\n\n**Reference solution:** [`solutions/phase-5-python/weekly_pm_report.py`](../../solutions/phase-5-python/weekly_pm_report.py). Try building your own first, and peek only when stuck.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-design-then-the-data-functions","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Design, then the data functions","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. **Write the spec first** (as a docstring at the top of `my_work\\pm_report.py`):\n   - *Input:* a folder of `WO_Export_*.csv` files, and a report date (the last day of the week).\n   - *Output:* `PM_Report_YYYY-MM-DD.xlsx` with sheets **Summary** (KPIs + 2 charts), **Compliance by Zone** (13 weeks), **Overdue PMs**, and **Top 10 Downtime** (13 weeks).\n   - *Definitions:* on time = completed ≤ due. Overdue = PM, status Open, due before the report date.\n2. Write and test each function in a notebook, then move it into the script:\n\n   ```python\n   def load_exports(folder):          # -> one DataFrame, dates parsed, duplicates removed\n   def pm_compliance(pm, start, end, by=None)\n   def build_report(df, report_date)  # -> summary, by_zone, overdue, top10, weekly\n   ```\n\n3. **Test against known answers.** For report date 28 June 2026:\n\n   | KPI | Expected |\n   |---|---|\n   | Files loaded / work orders | 24 / 11,902 |\n   | PM compliance this week | 80.2 % (96 due) |\n   | PM compliance 13 weeks | 79.8 % (1,314 due) |\n   | Worst zone (13 wks) | Primary Packing 63.8 % |\n   | Overdue PMs | 14 |\n   | Reactive jobs, 13 weeks | 121 |\n   | Top downtime asset (13 wks) | LD-FLK-010, 9.6 h |\n\n### Why it works\n\nSmall functions that each do **one** thing can be tested on their own, and you can check each against a number you already trust (from SQL or Power BI). When the report is wrong, you'll know which function to look at.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-charts-and-excel-output","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Charts and Excel output","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Write `chart_compliance(by_zone, path)` and `chart_weekly(weekly, path)` with matplotlib (from Week 39), saving PNGs. Use `matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")` at the top so it works without a screen, which matters for scheduled runs.\n2. Write the Excel file:\n\n   ```python\n   with pd.ExcelWriter(out_file, engine=\"xlsxwriter\", date_format=\"dd/mm/yyyy\") as xw:\n       summary.to_excel(xw, sheet_name=\"Summary\", index=False, startrow=1)\n       ws = xw.sheets[\"Summary\"]\n       ws.insert_image(\"D2\", \"compliance_by_zone.png\", {\"x_scale\": 0.75, \"y_scale\": 0.75})\n       by_zone.to_excel(xw, sheet_name=\"Compliance by Zone\", index=False)\n       ...\n   ```\n\n3. Add formatting: a bold coloured header row, column widths, `freeze_panes(1, 0)`, `autofilter`, and a `0.0%` format for compliance.\n4. **Command-line options**:\n\n   ```python\n   import argparse\n   from datetime import date\n   p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Build the weekly PM report.\")\n   p.add_argument(\"--folder\", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_FOLDER)\n   p.add_argument(\"--report-date\", type=date.fromisoformat, default=date(2026, 6, 28))\n   p.add_argument(\"--out\", type=Path, default=Path(\"reports\"))\n   args = p.parse_args()\n   ```\n\n   Run it: `python my_work\\pm_report.py --report-date 2026-06-21`. Run `python my_work\\pm_report.py --help` and argparse writes the help text for you.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-make-it-run-itself","title":"Weekend (2 h): Make it run itself","kind":"hands","md":"1. **A batch file** `run_pm_report.bat` in the repo root:\n\n   ```bat\n   @echo off\n   cd /d \"%~dp0\"\n   call .venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat\n   python my_work\\pm_report.py --out reports >> reports\\log.txt 2>&1\n   ```\n\n2. **Task Scheduler**: Start → *Task Scheduler* → Create Basic Task → \"Weekly PM report\" → Weekly, Monday 06:00 → *Start a program* → browse to `run_pm_report.bat`. Right-click the task → **Run** to test it now.\n3. Check `reports\\log.txt` and the new `.xlsx`.\n4. **Make the script robust**: what happens if the folder is empty? If one export has a missing column? Add a clear error message for each (`raise SystemExit(\"…\")`) rather than a 40-line traceback.\n5. Time how long the Excel version of this report would take you by hand. Write that number in your portfolio README. That's the \"hours saved\" claim.\n\n> **At work:** never point a script at live systems or schedule anything on a work PC without permission. Build the habit at home, then propose it.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python argparse\"* (or the official argparse tutorial, search *\"argparse tutorial python docs\"*).\n- **Automate the Boring Stuff**: chapter 13 (Excel) and chapter 17 (scheduling).\n- **xlsxwriter docs**: search *\"xlsxwriter working with pandas\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does the script use `matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")`?\n2. Why should the report date be an argument, not `date.today()`?\n3. Your scheduled task \"runs\" but no report appears. What do you check?\n4. How do you stop the same work order appearing twice if an export is accidentally copied into the folder twice?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. `Agg` draws charts to files without opening a window. Scheduled tasks often run with no desktop session, and a GUI backend would fail.\n2. So you can re-run any past week, and test against known answers. The default can still be \"last Sunday\" in real use: `date.today() - timedelta(days=date.today().weekday() + 1)`.\n3. The log file (that's why output is redirected with `>> log.txt 2>&1`), the task's *Start in* folder, whether the venv path is right, and the task's *Last Run Result* code.\n4. `df.drop_duplicates(subset=\"WO Number\", keep=\"last\")` after loading. The reference script does this.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's spec and the three data functions tested against the expected-values table, Session 2 steps 1, 2 and 4 (charts with `Agg`, the multi-sheet Excel with images, argparse), and weekend steps 1 to 4 (batch file, Task Scheduler, log, a clear error on an empty folder). Week 42's done-when runs this script, so give it two sittings if you need them.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Corey Schafer argparse video, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapters 13 and 17 and the \"Why it works\" note on small testable functions, and answer Check yourself in your head. On the bus, estimate how long this report would take you by hand in Excel for weekend step 5.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Session 2 step 3 (bold header, column widths, freeze panes, autofilter) is cosmetic and can wait, along with the xlsxwriter docs."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Spec written before code\n- [ ] Each function tested against the expected-values table\n- [ ] Formatted Excel output with charts embedded\n- [ ] argparse options and `--help`\n- [ ] Runs from Task Scheduler via a batch file, with a log"}],"quiz":[{"id":"40-1","n":1,"q":"Why does the script use `matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")`?","a":"`Agg` draws charts to files without opening a window. Scheduled tasks often run with no desktop session, and a GUI backend would fail."},{"id":"40-2","n":2,"q":"Why should the report date be an argument, not `date.today()`?","a":"So you can re-run any past week, and test against known answers. The default can still be \"last Sunday\" in real use: `date.today() - timedelta(days=date.today().weekday() + 1)`."},{"id":"40-3","n":3,"q":"Your scheduled task \"runs\" but no report appears. What do you check?","a":"The log file (that's why output is redirected with `>> log.txt 2>&1`), the task's *Start in* folder, whether the venv path is right, and the task's *Last Run Result* code."},{"id":"40-4","n":4,"q":"How do you stop the same work order appearing twice if an export is accidentally copied into the folder twice?","a":"`df.drop_duplicates(subset=\"WO Number\", keep=\"last\")` after loading. The reference script does this."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Corey Schafer**: *\"Corey Schafer Python argparse\"* (or the official argparse tutorial, search *\"argparse tutorial python docs\"*).","searches":["Corey Schafer Python argparse","argparse tutorial python docs"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Automate the Boring Stuff**: chapter 13 (Excel) and chapter 17 (scheduling).","searches":[],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**xlsxwriter docs**: search *\"xlsxwriter working with pandas\"*.","searches":["xlsxwriter working with pandas"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's spec and the three data functions tested against the expected-values table, Session 2 steps 1, 2 and 4 (charts with `Agg`, the multi-sheet Excel with images, argparse), and weekend steps 1 to 4 (batch file, Task Scheduler, log, a clear error on an empty folder). Week 42's done-when runs this script, so give it two sittings if you need them."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Corey Schafer argparse video, read *Automate the Boring Stuff* chapters 13 and 17 and the \"Why it works\" note on small testable functions, and answer Check yourself in your head. On the bus, estimate how long this report would take you by hand in Excel for weekend step 5."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Session 2 step 3 (bold header, column widths, freeze panes, autofilter) is cosmetic and can wait, along with the xlsxwriter docs."}}},{"week":41,"phase":5,"title":"Vibration trend analysis","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-41-vibration-trend-analysis.md","ticks":["Trend plotted with `transform` + rolling, failed bearings highlighted","Absolute and baseline-relative limits applied","Warning-days table matching the expected values","Can explain the P-F interval and route frequency using your own data","Packaged as `vibration_trend.py` with argparse"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 9 Aug – Sun 15 Aug 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nCondition monitoring produces exactly the kind of data Excel struggles with: lots of readings, many sensors, and you need trends, thresholds and \"how much warning did we get?\". This week you build a reusable trend analysis, ready to point at real vibration-system exports once they exist.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- trend overall vibration (RMS velocity) per bearing, with rolling smoothing\n- apply alert and alarm limits, both absolute and relative to baseline\n- measure the **warning time** each alarm gave before failure\n- explain the P-F interval with your own data\n\n**Data:** `data/vibration/bearing_trend.csv`. That's daily readings from 6 dryer fan bearings, July 2025 – June 2026. Two of them degrade and fail.\n\n**Reference solution:** [`solutions/phase-5-python/vibration_trend.py`](../../solutions/phase-5-python/vibration_trend.py)","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-explore-and-trend","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Explore and trend","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nv = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"vibration\" / \"bearing_trend.csv\", parse_dates=[\"date\"])\nv.info()\nv.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[[\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\"]].agg([\"min\", \"median\", \"max\"]).round(2)\nv[v[\"status\"] == \"Failed\"]\n```\n\nBearings **B2** and **B5** have a `Failed` row. B5 failed on 15 Feb 2026, and B2 on 26 May 2026. Their readings stop there.\n\n```python\nv = v.sort_values([\"bearing_id\", \"date\"])\nv[\"rms_7d\"] = v.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"rms_velocity_mm_s\"].transform(lambda s: s.rolling(7, min_periods=1).mean())\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 4.5))\nfor bid, g in v.groupby(\"bearing_id\"):\n    failed = (g[\"status\"] == \"Failed\").any()\n    ax.plot(g[\"date\"], g[\"rms_7d\"], color=\"#C62828\" if failed else \"#9E9E9E\",\n            linewidth=2 if failed else 1, label=bid if failed else None)\nax.legend(frameon=False)\nax.set_ylabel(\"RMS velocity, 7-day mean (mm/s)\")\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\n**`transform`** applies a function per group but returns a result the same length as the original, so it lines up row for row. That's what you want for a rolling value per bearing. (`agg` would collapse it to one row per bearing.)\n\n**Overall RMS velocity** (mm/s) is the standard \"how rough is it running\" number for rotating machines. It's good at catching imbalance, misalignment and looseness, and late-stage bearing damage. **Kurtosis** and **crest factor** react to the sharp impacts of an *early* bearing defect, often before RMS rises. Compare when each one starts to climb.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-alarms-and-warning-time","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Alarms and warning time","kind":"hands","md":"### Limits\n\nTwo common approaches. Use both.\n\n1. **Absolute limits** from a standard. ISO 10816-3 / ISO 20816-3 give guide values for industrial machines by size and foundation type. For a medium machine on a rigid foundation, typical zone boundaries are about **1.4 / 2.8 / 4.5 mm/s RMS** (good / acceptable / alert / alarm). *Check the standard and your site's CM policy for the real values for each machine.*\n2. **Relative to baseline**: alarm when the trend reaches, for example, **2× its own normal**. This works even when you don't know the right absolute value for a machine, and it catches change early on machines that normally run very smoothly.\n\n### Do it\n\n```python\nv[\"baseline\"] = v.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"rms_velocity_mm_s\"].transform(lambda s: s.iloc[:30].median())\nv[\"x_baseline\"] = v[\"rms_7d\"] / v[\"baseline\"]\n\ndef first_date(df, col, limit):\n    return df[df[col] >= limit].groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"date\"].min()\n\nfail = v[v[\"status\"] == \"Failed\"].set_index(\"bearing_id\")[\"date\"]\nsummary = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"alert_2.8\": first_date(v, \"rms_7d\", 2.8),\n    \"alarm_4.5\": first_date(v, \"rms_7d\", 4.5),\n    \"2x_baseline\": first_date(v, \"x_baseline\", 2.0),\n    \"failed\": fail,\n})\nfor c in [\"alert_2.8\", \"alarm_4.5\", \"2x_baseline\"]:\n    summary[f\"warn_days_{c}\"] = (summary[\"failed\"] - summary[c]).dt.days\nsummary\n```\n\n**Expected:**\n\n| Bearing | Alert (2.8) | Alarm (4.5) | 2× baseline | Failed | Warning from alert | from alarm |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DRY-FAN-B2 | 23 Mar 2026 | 23 Apr 2026 | 7 Apr 2026 | 26 May 2026 | **64 days** | 33 days |\n| DRY-FAN-B5 | 16 Jan 2026 | 3 Feb 2026 | 17 Jan 2026 | 15 Feb 2026 | **30 days** | 12 days |\n\nThe four healthy bearings never crossed any limit. That's **no false alarms**, which matters as much as catching the real ones.\n\n### Why it matters: the P-F interval\n\nThe **P-F curve**: a defect starts (P = *potential* failure, detectable) and eventually becomes a functional failure (F). The **P-F interval** is the warning time you get. Your inspection interval must be **shorter** than the P-F interval, often half of it, or you'll miss failures between checks.\n\nHere B5's alert gave only 30 days of warning. **A monthly vibration route could miss it entirely.** That's the argument for continuous wireless sensors on critical fans, made with your own numbers. You'll go deeper in Phase 6.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend-package-it","title":"Weekend (2 h): Package it","kind":"hands","md":"1. Turn your notebook into `my_work\\vibration_trend.py`, with `load_readings()`, `add_trend_features()`, `summarise()` and `plot()`, and argparse options for `--csv`, `--alert` and `--alarm`. Keep `load_readings()` as the **only** function that knows the file layout. When real exports arrive, only that function changes.\n2. Add kurtosis to the plot (a second subplot). Does kurtosis rise before RMS for B5?\n3. **Optional, real data:** the **NASA IMS bearing** run-to-failure data set (search *\"NASA prognostics data repository IMS bearing\"*) has raw vibration snapshots from real test-rig bearings running to failure. It's large (~6 GB unzipped). If you download it, compute RMS per snapshot file and trend it with your same functions. Phase 7 uses it again.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Mobius Institute** (YouTube): search *\"Mobius Institute P-F curve\"* and *\"Mobius Institute vibration analysis basics\"*.\n- Search *\"overall vibration trending ISO 10816 explained\"* and pick a short explainer from a CM vendor or reliability channel."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why use a 7-day rolling mean before comparing to limits?\n2. Why have a relative (× baseline) limit as well as an absolute one?\n3. B5 gave 30 days' warning from the alert limit. What's the longest route interval that would reliably catch it?\n4. Why keep the file-reading logic in a single function?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Daily readings are noisy. One spike shouldn't trigger an alarm. Smoothing trades a few days of reaction time for far fewer false alarms. (Real CM systems often require *n* consecutive readings over the limit for the same reason.)\n2. Machines differ: a bearing that normally runs at 1.0 mm/s and rises to 2.5 has doubled (worth acting on) but is still under an absolute 2.8 alert. A relative limit catches change. An absolute limit catches machines that are simply rough.\n3. Roughly half the P-F interval, so about **2 weeks**. With monthly routes, the reading before failure might be taken before the defect is detectable, and the next one after the bearing has already failed.\n4. So the analysis can be reused on any data source, whether a different CM system's export or the NASA data, by changing one function. That's called *separating I/O from logic*.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's 7-day trend with `transform` and the failed bearings highlighted, Session 2's absolute and 2× baseline limits with the warning-days table (64 and 30 days from the alert), and weekend step 1 packaging it as `vibration_trend.py` with argparse. Week 42's portfolio uses that script.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the Mobius Institute P-F curve and vibration basics videos, read \"Why it matters: the P-F interval\" and the RMS versus kurtosis note, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask what alert and alarm limits your site's CM policy uses.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 2 (the kurtosis subplot) and step 3 (the optional NASA IMS download) can be skipped."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Trend plotted with `transform` + rolling, failed bearings highlighted\n- [ ] Absolute and baseline-relative limits applied\n- [ ] Warning-days table matching the expected values\n- [ ] Can explain the P-F interval and route frequency using your own data\n- [ ] Packaged as `vibration_trend.py` with argparse"}],"quiz":[{"id":"41-1","n":1,"q":"Why use a 7-day rolling mean before comparing to limits?","a":"Daily readings are noisy. One spike shouldn't trigger an alarm. Smoothing trades a few days of reaction time for far fewer false alarms. (Real CM systems often require *n* consecutive readings over the limit for the same reason.)"},{"id":"41-2","n":2,"q":"Why have a relative (× baseline) limit as well as an absolute one?","a":"Machines differ: a bearing that normally runs at 1.0 mm/s and rises to 2.5 has doubled (worth acting on) but is still under an absolute 2.8 alert. A relative limit catches change. An absolute limit catches machines that are simply rough."},{"id":"41-3","n":3,"q":"B5 gave 30 days' warning from the alert limit. What's the longest route interval that would reliably catch it?","a":"Roughly half the P-F interval, so about **2 weeks**. With monthly routes, the reading before failure might be taken before the defect is detectable, and the next one after the bearing has already failed."},{"id":"41-4","n":4,"q":"Why keep the file-reading logic in a single function?","a":"So the analysis can be reused on any data source, whether a different CM system's export or the NASA data, by changing one function. That's called *separating I/O from logic*."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Mobius Institute** (YouTube): search *\"Mobius Institute P-F curve\"* and *\"Mobius Institute vibration analysis basics\"*.","searches":["Mobius Institute P-F curve","Mobius Institute vibration analysis basics"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"overall vibration trending ISO 10816 explained\"* and pick a short explainer from a CM vendor or reliability channel.","searches":["overall vibration trending ISO 10816 explained"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's 7-day trend with `transform` and the failed bearings highlighted, Session 2's absolute and 2× baseline limits with the warning-days table (64 and 30 days from the alert), and weekend step 1 packaging it as `vibration_trend.py` with argparse. Week 42's portfolio uses that script."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the Mobius Institute P-F curve and vibration basics videos, read \"Why it matters: the P-F interval\" and the RMS versus kurtosis note, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask what alert and alarm limits your site's CM policy uses."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 2 (the kurtosis subplot) and step 3 (the optional NASA IMS download) can be skipped."}}},{"week":42,"phase":5,"title":"Phase 5 done-when, portfolio, and the PLC stretch","path":"course/phase-5-python/week-42-phase5-done-when-plc.md","ticks":["Done-when passed: one command, three weeks, all KPIs correct","Ruff clean, docstrings, clear errors","Portfolio folders 04 and 05 with READMEs and screenshots","pycomm3 stretch attempted (rig) or simulated (no rig)"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 5 · Python for data** · Mon 16 Aug – Sun 22 Aug 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n> **Done when:** a task that took you an hour a week in Excel now runs from a **single command**.\n\nThis week you finish and prove that, package two portfolio projects, and (as a stretch) read live tags from a PLC with Python.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-done-when-test","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Done-when test","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n1. Close everything. Open a **fresh** terminal in the repo.\n2. Run your report with one command:\n\n   ```powershell\n   .\\.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n   python my_work\\pm_report.py --report-date 2026-06-28\n   ```\n\n3. Open the Excel file. Check every KPI against the Week 40 expected-values table.\n4. Run it for **three different weeks** (`--report-date 2026-06-14`, `2026-05-31`, `2026-03-29`). Each run should work with no edits to the code.\n5. Time it. Compare that with your estimate of doing the same report by hand in Excel.\n6. If all of that holds: **Phase 5 passed.** 🎉\n\n### Code quality pass\n\n- Every function has a docstring.\n- There are no hard-coded paths except defaults built from `Path(__file__)`.\n- The script fails with a clear message (not a traceback) on an empty folder.\n- Run `pip install ruff` then `ruff check my_work`, and fix what it reports. *Linting* is how professionals keep code tidy.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-portfolio","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Portfolio","kind":"hands","md":"Create two folders in your portfolio repo:\n\n**`05-automated-pm-report/`**\n- `pm_report.py`, `requirements.txt`, `run_pm_report.bat`\n- `sample_output/`: one generated Excel report and the PNG charts\n- `README.md`: the problem (*\"a weekly PM report took ~1 h of copy-paste\"*), what it does, how to run it, the KPI definitions, and the time saved\n\n**`06-vibration-trend/`**\n- `vibration_trend.py` and the trend chart\n- `README.md`: the P-F story in plain English. *\"Two bearings failed. An alert at 2.8 mm/s gave 30 and 64 days' warning. With monthly routes, B5 could have been missed, which supports continuous monitoring on critical fans.\"*\n\nA good README has a **screenshot at the top**. People decide in five seconds whether to keep reading.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-stretch-reading-plc-tags-with-py","title":"Weekend stretch (2 h): Reading PLC tags with pycomm3","kind":"hands","md":"You work with Allen-Bradley kit, so this is the bridge between your two worlds. [`pycomm3`](https://github.com/ottowayi/pycomm3) is an open-source Python library that talks EtherNet/IP to Logix controllers (ControlLogix, CompactLogix) and, through `SLCDriver`, to SLC 500 and MicroLogix.\n\n> ⚠️ **Safety and permission first.** Only ever connect to a **training rig or bench PLC** you're authorised to use, on an isolated network. **Never** connect to a production controller without written permission from the controls/automation owner. Even read-only traffic can affect a busy controller or network. **Never write tags** to anything but a bench rig.\n\n### If you have access to a bench CompactLogix/ControlLogix\n\n```python\nfrom pycomm3 import LogixDriver\n\nwith LogixDriver(\"192.168.1.10\") as plc:        # the bench PLC's IP (and /slot if needed)\n    print(plc.info)                               # controller name, revision\n    print(plc.read(\"Motor1_Running\"))             # one tag\n    print(plc.read(\"Motor1_Current\", \"Line_Speed\"))\n    tags = plc.get_tag_list()                     # browse the controller's tags\n```\n\nThen log a tag every 5 seconds to CSV for 10 minutes, and plot it with your Week 39 code. Notice what you've built: a tiny **historian**. Real ones (FactoryTalk Historian, AVEVA PI) do the same at scale, and they're where real process data lives for Phases 6 and 7.\n\n### If you don't have a rig\n\n- Read the pycomm3 docs and examples. Note how `read()` returns a `Tag` object with `.value` and `.error`.\n- Write the logging loop anyway, against a **fake** reader:\n  ```python\n  import random, time\n  def read_tag(name):\n      return 20 + random.gauss(0, 0.5)\n  ```\n  When you get rig access, you swap one function. (It's the same \"separate I/O from logic\" idea as Week 41.)\n- Ask at work whether there's a training panel you could use under supervision.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **pycomm3 docs**: search *\"pycomm3 documentation LogixDriver\"*.\n- **Ruff**: search *\"ruff python linter\"* and skim the quick start.\n- Search *\"what is a process historian\"* for a short explainer. It's worth understanding before Phase 6."},{"id":"check-yourself-phase-5-review","title":"Check yourself: Phase 5 review","kind":"check","md":"1. Write a pandas pipeline from memory: read `work_orders.csv`, keep Reactive, merge zone, and count jobs per zone, sorted.\n2. What does `validate=\"many_to_one\"` do in a merge?\n3. Explain `resample` vs `groupby` for time data.\n4. What are the three parts of a scheduled-script setup on Windows?\n5. Why should a production PLC never be your test target?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. ```python\n   (pd.read_csv(\"work_orders.csv\")\n      .query(\"wo_type == 'Reactive'\")\n      .merge(pd.read_csv(\"assets.csv\")[[\"asset_id\", \"zone\"]], on=\"asset_id\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\n      .groupby(\"zone\").size().sort_values(ascending=False))\n   ```\n2. It checks that each key on the right appears only once, and raises an error instead of silently duplicating rows.\n3. `resample` buckets by regular time periods using a DatetimeIndex, and keeps empty periods. `groupby` groups by whatever values exist, so empty periods disappear.\n4. The script (with argparse defaults), a batch file that activates the venv and logs output, and a Task Scheduler trigger.\n5. Safety, production risk, and company policy: extra network and CPU load, the chance of a bad write, and it's someone else's system. Only use authorised bench equipment.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 5 done-when, so it isn't skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (one command from a fresh terminal, KPIs checked, three more report dates with no code edits, timed), the code quality pass with Ruff, and both Session 2 portfolio folders with READMEs and screenshots.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Do the Phase 5 review in Check yourself in your head, read the pycomm3 docs and a \"what is a process historian\" explainer, draft the P-F story for the `06-vibration-trend` README on paper, and ask at work whether there's a training panel you could use under supervision.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The weekend pycomm3 stretch, on a rig or with the fake reader, is the only part you can drop this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] **Done-when passed**: one command, three weeks, all KPIs correct\n- [ ] Ruff clean, docstrings, clear errors\n- [ ] Portfolio folders 04 and 05 with READMEs and screenshots\n- [ ] pycomm3 stretch attempted (rig) or simulated (no rig)"}],"quiz":[{"id":"42-1","n":1,"q":"Write a pandas pipeline from memory: read `work_orders.csv`, keep Reactive, merge zone, and count jobs per zone, sorted.","a":"```python\n(pd.read_csv(\"work_orders.csv\")\n   .query(\"wo_type == 'Reactive'\")\n   .merge(pd.read_csv(\"assets.csv\")[[\"asset_id\", \"zone\"]], on=\"asset_id\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\n   .groupby(\"zone\").size().sort_values(ascending=False))\n```"},{"id":"42-2","n":2,"q":"What does `validate=\"many_to_one\"` do in a merge?","a":"It checks that each key on the right appears only once, and raises an error instead of silently duplicating rows."},{"id":"42-3","n":3,"q":"Explain `resample` vs `groupby` for time data.","a":"`resample` buckets by regular time periods using a DatetimeIndex, and keeps empty periods. `groupby` groups by whatever values exist, so empty periods disappear."},{"id":"42-4","n":4,"q":"What are the three parts of a scheduled-script setup on Windows?","a":"The script (with argparse defaults), a batch file that activates the venv and logs output, and a Task Scheduler trigger."},{"id":"42-5","n":5,"q":"Why should a production PLC never be your test target?","a":"Safety, production risk, and company policy: extra network and CPU load, the chance of a bad write, and it's someone else's system. Only use authorised bench equipment."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**pycomm3 docs**: search *\"pycomm3 documentation LogixDriver\"*.","searches":["pycomm3 documentation LogixDriver"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Ruff**: search *\"ruff python linter\"* and skim the quick start.","searches":["ruff python linter"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"what is a process historian\"* for a short explainer. It's worth understanding before Phase 6.","searches":["what is a process historian"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 5 done-when, so it isn't skippable: Session 1 steps 1 to 6 (one command from a fresh terminal, KPIs checked, three more report dates with no code edits, timed), the code quality pass with Ruff, and both Session 2 portfolio folders with READMEs and screenshots."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Do the Phase 5 review in Check yourself in your head, read the pycomm3 docs and a \"what is a process historian\" explainer, draft the P-F story for the `06-vibration-trend` README on paper, and ask at work whether there's a training panel you could use under supervision."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The weekend pycomm3 stretch, on a rig or with the fake reader, is the only part you can drop this week."}}},{"week":43,"phase":6,"title":"Descriptive statistics and distributions","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-43-descriptive-stats-distributions.md","ticks":["Can explain why mean ≠ median for downtime (112 vs 73 min)","Percentiles used to answer a planning question","Four distributions described in my own words","Lognormal found to be the best fit for repair time, by AIC","Khan Academy units done"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 23 Aug – Sun 29 Aug 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 6 goal:** defend a PM interval change **with numbers**, in front of an engineering manager.\n\nThis is the phase that turns \"good with data\" into reliability engineering. It starts with how numbers are *spread*, because maintenance data is almost never the nice bell curve from school, and using the wrong summary leads to wrong decisions.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- choose mean vs median, and explain standard deviation and percentiles\n- recognise skewed data, and why averages mislead with it\n- describe the normal, exponential, lognormal and Weibull distributions in plain English\n- fit distributions in Python and compare them\n\n**Work in:** `my_work\\week35.ipynb`. Reference code is in [`solutions/phase-6-reliability/w43_w45_statistics.py`](../../solutions/phase-6-reliability/w43_w45_statistics.py).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-summarising-spread","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Summarising spread","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\")\nd = dt.loc[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\", \"duration_min\"]\nd.describe().round(1)\nstats.skew(d), d.quantile([0.5, 0.9, 0.99])\n```\n\n| Stat | Value (minutes) |\n|---|---|\n| count | 710 |\n| mean | 111.7 |\n| median (50 %) | **73** |\n| std | 129.9 |\n| 90th percentile | 242 |\n| max | 1,375 |\n| skew | 4.4 |\n\n**The mean (112) is 50 % higher than the median (73).** A handful of very long jobs drag the mean up. If someone asks \"how long is a typical breakdown?\", the honest answer is **about 73 minutes; 1 in 10 takes over 4 hours**.\n\nPlot it: `d.plot.hist(bins=50)`, then on a log scale `np.log10(d).plot.hist(bins=30)`. The log version looks almost symmetrical, which is a clue for Session 2.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- **Mean** = add up and divide. It's sensitive to outliers. Use it when the total matters (total downtime = mean × count).\n- **Median** = the middle value. It's robust. Use it to describe a \"typical\" case.\n- **Standard deviation** = the typical distance from the mean. With skewed data it's hard to interpret on its own.\n- **Percentiles** (P90, P95) answer planning questions directly: \"how long should we allow for 90 % of jobs?\"\n- **Skew > 1** means a long right tail. Repair times, downtime and costs are almost always right-skewed.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-four-distributions-every-relia","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Four distributions every reliability engineer uses","kind":"hands","md":"| Distribution | Shape | Typical maintenance use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal** | symmetric bell | measurement error, fill weights, dimensions (Week 46 SPC) |\n| **Exponential** | steep decay, *memoryless* | time between **random** failures (electronics, many controls faults). Constant failure rate. |\n| **Lognormal** | right-skewed, log is normal | **repair times**, fatigue crack growth |\n| **Weibull** | flexible: shape β changes everything | **time to failure** of components. The workhorse of reliability (Weeks 48–49). |\n\n### Do it: which fits repair time best?\n\n```python\nfor name, dist in [(\"norm\", stats.norm), (\"expon\", stats.expon),\n                   (\"lognorm\", stats.lognorm), (\"weibull_min\", stats.weibull_min)]:\n    params = dist.fit(d) if name == \"norm\" else dist.fit(d, floc=0)\n    ll = np.sum(dist.logpdf(d, *params))\n    k = len(params) - (0 if name == \"norm\" else 1)\n    print(f\"{name:12} AIC = {2*k - 2*ll:,.0f}\")\n```\n\n| Distribution | AIC (lower is better) |\n|---|---|\n| normal | 8,929 |\n| exponential | 8,118 |\n| Weibull | 8,108 |\n| **lognormal** | **7,949** ✅ |\n\nLognormal wins clearly, which is the textbook result for repair times. The normal distribution is the worst, because it would predict *negative* repair times.\n\n### Why it works\n\n**AIC** (Akaike Information Criterion) scores how well a distribution fits, with a penalty for extra parameters. Compare it only between fits to the **same** data. Always look at a plot too: overlay the fitted curves on the histogram, or use a probability plot (Week 48).\n\nThe **exponential** distribution's \"memoryless\" property means a part that has run 1,000 hours is as likely to fail in the next hour as a new one. That's true for random failures, and it's why **time-based replacement can't help them**. Hold on to that idea; it's the core of Weeks 48–49.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Khan Academy Statistics**: [khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability](https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability). Do the units *Summarizing quantitative data* and *Modeling data distributions*.\n2. Describe the **labour hours** of reactive jobs: mean, median, P90 and skew. Which zone has the most variable repair effort? Use the coefficient of variation, std ÷ mean.\n3. Fit lognormal and Weibull to reactive labour hours and compare the AICs.\n4. For **flow wrappers**, compute the days between successive breakdowns (any failure code) and plot the histogram. Does it look exponential? (It should, roughly. Mixing several failure modes together tends to look random. Week 48 separates them.)","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest with Josh Starmer** (YouTube): *\"StatQuest histograms\"*, *\"StatQuest the normal distribution\"*, *\"StatQuest mean variance standard deviation\"*. It's the clearest stats teaching anywhere.\n- **3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown binomial distributions probability\"*, for intuition.\n- Book: *Practical Statistics for Data Scientists* (O'Reilly), chapter 1."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Your manager wants \"average downtime per breakdown\" for a budget. Mean or median?\n2. Why can't a normal distribution model repair times?\n3. What does \"memoryless\" mean for a failure mode, and what does it imply for PM?\n4. Why compare AIC only between fits on the same data?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. For a **budget** (a total), use the **mean**: total = mean × count. To describe a *typical* job, use the median. Say which one you're using.\n2. It's symmetric and allows negative values, while repair times are positive and right-skewed.\n3. The chance of failing in the next hour doesn't depend on age. Replacing the part early gives no benefit, because a new one is as likely to fail. Look for condition monitoring, redesign, or run to failure instead.\n4. AIC is based on likelihood, whose size depends on the data set. Comparing across data sets is meaningless.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's `describe`, skew and percentiles on breakdown durations (mean 112 against median 73), the two histograms, and the Session 2 AIC comparison showing lognormal fits repair time best. The memoryless idea comes back in Weeks 48 and 49.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest videos on histograms, the normal distribution and mean, variance and standard deviation, read both \"Why it works\" parts and the four-distributions table, work through the Khan Academy units in your phone browser, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend items 2 to 4 (labour hours spread, the labour-hours fits and the flow wrapper gap histogram), the 3Blue1Brown video and the *Practical Statistics* chapter can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Can explain why mean ≠ median for downtime (112 vs 73 min)\n- [ ] Percentiles used to answer a planning question\n- [ ] Four distributions described in my own words\n- [ ] Lognormal found to be the best fit for repair time, by AIC\n- [ ] Khan Academy units done"}],"quiz":[{"id":"43-1","n":1,"q":"Your manager wants \"average downtime per breakdown\" for a budget. Mean or median?","a":"For a **budget** (a total), use the **mean**: total = mean × count. To describe a *typical* job, use the median. Say which one you're using."},{"id":"43-2","n":2,"q":"Why can't a normal distribution model repair times?","a":"It's symmetric and allows negative values, while repair times are positive and right-skewed."},{"id":"43-3","n":3,"q":"What does \"memoryless\" mean for a failure mode, and what does it imply for PM?","a":"The chance of failing in the next hour doesn't depend on age. Replacing the part early gives no benefit, because a new one is as likely to fail. Look for condition monitoring, redesign, or run to failure instead."},{"id":"43-4","n":4,"q":"Why compare AIC only between fits on the same data?","a":"AIC is based on likelihood, whose size depends on the data set. Comparing across data sets is meaningless."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest with Josh Starmer** (YouTube): *\"StatQuest histograms\"*, *\"StatQuest the normal distribution\"*, *\"StatQuest mean variance standard deviation\"*. It's the clearest stats teaching anywhere.","searches":["StatQuest histograms","StatQuest the normal distribution","StatQuest mean variance standard deviation"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown binomial distributions probability\"*, for intuition.","searches":["3Blue1Brown binomial distributions probability"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book: *Practical Statistics for Data Scientists* (O'Reilly), chapter 1.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's `describe`, skew and percentiles on breakdown durations (mean 112 against median 73), the two histograms, and the Session 2 AIC comparison showing lognormal fits repair time best. The memoryless idea comes back in Weeks 48 and 49."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest videos on histograms, the normal distribution and mean, variance and standard deviation, read both \"Why it works\" parts and the four-distributions table, work through the Khan Academy units in your phone browser, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend items 2 to 4 (labour hours spread, the labour-hours fits and the flow wrapper gap histogram), the 3Blue1Brown video and the *Practical Statistics* chapter can wait."}}},{"week":44,"phase":6,"title":"Confidence intervals and hypothesis testing","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-44-confidence-intervals-hypothesis-tests.md","ticks":["MTTR with 95 % CIs, both by formula and by bootstrap","t-test, Mann-Whitney, chi-square and binomial rate test run and interpreted","Can explain what a p-value is *not*","Before/after test plan written for a PM change"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 30 Aug – Sun 5 Sep 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n\"Breakdowns are down 10 % this year!\" Is that real, or luck? This week gives you the tools to answer, so you don't celebrate (or panic about) noise, and you can show that a real change is real.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- calculate and explain a 95 % confidence interval (CI), both by formula and by bootstrap\n- run a t-test, a Mann-Whitney test, a chi-square test and a rate test\n- interpret a p-value correctly, and know what it does *not* mean\n- tell the difference between statistically significant and practically important","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-confidence-intervals","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Confidence intervals","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\")\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nbd = dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"].merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\")\nppk = bd.loc[bd[\"zone_code\"] == \"PPK\", \"duration_min\"] / 60\nflk = bd.loc[bd[\"zone_code\"] == \"FLK\", \"duration_min\"] / 60\n\nfor name, x in ((\"PPK\", ppk), (\"FLK\", flk)):\n    lo, hi = stats.t.interval(0.95, len(x) - 1, loc=x.mean(), scale=stats.sem(x))\n    print(f\"{name}: n={len(x)}, MTTR={x.mean():.2f} h, 95% CI {lo:.2f}-{hi:.2f}\")\n```\n\n| Zone | n | MTTR | 95 % CI |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Primary Packing | 193 | 1.17 h | 1.01 – 1.33 |\n| Flaking | 73 | 3.88 h | 2.83 – 4.93 |\n\n**Bootstrap**, a CI without formulas, by resampling:\n\n```python\nrng = np.random.default_rng(1)\nboot = [rng.choice(ppk, len(ppk)).mean() for _ in range(5000)]\nnp.percentile(boot, [2.5, 97.5])      # about [1.02, 1.33]\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\nA 95 % CI is a range built by a method that captures the true value 95 % of the time. Wider means less certain: Flaking's interval is wide because there are fewer breakdowns and they vary more. **Always report a CI with an MTTR or MTBF based on a few dozen events.** \"3.9 h, but it could be anywhere from 2.8 to 4.9\" is far more honest than \"3.88 h\".\n\nThe **bootstrap** pretends your sample is the population, resamples it thousands of times, and looks at how much the mean wobbles. It works for medians, percentiles and anything else, with no distribution assumptions.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-hypothesis-tests","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Hypothesis tests","kind":"hands","md":"The logic: assume **no difference** (the *null hypothesis*). If data at least this extreme would be very unlikely under that assumption (p < 0.05), reject it.\n\n### Do it\n\n**1. Are flaking repairs really longer than packing?**\n\n```python\nstats.ttest_ind(flk, ppk, equal_var=False)     # Welch t-test: p ≈ 0.0000025\nstats.mannwhitneyu(flk, ppk)                   # rank test, no normality needed: p ≈ 4e-13\n```\n\nYes, overwhelmingly. (Use Mann-Whitney when the data is skewed, which repair times always are.)\n\n**2. Is Primary Packing's PM compliance really worse? (H1 2026)**\n\n```python\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"due_date\", \"completed_date\"])\npm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"].merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\")\npm[\"on_time\"] = pm[\"completed_date\"].le(pm[\"due_date\"])\nh1 = pm[pm[\"due_date\"].between(\"2026-01-01\", \"2026-06-30\")]\ntable = pd.crosstab(h1[\"zone_code\"] == \"PPK\", h1[\"on_time\"])\nstats.chi2_contingency(table)[:2]       # chi2 ≈ 91.9, p ≈ 1e-21\n```\n\n356 of 550 on time (64.7 %) vs 1,729 of 2,073 (83.4 %) for the rest of the line. That's not chance.\n\n**3. Did breakdowns really fall this year?**\n\nH1 2025 had **261** reactive jobs. H1 2026 had **236**, which is 10 % fewer. If the underlying rate hadn't changed, each failure would be equally likely to land in either half-year:\n\n```python\nstats.binomtest(236, 261 + 236, 0.5).pvalue     # ≈ 0.28\n```\n\n**p = 0.28. This is not evidence of improvement.** A 10 % swing happens by chance more than a quarter of the time. Anyone claiming credit for it, or blaming someone for the reverse, is reading noise.\n\n### Why it works (and what p-values are not)\n\n- p = the probability of seeing data this extreme **if there were no real effect**.\n- It is **not** the probability that the effect is real.\n- p > 0.05 does **not** prove \"no effect\". It means \"not enough evidence yet\". More data might show it.\n- With huge data sets, tiny unimportant differences become \"significant\". Always ask **how big** the difference is, too (the *effect size*), and whether it matters in £ or hours.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **CI for flow wrapper MTBF**: the failures are a count, so use the chi-square method for MTBF bounds. Look up *\"MTBF confidence interval chi-square\"*, implement it with `stats.chi2.ppf`, and compare it with a bootstrap of the days between failures.\n2. **Days vs Nights**: do breakdowns take longer to fix on night shift? (Use 06:00–18:00 = Days on `start_datetime`.) Run Mann-Whitney. Report the medians and the p-value in one sentence.\n3. **A before/after design.** Imagine the jaw replacement interval on flow wrappers changes on 1 July. Write down *before* you'd see any data: what you'll measure, over how long, which test you'll use, and what result would convince you. (This is how you avoid fooling yourself. Decide the test first.)\n4. Khan Academy: *Confidence intervals* and *Significance tests (hypothesis testing)*.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest p-values clearly explained\"*, *\"StatQuest confidence intervals\"*, *\"StatQuest bootstrapping\"*.\n- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest t-test\"* and *\"StatQuest Mann-Whitney\"* (or the Wilcoxon rank-sum test)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. A colleague says \"p = 0.03, so there's a 97 % chance the new PM works\". What's wrong?\n2. Why is the Flaking MTTR CI much wider than Packing's?\n3. Why use Mann-Whitney rather than a t-test for repair times?\n4. Breakdowns fell from 261 to 236. Write the one-sentence version you'd say in a meeting.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. A p-value isn't the probability the hypothesis is true. p = 0.03 means \"if the PM had no effect, results this good would happen 3 % of the time\". That's evidence, but not a 97 % guarantee. Prior plausibility, effect size and study design all matter.\n2. Fewer events (73 vs 193) and more variation (some flaking jobs take 20 hours). The CI width scales with std ÷ √n.\n3. Repair times are heavily right-skewed. Mann-Whitney compares ranks, so it doesn't assume normality and isn't dominated by a few huge values.\n4. \"We've had about 10 % fewer breakdowns than last year, but that's within normal variation (p ≈ 0.3), so we can't say yet that anything has actually improved.\"\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's t-interval and bootstrap CIs for PPK and FLK MTTR, the three Session 2 tests (Welch and Mann-Whitney, chi-square on PPK compliance, the binomial test giving p ≈ 0.28), and weekend item 3: your before/after test plan, written before any data.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest videos on p-values, confidence intervals, bootstrapping, the t-test and Mann-Whitney, read \"Why it works (and what p-values are not)\", do the Khan Academy confidence interval and significance test units, and say Check yourself answer 4 out loud. The weekend item 3 test plan works on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (the chi-square MTBF interval) and item 2 (Days against Nights repair times) can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] MTTR with 95 % CIs, both by formula and by bootstrap\n- [ ] t-test, Mann-Whitney, chi-square and binomial rate test run and interpreted\n- [ ] Can explain what a p-value is *not*\n- [ ] Before/after test plan written for a PM change"}],"quiz":[{"id":"44-1","n":1,"q":"A colleague says \"p = 0.03, so there's a 97 % chance the new PM works\". What's wrong?","a":"A p-value isn't the probability the hypothesis is true. p = 0.03 means \"if the PM had no effect, results this good would happen 3 % of the time\". That's evidence, but not a 97 % guarantee. Prior plausibility, effect size and study design all matter."},{"id":"44-2","n":2,"q":"Why is the Flaking MTTR CI much wider than Packing's?","a":"Fewer events (73 vs 193) and more variation (some flaking jobs take 20 hours). The CI width scales with std ÷ √n."},{"id":"44-3","n":3,"q":"Why use Mann-Whitney rather than a t-test for repair times?","a":"Repair times are heavily right-skewed. Mann-Whitney compares ranks, so it doesn't assume normality and isn't dominated by a few huge values."},{"id":"44-4","n":4,"q":"Breakdowns fell from 261 to 236. Write the one-sentence version you'd say in a meeting.","a":"\"We've had about 10 % fewer breakdowns than last year, but that's within normal variation (p ≈ 0.3), so we can't say yet that anything has actually improved.\""}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest p-values clearly explained\"*, *\"StatQuest confidence intervals\"*, *\"StatQuest bootstrapping\"*.","searches":["StatQuest p-values clearly explained","StatQuest confidence intervals","StatQuest bootstrapping"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest t-test\"* and *\"StatQuest Mann-Whitney\"* (or the Wilcoxon rank-sum test).","searches":["StatQuest t-test","StatQuest Mann-Whitney"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's t-interval and bootstrap CIs for PPK and FLK MTTR, the three Session 2 tests (Welch and Mann-Whitney, chi-square on PPK compliance, the binomial test giving p ≈ 0.28), and weekend item 3: your before/after test plan, written before any data."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest videos on p-values, confidence intervals, bootstrapping, the t-test and Mann-Whitney, read \"Why it works (and what p-values are not)\", do the Khan Academy confidence interval and significance test units, and say Check yourself answer 4 out loud. The weekend item 3 test plan works on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (the chi-square MTBF interval) and item 2 (Days against Nights repair times) can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":45,"phase":6,"title":"Regression, and did the PM actually make a difference?","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-45-regression-confounding.md","ticks":["Correlation and simple OLS run and interpreted","Confounding by asset type found and controlled","Poisson regression for counts","Half-page write-up: *\"Does late PM cause breakdowns on Line D?\"*"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 6 Sep – Sun 12 Sep 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nRegression measures how one thing changes with another while holding other things fixed. This week answers a question every PM pillar asks: *\"Do assets with worse PM compliance break down more?\"* The answer in this data is a lesson you'll use for the rest of your career.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- calculate and interpret correlation\n- fit linear regression with `statsmodels` and read the output (coefficient, p-value, R²)\n- spot **confounding** and fix it by adding controls\n- use Poisson regression for count data (like breakdowns)","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-simple-story","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The simple story","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"due_date\", \"completed_date\"])\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\n\npm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"].copy()\npm[\"on_time\"] = pm[\"completed_date\"].le(pm[\"due_date\"])\nbreakdowns = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"].groupby(\"asset_id\").size().rename(\"breakdowns\")\n\nper = (pm.groupby(\"asset_id\")[\"on_time\"].mean().rename(\"pm_on_time\").reset_index()\n         .merge(breakdowns.reset_index(), on=\"asset_id\", how=\"left\")\n         .fillna({\"breakdowns\": 0})\n         .merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\"))\n\nper[\"pm_on_time\"].corr(per[\"breakdowns\"])         # -0.28\n\nm1 = smf.ols(\"breakdowns ~ pm_on_time\", data=per).fit()\nprint(m1.summary())\n```\n\n**Read the output:**\n\n- coefficient on `pm_on_time` ≈ **−11.4**: going from 0 % to 100 % on time goes with about 11 fewer breakdowns. Or per 10 points of compliance, about 1.1 fewer breakdowns per asset over two years.\n- p-value < 0.001: very unlikely to be chance.\n- R² ≈ 0.08: compliance alone explains only 8 % of the variation in breakdowns.\n\nIt's tempting to stop here and put *\"every 10 % of PM compliance prevents 1.1 breakdowns per asset\"* on a slide. **Don't.**","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-confounding","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Confounding","kind":"hands","md":"### The problem\n\nWhich assets have bad PM compliance? Mostly **packing and end-of-line** assets (Weeks 8, 12, 19). And which assets break down most? Flow wrappers, case packers and carton erectors, also **packing and end-of-line**. Those machines fail often *because of what they are* (high-speed, many wear parts, frequent jams), regardless of PM timing.\n\nSo zone and asset type drive **both** compliance **and** breakdowns. That's a **confounder**: it creates a correlation between two things without one causing the other.\n\n### Do it: control for asset type\n\n```python\nm2 = smf.ols(\"breakdowns ~ pm_on_time + C(asset_type)\", data=per).fit()\nm2.params[\"pm_on_time\"], m2.pvalues[\"pm_on_time\"], m2.rsquared\n```\n\n| Model | Coefficient on PM on-time | p-value | R² |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| breakdowns ~ on-time | −11.4 | < 0.001 | 0.08 |\n| **+ asset type** | **−0.6** | **0.60** | **0.82** |\n\nOnce you compare like with like (flow wrappers with flow wrappers, conveyors with conveyors), **the effect of PM timing almost disappears**, and asset type explains 82 % of the variation.\n\nBreakdowns are **counts**, so the more correct model is **Poisson regression**:\n\n```python\nm3 = smf.poisson(\"breakdowns ~ pm_on_time + C(asset_type)\", data=per).fit()\nnp.exp(m3.params[\"pm_on_time\"] * 0.1)     # ≈ 0.98: +10 points of compliance gives about 2 % fewer breakdowns, p ≈ 0.54\n```\n\nSame conclusion.\n\n### Why this matters, and what it doesn't mean\n\nIt does **not** mean PMs are useless. It means **in this data, doing these PMs a few days late doesn't measurably change breakdowns**, which is exactly what you'd expect if:\n\n- most of these PMs are inspections that rarely catch anything, or\n- the PM interval is wrong for the failure mode (far too long, or aimed at random failures), so timing within that interval hardly matters.\n\nThat points straight at **Weeks 48–49**: look at each failure mode's Weibull shape and see whether the PM even targets it. Remember that the jaw replacement interval is 182 days, while jaws wear out at around 85.\n\n**Correlation is the start of a question, not the answer.** Before claiming X causes Y, ask: *what else is different between the groups?*","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Simple linear regression by hand**: for flow wrappers only, plot `pm_on_time` against `breakdowns` with `seaborn.regplot`. With only 6 points, what can you honestly conclude?\n2. **Asset age**: does age predict breakdowns once you control for asset type? Add `age_years` to `m2`.\n3. **Criticality**: A assets get priority 1. Do they get repaired faster? Regress breakdown `duration_min` on criticality plus asset type, using `np.log(duration_min)` as the outcome, since it's lognormal (Week 43).\n4. **Write it up**: half a page titled *\"Does late PM cause breakdowns on Line D?\"*, aimed at an engineering manager. Include the naive result, why it's misleading, the controlled result, and what you'd investigate next. This is portfolio material.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest linear regression\"*, *\"StatQuest R-squared\"* and *\"StatQuest linear models: design matrices\"*.\n- Search *\"confounding variable explained simpson's paradox\"*. Simpson's paradox is the extreme case.\n- Book: *Practical Statistics for Data Scientists*, chapter 4 (regression)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What does R² = 0.08 tell you?\n2. What's a confounder? Give the one in this week's data.\n3. Why is Poisson regression a better fit than ordinary linear regression for breakdown counts?\n4. The controlled model shows no effect of PM timing. Name two possible explanations that are about the **PM** rather than the statistics.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Only 8 % of the variation in breakdowns between assets is explained by PM compliance. Most of the differences come from something else.\n2. A variable that affects both the \"cause\" and the \"effect\", creating a misleading association. Here it's asset type (and zone): packing machines have both worse compliance and more breakdowns, for separate reasons.\n3. Counts are whole numbers ≥ 0, usually skewed, with variance that grows with the mean. Linear regression can predict negative counts and assumes constant variance. Poisson handles both.\n4. The PM interval doesn't match the failure mode's life (too long to prevent wear-out, or aimed at random failures that time-based PM can't prevent). Or the task content doesn't detect or prevent the actual failure modes (a visual check that can't see jaw wear). Data quality is another possibility, for example PMs signed off but not actually done.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's correlation and simple OLS (−11.4, R² 0.08), Session 2's controlled model with asset type (−0.6, p 0.60, R² 0.82) and the Poisson version, then weekend item 4, the half-page *\"Does late PM cause breakdowns on Line D?\"*. Weeks 48 and 49 pick up exactly where this leaves off.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest linear regression and R-squared videos and a Simpson's paradox explainer, read \"Why this matters, and what it doesn't mean\", answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the half-page write-up on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend items 1 to 3 (the flow wrapper regplot, asset age and the criticality repair-time model), the design matrices video and the *Practical Statistics* chapter can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Correlation and simple OLS run and interpreted\n- [ ] Confounding by asset type found and controlled\n- [ ] Poisson regression for counts\n- [ ] Half-page write-up: *\"Does late PM cause breakdowns on Line D?\"*"}],"quiz":[{"id":"45-1","n":1,"q":"What does R² = 0.08 tell you?","a":"Only 8 % of the variation in breakdowns between assets is explained by PM compliance. Most of the differences come from something else."},{"id":"45-2","n":2,"q":"What's a confounder? Give the one in this week's data.","a":"A variable that affects both the \"cause\" and the \"effect\", creating a misleading association. Here it's asset type (and zone): packing machines have both worse compliance and more breakdowns, for separate reasons."},{"id":"45-3","n":3,"q":"Why is Poisson regression a better fit than ordinary linear regression for breakdown counts?","a":"Counts are whole numbers ≥ 0, usually skewed, with variance that grows with the mean. Linear regression can predict negative counts and assumes constant variance. Poisson handles both."},{"id":"45-4","n":4,"q":"The controlled model shows no effect of PM timing. Name two possible explanations that are about the **PM** rather than the statistics.","a":"The PM interval doesn't match the failure mode's life (too long to prevent wear-out, or aimed at random failures that time-based PM can't prevent). Or the task content doesn't detect or prevent the actual failure modes (a visual check that can't see jaw wear). Data quality is another possibility, for example PMs signed off but not actually done."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest linear regression\"*, *\"StatQuest R-squared\"* and *\"StatQuest linear models: design matrices\"*.","searches":["StatQuest linear regression","StatQuest R-squared","StatQuest linear models: design matrices"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"confounding variable explained simpson's paradox\"*. Simpson's paradox is the extreme case.","searches":["confounding variable explained simpson's paradox"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book: *Practical Statistics for Data Scientists*, chapter 4 (regression).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's correlation and simple OLS (−11.4, R² 0.08), Session 2's controlled model with asset type (−0.6, p 0.60, R² 0.82) and the Poisson version, then weekend item 4, the half-page *\"Does late PM cause breakdowns on Line D?\"*. Weeks 48 and 49 pick up exactly where this leaves off."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest linear regression and R-squared videos and a Simpson's paradox explainer, read \"Why this matters, and what it doesn't mean\", answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the half-page write-up on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend items 1 to 3 (the flow wrapper regplot, asset age and the criticality repair-time model), the design matrices video and the *Practical Statistics* chapter can wait."}}},{"week":46,"phase":6,"title":"SPC, control charts, run rules and capability","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-46-spc-control-charts-capability.md","ticks":["c-chart of weekly reactive jobs; one signal found and investigated","I-MR chart of packing minor stops (stable)","Run rule 4 coded","Cp/Cpk calculated and explained (1.53 / 1.17)"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 13 Sep – Sun 19 Sep 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nStatistical Process Control (SPC) answers one question: **\"Has something changed, or is this normal variation?\"** It stops two expensive mistakes: reacting to noise (tampering), and missing a real shift. You'll use it on maintenance KPIs and on process data.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build I-MR (individuals) and c (count) control charts in Python\n- apply the Western Electric / Nelson run rules\n- tell common-cause from special-cause variation\n- calculate Cp and Cpk, and explain what they mean to production\n\nReference code is in [`solutions/phase-6-reliability/w46_w47_spc_reliability.py`](../../solutions/phase-6-reliability/w46_w47_spc_reliability.py).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-a-c-chart-for-weekly-breakdown","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): A c-chart for weekly breakdowns","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"raised_datetime\"])\nweekly = (wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"].set_index(\"raised_datetime\")[\"wo_id\"]\n          .resample(\"W-SUN\").count())\nweekly = weekly[(weekly.index > \"2024-07-01\") & (weekly.index <= \"2026-06-28\")]   # full weeks only\n\ncbar = weekly.mean()\nucl = cbar + 3 * np.sqrt(cbar)\nlcl = max(0, cbar - 3 * np.sqrt(cbar))\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 4))\nax.plot(weekly.index, weekly, marker=\"o\", markersize=3, color=\"#1F4E79\")\nfor y, s in ((cbar, \"-\"), (ucl, \"--\"), (lcl, \"--\")):\n    ax.axhline(y, color=\"#9E9E9E\", linestyle=s)\nout = weekly[weekly > ucl]\nax.scatter(out.index, out, color=\"#C62828\", zorder=3)\nax.set_title(f\"Reactive jobs per week: c̄ = {cbar:.1f}, UCL = {ucl:.1f}\", loc=\"left\")\n```\n\n**Results:** 104 full weeks, c̄ = **9.85** jobs a week, UCL = **19.3**, LCL = 0.4. **One** point is above the UCL: the week ending **13 July 2025**, with **22 jobs**.\n\n**Investigate the special cause:**\n\n```python\nr = wo[(wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\") & wo[\"raised_datetime\"].between(\"2025-07-07\", \"2025-07-13 23:59\")]\nr.merge(pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\"), on=\"asset_id\")[\"asset_type\"].value_counts()\n```\n\nThe jobs are spread over 11 asset types (5 conveyors, 4 flow wrappers, 3 sensors, …), with no common cause. In 104 points, one false signal is plausible. At work, you'd ask the shift team what happened that week (a heatwave? a new product? a short-staffed shift?) before concluding it was chance.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- **Common-cause** variation is the normal week-to-week noise of a stable process. Reacting to it (*\"why were there 13 this week?!\"*) wastes time and often makes things worse.\n- **Special-cause** variation is a signal that something changed. Investigate those.\n- **Control limits** come from the process itself (± 3 sigma), **not** from targets. A process can be \"in control\" and still be bad. That calls for improvement, not firefighting.\n- A **c-chart** is for counts of events per period, where σ = √c̄ (the Poisson assumption).","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-i-mr-chart-and-run-rules","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): I-MR chart and run rules","kind":"hands","md":"For a continuous weekly measure, use an **Individuals and Moving Range (I-MR)** chart.\n\n```python\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\", parse_dates=[\"start_datetime\"])\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nms = dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Minor stop\"].merge(assets, on=\"asset_id\")\nx = (ms[ms[\"zone_code\"] == \"PPK\"].set_index(\"start_datetime\")[\"duration_min\"]\n       .resample(\"W-SUN\").sum())\nx = x[(x.index > \"2024-07-01\") & (x.index <= \"2026-06-28\")]\n\nmr = x.diff().abs()\nxbar, mrbar = x.mean(), mr.mean()\nucl_x, lcl_x = xbar + 2.66 * mrbar, xbar - 2.66 * mrbar      # 2.66 = 3 / 1.128\nucl_mr = 3.267 * mrbar\n```\n\n**Results:** x̄ = **179.7** minor-stop minutes per week in packing, MR̄ = 35.7, limits **84.6 – 274.7**. **No points are outside.** Packing's minor stops are *stable*: about 3 hours a week, every week. To reduce them you need a **system change** (an SMED/jam root-cause project), not a reaction to individual bad weeks.\n\n### Run rules (a common subset)\n\nSignal a special cause if:\n\n1. one point is beyond 3σ\n2. 2 of 3 consecutive points are beyond 2σ on the same side\n3. 4 of 5 consecutive points are beyond 1σ on the same side\n4. **8 consecutive points are on the same side of the centre line** (a shift)\n5. 6 points are in a row steadily increasing or decreasing (a trend)\n\nCode rule 4:\n\n```python\nside = np.sign(x - xbar)\nrun_length = side.groupby((side != side.shift()).cumsum()).transform(\"size\")\n(run_length >= 8).any()\n```","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-capability-cp-and-cpk","title":"Weekend (2 h): Capability, Cp and Cpk","kind":"hands","md":"Capability asks whether a **stable** process fits inside its **specification**. The classic example on a cereal line is pack weight.\n\n```python\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\nw = rng.normal(503.0, 2.4, 250).round(1)          # 250 simulated 500 g packs\nLSL, USL = 495, 515\nmu, sd = w.mean(), w.std(ddof=1)\ncp = (USL - LSL) / (6 * sd)\ncpk = min(USL - mu, mu - LSL) / (3 * sd)\n```\n\n**Results:** mean 502.6 g, sd 2.17 g, **Cp = 1.53**, **Cpk = 1.17**.\n\n- **Cp** compares the spec width with the process spread, ignoring centring. 1.53 means \"the process is narrow enough\".\n- **Cpk** includes centring. 1.17 is lower because the mean sits 7.6 g from the lower limit but 12.4 g from the upper one.\n- Common targets: Cpk ≥ 1.33 for existing processes, and ≥ 1.67 for new or safety-critical ones.\n- So here the spread is fine, but the process is off-centre. Re-centring toward 505 g would raise Cpk to about Cp. (Re-centring *upwards* also means giving away more product. That trade-off is a real production conversation.)\n\n**Then:**\n\n1. Draw the histogram with LSL/USL lines.\n2. Build an X̄-R chart for subgroups of 5 (rows 0–4, 5–9, …). Look up the A2, D3 and D4 constants for n = 5.\n3. Ask at work (without exporting anything) whether your site uses SPC software or Minitab for checkweighers, and what the Cpk targets are.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"SPC control charts explained common cause special cause\"*. Pick a short manufacturing-focused video.\n- Search *\"Western Electric rules control chart\"*.\n- Search *\"Cp Cpk explained\"*. Many channels cover it in about 10 minutes.\n- Book (classic): *Understanding Variation* by Donald Wheeler. It's short, and it changes how you read every KPI chart."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. The manager sees 15 breakdowns this week (UCL 19.3) and wants an investigation. What do you say?\n2. Why are control limits not the same as targets?\n3. Cp = 1.5, Cpk = 0.9. What's going on, and what's the fix?\n4. Packing minor stops are \"in control\" at 180 min a week. Is that good?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. It's within normal variation for this process. There's no single cause to find, and investigating it as special would be tampering. If 15 is too high, the whole process needs improving: look at the Pareto of causes across many weeks.\n2. Control limits describe what the process **does** (the voice of the process). Targets and specs describe what we **want** (the voice of the customer). They answer different questions.\n3. The spread is fine but the process is off-centre, close to one spec limit. Fix the centring (the setpoint or target), not the variation.\n4. \"In control\" only means stable and predictable. It doesn't mean good. Three hours a week of stops is a predictable loss, and reducing it needs a structured improvement project.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's c-chart of weekly reactive jobs with the week ending 13 July 2025 investigated, Session 2's I-MR chart of packing minor stops with run rule 4 coded, and the weekend Cp and Cpk calculation (1.53 / 1.17) with the histogram.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch short videos on common and special cause variation, the Western Electric rules and Cp and Cpk, read the \"Why it works\" list and the Cpk bullets, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask whether your site uses SPC software or Minitab for checkweighers and what its Cpk targets are.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 2 (the X̄-R chart for subgroups of 5) and Donald Wheeler's *Understanding Variation* can be left for later."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] c-chart of weekly reactive jobs; one signal found and investigated\n- [ ] I-MR chart of packing minor stops (stable)\n- [ ] Run rule 4 coded\n- [ ] Cp/Cpk calculated and explained (1.53 / 1.17)"}],"quiz":[{"id":"46-1","n":1,"q":"The manager sees 15 breakdowns this week (UCL 19.3) and wants an investigation. What do you say?","a":"It's within normal variation for this process. There's no single cause to find, and investigating it as special would be tampering. If 15 is too high, the whole process needs improving: look at the Pareto of causes across many weeks."},{"id":"46-2","n":2,"q":"Why are control limits not the same as targets?","a":"Control limits describe what the process **does** (the voice of the process). Targets and specs describe what we **want** (the voice of the customer). They answer different questions."},{"id":"46-3","n":3,"q":"Cp = 1.5, Cpk = 0.9. What's going on, and what's the fix?","a":"The spread is fine but the process is off-centre, close to one spec limit. Fix the centring (the setpoint or target), not the variation."},{"id":"46-4","n":4,"q":"Packing minor stops are \"in control\" at 180 min a week. Is that good?","a":"\"In control\" only means stable and predictable. It doesn't mean good. Three hours a week of stops is a predictable loss, and reducing it needs a structured improvement project."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"SPC control charts explained common cause special cause\"*. Pick a short manufacturing-focused video.","searches":["SPC control charts explained common cause special cause"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"Western Electric rules control chart\"*.","searches":["Western Electric rules control chart"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"Cp Cpk explained\"*. Many channels cover it in about 10 minutes.","searches":["Cp Cpk explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":3,"md":"Book (classic): *Understanding Variation* by Donald Wheeler. It's short, and it changes how you read every KPI chart.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's c-chart of weekly reactive jobs with the week ending 13 July 2025 investigated, Session 2's I-MR chart of packing minor stops with run rule 4 coded, and the weekend Cp and Cpk calculation (1.53 / 1.17) with the histogram."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch short videos on common and special cause variation, the Western Electric rules and Cp and Cpk, read the \"Why it works\" list and the Cpk bullets, and answer Check yourself in your head. At work, ask whether your site uses SPC software or Minitab for checkweighers and what its Cpk targets are."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 2 (the X̄-R chart for subgroups of 5) and Donald Wheeler's *Understanding Variation* can be left for later."}}},{"week":47,"phase":6,"title":"Reliability metrics, failure rate and the bathtub curve","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-47-reliability-metrics-bathtub.md","ticks":["R(t), F(t) and λ(t) explained in my own words","Flow wrapper MTBF, availability and weekly survival probability calculated","Can explain the bathtub curve and why Nowlan & Heap matters for PM","Series and parallel availability; PPK zone ≈ 0.987"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 20 Sep – Sun 26 Sep 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nYou calculated MTBF and MTTR in SQL and DAX. This week you go underneath them: reliability as a **probability over time**, failure rate, the bathtub curve, and how individual asset availabilities combine into a **line** availability.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain R(t), F(t), the failure rate λ(t), MTBF and MTTR, and how they connect\n- use the exponential model R(t) = e^(−t/MTBF), and know when it's valid\n- describe the bathtub curve and Nowlan & Heap's six failure patterns\n- calculate series and parallel (redundant) availability","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-from-mtbf-to-probability","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): From MTBF to probability","kind":"hands","md":"### The core functions\n\n| Symbol | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| R(t) | reliability (survival) | chance it's still working at age t |\n| F(t) = 1 − R(t) | unreliability (CDF) | chance it has failed by age t |\n| f(t) | failure density (PDF) | when failures happen |\n| λ(t) = f(t) / R(t) | hazard (failure) rate | the chance of failing *now*, given it has survived so far |\n\nFor **random** failures (a constant λ), everything follows from MTBF:\n\n$$R(t) = e^{-t/\\text{MTBF}} \\qquad \\lambda = 1/\\text{MTBF}$$\n\n### Do it\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\")\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\")\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nfw = assets.loc[assets[\"asset_type\"] == \"Flow wrapper\", \"asset_id\"]\n\nhours = 17520\nn_fail = wo[(wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\") & wo[\"asset_id\"].isin(fw)].shape[0]\nmtbf = hours * len(fw) / n_fail\nmttr = dt[(dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\") & dt[\"asset_id\"].isin(fw)][\"duration_min\"].mean() / 60\nA = mtbf / (mtbf + mttr)\nprint(n_fail, round(mtbf), round(mttr, 2), f\"{A:.3%}\", f\"{1/mtbf:.5f}/h\")\nprint(\"Chance a flow wrapper runs a full week (168 h) without a breakdown:\", round(np.exp(-168 / mtbf), 3))\n```\n\n**Results:** 6 flow wrappers, 161 breakdowns. MTBF ≈ **653 h**, MTTR ≈ 1.01 h, A ≈ 99.85 %, λ ≈ 0.0015 per hour. **R(168 h) ≈ 0.77**, so in any given week, each flow wrapper has about a 23 % chance of breaking down.\n\n### Why it works (and the big caveat)\n\n`e^(−t/MTBF)` assumes failures are **random**, with a constant rate. That's valid for a *mix* of failure modes on a complex machine (which is why it roughly works for \"flow wrapper, any cause\"), but **wrong for a single wear-out mode**. Next week's Weibull analysis shows flow wrapper **jaws** don't fail randomly at all.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-the-bathtub-curve-and-the-six","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): The bathtub curve and the six patterns","kind":"hands","md":"### The classic bathtub\n\n1. **Infant mortality** (falling failure rate): installation errors and manufacturing defects. The fix is better commissioning, burn-in and QA.\n2. **Useful life** (constant rate): random failures. The fix is condition monitoring, or redesign.\n3. **Wear-out** (rising rate): fatigue, abrasion, corrosion. The fix is time-based replacement, *if* you know when wear-out starts.\n\n### Nowlan & Heap (1978, airline data)\n\nThe famous study that founded **RCM** found that most components **don't** follow the bathtub. It identified six patterns:\n\n| Pattern | Shape | Share (airline study) |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | bathtub | ~4 % |\n| B | constant, then wear-out | ~2 % |\n| C | slowly rising | ~5 % |\n| D | low at first, then constant | ~7 % |\n| E | constant (random) | ~14 % |\n| F | infant mortality, then constant | **~68 %** |\n\nOnly about 11 % (A, B, C) show wear-out, which is where **time-based replacement helps**. For the rest, replacing on a schedule does nothing, or makes things **worse** by resetting parts back into infant mortality. Industrial plant proportions differ, but the lesson stands: **don't assume age-related failure. Check it.** That's exactly what Weibull β tells you.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-system-availability","title":"Weekend (2 h): System availability","kind":"hands","md":"### Series and parallel\n\n- **Series** (everything must work; a line): A_sys = A₁ × A₂ × … × Aₙ\n- **Parallel** (any one is enough; redundancy): A_sys = 1 − (1 − A₁)(1 − A₂)…\n\n```python\nA_fw = 0.99846\nprint(\"6 wrappers all needed:\", round(A_fw ** 6, 4))        # 0.9908\nprint(\"2 pumps, either is enough:\", round(1 - (1 - 0.98) ** 2, 4))   # 0.9996\n```\n\n### Do it\n\n1. **Primary Packing as a series system.** For each PPK asset, compute its availability as 1 − (breakdown downtime hours ÷ 17,520). Multiply them all together. You should get about **0.987**: the zone was down for breakdowns about 1.3 % of the time, which is about **226 hours** over two years. That's one asset's 99.8 % multiplied across 43 assets.\n2. **Add minor stops**, which aren't in \"availability\" but are real lost time. How much worse does it get?\n3. **What-if**: if flow wrapper MTBF doubled (half the breakdowns), what would the zone availability be?\n4. **Read about OEE**: Availability × Performance × Quality. Which of the three do minor stops belong to? (Performance.)","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"bathtub curve reliability explained\"* and *\"Nowlan and Heap six failure patterns\"*.\n- Search *\"MTBF vs MTTF vs failure rate\"* for a short explainer.\n- Read the [`reliability` library docs](https://reliability.readthedocs.io), section *\"Equations of supported distributions\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. MTBF = 653 h. Is the chance of surviving 653 h equal to 50 %?\n2. Why can replacing a component on a schedule *increase* failures?\n3. A line has 20 machines in series, each with 99.5 % availability. What's the line availability?\n4. What's the difference between MTBF and MTTF?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. No. Under the exponential model, R(MTBF) = e^(−1) ≈ **36.8 %**. Most units fail *before* the MTBF. A mean life is not a median life.\n2. If a part's failures are infant mortality or random (patterns D, E, F), a new part is no better, or is worse, than the old one. Replacement adds the risk of installation errors and early-life failures.\n3. 0.995²⁰ ≈ **0.905**, so about 90 %. Small losses multiply.\n4. **MTTF** (mean time *to* failure) is for non-repairable items: the average life of a bearing. **MTBF** is for repairable systems: the average time between failures of a machine. People often use them loosely.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's flow wrapper block (MTBF about 653 h, A about 99.85 %, R(168 h) about 0.77), and weekend step 1: Primary Packing as a series system, about 0.987 or 226 hours.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Session 2 is reading: the bathtub curve and Nowlan & Heap's six patterns. Watch the bathtub curve and Nowlan and Heap videos, read the \"big caveat\" on the exponential model, and work Check yourself question 3 (0.995 to the power 20) on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend steps 2 to 4 (adding minor stops, the flow wrapper what-if and the OEE reading) and the `reliability` docs equations page can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] R(t), F(t) and λ(t) explained in my own words\n- [ ] Flow wrapper MTBF, availability and weekly survival probability calculated\n- [ ] Can explain the bathtub curve and why Nowlan & Heap matters for PM\n- [ ] Series and parallel availability; PPK zone ≈ 0.987"}],"quiz":[{"id":"47-1","n":1,"q":"MTBF = 653 h. Is the chance of surviving 653 h equal to 50 %?","a":"No. Under the exponential model, R(MTBF) = e^(−1) ≈ **36.8 %**. Most units fail *before* the MTBF. A mean life is not a median life."},{"id":"47-2","n":2,"q":"Why can replacing a component on a schedule *increase* failures?","a":"If a part's failures are infant mortality or random (patterns D, E, F), a new part is no better, or is worse, than the old one. Replacement adds the risk of installation errors and early-life failures."},{"id":"47-3","n":3,"q":"A line has 20 machines in series, each with 99.5 % availability. What's the line availability?","a":"0.995²⁰ ≈ **0.905**, so about 90 %. Small losses multiply."},{"id":"47-4","n":4,"q":"What's the difference between MTBF and MTTF?","a":"**MTTF** (mean time *to* failure) is for non-repairable items: the average life of a bearing. **MTBF** is for repairable systems: the average time between failures of a machine. People often use them loosely."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"bathtub curve reliability explained\"* and *\"Nowlan and Heap six failure patterns\"*.","searches":["bathtub curve reliability explained","Nowlan and Heap six failure patterns"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"MTBF vs MTTF vs failure rate\"* for a short explainer.","searches":["MTBF vs MTTF vs failure rate"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Read the [`reliability` library docs](https://reliability.readthedocs.io), section *\"Equations of supported distributions\"*.","searches":["Equations of supported distributions"],"urls":["https://reliability.readthedocs.io"]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's flow wrapper block (MTBF about 653 h, A about 99.85 %, R(168 h) about 0.77), and weekend step 1: Primary Packing as a series system, about 0.987 or 226 hours."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Session 2 is reading: the bathtub curve and Nowlan & Heap's six patterns. Watch the bathtub curve and Nowlan and Heap videos, read the \"big caveat\" on the exponential model, and work Check yourself question 3 (0.995 to the power 20) on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend steps 2 to 4 (adding minor stops, the flow wrapper what-if and the OEE reading) and the `reliability` docs equations page can wait."}}},{"week":48,"phase":6,"title":"Weibull analysis","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-48-weibull-analysis.md","ticks":["Can explain β < 1, ≈ 1 and > 1 and what each means for PM","Built failure and suspension life data for one mode","Jaws: η ≈ 86 d, β ≈ 3.0. Jams: β ≈ 0.9.","Showed what ignoring suspensions and mixing modes does","Table of β/η for at least four failure modes"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 27 Sep – Sun 3 Oct 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThe Weibull distribution is the reliability engineer's main tool. Its **shape parameter β** tells you which part of the bathtub a failure mode lives in, and therefore **whether time-based PM can work at all**. This week you fit Weibulls properly, including the step most people get wrong: **suspensions**.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- interpret Weibull β (shape) and η (scale, the characteristic life)\n- build life data for one failure mode, with failures and **suspensions** (right-censored data)\n- fit Weibull with the Python `reliability` library and read the probability plot\n- show that mixing failure modes, or ignoring suspensions, gives the wrong answer\n\n**Library:** `reliability` (already in `requirements.txt`). Docs: [reliability.readthedocs.io](https://reliability.readthedocs.io).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-beta-and-eta","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): β and η","kind":"hands","md":"$$R(t) = e^{-(t/\\eta)^\\beta}$$\n\n| β | Failure rate | Meaning | PM implication |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **< 1** | falling | infant mortality | **don't** replace on time; improve installation, commissioning and quality |\n| **≈ 1** | constant | random | time-based replacement **can't help**; use condition monitoring, redesign, or run to failure |\n| **1 – 2** | slowly rising | early wear-out | time-based replacement *may* pay off; check costs |\n| **> 2** | rising fast | clear wear-out | **time-based replacement works**, if the interval is right |\n\n**η** is the age by which **63.2 %** of units have failed, whatever β is.\n\n### Do it: feel the shapes\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom reliability.Distributions import Weibull_Distribution\n\nfor beta in (0.7, 1.0, 3.0):\n    Weibull_Distribution(alpha=80, beta=beta).HF(label=f\"β = {beta}\")   # hazard function\nplt.legend(); plt.title(\"Failure rate vs age, η = 80 days\"); plt.show()\n```\n\nThe β = 3 curve rises steeply. That's a part that \"wears out\" around 60–100 days.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-life-data-done-properly","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Life data done properly","kind":"hands","md":"**The question:** what's the life of a set of **flow wrapper sealing jaws**?\n\nA jaw set's \"life\" starts when it's new: after a breakdown repair, a planned repair, or a scheduled replacement. Its life ends in one of two ways:\n\n- it **fails** (a reactive WO with code *Mechanical - jaws*) → a **failure** time\n- it's **replaced before failing** (the *Replace sealing jaws* PM, or a planned corrective found on inspection), or the data simply ends → a **suspension**: \"it lasted *at least* this long\"\n\n**Ignoring suspensions throws away the information that many jaw sets survived a long time**, and makes life look shorter than it is. **Treating every time-between-breakdowns as a life** (mixing in jams and sensor faults) blurs everything towards β ≈ 1.\n\n### Do it\n\nUse the study script, then read its `life_data()` function line by line:\n\n```powershell\npython solutions\\phase-6-reliability\\weibull_study.py\n```\n\nOr in a notebook:\n\n```python\nimport sys; sys.path.append(\"../solutions/phase-6-reliability\")\nfrom weibull_study import life_data\nfrom reliability.Fitters import Fit_Weibull_2P\n\nf, s = life_data(\"Flow wrapper\", \"Mechanical - jaws\", \"Replace sealing jaws\")\nlen(f), len(s)                       # 32 failures, 48 suspensions\nfit = Fit_Weibull_2P(failures=f, right_censored=s)   # prints the results and shows the probability plot\n```\n\n**Results:** η ≈ **85.7 days** (95 % CI 76–97), **β ≈ 2.98** (95 % CI 2.3–3.9). That's clear **wear-out**.\n\n### Now see how it goes wrong\n\n```python\n# Mistake 1: ignore suspensions\nFit_Weibull_2P(failures=f, print_results=False, show_probability_plot=False).alpha   # ≈ 68 d: life looks 20 % shorter\n\n# Mistake 2: mix every failure mode, using days between any two breakdowns\n#   (your Week 38 'days_since_last' for flow wrappers): β ≈ 1.1, η ≈ 28 d. It looks random!\n\n# Contrast: a mode that really is random\nfj, sj = life_data(\"Flow wrapper\", \"Operational - jam\", \"NO_PM\")\nFit_Weibull_2P(failures=fj, right_censored=sj, print_results=False, show_probability_plot=False).beta   # ≈ 0.92\n```\n\n**Jams: β ≈ 0.92, η ≈ 49 days.** They're random. No PM interval will prevent them, so they need a root-cause and design fix (product, film, settings).\n\n### Reading the probability plot\n\nThe axes are transformed so that Weibull data falls on a **straight line**, with slope = β. Points that follow the line support the Weibull model. A **kink** often means two failure modes mixed together. Points curving away at the start suggest a 3-parameter Weibull (failure-free period) or a different distribution.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Run `life_data` + `Fit_Weibull_2P` for:\n   - Flaking roll, *Mechanical - roll wear*, PM prefix `Regrind`. You should get β ≈ **3.3**, η ≈ **137 d**.\n   - Conveyor, *Mechanical - belt/chain*, PM prefix `Replace conveyor belt`, which gives β ≈ 2.2.\n   - Pump, *Mechanical - seal*, PM prefix `Replace pump mechanical seal`.\n2. Make a table of every mode: n failures, n suspensions, β (with CI), η, and the verdict (infant / random / wear-out).\n3. Try `Fit_Everything(failures=f, right_censored=s)` on the jaws. Does Weibull still win? What about lognormal?\n4. Read the `reliability` docs page *\"What is censored data\"* and the *Fit_Weibull_2P* page.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"Weibull analysis explained beta shape parameter\"*. Look for a reliability engineering channel.\n- Search *\"right censored data suspensions reliability\"*.\n- Book: *The New Weibull Handbook* by Robert Abernethy, the industry standard reference (if your employer has a copy)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. β = 0.6 for a drive's electronics board. Should you replace boards every 2 years?\n2. Why is a PM replacement treated as a suspension, not a failure?\n3. Why does mixing failure modes push β towards 1?\n4. What does η = 85.7 days mean in plain English?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. **No.** β < 1 is infant mortality: new boards are *more* likely to fail than old ones. Replacing them would increase failures. Focus on handling, ESD control, burn-in and supplier quality.\n2. The part hadn't failed. All we know is that it lasted *at least* that long. Recording it as a failure would wrongly shorten the estimated life.\n3. Several overlapping wear-out curves at different ages, plus random modes, add up to a roughly constant overall failure rate. Each mode's signature gets averaged away, which is the Drenick result.\n4. By 86 days of running, about 63 % of jaw sets will have failed. With β ≈ 3, failures cluster between roughly 40 and 120 days.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** The Session 1 hazard plot for the three β values, Session 2's jaw fit with suspensions (η about 85.7 d, β about 2.98), both \"goes wrong\" mistakes and the jam fit (β about 0.92), and weekend steps 1 and 2: fits for the flaking roll, conveyor and pump modes and your β/η table. Week 49 and the Week 50 study use these numbers.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read the β table, \"Life data done properly\" and \"Reading the probability plot\", watch the Weibull beta and right-censored data videos, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend step 3 (`Fit_Everything` on the jaws), the `reliability` docs pages in step 4 and *The New Weibull Handbook* can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Can explain β < 1, ≈ 1 and > 1 and what each means for PM\n- [ ] Built failure and suspension life data for one mode\n- [ ] Jaws: η ≈ 86 d, β ≈ 3.0. Jams: β ≈ 0.9.\n- [ ] Showed what ignoring suspensions and mixing modes does\n- [ ] Table of β/η for at least four failure modes"}],"quiz":[{"id":"48-1","n":1,"q":"β = 0.6 for a drive's electronics board. Should you replace boards every 2 years?","a":"**No.** β < 1 is infant mortality: new boards are *more* likely to fail than old ones. Replacing them would increase failures. Focus on handling, ESD control, burn-in and supplier quality."},{"id":"48-2","n":2,"q":"Why is a PM replacement treated as a suspension, not a failure?","a":"The part hadn't failed. All we know is that it lasted *at least* that long. Recording it as a failure would wrongly shorten the estimated life."},{"id":"48-3","n":3,"q":"Why does mixing failure modes push β towards 1?","a":"Several overlapping wear-out curves at different ages, plus random modes, add up to a roughly constant overall failure rate. Each mode's signature gets averaged away, which is the Drenick result."},{"id":"48-4","n":4,"q":"What does η = 85.7 days mean in plain English?","a":"By 86 days of running, about 63 % of jaw sets will have failed. With β ≈ 3, failures cluster between roughly 40 and 120 days."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"Weibull analysis explained beta shape parameter\"*. Look for a reliability engineering channel.","searches":["Weibull analysis explained beta shape parameter"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"right censored data suspensions reliability\"*.","searches":["right censored data suspensions reliability"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book: *The New Weibull Handbook* by Robert Abernethy, the industry standard reference (if your employer has a copy).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"The Session 1 hazard plot for the three β values, Session 2's jaw fit with suspensions (η about 85.7 d, β about 2.98), both \"goes wrong\" mistakes and the jam fit (β about 0.92), and weekend steps 1 and 2: fits for the flaking roll, conveyor and pump modes and your β/η table. Week 49 and the Week 50 study use these numbers."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read the β table, \"Life data done properly\" and \"Reading the probability plot\", watch the Weibull beta and right-censored data videos, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend step 3 (`Fit_Everything` on the jaws), the `reliability` docs pages in step 4 and *The New Weibull Handbook* can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":49,"phase":6,"title":"From Weibull to PM decisions, optimal intervals, P-F, RCM and FMEA","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-49-pm-intervals-pf-rcm-fmea.md","ticks":["Optimal interval found for the jaws (37 d), with sensitivity to cost","Can explain why the current 182-day PM equals run to failure","RCM task choice made for five Line D failure modes","Inspection interval set from the P-F interval","FMEA for one flow wrapper, with at least 6 modes"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 4 Oct – Sun 10 Oct 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nA Weibull fit is only useful if it changes a decision. This week you turn β and η into **a PM interval**, put a cost on it, and place it inside the frameworks your engineering manager will recognise: the **P-F curve**, **RCM** task selection, and **FMEA**.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- calculate the cost-optimal age-replacement interval from Weibull parameters and costs\n- test how sensitive that answer is to the cost estimates\n- choose a task type with RCM logic (time-based, condition-based, failure-finding, redesign, run to failure)\n- set an inspection interval from the P-F interval\n- run a simple FMEA with RPN scoring","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-optimal-replacement-interv","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The optimal replacement interval","kind":"hands","md":"### The idea\n\nReplace too often and you waste parts and labour. Replace too rarely and you pay for breakdowns. For a **wear-out** mode there's a sweet spot:\n\n$$\\text{cost per day}(T) = \\frac{C_{PM} \\cdot R(T) + C_{CM} \\cdot (1 - R(T))}{\\int_0^T R(t)\\,dt}$$\n\nThat's the expected cost per jaw-set life, divided by the expected length of that life, if you replace at age T or at failure, whichever comes first.\n\n### Do it\n\nThe costs are **assumptions you must agree with the business**. For practice:\n\n- **C_PM = £400** per planned jaw change: parts plus 1 h of labour, done during a planned stop\n- **C_CM = £2,900** per jaw breakdown: parts plus labour plus ~1 h of lost packing output plus scrap\n\n```powershell\npython solutions\\phase-6-reliability\\weibull_study.py --cost-pm 400 --cost-cm 2900\n```\n\n| Output | Value |\n|---|---|\n| η, β | 85.7 d, 2.98 |\n| B10 life | **40 days** (10 % of jaw sets fail by then) |\n| Chance of reaching the current 182-day interval | **~0 %** |\n| **Optimal interval** | **37 days** |\n| Cost per day at 182 d (current) | £37.88, the **same as run to failure** |\n| Cost per day at 37 d | **£16.42** |\n\nLook at the `cost_vs_interval.png` chart. The current 182-day PM is on the flat right-hand end. **Almost no jaw set survives to its PM**, so the PM is doing nothing, and you're effectively running to failure while also paying for a PM. That's why late PMs didn't matter in Week 45.\n\n### Sensitivity: how sure are we?\n\n| C_CM (with C_PM = £400) | Optimal interval |\n|---|---|\n| £1,500 | 49 days |\n| **£2,900** | **37 days** |\n| £5,000 | 30 days |\n\nAnd the curve is **flat near the bottom**: at 42 days it's £16.68/day, only 2 % above the optimum. So a **practical 6-week (42-day) interval**, aligned with the planned-stop schedule, captures nearly all the saving. That's worth about **£7,700 per wrapper per year, or ~£46k a year across 6 wrappers** at these assumed costs.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-rcm-task-selection-and-the-p-f","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): RCM task selection and the P-F interval","kind":"hands","md":"### RCM decision logic (simplified)\n\nFor each **failure mode**, ask in order:\n\n1. **Is there a detectable warning (a P-F interval) that's long enough to act on?** → **Condition-based task** (vibration, thermography, oil analysis, wear measurement). This is usually the best option when it's possible.\n2. **Does it wear out (β > 1, ideally > 2), with a known life?** → **Time-based restoration or replacement** at the optimal interval.\n3. **Is it a hidden failure** (a protective device that only shows its failure on demand, like a guard interlock, a metal detector reject or a relief valve)? → **Failure-finding task** (a functional test), at an interval set by the required availability.\n4. **None of the above, and the consequences are unacceptable?** → **Redesign**.\n5. **Consequences acceptable?** → **Run to failure**, deliberately.\n\n### The P-F interval sets inspection frequency\n\nFrom Week 41: a bearing defect becomes detectable (P) some time before functional failure (F).\n\n- **Inspection interval ≤ P-F interval ÷ 2** (a common rule), so at least one inspection falls inside the window, with time left to plan the repair.\n- Fan bearing B5 gave **30 days** from alert to failure, so inspect **at least every ~2 weeks**, or monitor continuously.\n\n### Apply it to Line D\n\n| Failure mode | β | Evidence | RCM choice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Flow wrapper jaws | 3.0 | clear wear-out, η 86 d | **Time-based** replacement every ~6 weeks, plus a jaw wear check on the weekly inspection to catch early ones |\n| Flow wrapper jams | 0.9 | random | **No time-based task.** A root-cause and redesign project (film, settings, product) |\n| Flaking roll wear | 3.3 | wear-out, η 137 d, current regrind every 120 d | Shorten towards the optimum (`weibull_study.py --asset-type \"Flaking roll\" --mode \"Mechanical - roll wear\" --pm-prefix Regrind --current-interval 120 --cost-pm 1200 --cost-cm 6000` gives ≈ 70 d), **or** measure the roll gap and wear (condition-based) |\n| Dryer fan bearing | rising vibration trend | P-F ≈ 30–64 d | **Condition-based**: continuous vibration sensors (supports the rollout) |\n| Metal detector | n/a | hidden function | **Failure-finding**: the test-piece check (already daily) |","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-fmea","title":"Weekend (2 h): FMEA","kind":"hands","md":"A **Failure Modes and Effects Analysis** lists how something can fail, what happens, and how bad it is. It's the structured way to decide where to focus.\n\nFor **one flow wrapper**, build an FMEA in Excel with these columns:\n\n| Function | Failure mode | Effect | Cause | Current controls | S (1–10) | O (1–10) | D (1–10) | RPN = S×O×D | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n- **S**everity of the effect: 10 is a safety or food-safety issue, 1 is no noticeable effect.\n- **O**ccurrence: use your data. Jams (most frequent) score high, drive faults low.\n- **D**etection: how likely current controls are to catch it **before** it causes the effect (10 = can't detect).\n\nFill in at least 6 failure modes (jaws, jams, sensor, drive, knife, film splice) and rank them by RPN. Then add a column for **action priority** ignoring RPN: any S ≥ 9 gets action, whatever its RPN. That's why newer FMEA standards (AIAG-VDA) moved away from pure RPN.\n\nRead the classic: *Reliability-Centered Maintenance* (2nd ed.) by John Moubray, chapters 1–4. Ask whether your site library or engineering team has a copy.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"RCM decision diagram explained\"* and *\"P-F interval inspection frequency\"*.\n- Search *\"FMEA tutorial RPN example\"*.\n- Search *\"optimal replacement interval Weibull cost\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. The jaw replacement interval is 182 days. Why did doing it late make no difference to breakdowns?\n2. The cost ratio C_CM/C_PM falls to 2. What happens to the optimal interval, and why?\n3. A mode has β = 1.0. A manager suggests halving its PM interval. What's your answer?\n4. Fan bearing P-F is ~30 days. The vibration route is monthly. What's the risk, and what are the options?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Jaws fail at around 40–120 days (η 86, β 3), so nearly all jaw sets had already failed long before the 182-day PM came due. The PM's timing was irrelevant, because it almost never got the chance to prevent anything.\n2. It gets **longer**, and if the ratio is low enough, replacement stops being worth it at all. When breakdowns cost little more than planned changes, you save little by pre-empting them.\n3. With β ≈ 1, failures are random: a new part is no better than an old one, so a shorter interval just doubles the cost with no reduction in failures. Look for a condition-based task, a redesign, or accept run to failure.\n4. A defect could start just after one reading and fail before the next, giving no warning. The options: route every ≤ 2 weeks on critical fans, continuous wireless sensors, or accept the risk on non-critical ones.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1: run `weibull_study.py` with £400 and £2,900, read the cost curve (37 days optimal, 182 days equals run to failure), and rerun at £1,500 and £5,000 for the sensitivity. Then build the weekend FMEA in Excel with at least 6 flow wrapper modes ranked by RPN. Week 50's recommendation is built from these numbers.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Session 2 is reading: the RCM decision logic, the P-F rule and the Line D table. Watch the RCM decision diagram and P-F interval videos, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft your FMEA modes and S, O and D scores on paper before you type them up.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The flaking roll `weibull_study.py` run in the Line D table, the action priority column and Moubray's *Reliability-Centered Maintenance* chapters can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Optimal interval found for the jaws (37 d), with sensitivity to cost\n- [ ] Can explain why the current 182-day PM equals run to failure\n- [ ] RCM task choice made for five Line D failure modes\n- [ ] Inspection interval set from the P-F interval\n- [ ] FMEA for one flow wrapper, with at least 6 modes"}],"quiz":[{"id":"49-1","n":1,"q":"The jaw replacement interval is 182 days. Why did doing it late make no difference to breakdowns?","a":"Jaws fail at around 40–120 days (η 86, β 3), so nearly all jaw sets had already failed long before the 182-day PM came due. The PM's timing was irrelevant, because it almost never got the chance to prevent anything."},{"id":"49-2","n":2,"q":"The cost ratio C_CM/C_PM falls to 2. What happens to the optimal interval, and why?","a":"It gets **longer**, and if the ratio is low enough, replacement stops being worth it at all. When breakdowns cost little more than planned changes, you save little by pre-empting them."},{"id":"49-3","n":3,"q":"A mode has β = 1.0. A manager suggests halving its PM interval. What's your answer?","a":"With β ≈ 1, failures are random: a new part is no better than an old one, so a shorter interval just doubles the cost with no reduction in failures. Look for a condition-based task, a redesign, or accept run to failure."},{"id":"49-4","n":4,"q":"Fan bearing P-F is ~30 days. The vibration route is monthly. What's the risk, and what are the options?","a":"A defect could start just after one reading and fail before the next, giving no warning. The options: route every ≤ 2 weeks on critical fans, continuous wireless sensors, or accept the risk on non-critical ones."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"RCM decision diagram explained\"* and *\"P-F interval inspection frequency\"*.","searches":["RCM decision diagram explained","P-F interval inspection frequency"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"FMEA tutorial RPN example\"*.","searches":["FMEA tutorial RPN example"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Search *\"optimal replacement interval Weibull cost\"*.","searches":["optimal replacement interval Weibull cost"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1: run `weibull_study.py` with £400 and £2,900, read the cost curve (37 days optimal, 182 days equals run to failure), and rerun at £1,500 and £5,000 for the sensitivity. Then build the weekend FMEA in Excel with at least 6 flow wrapper modes ranked by RPN. Week 50's recommendation is built from these numbers."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Session 2 is reading: the RCM decision logic, the P-F rule and the Line D table. Watch the RCM decision diagram and P-F interval videos, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft your FMEA modes and S, O and D scores on paper before you type them up."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The flaking roll `weibull_study.py` run in the Line D table, the action priority column and Moubray's *Reliability-Centered Maintenance* chapters can wait."}}},{"week":50,"phase":6,"title":"Phase 6 build, the Weibull PM interval study","path":"course/phase-6-statistics-reliability/week-50-weibull-study-done-when.md","ticks":["Evidence pack: plot, R(current), cost curve, sensitivity, annual impact","One-page recommendation plus appendix","Presented and defended against the five questions. Done-when passed.","`portfolio/07-weibull-pm-study/` committed"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 6 · Statistics & reliability analytics** · Mon 11 Oct – Sun 17 Oct 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n> **Done when:** you can **defend a PM interval change with numbers** in front of an engineering manager.\n\nThis week you write it up as a professional, one-page-plus-appendix recommendation, then present it and take questions.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-build-the-evidence-pack","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Build the evidence pack","kind":"hands","md":"Pick **one** recurring packing-zone failure mode. The obvious one is **flow wrapper sealing jaws**, but you could choose another for a fresh analysis (carton erector suction cups, case packer grippers).\n\nProduce, from your own code:\n\n1. **Life data summary**: n failures, n suspensions, and how they were defined.\n2. **Weibull probability plot**, with β and η and their 95 % CIs.\n3. **The current interval in context**: R(current interval). What % of units reach their PM?\n4. **Cost curve** with the current, optimal and proposed points marked.\n5. **Sensitivity table**: the optimal interval for low, central and high breakdown costs.\n6. **Annual impact**: the expected breakdowns per year now vs proposed (number of units × 365 ÷ the mean cycle length × F(T)), plus labour hours and £.\n\n> **Tip for 6:** with age replacement at T, the expected cycle length is ∫₀ᵀ R(t) dt, and the chance a cycle ends in failure is F(T). So breakdowns per unit per day = F(T) ÷ ∫₀ᵀ R(t) dt.\n>\n> **Check values (jaws, 6 wrappers):** at 182 d the model predicts ~**28.6** jaw failures a year with no inspection. The data shows 35 breakdowns + 24 caught on inspection over two years, about 29.5 a year, so the model matches reality. At 42 d: ~**6** failures a year, plus ~48 planned changes.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-write-the-recommendation","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Write the recommendation","kind":"hands","md":"**Format: one page plus appendix.** Managers read the first page. Engineers check the appendix.\n\n### Page 1 template\n\n> **Recommendation:** Change *Replace sealing jaws* on the 6 Primary Packing flow wrappers from **182 days** to **42 days (6 weeks)**, aligned with the planned-stop schedule, and add a jaw-wear check to the existing weekly inspection.\n>\n> **Why:** Jaw failures are clearly wear-out (Weibull β ≈ 3.0, 95 % CI 2.3–3.9). Their characteristic life is ~86 days and 10 % fail by day 40. **Practically none reach the current 182-day replacement**, so today's PM prevents nothing: we pay for the PM *and* the breakdowns.\n>\n> **Expected benefit:** jaw breakdowns fall by about **X per year** (from ~Y to ~Z). Cost per wrapper per day falls from **£37.88 to £16.68**, about **£46k a year across 6 wrappers** at agreed costs (planned change £400, breakdown £2,900).\n>\n> **Confidence:** the result holds for any breakdown cost between £1,500 and £5,000 (optimum 30–49 days). The main uncertainty is the cost per breakdown, which needs to be agreed with Production.\n>\n> **How we'll know it worked:** we track jaw breakdowns for 6 months after the change and compare them with the prior rate using a rate test (decided now, before seeing the data).\n>\n> **Ask:** approve a 6-month trial on 2 wrappers first (LD-PPK-005 and LD-PPK-003, the worst two), with a review in [month].\n\nFill in X, Y and Z from your own calculation.\n\n### Appendix\n\nThe method (life-data rules, suspensions), the plots, the β/η table, the sensitivity table, the assumptions, and the limitations. **State your limitations honestly**: synthetic data, assumed costs, the first life of each asset excluded (its age was unknown at the start of the data), and a 24/7 running assumption.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-the-done-when-test","title":"Weekend (2 h): The done-when test","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Present it** to someone in 5 minutes: a supervisor, an engineer, or (at minimum) a friend you brief to act sceptical.\n2. Have them ask these, and practise your answers:\n   - \"How do you know it's wear-out and not random?\"\n   - \"Why should I trust 32 failures?\"\n   - \"What if the breakdown cost is lower than you think?\"\n   - \"Won't more frequent changes mean more installation errors?\"\n   - \"Why not just inspect more?\"\n3. If you can answer every one **with a number or a chart** from your pack, **Phase 6 is passed.** 🎉\n\n### Portfolio\n\n`portfolio/07-weibull-pm-study/`: the script, plots, one-page recommendation (as PDF) and appendix. This is the strongest piece in your portfolio for reliability roles, so make the README excellent.\n\n> **Credit twice:** this is exactly the kind of project an HNC/HND assignment or a CMRP application can use. Keep it.","mins":120},{"id":"check-yourself-model-answers-to-the-scep","title":"Check yourself: model answers to the sceptic's questions","kind":"check","md":"<details>\n<summary><strong>Show<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. **Wear-out?** β ≈ 3.0, and the whole 95 % interval (2.3–3.9) is well above 1. The probability plot is a straight line with no kink. Compare jams on the same machine: β ≈ 0.9, which is genuinely random. The method can tell the difference.\n2. **Only 32 failures?** There are also 48 suspensions (jaw sets replaced or still running), which the analysis uses. The confidence interval already reflects the sample size, and even its pessimistic end gives the same decision. The trial on 2 wrappers is also designed to confirm it.\n3. **Lower breakdown cost?** Down to £1,500, the optimum is still 49 days, far below 182. The decision doesn't change. Only the exact interval does.\n4. **Installation errors?** It's a fair risk (β < 1 behaviour after a change). Mitigate it with a standard work card, a torque or alignment check, and the trial, monitoring failures in the first week after each change. The data would show it.\n5. **Inspect more instead?** The weekly inspection already exists and catches about 4 in 10 jaw failures early (24 Corrective vs 35 Reactive jobs coded *Mechanical - jaws* over two years). That still leaves ~17 breakdowns a year. A better jaw-wear check is part of the recommendation, alongside the replacement, not instead of it.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 6 done-when, so none of it is skippable: the six-item evidence pack from Session 1, the one-page recommendation with X, Y and Z filled in plus the appendix, presenting it and answering all five sceptic's questions with a number or chart, and the `portfolio/07-weibull-pm-study/` folder. Give the write-up and the presentation separate sittings.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Practise your answers to the five sceptic's questions against the model answers in Check yourself, draft page 1 from the template on paper, and line up a supervisor or engineer at work to hear your 5-minute pitch.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only stretch items can wait: analysing a fresh failure mode instead of the jaws, and extra README polish beyond a clear summary and the plots."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Evidence pack: plot, R(current), cost curve, sensitivity, annual impact\n- [ ] One-page recommendation plus appendix\n- [ ] Presented and defended against the five questions. **Done-when passed.**\n- [ ] `portfolio/07-weibull-pm-study/` committed"}],"quiz":[{"id":"50-1","n":1,"q":"Wear-out?","a":"β ≈ 3.0, and the whole 95 % interval (2.3–3.9) is well above 1. The probability plot is a straight line with no kink. Compare jams on the same machine: β ≈ 0.9, which is genuinely random. The method can tell the difference."},{"id":"50-2","n":2,"q":"Only 32 failures?","a":"There are also 48 suspensions (jaw sets replaced or still running), which the analysis uses. The confidence interval already reflects the sample size, and even its pessimistic end gives the same decision. The trial on 2 wrappers is also designed to confirm it."},{"id":"50-3","n":3,"q":"Lower breakdown cost?","a":"Down to £1,500, the optimum is still 49 days, far below 182. The decision doesn't change. Only the exact interval does."},{"id":"50-4","n":4,"q":"Installation errors?","a":"It's a fair risk (β < 1 behaviour after a change). Mitigate it with a standard work card, a torque or alignment check, and the trial, monitoring failures in the first week after each change. The data would show it."},{"id":"50-5","n":5,"q":"Inspect more instead?","a":"The weekly inspection already exists and catches about 4 in 10 jaw failures early (24 Corrective vs 35 Reactive jobs coded *Mechanical - jaws* over two years). That still leaves ~17 breakdowns a year. A better jaw-wear check is part of the recommendation, alongside the replacement, not instead of it."}],"watch":[],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 6 done-when, so none of it is skippable: the six-item evidence pack from Session 1, the one-page recommendation with X, Y and Z filled in plus the appendix, presenting it and answering all five sceptic's questions with a number or chart, and the `portfolio/07-weibull-pm-study/` folder. Give the write-up and the presentation separate sittings."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Practise your answers to the five sceptic's questions against the model answers in Check yourself, draft page 1 from the template on paper, and line up a supervisor or engineer at work to hear your 5-minute pitch."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only stretch items can wait: analysing a fresh failure mode instead of the jaws, and extra README polish beyond a clear summary and the plots."}}},{"week":51,"phase":7,"title":"Machine learning basics, and the leakage trap","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-51-ml-basics.md","ticks":["Can explain features, labels, train/test, supervised vs unsupervised","Saw that accuracy ≈ the \"always no\" baseline","Overfitting shown with tree depth","Re-split by bearing with `GroupShuffleSplit`","Kaggle Intro to ML done"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 18 Oct – Sun 24 Oct 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n**Phase 7 goal:** an anomaly detector that flags bearing degradation before failure, and the ability to explain to a non-specialist **why its alerts are worth acting on and how many false alarms to expect**.\n\nML comes last on purpose. In maintenance, real failure data is scarce, so good trending and thresholds (Phases 5–6) often beat a model. You're learning ML to know **when it's worth it**, not to use it everywhere.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- explain supervised vs unsupervised learning, features, labels, training and testing\n- train your first scikit-learn model\n- explain overfitting, and why you **always** compare with a simple baseline\n- spot **data leakage**, and split by asset rather than by row\n\n**Data:** `data/ml/bearing_fleet.csv`. That's 60 bearings run to failure, with daily readings (RMS, peak, crest factor, kurtosis, temperature), the remaining useful life (`rul_days`), and a label `fail_within_14d`.\n\n> **Health warning:** this is synthetic data, and it's much cleaner than real data. The scores you get here will be far higher than on real plant data. The *methods* transfer; the numbers don't.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-vocabulary-and-a-first-mod","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The vocabulary and a first model","kind":"hands","md":"| Term | Meaning here |\n|---|---|\n| **Features (X)** | the inputs: RMS, kurtosis, temperature… |\n| **Label (y)** | what we want to predict: `fail_within_14d` (1/0) |\n| **Supervised** | learn from examples where the answer is known |\n| **Unsupervised** | find structure with no answers given (clusters, anomalies; Weeks 56–57) |\n| **Classification / regression** | predict a category / predict a number (like RUL, Week 60) |\n| **Training / test set** | learn on one part, judge on data the model has **never seen** |\n\n### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"ml\" / \"bearing_fleet.csv\")\ndf[\"fail_within_14d\"].mean()        # ≈ 0.05: only 5 % of days are 'about to fail'\n\nX = df[[\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"peak_accel_g\", \"crest_factor\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\"]]\ny = df[\"fail_within_14d\"]\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=0, stratify=y)\n\ntree = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=3, random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\ntree.score(X_test, y_test)          # accuracy\n```\n\nYou'll get about **98.7 %** accuracy. **Now compare it with the dumbest possible model, \"never predict failure\":**\n\n```python\n(y_test == 0).mean()                # ≈ 0.95\n```\n\n95 % accuracy for doing nothing. **Accuracy is useless when failures are rare.** Next week replaces it with precision and recall.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-overfitting-and-leakage","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Overfitting and leakage","kind":"hands","md":"### Overfitting\n\n```python\nfor depth in (1, 2, 3, 5, 10, None):\n    t = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=depth, random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\n    print(depth, round(t.score(X_train, y_train), 3), round(t.score(X_test, y_test), 3))\n```\n\nAs the depth grows, the **training** score heads to 100 % (depth unlimited: 1.000) while the **test** score falls (98.7 % → 97.9 %). The model is memorising, not learning. **Only the test score counts.**\n\n### The leakage trap\n\nThe split above shuffles **days** at random. So the model trains on day 200 of bearing B007 and is tested on day 201 of the same bearing. It's being tested on bearings it has already seen, which is like marking your own homework. In real use, you predict **new bearings**.\n\nSplit by **bearing** instead:\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupShuffleSplit\ngss = GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.25, random_state=0)\ntrain_idx, test_idx = next(gss.split(X, y, groups=df[\"bearing_id\"]))\nX_train, X_test = X.iloc[train_idx], X.iloc[test_idx]\ny_train, y_test = y.iloc[train_idx], y.iloc[test_idx]\ndf[\"bearing_id\"].iloc[test_idx].nunique()     # 15 bearings held out completely\n```\n\nWith the full feature set in the reference solution, a random forest scores F1 ≈ **0.90** with the leaky row split and ≈ **0.87** split by bearing. The gap is small here because the synthetic data is clean. **On real data, leakage routinely turns a useless model into a \"95 % accurate\" one.**\n\n### Why it works\n\n**Leakage** is any way information from the test set (or the future) gets into training. Common forms in maintenance:\n\n- the same asset in both train and test\n- features calculated *after* the event (labour hours on the breakdown WO, used to \"predict\" the breakdown)\n- random splits on time series, so the model learns from the future\n\nThe rule: **split the way you'll use it**. For new assets, split by asset. For future periods, train on the past and test on later dates.","mins":90,"why":[2],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Kaggle Learn: Intro to Machine Learning** ([kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-machine-learning](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-machine-learning)), all lessons.\n2. Start **Andrew Ng's Machine Learning Specialization** on Coursera (free to audit): [coursera.org/specializations/machine-learning-introduction](https://www.coursera.org/specializations/machine-learning-introduction). Watch course 1, week 1.\n3. Redo this week's tree with a **time-based** split: train on each bearing's days up to day 150 and test on later days. What changes, and why is this still not quite right for \"new bearings\"?\n4. Write 5 lines: where at work could ML genuinely help, and where would a threshold or a Weibull do the job better?","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest machine learning fundamentals\"*, *\"StatQuest decision trees\"*, *\"StatQuest bias and variance\"*.\n- **3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown but what is a neural network\"*. It's not used in this course, but it's great context.\n- Book: *Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras & TensorFlow* by Aurélien Géron, chapters 1–2."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why is 95 % accuracy meaningless here?\n2. What's overfitting, and how do you detect it?\n3. Give two ways data leakage can happen with maintenance data.\n4. You'll use the model on the **new** fans being installed next year. How should you split?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. 95 % of days are \"not about to fail\", so predicting \"never\" scores 95 % while catching zero failures.\n2. The model fits noise in the training data: a high training score with a much lower test score. Detect it by comparing train and test, or with cross-validation (next week).\n3. The same asset (or the same failure event) appearing in both train and test; features only known after the event (repair labour, a WO closed as \"bearing\"); a random split of a time series.\n4. By **bearing/asset**, ideally holding out whole machines, or better still a whole later batch, so the test mimics \"never seen before\".\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's first tree compared with the \"never predict failure\" baseline (98.7 % against 95 %), and Session 2's tree-depth loop for overfitting and the `GroupShuffleSplit` by bearing. That bearing split is used every week of this phase.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest machine learning fundamentals, decision trees and bias and variance videos, read the vocabulary table and the leakage \"Why it works\", answer Check yourself in your head, and write your 5 lines on where ML could help at work (weekend item 4) on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 3 (the time-based split), Andrew Ng's course 1 week 1 and the Géron chapters can wait, and the Kaggle Intro to ML lessons can be spread over the next couple of weeks."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Can explain features, labels, train/test, supervised vs unsupervised\n- [ ] Saw that accuracy ≈ the \"always no\" baseline\n- [ ] Overfitting shown with tree depth\n- [ ] Re-split by bearing with `GroupShuffleSplit`\n- [ ] Kaggle Intro to ML done"}],"quiz":[{"id":"51-1","n":1,"q":"Why is 95 % accuracy meaningless here?","a":"95 % of days are \"not about to fail\", so predicting \"never\" scores 95 % while catching zero failures."},{"id":"51-2","n":2,"q":"What's overfitting, and how do you detect it?","a":"The model fits noise in the training data: a high training score with a much lower test score. Detect it by comparing train and test, or with cross-validation (next week)."},{"id":"51-3","n":3,"q":"Give two ways data leakage can happen with maintenance data.","a":"The same asset (or the same failure event) appearing in both train and test; features only known after the event (repair labour, a WO closed as \"bearing\"); a random split of a time series."},{"id":"51-4","n":4,"q":"You'll use the model on the **new** fans being installed next year. How should you split?","a":"By **bearing/asset**, ideally holding out whole machines, or better still a whole later batch, so the test mimics \"never seen before\"."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest machine learning fundamentals\"*, *\"StatQuest decision trees\"*, *\"StatQuest bias and variance\"*.","searches":["StatQuest machine learning fundamentals","StatQuest decision trees","StatQuest bias and variance"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown but what is a neural network\"*. It's not used in this course, but it's great context.","searches":["3Blue1Brown but what is a neural network"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Book: *Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras & TensorFlow* by Aurélien Géron, chapters 1–2.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's first tree compared with the \"never predict failure\" baseline (98.7 % against 95 %), and Session 2's tree-depth loop for overfitting and the `GroupShuffleSplit` by bearing. That bearing split is used every week of this phase."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest machine learning fundamentals, decision trees and bias and variance videos, read the vocabulary table and the leakage \"Why it works\", answer Check yourself in your head, and write your 5 lines on where ML could help at work (weekend item 4) on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 3 (the time-based split), Andrew Ng's course 1 week 1 and the Géron chapters can wait, and the Kaggle Intro to ML lessons can be spread over the next couple of weeks."}}},{"week":52,"phase":7,"title":"Evaluation, precision, recall and the cost of false alarms","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-52-evaluation-metrics.md","ticks":["Confusion matrix, precision, recall and F1 calculated","The rule baseline (F1 0.74) is the bar to beat","Threshold trade-off table and a PR curve","GroupKFold cross-validation with its spread","Metrics translated into false alarms per month and £"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 25 Oct – Sun 31 Oct 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nThe phase goal ends with *\"…and how many false alarms to expect\"*. This week gives you the language for that. When failures are rare, **precision and recall** matter far more than accuracy, and choosing the alarm threshold is a **business decision** about the cost of a missed failure vs the cost of a wasted callout.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- read a confusion matrix\n- calculate and explain precision, recall and F1\n- use precision-recall curves and choose a threshold\n- cross-validate properly with `GroupKFold`\n- turn model metrics into \"alarms per month\" and \"failures caught\"\n\nReference code is in [`solutions/phase-7-ml/w51_w55_supervised.py`](../../solutions/phase-7-ml/w51_w55_supervised.py).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-confusion-matrix","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The confusion matrix","kind":"hands","md":"|  | Predicted: fail soon | Predicted: OK |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Actually failed soon** | **TP**: caught it ✅ | **FN**: missed it ❌ (breakdown) |\n| **Actually OK** | **FP**: false alarm ⚠️ (wasted check) | **TN**: correctly quiet |\n\n- **Precision** = TP ÷ (TP + FP): *\"when it alarms, how often is it right?\"* Low precision means people stop trusting it.\n- **Recall** = TP ÷ (TP + FN): *\"of the real failures, how many did it catch?\"* Low recall means breakdowns get through.\n- **F1** = the harmonic mean of the two, a single number for comparing models.\n\n### Do it\n\nUsing the bearing-split train/test from last week, plus the 7-day trend features (`_r7` rolling means and `_d7` 7-day changes; copy the feature code from the reference solution):\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, precision_score, recall_score, f1_score\ndef report(name, pred):\n    tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(y_test, pred).ravel()\n    print(f\"{name:20} TP={tp} FP={fp} FN={fn}  P={precision_score(y_test, pred):.2f} \"\n          f\"R={recall_score(y_test, pred):.2f} F1={f1_score(y_test, pred):.2f}\")\n\nreport(\"always no\", np.zeros(len(y_test), int))\nreport(\"rule RMS >= 4.5\", (X_test[\"rms_velocity_mm_s\"] >= 4.5).astype(int))\n```\n\n| Model (15 unseen bearings, daily rows) | TP | FP | FN | Precision | Recall | F1 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Always \"no\" | 0 | 0 | 210 | – | 0.00 | 0.00 |\n| **Rule: RMS ≥ 4.5 mm/s** (the ISO-style alarm) | 166 | 73 | 44 | 0.69 | 0.79 | **0.74** |\n\n**The simple alarm rule is a strong baseline.** Any ML model has to beat 0.74 to be worth its complexity.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-thresholds-and-cross-validatio","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Thresholds and cross-validation","kind":"hands","md":"### Probability, then threshold\n\nMost classifiers output a **probability**. You choose the cut-off:\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, min_samples_leaf=5, class_weight=\"balanced\",\n                            random_state=0, n_jobs=-1).fit(X_train, y_train)\np = rf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\nfor t in (0.3, 0.5, 0.7):\n    report(f\"RF threshold {t}\", (p >= t).astype(int))\n```\n\n| Threshold | FP | FN | Precision | Recall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.3 | 103 | 0 | 0.67 | 1.00 |\n| 0.5 | 59 | 1 | 0.78 | 1.00 |\n| 0.7 | 37 | 6 | 0.85 | 0.97 |\n\nA **lower threshold** catches everything but cries wolf more. A **higher threshold** gives fewer false alarms but misses more. **There is no free lunch, only a choice.**\n\n### Precision-recall curve\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.metrics import PrecisionRecallDisplay\nPrecisionRecallDisplay.from_predictions(y_test, p)\n```\n\nThe area under it (*average precision*, PR-AUC) summarises the whole trade-off. Here it's ≈ 0.98, but remember: synthetic data.\n\n### Cross-validation by group\n\nOne split can be lucky. **GroupKFold** rotates which bearings are held out:\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold, cross_val_score\nscores = cross_val_score(rf, X, y, groups=df[\"bearing_id\"], cv=GroupKFold(5), scoring=\"average_precision\")\nscores.round(2), scores.mean()      # five folds of ≈ 0.96–0.98\n```\n\nReport the **spread** as well as the mean: it tells you how much the result depends on which bearings you happened to test on.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-translate-it-into-plant-language","title":"Weekend (2 h): Translate it into plant language","kind":"hands","md":"Nobody on the shop floor cares about F1. Translate:\n\n1. The test set is 15 bearings over about 4,500 bearing-days. At threshold 0.5 there were **59 false-alarm days**. Per bearing-month, that's about 59 ÷ (4,484 ÷ 30) ≈ **0.4 false-alarm days per bearing per month**. With persistence (alarm only after 3 consecutive flags, Week 61), that drops a lot. Calculate it.\n2. **Cost it.** Say a false alarm costs a 30-minute check (£40), and a missed bearing failure costs £3,000 (downtime plus secondary damage). Calculate the total cost at thresholds 0.3, 0.5 and 0.7 on the test set. Which threshold is cheapest?\n3. **Event view.** Daily rows overstate things: one failing bearing produces 14 positive days. Re-score per *bearing*: did each one get **any** alarm in its last 14 days? Did any **healthy** period (RUL > 60) get an alarm?\n4. Coursera ML Specialization, course 1, weeks 2–3.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest confusion matrix\"*, *\"StatQuest sensitivity and specificity\"*, *\"StatQuest ROC and AUC\"*.\n- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest cross validation\"*.\n- **scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn precision recall\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. A model has precision 0.95 and recall 0.30. Describe what the maintenance team would experience.\n2. Why is a precision-recall curve better than ROC when failures are rare?\n3. Why cross-validate with `GroupKFold` rather than `KFold`?\n4. Your manager asks, \"How good is it?\" Give a one-sentence answer in plant terms for the RF at threshold 0.5.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Its alarms are almost always real, so the team trusts them, but it misses 70 % of failures, so most breakdowns still happen without warning.\n2. ROC uses the false positive rate, which stays tiny when negatives are huge (4,000+ healthy days), so models look great. Precision shows directly how many alarms are false.\n3. `KFold` would put days from the same bearing in both train and test (leakage). `GroupKFold` keeps each bearing entirely in one fold.\n4. \"On bearings it had never seen, it warned about every failure but one in the final two weeks, and about 1 alarm-day in 5 was a false alarm, mostly in the days just before the real warning period.\"\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's confusion matrix with the \"always no\" and RMS ≥ 4.5 rule baselines (F1 0.74), Session 2's random forest at thresholds 0.3, 0.5 and 0.7, the PR curve and `GroupKFold` with its spread, and weekend items 1 and 2: false alarms per bearing-month and the cost at each threshold.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest videos on the confusion matrix, sensitivity and specificity, ROC and AUC, and cross validation, read the precision and recall definitions, and practise Check yourself answer 4 as a one-sentence plant explanation.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 3 (the per-bearing event view, which Week 61 covers properly) and Coursera course 1 weeks 2 and 3 can be deferred."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Confusion matrix, precision, recall and F1 calculated\n- [ ] The rule baseline (F1 0.74) is the bar to beat\n- [ ] Threshold trade-off table and a PR curve\n- [ ] GroupKFold cross-validation with its spread\n- [ ] Metrics translated into false alarms per month and £"}],"quiz":[{"id":"52-1","n":1,"q":"A model has precision 0.95 and recall 0.30. Describe what the maintenance team would experience.","a":"Its alarms are almost always real, so the team trusts them, but it misses 70 % of failures, so most breakdowns still happen without warning."},{"id":"52-2","n":2,"q":"Why is a precision-recall curve better than ROC when failures are rare?","a":"ROC uses the false positive rate, which stays tiny when negatives are huge (4,000+ healthy days), so models look great. Precision shows directly how many alarms are false."},{"id":"52-3","n":3,"q":"Why cross-validate with `GroupKFold` rather than `KFold`?","a":"`KFold` would put days from the same bearing in both train and test (leakage). `GroupKFold` keeps each bearing entirely in one fold."},{"id":"52-4","n":4,"q":"Your manager asks, \"How good is it?\" Give a one-sentence answer in plant terms for the RF at threshold 0.5.","a":"\"On bearings it had never seen, it warned about every failure but one in the final two weeks, and about 1 alarm-day in 5 was a false alarm, mostly in the days just before the real warning period.\""}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest confusion matrix\"*, *\"StatQuest sensitivity and specificity\"*, *\"StatQuest ROC and AUC\"*.","searches":["StatQuest confusion matrix","StatQuest sensitivity and specificity","StatQuest ROC and AUC"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest cross validation\"*.","searches":["StatQuest cross validation"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn precision recall\"*.","searches":["scikit-learn precision recall"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's confusion matrix with the \"always no\" and RMS ≥ 4.5 rule baselines (F1 0.74), Session 2's random forest at thresholds 0.3, 0.5 and 0.7, the PR curve and `GroupKFold` with its spread, and weekend items 1 and 2: false alarms per bearing-month and the cost at each threshold."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest videos on the confusion matrix, sensitivity and specificity, ROC and AUC, and cross validation, read the precision and recall definitions, and practise Check yourself answer 4 as a one-sentence plant explanation."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 3 (the per-bearing event view, which Week 61 covers properly) and Coursera course 1 weeks 2 and 3 can be deferred."}}},{"week":53,"phase":7,"title":"Linear and logistic regression in scikit-learn","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-53-linear-logistic-regression.md","ticks":["Rolling and 7-day-change features engineered per bearing","Logistic regression pipeline, F1 ≈ 0.84 on unseen bearings","Coefficients interpreted","Feature ablation shows the value of trend features"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 1 Nov – Sun 7 Nov 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nYou met regression for *explaining* things in Week 45 (statsmodels). This week it's used for *predicting* things in scikit-learn, the standard ML library. You'll also learn the scikit-learn workflow (pipelines, scaling, fit/predict) that every later model uses.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- use the scikit-learn API: `fit`, `predict`, `predict_proba`, `score`\n- build a `Pipeline` with `StandardScaler`\n- train logistic regression for \"fails within 14 days\" and read its coefficients\n- engineer **trend features** (rolling mean, 7-day change), the biggest win in condition monitoring","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-feature-engineering-first","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Feature engineering first","kind":"hands","md":"A single day's reading says \"how rough is it now\". A **trend** says \"it's getting worse\", and that's what predicts failure.\n\n### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"ml\" / \"bearing_fleet.csv\").sort_values([\"bearing_id\", \"day\"])\nfor c in [\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\"]:\n    df[f\"{c}_r7\"] = df.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[c].transform(lambda s: s.rolling(7, min_periods=1).mean())\n    df[f\"{c}_d7\"] = df.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[c].diff(7)       # change over 7 days\ndf = df.dropna()\n\nFEATURES = [\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"peak_accel_g\", \"crest_factor\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\"] + \\\n           [c for c in df.columns if c.endswith((\"_r7\", \"_d7\"))]\nX, y, groups = df[FEATURES], df[\"fail_within_14d\"], df[\"bearing_id\"]\n```\n\nEvery feature is calculated **only from past and present readings of the same bearing**, so there's no peeking into the future. `diff(7)` within `groupby` makes sure one bearing's data never leaks into another's.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-logistic-regression-with-a-pip","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Logistic regression with a pipeline","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupShuffleSplit\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\ntr, te = next(GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.25, random_state=0).split(X, y, groups))\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = X.iloc[tr], X.iloc[te], y.iloc[tr], y.iloc[te]\n\nlogit = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(),\n                      LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, class_weight=\"balanced\"))\nlogit.fit(X_train, y_train)\npred = logit.predict(X_test)\n```\n\n**Result** (15 unseen bearings): TP 210, FP 82, FN 0. That's precision 0.72, recall **1.00**, F1 **0.84**, already better than the RMS rule (0.74).\n\n**Coefficients**: what the model learned:\n\n```python\ncoefs = pd.Series(logit[-1].coef_[0], index=FEATURES).sort_values()\ncoefs\n```\n\nBecause the features are **standardised** (mean 0, sd 1), the coefficients are comparable: bigger means more influence, and positive means it raises the chance of failure. The trend features (`_d7`, `_r7`) and temperature should stand out.\n\n### Why it works\n\n- **Logistic regression** is linear regression squashed through an S-curve into a probability between 0 and 1. It's simple, fast, explainable, and often hard to beat.\n- **StandardScaler** puts every feature on the same scale. Without it, temperature (around 40) would dominate kurtosis (around 3) just because of units.\n- **The Pipeline** fits the scaler on training data only, then applies the same scaling to test data. Fitting a scaler on all the data is another subtle leak.\n- **`class_weight=\"balanced\"`** tells the model that the rare class (failures) matters, instead of letting it hide behind 95 % \"no\".","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Linear regression for RUL**: predict `rul_days` from the same features with `LinearRegression`. Look at the mean absolute error (MAE) and plot predicted vs actual. Where is it worst? (Week 60 picks this up.)\n2. **Feature ablation**: retrain logistic regression with only the 5 raw features (no trends). How much worse is F1? That's the value of feature engineering, which is usually worth more than a fancier model.\n3. **Simplest useful model**: logistic regression on **two** features only, `rms_velocity_mm_s_d7` and `temp_c_d7`. Plot the decision boundary. Could a technician understand this rule?\n4. Coursera ML Specialization, course 1, week 3 (logistic regression).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest logistic regression\"* and *\"StatQuest regularization ridge lasso\"*.\n- **scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn pipelines and composite estimators\"*.\n- Géron, chapter 4 (training models)."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why must the scaler be fitted on training data only?\n2. What does a positive standardised coefficient on `temp_c_d7` mean?\n3. Why is a 7-day change often a better feature than today's value?\n4. When would you choose logistic regression over a random forest, even if the forest scores slightly better?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. If you scale using test data, the training process has \"seen\" the test data's mean and spread, which is a leak. The pipeline prevents it automatically.\n2. A faster rise in bearing temperature over the last week increases the predicted chance of failure within 14 days, holding the other features fixed.\n3. Different bearings have different \"normal\" levels. Change from *its own* recent past removes that difference and captures degradation, which is what the P-F curve is about.\n4. When explainability matters (you need to justify alarms to technicians and managers), when data is limited, or when it must run in a simple system (even a PLC or Excel could evaluate a linear formula).\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's `_r7` and `_d7` trend features built per bearing, Session 2's scaled logistic regression pipeline on the bearing split (F1 about 0.84) with its coefficients read, and weekend item 2, the ablation with raw features only. Later weeks reuse this feature set.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest logistic regression and regularization videos, read \"Why it works\" on scaling, pipelines and `class_weight`, and answer Check yourself in your head.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (linear regression for RUL, which Week 60 covers), item 3 (the two-feature decision boundary), the scikit-learn pipelines docs and Géron chapter 4 can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Rolling and 7-day-change features engineered per bearing\n- [ ] Logistic regression pipeline, F1 ≈ 0.84 on unseen bearings\n- [ ] Coefficients interpreted\n- [ ] Feature ablation shows the value of trend features"}],"quiz":[{"id":"53-1","n":1,"q":"Why must the scaler be fitted on training data only?","a":"If you scale using test data, the training process has \"seen\" the test data's mean and spread, which is a leak. The pipeline prevents it automatically."},{"id":"53-2","n":2,"q":"What does a positive standardised coefficient on `temp_c_d7` mean?","a":"A faster rise in bearing temperature over the last week increases the predicted chance of failure within 14 days, holding the other features fixed."},{"id":"53-3","n":3,"q":"Why is a 7-day change often a better feature than today's value?","a":"Different bearings have different \"normal\" levels. Change from *its own* recent past removes that difference and captures degradation, which is what the P-F curve is about."},{"id":"53-4","n":4,"q":"When would you choose logistic regression over a random forest, even if the forest scores slightly better?","a":"When explainability matters (you need to justify alarms to technicians and managers), when data is limited, or when it must run in a simple system (even a PLC or Excel could evaluate a linear formula)."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest logistic regression\"* and *\"StatQuest regularization ridge lasso\"*.","searches":["StatQuest logistic regression","StatQuest regularization ridge lasso"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn pipelines and composite estimators\"*.","searches":["scikit-learn pipelines and composite estimators"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Géron, chapter 4 (training models).","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's `_r7` and `_d7` trend features built per bearing, Session 2's scaled logistic regression pipeline on the bearing split (F1 about 0.84) with its coefficients read, and weekend item 2, the ablation with raw features only. Later weeks reuse this feature set."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest logistic regression and regularization videos, read \"Why it works\" on scaling, pipelines and `class_weight`, and answer Check yourself in your head."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (linear regression for RUL, which Week 60 covers), item 3 (the two-feature decision boundary), the scikit-learn pipelines docs and Géron chapter 4 can wait."}}},{"week":54,"phase":7,"title":"Decision trees and random forests","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-54-decision-trees-random-forest.md","ticks":["Depth-3 tree read and written out as a fault-finding rule","Random forest F1 ≈ 0.87 on unseen bearings","Impurity and permutation importance compared","Tested the forest without temperature features"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 8 Nov – Sun 14 Nov 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nDecision trees think like a fault-finding flowchart: *\"Is temperature above 45 °C? → Is RMS rising? → …\"*. That makes them the most explainable non-linear model. **Random forests** combine hundreds of trees for accuracy. This week you'll build both, read a tree like a fault-finding guide, and find which features matter.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- train, read and plot a decision tree\n- explain how a random forest reduces overfitting (bagging plus random features)\n- use feature importance (impurity-based and permutation), and know its pitfalls\n- tune `max_depth` and `min_samples_leaf`","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-a-tree-you-can-read","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): A tree you can read","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n(Use the feature set and bearing split from Week 53.)\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier, export_text, plot_tree\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ntree = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=3, class_weight=\"balanced\", random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint(export_text(tree, feature_names=FEATURES))\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(14, 6))\nplot_tree(tree, feature_names=FEATURES, class_names=[\"OK\", \"fail<14d\"], filled=True, fontsize=8)\n```\n\nThe first split is on **`temp_c ≤ 45.3 °C`**. Below that, the tree checks whether **RMS has risen more than ~0.74 mm/s in 7 days**. Read it like a fault-finding chart and write it out in plain English.\n\n**Score**: depth-3 tree TP 207, FP 109, FN 3. That's precision 0.66, recall 0.99, F1 0.79. It beats the RMS rule (0.74), but it's noisier than logistic regression (0.84).\n\n### Why it works\n\nAt each node the tree picks the feature and cut-off that best **separate** the classes (the biggest drop in \"impurity\"). Deep trees keep splitting until every leaf is pure, which means memorising noise. **Limit the depth** (or `min_samples_leaf`) to keep them general.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-random-forest","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Random forest","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, min_samples_leaf=5, class_weight=\"balanced\",\n                            random_state=0, n_jobs=-1).fit(X_train, y_train)\n```\n\n**Score**: TP 209, FP 59, FN 1. That's precision 0.78, recall 1.00, **F1 0.87**.\n\n**Feature importance:**\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\npd.Series(rf.feature_importances_, index=FEATURES).sort_values(ascending=False).head(6)\n```\n\nYou'll see `temp_c` (≈ 0.28) and `temp_c_r7` (≈ 0.21) at the top, then `rms_velocity_mm_s`, `peak_accel_g` and `kurtosis_r7`.\n\n**Permutation importance**, which is more trustworthy: shuffle one feature in the **test** set and see how much the score drops.\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\npi = permutation_importance(rf, X_test, y_test, scoring=\"average_precision\", n_repeats=10, random_state=0)\npd.Series(pi.importances_mean, index=FEATURES).sort_values(ascending=False).head(6)\n```\n\n### Why it works\n\nA random forest trains many trees, each on a random **bootstrap sample** of rows (Week 44's bootstrap again) and considering a random subset of features at each split. Each tree overfits differently, and **averaging** cancels much of it out. It's robust, needs little tuning, and is a sensible default for tabular data.\n\n**Importance pitfalls:**\n\n- Impurity importance favours features with many distinct values, and splits credit between **correlated** features (`temp_c` vs `temp_c_r7`).\n- Neither kind is *causation*. Temperature ranks top because, in this synthetic data, temperature rises cleanly with damage. On real fans, temperature often rises **late**, so you'd expect vibration features to matter more. **Always ask whether the importances make engineering sense.**","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Depth sweep**: train trees with `max_depth` 1–12 and plot train vs test F1. Where does overfitting start?\n2. **Forest tuning**: try `min_samples_leaf` in (1, 5, 20, 50). Use `GroupKFold` CV, not a single split.\n3. **Drop temperature**: retrain the forest without `temp_c*` features. How much does it lose? That's a realistic test, since many wireless vibration sensors measure temperature at the sensor, which lags.\n4. **Explain one alarm**: pick a test bearing's first alarm day, and look at its feature values against the tree's thresholds. Write the explanation a technician would accept.\n5. Kaggle Learn: **Intermediate Machine Learning** ([kaggle.com/learn/intermediate-machine-learning](https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intermediate-machine-learning)), lessons 1–4.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest decision trees\"*, *\"StatQuest random forests part 1\"*, *\"StatQuest random forests part 2\"*.\n- **scikit-learn docs**: search *\"permutation importance vs impurity importance scikit-learn\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. How does a random forest overfit less than a single deep tree?\n2. Why might `temp_c` and `temp_c_r7` *both* look important, or split the credit between them?\n3. Why use permutation importance on the **test** set?\n4. Why does the top feature here (temperature) worry you, from a real-world point of view?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Each tree sees a different bootstrap sample and random feature subsets, so their errors are partly independent. Averaging many noisy-but-different trees reduces variance.\n2. They carry almost the same information (highly correlated). The forest uses whichever one is available in each tree, so the credit gets spread across both, and dropping either alone may hardly change the score.\n3. It measures how much the model relies on a feature **for predictions on new data**. Training-set importance can reward features the model has overfitted.\n4. On real machines, bearing temperature usually rises late in the failure process, close to F on the P-F curve, so it gives little warning. The synthetic data makes it unrealistically early and clean. A model that leans on it could disappoint on real data.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's depth-3 tree written out as a plain-English fault-finding rule (F1 about 0.79), Session 2's random forest (F1 about 0.87) with impurity and permutation importance compared, and weekend item 3, retraining without the `temp_c` features.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest decision tree and random forest videos, read \"Why it works\" and the importance pitfalls, and answer Check yourself in your head, especially question 4 on why temperature worries you.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend items 1 and 2 (the depth sweep and `min_samples_leaf` tuning), item 4 (explaining one alarm) and the Kaggle Intermediate ML lessons can be deferred."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Depth-3 tree read and written out as a fault-finding rule\n- [ ] Random forest F1 ≈ 0.87 on unseen bearings\n- [ ] Impurity and permutation importance compared\n- [ ] Tested the forest without temperature features"}],"quiz":[{"id":"54-1","n":1,"q":"How does a random forest overfit less than a single deep tree?","a":"Each tree sees a different bootstrap sample and random feature subsets, so their errors are partly independent. Averaging many noisy-but-different trees reduces variance."},{"id":"54-2","n":2,"q":"Why might `temp_c` and `temp_c_r7` *both* look important, or split the credit between them?","a":"They carry almost the same information (highly correlated). The forest uses whichever one is available in each tree, so the credit gets spread across both, and dropping either alone may hardly change the score."},{"id":"54-3","n":3,"q":"Why use permutation importance on the **test** set?","a":"It measures how much the model relies on a feature **for predictions on new data**. Training-set importance can reward features the model has overfitted."},{"id":"54-4","n":4,"q":"Why does the top feature here (temperature) worry you, from a real-world point of view?","a":"On real machines, bearing temperature usually rises late in the failure process, close to F on the P-F curve, so it gives little warning. The synthetic data makes it unrealistically early and clean. A model that leans on it could disappoint on real data."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest decision trees\"*, *\"StatQuest random forests part 1\"*, *\"StatQuest random forests part 2\"*.","searches":["StatQuest decision trees","StatQuest random forests part 1","StatQuest random forests part 2"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**scikit-learn docs**: search *\"permutation importance vs impurity importance scikit-learn\"*.","searches":["permutation importance vs impurity importance scikit-learn"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's depth-3 tree written out as a plain-English fault-finding rule (F1 about 0.79), Session 2's random forest (F1 about 0.87) with impurity and permutation importance compared, and weekend item 3, retraining without the `temp_c` features."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest decision tree and random forest videos, read \"Why it works\" and the importance pitfalls, and answer Check yourself in your head, especially question 4 on why temperature worries you."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend items 1 and 2 (the depth sweep and `min_samples_leaf` tuning), item 4 (explaining one alarm) and the Kaggle Intermediate ML lessons can be deferred."}}},{"week":55,"phase":7,"title":"Gradient boosting, tuning, and \"is ML worth it?\"","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-55-gradient-boosting-tuning.md","ticks":["Gradient boosting trained (F1 ≈ 0.93)","Hyperparameters tuned with GroupKFold CV, test used once","The full comparison table filled in","Half-page \"rule vs model\" recommendation written"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 15 Nov – Sun 21 Nov 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nGradient boosting is usually the most accurate model for tabular data. It wins most Kaggle competitions on spreadsheets. This week you'll train it, tune hyperparameters properly with grouped cross-validation, and then answer the question that matters: **is the improvement over a simple rule worth the complexity?**\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- train `HistGradientBoostingClassifier`\n- tune hyperparameters with `GridSearchCV` + `GroupKFold`\n- compare all your models fairly in one table\n- make (and defend) a \"rule vs model\" recommendation","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-boosting","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Boosting","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingClassifier\ngb = HistGradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=0, class_weight=\"balanced\").fit(X_train, y_train)\n```\n\n**Score** (15 unseen bearings): TP 206, FP 29, FN 4. That's precision **0.88**, recall 0.98, **F1 0.93**, the best so far.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA random forest builds trees **independently** and averages them. Boosting builds them **one after another**: each new small tree focuses on the mistakes the previous trees made. `learning_rate` controls how big each correction is, and `max_iter` sets how many trees. It's powerful, but **easier to overfit** than a forest, so validation matters more.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-tuning-properly","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Tuning properly","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV, GroupKFold\ngrid = {\n    \"learning_rate\": [0.03, 0.1, 0.3],\n    \"max_depth\": [3, None],\n    \"min_samples_leaf\": [20, 100],\n}\nsearch = GridSearchCV(HistGradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=0, class_weight=\"balanced\"),\n                      grid, cv=GroupKFold(5), scoring=\"average_precision\", n_jobs=-1)\nsearch.fit(X_train, y_train, groups=groups.iloc[tr])\nsearch.best_params_, round(search.best_score_, 3)\n```\n\nThen evaluate `search.best_estimator_` **once** on the held-out test bearings.\n\n### The golden rule\n\nThe test set is for **one final check**. If you tune on it (\"try settings until the test score looks good\"), it stops being a fair test, and your reported score is optimistic. Tune with CV on the training bearings only.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-the-comparison-and-the-decision","title":"Weekend (2 h): The comparison and the decision","kind":"hands","md":"Fill in this table on the same 15 test bearings:\n\n| Approach | Precision | Recall | F1 | Explainable? | Effort to maintain |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rule: RMS ≥ 4.5 mm/s | 0.69 | 0.79 | 0.74 | ✅ very | none |\n| Rule: RMS 7-day mean ≥ 4.5 | 0.73 | 0.68 | 0.71 | ✅ very | none |\n| Logistic regression | 0.72 | 1.00 | 0.84 | ✅ mostly | low |\n| Decision tree (depth 3) | 0.66 | 0.99 | 0.79 | ✅ yes | low |\n| Random forest | 0.78 | 1.00 | 0.87 | ⚠️ partly | medium |\n| Gradient boosting | 0.88 | 0.98 | 0.93 | ⚠️ partly | medium |\n| **Your tuned GB** | | | | | |\n\nThen write a **half-page recommendation**:\n\n- What does the best model gain over the RMS alarm, in plant terms (more failures caught, fewer false alarms)?\n- What would it cost: a data pipeline, retraining, someone to own it, and explaining alarms?\n- **What's the honest risk?** This is clean synthetic data. On real data with sensor dropouts, different machines and only a handful of real failures, the gap may shrink or disappear.\n- Your recommendation. A strong answer is often: *\"Start with trend-based rules (Week 41) plus a simple anomaly detector (Week 61). Collect labelled failures for a year. Revisit supervised ML when there are at least 20–30 real failures of the same type.\"*","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest gradient boost part 1\"* and *\"StatQuest XGBoost part 1\"* (the same family).\n- **scikit-learn docs**: search *\"HistGradientBoostingClassifier\"* and *\"GridSearchCV\"*.\n- Kaggle Learn Intermediate ML: the *XGBoost* and *Data Leakage* lessons."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. What's the difference between bagging (random forest) and boosting?\n2. Why mustn't you tune hyperparameters on the test set?\n3. GB scores F1 0.93 vs 0.74 for the rule. Give two reasons the real-world gap might be much smaller.\n4. When is a model clearly worth deploying over a rule?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Bagging trains trees independently on resampled data and averages them, which reduces variance. Boosting trains trees in sequence, each correcting the previous errors, which reduces bias. Boosting is usually more accurate but more sensitive to tuning and noise.\n2. Each tuning decision based on the test score leaks test information into the model choice, so the final test score is no longer an unbiased estimate of performance on new data.\n3. Any two of: real data is noisier (dropouts, mounting changes, speed and load variation); there are far fewer real failures to learn from; failure modes differ between machines; the synthetic features (especially temperature) are unrealistically clean; and the \"14-day\" label is fuzzy in reality.\n4. When it beats the rule by a margin that matters **in £ or hours** on realistic validation (grouped, time-ordered), the alarms can be explained well enough for people to act on them, and someone owns the pipeline, monitoring and retraining.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's gradient boosting on the bearing split (F1 about 0.93), Session 2's `GridSearchCV` with `GroupKFold` on the training bearings and one final test, then the weekend comparison table and your half-page \"rule vs model\" recommendation.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest gradient boost and XGBoost videos, read \"Why it works\" and the golden rule on the test set, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the half-page recommendation on paper, starting from the four bullet questions.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** The Kaggle XGBoost and Data Leakage lessons and the scikit-learn docs pages can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Gradient boosting trained (F1 ≈ 0.93)\n- [ ] Hyperparameters tuned with GroupKFold CV, test used once\n- [ ] The full comparison table filled in\n- [ ] Half-page \"rule vs model\" recommendation written"}],"quiz":[{"id":"55-1","n":1,"q":"What's the difference between bagging (random forest) and boosting?","a":"Bagging trains trees independently on resampled data and averages them, which reduces variance. Boosting trains trees in sequence, each correcting the previous errors, which reduces bias. Boosting is usually more accurate but more sensitive to tuning and noise."},{"id":"55-2","n":2,"q":"Why mustn't you tune hyperparameters on the test set?","a":"Each tuning decision based on the test score leaks test information into the model choice, so the final test score is no longer an unbiased estimate of performance on new data."},{"id":"55-3","n":3,"q":"GB scores F1 0.93 vs 0.74 for the rule. Give two reasons the real-world gap might be much smaller.","a":"Any two of: real data is noisier (dropouts, mounting changes, speed and load variation); there are far fewer real failures to learn from; failure modes differ between machines; the synthetic features (especially temperature) are unrealistically clean; and the \"14-day\" label is fuzzy in reality."},{"id":"55-4","n":4,"q":"When is a model clearly worth deploying over a rule?","a":"When it beats the rule by a margin that matters **in £ or hours** on realistic validation (grouped, time-ordered), the alarms can be explained well enough for people to act on them, and someone owns the pipeline, monitoring and retraining."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest gradient boost part 1\"* and *\"StatQuest XGBoost part 1\"* (the same family).","searches":["StatQuest gradient boost part 1","StatQuest XGBoost part 1"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**scikit-learn docs**: search *\"HistGradientBoostingClassifier\"* and *\"GridSearchCV\"*.","searches":["HistGradientBoostingClassifier","GridSearchCV"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"Kaggle Learn Intermediate ML: the *XGBoost* and *Data Leakage* lessons.","searches":[],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's gradient boosting on the bearing split (F1 about 0.93), Session 2's `GridSearchCV` with `GroupKFold` on the training bearings and one final test, then the weekend comparison table and your half-page \"rule vs model\" recommendation."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest gradient boost and XGBoost videos, read \"Why it works\" and the golden rule on the test set, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the half-page recommendation on paper, starting from the four bullet questions."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"The Kaggle XGBoost and Data Leakage lessons and the scikit-learn docs pages can wait."}}},{"week":56,"phase":7,"title":"Unsupervised learning, clustering assets with k-means","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-56-clustering-kmeans.md","ticks":["Asset profile table built from CMMS data","k chosen with elbow plus silhouette (k = 4)","Clusters named and mapped to strategies","PCA scatter and a one-page segmentation note"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 22 Nov – Sun 28 Nov 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nUnsupervised learning finds structure **without labels**. You don't tell it what to look for. This week you'll cluster Line D's 292 assets by their maintenance behaviour, and find natural groups that could each get a different maintenance strategy.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build an asset \"profile\" table from CMMS data\n- scale features and run k-means\n- choose k with the elbow method and silhouette scores\n- name and interpret clusters, and turn them into strategy\n\nReference code is in [`solutions/phase-7-ml/w56_w60_unsupervised_signals_rul.py`](../../solutions/phase-7-ml/w56_w60_unsupervised_signals_rul.py).","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-profiles-and-k-means","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Profiles and k-means","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nwo = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"work_orders.csv\", parse_dates=[\"due_date\", \"completed_date\"])\ndt = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"downtime_events.csv\")\nassets = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"csv\" / \"assets.csv\")\nr = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"Reactive\"]\npm = wo[wo[\"wo_type\"] == \"PM\"].assign(on_time=lambda t: t[\"completed_date\"].le(t[\"due_date\"]))\n\nprof = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"breakdowns\":  r.groupby(\"asset_id\").size(),\n    \"mttr_hrs\":    dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Breakdown\"].groupby(\"asset_id\")[\"duration_min\"].mean() / 60,\n    \"pm_on_time\":  pm.groupby(\"asset_id\")[\"on_time\"].mean(),\n    \"minor_stops\": dt[dt[\"category\"] == \"Minor stop\"].groupby(\"asset_id\").size(),\n}).reindex(assets[\"asset_id\"]).fillna({\"breakdowns\": 0, \"mttr_hrs\": 0, \"minor_stops\": 0, \"pm_on_time\": 1})\n\nZ = StandardScaler().fit_transform(prof)\nfor k in range(2, 7):\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(Z)\n    print(k, round(km.inertia_), round(silhouette_score(Z, km.labels_), 3))\n```\n\n| k | inertia | silhouette |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 768 | 0.42 |\n| 3 | 606 | 0.37 |\n| **4** | **450** | **0.39** |\n| 5 | 382 | 0.30 |\n| 6 | 336 | 0.29 |\n\nThe **elbow** is at k = 4 (a big drop in inertia, and silhouette holds up). k = 2 scores higher on silhouette but just splits \"bad actors\" from \"the rest\", which is too coarse to act on.\n\n### Why it works\n\nk-means places k centres and assigns each asset to the nearest one, then moves each centre to the middle of its assets, and repeats. **Scaling is essential**: without it, minor-stop counts (up to ~100) would swamp PM on-time (0–1). **Inertia** is the total distance to the centres, which always falls as k grows. Look for the bend. **Silhouette** (−1 to 1) measures how well separated the clusters are.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-name-the-clusters","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Name the clusters","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nkm = KMeans(n_clusters=4, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(Z)\nprof[\"cluster\"] = km.labels_\nsummary = prof.groupby(\"cluster\").mean().round(2)\nsummary[\"n\"] = prof.groupby(\"cluster\").size()\nsummary\nprof.join(assets.set_index(\"asset_id\")[\"asset_type\"]).groupby(\"cluster\")[\"asset_type\"].value_counts().groupby(level=0).head(3)\n```\n\n| Cluster | n | Breakdowns | MTTR (h) | PM on time | Minor stops | Mostly… | A name |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 170 | 2.3 | 0.96 | 87 % | 9 | conveyors, sensors | **Steady majority** |\n| 1 | 44 | 3.2 | **4.80** | 83 % | 9 | drive motors, flaking rolls, gearboxes | **Rare but long** |\n| 2 | 13 | **20.8** | 0.92 | 70 % | **49** | flow wrappers, label printers, case packers | **Bad actors** |\n| 3 | 65 | 3.4 | 1.29 | **68 %** | 44 | packing/EOL conveyors, servos, cylinders | **Stop-start packing kit** |\n\n(Cluster numbers can come out in a different order on your PC, but the groups will be the same.)\n\n### Turn clusters into strategy\n\n| Cluster | Strategy |\n|---|---|\n| Steady majority | keep the current PM, and review whether some inspections could be stretched (free up labour) |\n| Rare but long | **spares and repair planning**: critical spares on the shelf, repair procedures, condition monitoring (vibration and oil on gearboxes and motors) |\n| Bad actors | **RCA and redesign focus** (Week 49: jaws and jams), weekly review |\n| Stop-start packing kit | minor-stop elimination (SMED, sensor setup, guarding) and **fixing PM compliance** in packing |","mins":90},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Visualise**: run PCA to 2D (`from sklearn.decomposition import PCA`) and scatter the assets coloured by cluster. Label the bad actors.\n2. **Add features**: criticality (A=3, B=2, C=1) and asset age. Do the clusters change? Do they still make sense?\n3. **Try DBSCAN** (`sklearn.cluster.DBSCAN`), which finds dense groups and marks outliers as −1. Which assets does it call outliers?\n4. Write a one-page **\"asset segmentation\"** note for the PM pillar: four groups, what defines each, and the strategy for each. It's a genuinely useful document at work.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest k-means clustering\"*, *\"StatQuest PCA main ideas\"*.\n- **scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn clustering overview\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why scale before k-means?\n2. Why not just pick the k with the highest silhouette score?\n3. Cluster labels change between runs (0 becomes 2). Is that a problem?\n4. How is this different from sorting assets by breakdowns and taking the top 10?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. k-means uses distances. Features with big numbers would dominate and features with small ranges would be ignored. Scaling gives each feature equal say.\n2. Silhouette rewards clean separation, not usefulness. k = 2 is the cleanest, but it lumps very different assets together. Choose a k that's well separated **and** actionable.\n3. No. The labels are arbitrary names. Interpret clusters by their **profiles**, not their numbers, and fix `random_state` for repeatability.\n4. A top-10 uses one dimension. Clustering uses several together, so it separates \"fails rarely but takes 5 hours\" from \"fails constantly but takes 1 hour\", which need different strategies.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's asset profile table, scaling and the k = 2 to 6 loop (elbow at k = 4), Session 2's cluster summary named and mapped to strategies, and weekend items 1 and 4: the PCA scatter and the one-page segmentation note.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the StatQuest k-means and PCA main ideas videos, read the \"Why it works\" on scaling, inertia and silhouette and the cluster-to-strategy table, and draft the segmentation note on paper. Check yourself question 4 is a good one to talk through at work.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 2 (adding criticality and age), item 3 (DBSCAN) and the scikit-learn clustering overview can be skipped this week."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Asset profile table built from CMMS data\n- [ ] k chosen with elbow plus silhouette (k = 4)\n- [ ] Clusters named and mapped to strategies\n- [ ] PCA scatter and a one-page segmentation note"}],"quiz":[{"id":"56-1","n":1,"q":"Why scale before k-means?","a":"k-means uses distances. Features with big numbers would dominate and features with small ranges would be ignored. Scaling gives each feature equal say."},{"id":"56-2","n":2,"q":"Why not just pick the k with the highest silhouette score?","a":"Silhouette rewards clean separation, not usefulness. k = 2 is the cleanest, but it lumps very different assets together. Choose a k that's well separated **and** actionable."},{"id":"56-3","n":3,"q":"Cluster labels change between runs (0 becomes 2). Is that a problem?","a":"No. The labels are arbitrary names. Interpret clusters by their **profiles**, not their numbers, and fix `random_state` for repeatability."},{"id":"56-4","n":4,"q":"How is this different from sorting assets by breakdowns and taking the top 10?","a":"A top-10 uses one dimension. Clustering uses several together, so it separates \"fails rarely but takes 5 hours\" from \"fails constantly but takes 1 hour\", which need different strategies."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**StatQuest**: *\"StatQuest k-means clustering\"*, *\"StatQuest PCA main ideas\"*.","searches":["StatQuest k-means clustering","StatQuest PCA main ideas"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**scikit-learn docs**: search *\"scikit-learn clustering overview\"*.","searches":["scikit-learn clustering overview"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's asset profile table, scaling and the k = 2 to 6 loop (elbow at k = 4), Session 2's cluster summary named and mapped to strategies, and weekend items 1 and 4: the PCA scatter and the one-page segmentation note."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the StatQuest k-means and PCA main ideas videos, read the \"Why it works\" on scaling, inertia and silhouette and the cluster-to-strategy table, and draft the segmentation note on paper. Check yourself question 4 is a good one to talk through at work."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 2 (adding criticality and age), item 3 (DBSCAN) and the scikit-learn clustering overview can be skipped this week."}}},{"week":57,"phase":7,"title":"Anomaly detection, finding \"not normal\" without failure labels","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-57-anomaly-detection.md","ticks":["Baseline z-score detector with persistence (91 / 55 days' lead, 0 false alarms)","Isolation Forest detector built and compared","Threshold and persistence sweep table","Robust (median/MAD) baseline tried"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 29 Nov – Sun 5 Dec 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nSupervised models need many labelled failures, which you rarely have. **Anomaly detection** only needs examples of **normal** running: learn what \"healthy\" looks like, then flag anything that drifts away. That's how most real condition-monitoring analytics work, and it's the basis of your Phase 7 build.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- build a baseline-based detector (z-score against each machine's own normal)\n- use scikit-learn's **Isolation Forest**\n- add a **persistence filter** to stop single-reading false alarms\n- measure lead time and false alarms on real-shaped data\n\n**Data:** `data/vibration/bearing_trend.csv`, the 6 dryer fan bearings from Week 41.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-baseline-z-scores","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Baseline z-scores","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\nv = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"vibration\" / \"bearing_trend.csv\", parse_dates=[\"date\"]).sort_values([\"bearing_id\", \"date\"])\nF = [\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\", \"crest_factor\"]\nv[\"d\"] = v.groupby(\"bearing_id\").cumcount()\n\n# 'Normal' = each bearing's first 90 days\ntrain = v[v[\"d\"] < 90]\nbase = train.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[F].agg([\"mean\", \"std\"])\nz = pd.DataFrame({f: (v[f] - v[\"bearing_id\"].map(base[(f, \"mean\")]))\n                      / v[\"bearing_id\"].map(base[(f, \"std\")]) for f in F})\nv[\"z_flag\"] = (z.abs() > 4).any(axis=1)\n\n# Persistence: alarm only after 3 flagged readings in a row\nv[\"z_alarm\"] = v.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"z_flag\"].transform(lambda s: s.rolling(3).sum() >= 3)\nfirst = v[v[\"z_alarm\"]].groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"date\"].min()\nfail = v[v[\"status\"] == \"Failed\"].set_index(\"bearing_id\")[\"date\"]\npd.DataFrame({\"first_alarm\": first, \"failed\": fail}).assign(lead_days=lambda t: (t[\"failed\"] - t[\"first_alarm\"]).dt.days)\n```\n\n| Bearing | First alarm | Failed | Lead |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DRY-FAN-B2 | 24 Feb 2026 | 26 May 2026 | **91 days** |\n| DRY-FAN-B5 | 22 Dec 2025 | 15 Feb 2026 | **55 days** |\n| B1, B3, B4, B6 (healthy) | none | | **0 false alarms** |\n\nCompare that with Week 41's fixed 2.8 mm/s alert, which gave **64** and **30** days. Learning *each bearing's own normal* gives roughly **4 extra weeks** of warning.\n\n### Why it works\n\nA fixed limit treats every machine the same. A baseline detector asks *\"is this machine unlike its own past?\"*, which catches change much earlier, especially on machines that normally run smoothly. Using several features at once (vibration, kurtosis, temperature) catches different failure signatures.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-isolation-forest","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Isolation Forest","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import IsolationForest\niso = IsolationForest(n_estimators=300, contamination=0.01, random_state=0).fit(train[F])\nv[\"iso_flag\"] = iso.predict(v[F]) == -1          # -1 = anomaly\nv[\"iso_alarm\"] = v.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[\"iso_flag\"].transform(lambda s: s.rolling(3).sum() >= 3)\n```\n\n**Result:** B2 alarms 18 Feb (97 days' lead) and B5 on 18 Dec (59 days). There are no alarms on the healthy bearings.\n\n### Why it works\n\nIsolation Forest builds random trees that split the data at random. **Unusual points get isolated in fewer splits**, so a short average path means an anomaly. It handles several features together and needs no labels. `contamination` is your guess at the share of anomalies in the training data, and it sets the threshold.\n\n### Compare\n\n| Method | B2 lead | B5 lead | False alarms | Explain to a technician? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fixed 2.8 mm/s alert (Week 41) | 64 d | 30 d | 0 | ✅ easy |\n| Baseline z > 4, 3 in a row | 91 d | 55 d | 0 | ✅ \"4× its normal spread, three days running\" |\n| Isolation Forest, 3 in a row | 97 d | 59 d | 0 | ⚠️ harder |\n\nThe simple z-score gets **most of the benefit**, and you can explain it in one sentence. That's usually the right first deployment.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Threshold sweep**: try z thresholds of 3, 4, 5 and 6, and persistence of 1, 3 and 5. Make a table of lead days and false alarms. Where does it start crying wolf?\n2. **Robust baseline**: replace mean/std with median/MAD (`(x - median).abs().median() * 1.4826`). Why is that safer if the baseline period accidentally includes a few bad readings?\n3. **Which feature fired first?** For B5, find the first day each feature's |z| exceeded 4. Does kurtosis lead RMS? (That's the early-impact signature from Week 41.)\n4. **Read**: the scikit-learn docs page *\"Novelty and Outlier Detection\"*.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"isolation forest explained\"*.\n- Search *\"anomaly detection predictive maintenance baseline\"*. Vendor webinars (SKF, Emerson, AVEVA) often show this exact approach."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does anomaly detection suit maintenance better than supervised learning, at first?\n2. What does the persistence filter trade away, and what does it gain?\n3. A machine is overhauled and its vibration drops permanently. What happens to your detector, and what should you do?\n4. The baseline period is the first 90 days. When would that be a bad choice?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. You need examples of **normal**, which you have plenty of, not labelled failures, which are rare. It works from day one on a new sensor once a baseline exists.\n2. It costs a couple of days of reaction time and gains far fewer false alarms from single noisy readings. That's usually a good trade, given that the P-F interval is weeks long.\n3. The baseline is now wrong. Lower readings might look \"anomalous\", or real degradation might take longer to stand out against the old, rougher baseline. **Re-baseline after any maintenance intervention or change in operating condition.**\n4. If the machine was already degrading, newly installed (running-in), or running at a different speed or load then. The baseline must be known-good, representative running.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's baseline z-score detector with the 3-in-a-row persistence filter (91 and 55 days' lead, 0 false alarms), Session 2's Isolation Forest and the compare table, and weekend items 1 and 2: the threshold and persistence sweep and the median/MAD baseline. Week 61 builds directly on this.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch an \"isolation forest explained\" video and a vendor webinar on baseline anomaly detection, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and answer Check yourself in your head, especially question 3 on re-baselining after an overhaul.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 3 (which feature fired first on B5) and the scikit-learn *Novelty and Outlier Detection* page can wait."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Baseline z-score detector with persistence (91 / 55 days' lead, 0 false alarms)\n- [ ] Isolation Forest detector built and compared\n- [ ] Threshold and persistence sweep table\n- [ ] Robust (median/MAD) baseline tried"}],"quiz":[{"id":"57-1","n":1,"q":"Why does anomaly detection suit maintenance better than supervised learning, at first?","a":"You need examples of **normal**, which you have plenty of, not labelled failures, which are rare. It works from day one on a new sensor once a baseline exists."},{"id":"57-2","n":2,"q":"What does the persistence filter trade away, and what does it gain?","a":"It costs a couple of days of reaction time and gains far fewer false alarms from single noisy readings. That's usually a good trade, given that the P-F interval is weeks long."},{"id":"57-3","n":3,"q":"A machine is overhauled and its vibration drops permanently. What happens to your detector, and what should you do?","a":"The baseline is now wrong. Lower readings might look \"anomalous\", or real degradation might take longer to stand out against the old, rougher baseline. **Re-baseline after any maintenance intervention or change in operating condition.**"},{"id":"57-4","n":4,"q":"The baseline period is the first 90 days. When would that be a bad choice?","a":"If the machine was already degrading, newly installed (running-in), or running at a different speed or load then. The baseline must be known-good, representative running."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"isolation forest explained\"*.","searches":["isolation forest explained"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"anomaly detection predictive maintenance baseline\"*. Vendor webinars (SKF, Emerson, AVEVA) often show this exact approach.","searches":["anomaly detection predictive maintenance baseline"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's baseline z-score detector with the 3-in-a-row persistence filter (91 and 55 days' lead, 0 false alarms), Session 2's Isolation Forest and the compare table, and weekend items 1 and 2: the threshold and persistence sweep and the median/MAD baseline. Week 61 builds directly on this."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch an \"isolation forest explained\" video and a vendor webinar on baseline anomaly detection, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and answer Check yourself in your head, especially question 3 on re-baselining after an overhaul."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 3 (which feature fired first on B5) and the scikit-learn *Novelty and Outlier Detection* page can wait."}}},{"week":58,"phase":7,"title":"Vibration signal features, RMS, peak, crest factor and kurtosis","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-58-vibration-signal-features.md","ticks":["Waveforms plotted, impacts visible on the faulty ones","Nyquist and frequency resolution explained","RMS, peak, crest factor and kurtosis calculated from raw data","Can explain why crest factor and kurtosis lead RMS for early faults"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 6 Dec – Sun 12 Dec 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nSo far you've used ready-made daily readings. A real sensor captures a **waveform**: thousands of acceleration samples a second. This week you calculate the standard **time-domain features** from raw waveforms yourself, and learn what each one tells you about a bearing. It links directly to sampling and signals in your Digital Principles studies.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- load and plot a vibration waveform, and explain sampling rate and Nyquist\n- calculate RMS, peak, peak-to-peak, crest factor and kurtosis\n- explain why crest factor and kurtosis catch **early** bearing faults\n- convert acceleration to velocity in principle, and know why mm/s is the standard\n\n**Data:** `data/vibration/waveforms/`. Three 1-second captures from a fan bearing at **5,120 samples/s**, shaft speed **24.6 Hz** (1,476 rpm): `fan_bearing_healthy.csv`, `fan_bearing_early_fault.csv` and `fan_bearing_late_fault.csv`.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-look-at-the-waves","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Look at the waves","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\" / \"vibration\" / \"waveforms\"\n\nwaves = {n: pd.read_csv(DATA / f\"fan_bearing_{n}.csv\") for n in (\"healthy\", \"early_fault\", \"late_fault\")}\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(3, 1, figsize=(10, 7), sharex=True, sharey=True)\nfor ax, (name, w) in zip(axes, waves.items()):\n    seg = w[w[\"time_s\"] < 0.2]                    # first 200 ms\n    ax.plot(seg[\"time_s\"], seg[\"accel_g\"], linewidth=0.7)\n    ax.set_title(name, loc=\"left\")\naxes[-1].set_xlabel(\"time (s)\")\n```\n\nThe healthy signal is a smooth wave (shaft rotation) plus noise. The faulty signals show **sharp spikes at regular intervals**: a rolling element hitting a defect on the outer race, again and again. The late fault spikes are bigger.\n\n### Sampling basics\n\n- **Sampling rate fs** = 5,120 Hz, so one sample every 0.195 ms.\n- **Nyquist**: you can only see frequencies up to **fs ÷ 2 = 2,560 Hz**. Higher frequencies *alias*, folding down and appearing as false lower ones. That's why sensors have anti-aliasing filters.\n- **Frequency resolution** = 1 ÷ capture length = **1 Hz** for a 1-second capture.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-time-domain-features","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Time-domain features","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ndef features(x):\n    rms = np.sqrt(np.mean(x ** 2))\n    peak = np.max(np.abs(x))\n    return {\n        \"rms_g\": rms,\n        \"peak_g\": peak,\n        \"peak_to_peak_g\": x.max() - x.min(),\n        \"crest_factor\": peak / rms,\n        \"kurtosis\": stats.kurtosis(x, fisher=False),     # 3.0 for a normal distribution\n    }\n\npd.DataFrame({n: features(w[\"accel_g\"].to_numpy()) for n, w in waves.items()}).T.round(2)\n```\n\n| | RMS (g) | Peak (g) | Crest factor | Kurtosis |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| healthy | 0.25 | 0.72 | 2.85 | 2.2 |\n| early fault | 0.29 | 1.09 | **3.81** | 2.7 |\n| late fault | 0.49 | 2.07 | 4.20 | **4.9** |\n\n### Why it works\n\n- **RMS** measures overall energy. It hardly moves for the early fault (+14 %), because a few short spikes don't add much energy. It rises clearly when damage is widespread.\n- **Peak** and **crest factor** (peak ÷ RMS) react to **impacts**. Crest factor jumps 34 % at the early fault, **before RMS notices**. In very late stages the crest factor can *fall* again as damage spreads and RMS catches up. That's a classic trap: \"crest factor dropped, it must be better\" is wrong.\n- **Kurtosis** measures \"spikiness\". About 3 is normal noise, and well above 3 means impulsive. (The healthy value is below 3 here because a pure sine wave has kurtosis 1.5, and this signal is part sine, part noise.)\n- **Velocity (mm/s)** is the standard overall measure for machine health from about 10 Hz to 1 kHz (ISO 10816/20816). It's acceleration integrated once. **Acceleration (g)** emphasises high frequencies, which suits early bearing faults. You need both.","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Feature over time**: split each 1-second waveform into ten 0.1 s windows and calculate the features per window. How stable is each feature? Which is the noisiest?\n2. **Velocity**: integrate acceleration to velocity in the frequency domain (preview of next week), or approximately with `np.cumsum(x) / fs` after removing the mean and high-pass filtering. Calculate the RMS velocity in mm/s (1 g = 9,806.65 mm/s²).\n3. **Real data (optional)**: if you downloaded the NASA IMS or Case Western bearing data, calculate these features on a few files from early and late in a test. Search *\"Case Western Reserve University bearing data center\"*. The files are small `.mat` files; use `scipy.io.loadmat`.\n4. **Link it**: how would you get these features out of a real sensor system? Many wireless CM systems export overall values (RMS, peak) as CSV and waveforms on request. Your Week 41 script is the consumer.","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **Mobius Institute**: search *\"Mobius Institute crest factor kurtosis\"* and *\"Mobius Institute acceleration velocity displacement\"*.\n- Search *\"Nyquist sampling aliasing explained\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why does RMS barely change for an early bearing fault?\n2. Crest factor goes 2.9 → 3.8 → 4.2. Later still, it falls to 3.0 while RMS doubles. What's happening?\n3. With fs = 5,120 Hz, can you measure a 3 kHz bearing resonance? Why or why not?\n4. Why monitor both velocity and acceleration?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The impacts are short and only happen a few times per revolution, so they add little to the total energy that RMS measures. They're \"loud but brief\".\n2. The damage has spread (many defects and a rough surface), so impacts merge into continuous high vibration. RMS rises and catches up with the peaks, so their ratio falls. It's a sign of a **worsening**, late-stage fault.\n3. No. 3 kHz is above Nyquist (2,560 Hz). It would alias, appearing at 5,120 − 3,000 = 2,120 Hz, if the anti-aliasing filter didn't remove it. You'd need a higher sampling rate.\n4. Velocity tracks overall machine condition in the 10–1,000 Hz range (imbalance, misalignment, looseness) and maps onto the ISO severity zones. Acceleration emphasises the high-frequency impacts of early bearing and gear faults. Each catches what the other misses.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's waveform plots for the three captures, and Session 2's `features()` function giving RMS, peak, crest factor and kurtosis for each, checked against the table.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read the \"Sampling basics\" and the Session 2 \"Why it works\" bullets, watch the Mobius Institute crest factor and kurtosis video and a Nyquist aliasing explainer, and work Check yourself question 3 (the 3 kHz resonance) on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (features per 0.1 s window), item 2 (integrating to velocity, which Week 59 previews) and item 3 (the optional Case Western or NASA data) can be skipped."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] Waveforms plotted, impacts visible on the faulty ones\n- [ ] Nyquist and frequency resolution explained\n- [ ] RMS, peak, crest factor and kurtosis calculated from raw data\n- [ ] Can explain why crest factor and kurtosis lead RMS for early faults"}],"quiz":[{"id":"58-1","n":1,"q":"Why does RMS barely change for an early bearing fault?","a":"The impacts are short and only happen a few times per revolution, so they add little to the total energy that RMS measures. They're \"loud but brief\"."},{"id":"58-2","n":2,"q":"Crest factor goes 2.9 → 3.8 → 4.2. Later still, it falls to 3.0 while RMS doubles. What's happening?","a":"The damage has spread (many defects and a rough surface), so impacts merge into continuous high vibration. RMS rises and catches up with the peaks, so their ratio falls. It's a sign of a **worsening**, late-stage fault."},{"id":"58-3","n":3,"q":"With fs = 5,120 Hz, can you measure a 3 kHz bearing resonance? Why or why not?","a":"No. 3 kHz is above Nyquist (2,560 Hz). It would alias, appearing at 5,120 − 3,000 = 2,120 Hz, if the anti-aliasing filter didn't remove it. You'd need a higher sampling rate."},{"id":"58-4","n":4,"q":"Why monitor both velocity and acceleration?","a":"Velocity tracks overall machine condition in the 10–1,000 Hz range (imbalance, misalignment, looseness) and maps onto the ISO severity zones. Acceleration emphasises the high-frequency impacts of early bearing and gear faults. Each catches what the other misses."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**Mobius Institute**: search *\"Mobius Institute crest factor kurtosis\"* and *\"Mobius Institute acceleration velocity displacement\"*.","searches":["Mobius Institute crest factor kurtosis","Mobius Institute acceleration velocity displacement"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"Nyquist sampling aliasing explained\"*.","searches":["Nyquist sampling aliasing explained"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's waveform plots for the three captures, and Session 2's `features()` function giving RMS, peak, crest factor and kurtosis for each, checked against the table."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read the \"Sampling basics\" and the Session 2 \"Why it works\" bullets, watch the Mobius Institute crest factor and kurtosis video and a Nyquist aliasing explainer, and work Check yourself question 3 (the 3 kHz resonance) on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (features per 0.1 s window), item 2 (integrating to velocity, which Week 59 previews) and item 3 (the optional Case Western or NASA data) can be skipped."}}},{"week":59,"phase":7,"title":"FFT, frequency bands and bearing defect frequencies","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-59-fft-bearing-frequencies.md","ticks":["FFT and Welch spectra plotted; 1× and 2× identified","Resonance band energy: 3 % → 21 % → 59 %","Envelope analysis finds 88 Hz = BPFO","`band_energy()` feature function written","Fault frequency table made"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 13 Dec – Sun 19 Dec 2027 · about 5 hours\n\nTime-domain features tell you **something** is wrong. The **frequency domain** tells you **what**: imbalance at 1× shaft speed, misalignment at 2×, an outer-race bearing defect at its BPFO. This week you'll take waveforms apart with the FFT and diagnose the fault in the data from first principles.\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- compute and plot an FFT spectrum and a Welch power spectrum\n- identify 1× and 2× shaft-speed peaks\n- calculate bearing defect frequencies (BPFO, BPFI, BSF, FTF) from geometry\n- use **envelope analysis** to reveal a bearing defect frequency\n- turn frequency bands into ML features","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-spectrum","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The spectrum","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import signal\nfrom pathlib import Path\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\" / \"vibration\" / \"waveforms\"\nfs, shaft = 5120, 24.6\n\nw = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"fan_bearing_late_fault.csv\")[\"accel_g\"].to_numpy()\nspec = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(w * np.hanning(len(w)))) / len(w) * 2\nfreqs = np.fft.rfftfreq(len(w), 1 / fs)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 4))\nax.plot(freqs, spec, linewidth=0.7)\nfor k in (1, 2):\n    ax.axvline(k * shaft, color=\"#C62828\", linestyle=\":\", label=f\"{k}× shaft\")\nax.set_xlim(0, 2560); ax.set_xlabel(\"Hz\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"g\"); ax.legend()\n```\n\nYou'll see:\n\n- a sharp peak at **24.6 Hz (1×)**: shaft rotation, a little imbalance (every machine has some)\n- a small one at **49 Hz (2×)**\n- in the faulty signals, a broad **hump around 1,000–1,400 Hz**: the structure's **resonance**, being \"rung\" by the bearing impacts\n\n**Welch spectrum** (averaged, smoother) and a band feature:\n\n```python\nfor name in (\"healthy\", \"early_fault\", \"late_fault\"):\n    x = pd.read_csv(DATA / f\"fan_bearing_{name}.csv\")[\"accel_g\"].to_numpy()\n    f, P = signal.welch(x, fs=fs, nperseg=2048)\n    share = P[(f > 1000) & (f < 1400)].sum() / P.sum()\n    print(name, round(share, 3))\n```\n\nThe 1.0–1.4 kHz band holds **3 %** of the energy when healthy, **21 %** at the early fault, and **59 %** at the late fault. That's a brilliant, simple feature for ML.\n\n### Why it works\n\nThe **FFT** (Fast Fourier Transform) breaks a signal into the sine waves that make it up: how much of each frequency is present. A **Hann window** (`np.hanning`) tapers the ends of the capture so the edges don't smear energy across the spectrum (called leakage, a different leakage from Week 51's).","mins":90,"why":[1],"readMins":2},{"id":"session-2-bearing-frequencies-and-envelo","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Bearing frequencies and envelope analysis","kind":"hands","md":"### Bearing defect frequencies\n\nFrom the bearing geometry: n rolling elements, ball diameter d, pitch diameter D, contact angle φ, shaft speed fr:\n\n$$\\text{BPFO} = \\frac{n}{2} f_r \\left(1 - \\frac{d}{D}\\cos\\varphi\\right) \\qquad \\text{BPFI} = \\frac{n}{2} f_r \\left(1 + \\frac{d}{D}\\cos\\varphi\\right)$$\n\n$$\\text{BSF} = \\frac{D}{2d} f_r \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{d}{D}\\cos\\varphi\\right)^2\\right) \\qquad \\text{FTF} = \\frac{f_r}{2} \\left(1 - \\frac{d}{D}\\cos\\varphi\\right)$$\n\nIn practice you look up the **multipliers** (× shaft speed) in the bearing maker's data. For the bearing in this data, **BPFO = 3.585 × shaft = 88.2 Hz**.\n\n### Envelope analysis: finding the hidden rhythm\n\nThe raw spectrum shows the resonance hump, but not *how often* the impacts happen. **Envelope analysis** does: band-pass filter around the resonance, take the envelope (the outline of the ringing bursts), then FFT the envelope.\n\n```python\nfor name in (\"healthy\", \"early_fault\", \"late_fault\"):\n    x = pd.read_csv(DATA / f\"fan_bearing_{name}.csv\")[\"accel_g\"].to_numpy()\n    b, a = signal.butter(4, [900, 1500], btype=\"bandpass\", fs=fs)\n    env = np.abs(signal.hilbert(signal.filtfilt(b, a, x)))\n    env -= env.mean()\n    es = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(env)) / len(env)\n    ef = np.fft.rfftfreq(len(env), 1 / fs)\n    m = (ef > 10) & (ef < 400)\n    print(name, \"envelope peak at\", round(ef[m][np.argmax(es[m])], 1), \"Hz\")\n```\n\n| Signal | Envelope peak | Diagnosis |\n|---|---|---|\n| healthy | 65 Hz (noise; no clear peak) | nothing |\n| early fault | **88 Hz** | **outer race defect** (BPFO 88.2 Hz) |\n| late fault | **88 Hz** (+ harmonics at 176, 264) | outer race defect, advanced |\n\nYou've just diagnosed a bearing fault from raw data, the way a vibration analyst does.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. Plot the envelope spectra for all three signals with vertical lines at 1×, 2× and 3× BPFO.\n2. **Band features for ML**: write `band_energy(x, fs, bands)`, which returns the share of energy in, say, 0–100, 100–500, 500–1,000, 1,000–1,500 and 1,500–2,560 Hz. These features, plus Week 58's, are what a real CM model uses.\n3. **Look up a real bearing**: find the defect-frequency multipliers for a **6205** or **6308** deep-groove bearing (manufacturers publish bearing frequency calculators). Calculate BPFO, BPFI, BSF and FTF at 1,475 rpm.\n4. **Fault table**: from a vibration chart (search *\"vibration analysis fault frequency chart\"*), make your own one-page table covering imbalance, misalignment, looseness, bearing faults, gear mesh, belt and electrical (2× line frequency = 100 Hz in the UK).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- **3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown but what is the Fourier transform\"*. It's the best intuition there is.\n- **Mobius Institute**: *\"Mobius Institute envelope analysis\"* and *\"Mobius Institute bearing defect frequencies\"*.\n- **SciPy docs**: search *\"scipy signal welch\"* and *\"scipy signal hilbert\"*."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. A big peak at 1× shaft speed, mostly radial. What's the likely fault?\n2. Why can't you see BPFO clearly in the raw spectrum, but can in the envelope spectrum?\n3. Why is 100 Hz a suspicious frequency on a UK motor?\n4. How would you turn this week's work into a feature for the Week 61 anomaly detector?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. **Imbalance** (1× radial). Misalignment shows more at 2× and axially. Looseness gives many harmonics.\n2. Each impact is a short burst that rings the structure at its resonance (~1.2 kHz), so the raw spectrum shows energy at the resonance, not at the impact *rate*. The envelope recovers the repetition rate, which is BPFO.\n3. It's 2× the 50 Hz supply frequency: the electromagnetic forces in an AC motor (stator problems, eccentric air gap, soft foot affecting the magnetic field).\n4. Calculate per capture: the 1.0–1.4 kHz band energy share, the envelope amplitude at BPFO ± 2 Hz, and the kurtosis. Trend them, give them a baseline per bearing, and feed them into the z-score detector alongside RMS.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's FFT with 1× and 2× marked and the Welch band share (3 %, 21 %, 59 %), Session 2's envelope analysis finding 88 Hz (BPFO), and weekend item 2, the `band_energy()` function.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Watch the 3Blue1Brown Fourier transform video and the Mobius Institute envelope analysis and bearing defect frequency videos, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and start your weekend item 4 fault frequency table on paper from a vibration chart.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (envelope plots with BPFO harmonics) and item 3 (looking up a 6205 or 6308 bearing) can wait, along with the SciPy docs."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] FFT and Welch spectra plotted; 1× and 2× identified\n- [ ] Resonance band energy: 3 % → 21 % → 59 %\n- [ ] Envelope analysis finds 88 Hz = BPFO\n- [ ] `band_energy()` feature function written\n- [ ] Fault frequency table made"}],"quiz":[{"id":"59-1","n":1,"q":"A big peak at 1× shaft speed, mostly radial. What's the likely fault?","a":"**Imbalance** (1× radial). Misalignment shows more at 2× and axially. Looseness gives many harmonics."},{"id":"59-2","n":2,"q":"Why can't you see BPFO clearly in the raw spectrum, but can in the envelope spectrum?","a":"Each impact is a short burst that rings the structure at its resonance (~1.2 kHz), so the raw spectrum shows energy at the resonance, not at the impact *rate*. The envelope recovers the repetition rate, which is BPFO."},{"id":"59-3","n":3,"q":"Why is 100 Hz a suspicious frequency on a UK motor?","a":"It's 2× the 50 Hz supply frequency: the electromagnetic forces in an AC motor (stator problems, eccentric air gap, soft foot affecting the magnetic field)."},{"id":"59-4","n":4,"q":"How would you turn this week's work into a feature for the Week 61 anomaly detector?","a":"Calculate per capture: the 1.0–1.4 kHz band energy share, the envelope amplitude at BPFO ± 2 Hz, and the kurtosis. Trend them, give them a baseline per bearing, and feed them into the z-score detector alongside RMS."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"**3Blue1Brown**: *\"3Blue1Brown but what is the Fourier transform\"*. It's the best intuition there is.","searches":["3Blue1Brown but what is the Fourier transform"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"**Mobius Institute**: *\"Mobius Institute envelope analysis\"* and *\"Mobius Institute bearing defect frequencies\"*.","searches":["Mobius Institute envelope analysis","Mobius Institute bearing defect frequencies"],"urls":[]},{"i":2,"md":"**SciPy docs**: search *\"scipy signal welch\"* and *\"scipy signal hilbert\"*.","searches":["scipy signal welch","scipy signal hilbert"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's FFT with 1× and 2× marked and the Welch band share (3 %, 21 %, 59 %), Session 2's envelope analysis finding 88 Hz (BPFO), and weekend item 2, the `band_energy()` function."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Watch the 3Blue1Brown Fourier transform video and the Mobius Institute envelope analysis and bearing defect frequency videos, read both \"Why it works\" parts, and start your weekend item 4 fault frequency table on paper from a vibration chart."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (envelope plots with BPFO harmonics) and item 3 (looking up a 6205 or 6308 bearing) can wait, along with the SciPy docs."}}},{"week":60,"phase":7,"title":"Remaining useful life (RUL) prediction, and its limits","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-60-rul-prediction-limits.md","ticks":["RUL random forest trained, MAE reported by RUL band","Can explain the \"flat then dropping\" prediction shape","Prediction interval from tree spread, with coverage checked","Planner-friendly decision rule written"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 20 Dec – Sun 26 Dec 2027 · about 5 hours\n\n\"How long has it got?\" is the question every manager asks once an alarm goes off. **Remaining useful life (RUL)** models try to answer it. This week you'll build one, then learn the most important lesson about RUL: **it's only useful close to failure, and its uncertainty matters more than its average.**\n\n**By Sunday you'll be able to:**\n\n- frame RUL as a regression problem\n- train and evaluate a RUL model with MAE, by RUL band\n- explain why RUL error is huge far from failure and small near it\n- present RUL as a range with confidence, not a single date\n- describe the NASA C-MAPSS benchmark","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-a-rul-model","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): A RUL model","kind":"hands","md":"### Do it\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupShuffleSplit\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nDATA = Path(\"..\") / \"data\"\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(DATA / \"ml\" / \"bearing_fleet.csv\").sort_values([\"bearing_id\", \"day\"])\nfor c in [\"rms_velocity_mm_s\", \"kurtosis\", \"temp_c\"]:\n    df[f\"{c}_r7\"] = df.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[c].transform(lambda s: s.rolling(7, min_periods=1).mean())\n    df[f\"{c}_d7\"] = df.groupby(\"bearing_id\")[c].diff(7)\ndf = df.dropna()\nX = df.drop(columns=[\"bearing_id\", \"day\", \"rul_days\", \"fail_within_14d\"])\ny = df[\"rul_days\"]\n\ntr, te = next(GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.25, random_state=0).split(X, y, df[\"bearing_id\"]))\nrf = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=300, min_samples_leaf=5, random_state=0, n_jobs=-1)\nrf.fit(X.iloc[tr], y.iloc[tr])\npred, true = rf.predict(X.iloc[te]), y.iloc[te]\nmean_absolute_error(true, pred)\n```\n\n**Overall MAE ≈ 51 days.** It sounds poor. Now split it by how far from failure each reading was:\n\n```python\nfor lo, hi in ((0, 30), (30, 90), (90, 500)):\n    m = (true >= lo) & (true < hi)\n    print(f\"true RUL {lo}-{hi} d: MAE {mean_absolute_error(true[m], pred[m]):.1f} days\")\n```\n\n| True RUL | MAE |\n|---|---|\n| 0–30 days | **3.5 days** |\n| 30–90 days | 38 days |\n| 90+ days | 62 days |\n\nPlot predicted against true RUL for 3 test bearings. The prediction is **flat** while the bearing is healthy, then **drops onto the true line** once degradation starts.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-why-rul-is-hard","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Why RUL is hard","kind":"hands","md":"### The physics\n\nBefore a defect starts (P on the P-F curve), a healthy bearing gives **no information** about when it will fail. Healthy ones all look the same. So any model can only guess something like the *average* life. Once degradation begins, the trend carries real information, and the error collapses.\n\n**So:** RUL is a tool for **\"we have an alarm, so when should we plan the job?\"**, not for \"how long will this healthy bearing last?\". For healthy units, the right tool is your **Weibull** model from Phase 6: a population-level probability, not an individual prediction.\n\n### Present it honestly\n\nNever say \"it will fail on 14 March\". Give a range. A simple way to get one from a random forest is to use the spread of the individual trees' predictions:\n\n```python\nall_trees = np.stack([t.predict(X.iloc[te].to_numpy()) for t in rf.estimators_])\nlo, hi = np.percentile(all_trees, [10, 90], axis=0)\n```\n\nCheck how often the true RUL falls inside [lo, hi]. If it's far from 80 %, the band is mis-calibrated. That's a known weakness of this quick method; *quantile regression* or *conformal prediction* do it properly.\n\n*\"Probably 10–20 days; plan the change for next week's stop\"* is a statement people can act on.\n\n### NASA C-MAPSS\n\nThe standard RUL benchmark is turbofan engine simulations from NASA. It has many sensors, run-to-failure trajectories in train, and test engines cut off at a random point. Search *\"NASA C-MAPSS turbofan dataset\"*. It's a good stretch project, and it appears in many papers, so you can compare your results with published ones.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend","title":"Weekend (2 h)","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Piecewise RUL target**: many papers cap RUL at a maximum, e.g. `y_capped = y.clip(upper=60)`, meaning \"healthy, > 60 days\". Retrain. Does the 0–30 day error improve? Why does that make sense physically?\n2. **Interval coverage**: calculate the 10–90 % band coverage for readings with true RUL under 30 days.\n3. **Decision rule**: write a rule that a planner could use. For example: *\"When the anomaly detector alarms, run the RUL model daily; when the lower bound falls below 14 days, schedule the replacement in the next planned stop.\"*\n4. (Optional) Download C-MAPSS FD001 and fit the same random forest. Compare your RMSE with published baselines (search *\"C-MAPSS FD001 RMSE benchmark\"*).","mins":120},{"id":"watch","title":"Watch","kind":"watch","md":"- Search *\"remaining useful life prediction explained\"* and *\"prognostics and health management introduction\"*.\n- Search *\"conformal prediction intervals explained\"*. It's an advanced but very practical way to get honest uncertainty bands."},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why is RUL error so large when the bearing is healthy?\n2. What's the right tool for \"how long will a healthy bearing last?\"\n3. Why give a range instead of a date?\n4. How does RUL connect to the P-F curve?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. Before degradation starts, the sensor readings are the same for a bearing with 300 days left as for one with 100 days left. There's no signal to learn from, only the population average.\n2. A **Weibull** (or other life distribution) model of the population: \"there's a 10 % chance of failing by day 40\" (Phase 6). It's probabilistic, not individual.\n3. The uncertainty is real and large. A single date creates false confidence: if the bearing fails early, people stop trusting the system, and if it fails late, parts get changed too early. A range supports planning (\"within the next two planned stops\").\n4. RUL models only work in the P-F interval, after the defect is detectable. The anomaly detector finds P, and the RUL model then estimates where on the way to F the bearing is.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** Session 1's RUL random forest with MAE split by RUL band (3.5, 38 and 62 days) and the predicted against true plot for 3 test bearings, Session 2's tree-spread interval with its coverage checked, and weekend item 3, your planner-friendly decision rule.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read \"The physics\" and \"Present it honestly\" in Session 2, watch a \"remaining useful life prediction explained\" video, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the weekend item 3 decision rule on paper.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Weekend item 1 (the capped RUL target), item 2 (coverage under 30 days only), the optional C-MAPSS download and the conformal prediction video can be skipped."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] RUL random forest trained, MAE reported by RUL band\n- [ ] Can explain the \"flat then dropping\" prediction shape\n- [ ] Prediction interval from tree spread, with coverage checked\n- [ ] Planner-friendly decision rule written"}],"quiz":[{"id":"60-1","n":1,"q":"Why is RUL error so large when the bearing is healthy?","a":"Before degradation starts, the sensor readings are the same for a bearing with 300 days left as for one with 100 days left. There's no signal to learn from, only the population average."},{"id":"60-2","n":2,"q":"What's the right tool for \"how long will a healthy bearing last?\"","a":"A **Weibull** (or other life distribution) model of the population: \"there's a 10 % chance of failing by day 40\" (Phase 6). It's probabilistic, not individual."},{"id":"60-3","n":3,"q":"Why give a range instead of a date?","a":"The uncertainty is real and large. A single date creates false confidence: if the bearing fails early, people stop trusting the system, and if it fails late, parts get changed too early. A range supports planning (\"within the next two planned stops\")."},{"id":"60-4","n":4,"q":"How does RUL connect to the P-F curve?","a":"RUL models only work in the P-F interval, after the defect is detectable. The anomaly detector finds P, and the RUL model then estimates where on the way to F the bearing is."}],"watch":[{"i":0,"md":"Search *\"remaining useful life prediction explained\"* and *\"prognostics and health management introduction\"*.","searches":["remaining useful life prediction explained","prognostics and health management introduction"],"urls":[]},{"i":1,"md":"Search *\"conformal prediction intervals explained\"*. It's an advanced but very practical way to get honest uncertainty bands.","searches":["conformal prediction intervals explained"],"urls":[]}],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"Session 1's RUL random forest with MAE split by RUL band (3.5, 38 and 62 days) and the predicted against true plot for 3 test bearings, Session 2's tree-spread interval with its coverage checked, and weekend item 3, your planner-friendly decision rule."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read \"The physics\" and \"Present it honestly\" in Session 2, watch a \"remaining useful life prediction explained\" video, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the weekend item 3 decision rule on paper."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Weekend item 1 (the capped RUL target), item 2 (coverage under 30 days only), the optional C-MAPSS download and the conformal prediction video can be skipped."}}},{"week":61,"phase":7,"title":"Phase 7 build, the bearing anomaly detector","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-61-anomaly-detector-build.md","ticks":["`score()`, `alarms()` and `evaluate()` written and tested","Threshold chosen with the evaluation table (6, persistence 3)","60/60 detected, 0 false alarms, median ~62 days' warning","Config file, alarm CSV and plain-English alarm sentences"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 27 Dec 2027 – Sun 2 Jan 2028 · about 5 hours\n\nEverything from Phases 5–7 comes together: a reusable **anomaly detector** that learns each bearing's normal, scores drift across several features, alarms only on persistent change, and is **evaluated honestly**: how much warning, and how many false alarms. It's built so you can point it at real condition-monitoring exports by changing one function.\n\n**Reference solution:** [`solutions/phase-7-ml/anomaly_detector.py`](../../solutions/phase-7-ml/anomaly_detector.py). Build your own version in `my_work\\`, then compare.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-design-and-core-functions","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): Design and core functions","kind":"hands","md":"### The design\n\n```\nload_*()      →  standard table: bearing_id, t (day index), features…\nscore()       →  per-bearing robust z-score vs baseline (median/MAD), smoothed, max across features\nalarms()      →  True when score ≥ threshold for N consecutive readings\nevaluate_*()  →  lead time per failure, false alarms, misses\nplot()        →  score vs time with the threshold line\n```\n\n**Why median/MAD?** It's robust (Week 57): a few odd readings in the baseline period don't distort \"normal\".\n\n**Why the max across features?** Different faults show first in different features: kurtosis for early impacts, RMS for spreading damage, temperature late. Taking the worst z-score means any of them can raise the alarm.\n\n### Do it\n\nWrite `score()` and `alarms()`, then test them on `bearing_trend.csv` until the two failed bearings break away from the healthy four. (Use the reference solution's chart as a target.)","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-evaluate-on-the-fleet","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Evaluate on the fleet","kind":"hands","md":"**Two test sets:**\n\n1. **The fleet** (`data/ml/bearing_fleet.csv`): 60 bearings, each run to failure. For each one: did it alarm? How many days before failure? Did it alarm **too early** (say > 150 days before failure), which counts as a false alarm because that alarm wasn't really about this failure?\n2. **The site trend** (`bearing_trend.csv`): alarms on B2 and B5, and **silence** on B1, B3, B4 and B6.\n\n### Choose the threshold, with numbers\n\n```powershell\npython solutions\\phase-7-ml\\anomaly_detector.py --threshold 4\npython solutions\\phase-7-ml\\anomaly_detector.py --threshold 6\npython solutions\\phase-7-ml\\anomaly_detector.py --threshold 8\n```\n\n| Threshold | Fleet detected | Fleet false alarms | Median warning | Site B2 / B5 warning | Healthy site bearings alarmed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 58 / 60 | **2** | 67 d | 96 / 52 d | **1 (B4)** ⚠️ |\n| **6** | **60 / 60** | **0** | **62 d** | **85 / 48 d** | **0** ✅ |\n| 8 | 60 / 60 | 0 | 56 d | 79 / 44 d | 0 |\n\n**Threshold 6 with 3-reading persistence** catches every failure with a median of ~2 months' warning and no false alarms in either test set. A lower threshold buys a few days and costs false alarms. A higher one gives up warning time for nothing.\n\nCompare with the fixed 2.8 mm/s ISO-style alert from Week 41 (B2 64 d, B5 30 d): the detector gives **+3 weeks** on both.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-make-it-production-shaped","title":"Weekend (2 h): Make it production-shaped","kind":"hands","md":"1. **Config, not code**: move the threshold, persistence and baseline days into a small JSON or YAML file the script reads. Changing a setting shouldn't need a code edit.\n2. **Re-baseline**: add a `rebaseline_after` option that takes a list of maintenance dates per bearing and restarts the baseline 7 days after each one.\n3. **Add Week 59's spectral features** if you have waveform data: band energy share and envelope amplitude at BPFO.\n4. **Output an alarm list** as a CSV (bearing, first alarm date, score, which feature fired) in a form a planner could read, or a CMMS could import as notifications.\n5. **Write the alarm explanation**: for each alarm, one sentence, e.g. *\"DRY-FAN-B5: vibration (RMS) and spikiness (kurtosis) both about 7× their normal spread for 3 days (first 29/12/2025). Bearing damage likely; confirm with a handheld check and plan a bearing change within ~6 weeks.\"*","mins":120},{"id":"check-yourself","title":"Check yourself","kind":"check","md":"1. Why evaluate on two different data sets?\n2. Why call an alarm > 150 days before failure \"false\"?\n3. Threshold 4 gives slightly more warning. Why not use it?\n4. What has to change to run this on real CM data?\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Answers<\/strong><\/summary>\n\n1. The fleet measures detection and lead time over many failures. The site file checks behaviour on **healthy** machines, where false alarms come from. You need both kinds of evidence.\n2. An alarm that early isn't a useful warning of *this* failure. It's either noise or something unrelated, and it would have sent someone out for nothing. (The cut-off is a judgement call. State it.)\n3. It alarmed on a healthy bearing (B4) and twice too early in the fleet. Each false alarm costs a callout and, worse, **trust**. After a few, people start ignoring alarms, and then real ones get missed.\n4. Only `load_*()`: map the export's columns to `bearing_id`, `t`/`date` and feature names, and handle gaps (sensors drop readings). Then re-evaluate thresholds on **your** machines once you have a few real events. Don't assume the synthetic settings transfer.\n\n<\/details>"},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 7 build, so it isn't skippable: Session 1's `score()` and `alarms()` tested on `bearing_trend.csv`, the Session 2 fleet and site evaluation at thresholds 4, 6 and 8 (pick 6 with persistence 3: 60/60, 0 false alarms, about 62 days), and weekend items 1, 4 and 5 (config file, alarm CSV, plain-English alarm sentences). Plan on two or three sittings.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Read \"The design\" and the reasons for median/MAD and the max across features, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the alarm sentence for each of B2 and B5 on paper using the weekend item 5 example.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only the stretch items can wait: weekend item 2 (the `rebaseline_after` option) and item 3 (adding Week 59's spectral features)."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] `score()`, `alarms()` and `evaluate()` written and tested\n- [ ] Threshold chosen with the evaluation table (6, persistence 3)\n- [ ] 60/60 detected, 0 false alarms, median ~62 days' warning\n- [ ] Config file, alarm CSV and plain-English alarm sentences"}],"quiz":[{"id":"61-1","n":1,"q":"Why evaluate on two different data sets?","a":"The fleet measures detection and lead time over many failures. The site file checks behaviour on **healthy** machines, where false alarms come from. You need both kinds of evidence."},{"id":"61-2","n":2,"q":"Why call an alarm > 150 days before failure \"false\"?","a":"An alarm that early isn't a useful warning of *this* failure. It's either noise or something unrelated, and it would have sent someone out for nothing. (The cut-off is a judgement call. State it.)"},{"id":"61-3","n":3,"q":"Threshold 4 gives slightly more warning. Why not use it?","a":"It alarmed on a healthy bearing (B4) and twice too early in the fleet. Each false alarm costs a callout and, worse, **trust**. After a few, people start ignoring alarms, and then real ones get missed."},{"id":"61-4","n":4,"q":"What has to change to run this on real CM data?","a":"Only `load_*()`: map the export's columns to `bearing_id`, `t`/`date` and feature names, and handle gaps (sensors drop readings). Then re-evaluate thresholds on **your** machines once you have a few real events. Don't assume the synthetic settings transfer."}],"watch":[],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 7 build, so it isn't skippable: Session 1's `score()` and `alarms()` tested on `bearing_trend.csv`, the Session 2 fleet and site evaluation at thresholds 4, 6 and 8 (pick 6 with persistence 3: 60/60, 0 false alarms, about 62 days), and weekend items 1, 4 and 5 (config file, alarm CSV, plain-English alarm sentences). Plan on two or three sittings."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Read \"The design\" and the reasons for median/MAD and the max across features, answer Check yourself in your head, and draft the alarm sentence for each of B2 and B5 on paper using the weekend item 5 example."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only the stretch items can wait: weekend item 2 (the `rebaseline_after` option) and item 3 (adding Week 59's spectral features)."}}},{"week":62,"phase":7,"title":"Phase 7 done-when, the finished portfolio, and what's next","path":"course/phase-7-ml-condition-monitoring/week-62-done-when-portfolio-next-steps.md","ticks":["Non-specialist explanation passed. Phase 7 done.","Portfolio: 8 projects, top-level README, pinned","Conversation with your manager about using it on real data (with permission)","Next-step plan: a cert, a book, a project","Course complete ✅"],"sections":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","kind":"intro","md":"**Phase 7 · Machine learning & condition monitoring** · Mon 3 Jan – Sun 9 Jan 2028 · about 5 hours\n\n> **Done when:** you can explain to a non-specialist **why your model's alerts are worth acting on**, and **how many false alarms to expect**.\n\nThe last week. Prove the Phase 7 done-when, finish the portfolio, and plan what comes after the course.","readMins":2},{"id":"session-1-the-non-specialist-test","title":"Session 1 (1.5 h): The non-specialist test","kind":"hands","md":"### Prepare a 3-minute explanation (no jargon)\n\nUse this structure, with **your** numbers:\n\n1. **What it does:** *\"Each sensor learns what normal looks like for its own bearing. If several readings in a row look very different from normal (6× the usual spread), it raises an alert.\"*\n2. **Why trust it:** *\"We tested it on 60 bearings that ran to failure. It warned about every one, on average about 2 months before failure, and never less than about 3 weeks.\"*\n3. **How many false alarms:** *\"In testing, none: not on the 4 healthy fans, and not too early on any of the 60. In real life expect some, especially after maintenance or when the fan runs at a different speed, so we'll review every alert for the first 6 months and tune it.\"*\n4. **What to do with an alert:** *\"Check it on the next shift with a handheld meter, then plan the bearing change in the next scheduled stop. You usually have weeks, not hours.\"*\n5. **What it won't do:** *\"It won't tell you the exact failure date, and it can't see faults on machines without a sensor.\"*\n\n### Do it\n\nExplain it to someone non-technical: a family member or a friend. Then ask them to explain it back to you. If their version covers points 2, 3 and 4, **you've passed Phase 7**. 🎉 And the whole course.","mins":90},{"id":"session-2-finish-the-portfolio","title":"Session 2 (1.5 h): Finish the portfolio","kind":"hands","md":"Your portfolio repo should now have:\n\n| # | Project | Skills | Payoff at work |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 01 | PM compliance workbook | Excel, Power Query, Power Pivot | replaces manual weekly tallies |\n| 02 | Mini CMMS database | SQL, data modelling | understanding of any CMMS's data model |\n| 03 | Line D dashboard | Power BI, DAX | visible PM-pillar KPIs |\n| 04 | OEE & maintenance impact | OEE, Tableau, SQL matching | links Redzone losses to CMMS assets |\n| 05 | Automated PM report | Python, pandas | hours saved every week |\n| 06 | Vibration trend | Python, CM | P-F evidence for sensor rollout |\n| 07 | Weibull PM interval study | statistics, reliability | evidence-based PM optimisation |\n| 08 | Bearing anomaly detector | ML, signal processing | ready for real CM data |\n\nPolish pass:\n\n- [ ] A top-level `README.md`: who you are (a mechatronics apprentice moving into reliability and data), 7 project cards with a screenshot each, and links.\n- [ ] Every project README opens with **the problem** and **the result in one line**, then how to run it.\n- [ ] Everything runs from a fresh clone with `pip install -r requirements.txt`.\n- [ ] No real company data anywhere. State that clearly: *\"All data is synthetic, generated by `generate_data.py`.\"*\n- [ ] Pin the repo on your GitHub profile. Add the link to your CV and LinkedIn.","mins":90},{"id":"weekend-what-s-next","title":"Weekend (2 h): What's next","kind":"hands","md":"### Use it at work (with permission)\n\n1. Show your manager the Weibull study (06) and the dashboard (03). Ask: *\"Could I try this on our real data?\"*\n2. Agree the **data rules** first: what you can export, where it can live, and who can see it. Never take work data home without written approval.\n3. Start with the **lowest-risk, highest-visibility** item: usually the dashboard, or the automated report, on data the team already sees.\n\n### Where the industrial tools fit\n\n| Tool | Why | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Power Automate / Power Apps** | paper-to-mobile forms, approvals, alerts | as soon as you have a work account (Week 23 designs) |\n| **Historians** (FactoryTalk Historian, AVEVA PI) | where real process data lives, so learn to query and export it | ask the controls team for read access |\n| **OPC UA / MQTT** | how shop-floor data reaches IT systems (IIoT) | next learning project; try a free MQTT broker and pycomm3 at home |\n| **Minitab** | common SPC tool in manufacturing | only if your site uses it; Python covers the same |\n| **Tableau** | where your Redzone production data lives (Phase 4) | keep it fresh: rebuild one Redzone view a month on Tableau Public |\n\n### Credentials\n\n| Credential | Value |\n|---|---|\n| **PL-300** (Power BI Data Analyst) | the most recognised data cert in UK manufacturing. Book it if you haven't. |\n| **CMRP** (SMRP) | a reliability credential that pairs with this course and your HNC/HND path |\n| Kaggle / Coursera certificates | light proof of Python and ML basics |\n\nAsk whether the training budget will fund PL-300 or CMRP, since both map directly onto the PM pillar.\n\n### Keep learning (pick one per quarter)\n\n- *The Definitive Guide to DAX* (Russo & Ferrari): deeper Power BI\n- *Reliability-Centered Maintenance* (Moubray): RCM properly\n- *Hands-On Machine Learning* (Géron): the full ML book\n- The NASA C-MAPSS or IMS data sets as stretch projects\n- Your **HNC/HND project**: reuse the Weibull study or the anomaly detector, and get credit twice","mins":120},{"id":"the-final-check","title":"The final check","kind":"other","md":"Go back to `course/README.md` and look at the seven *done when* tests (plus Phase 0). Can you still pass each one today, from memory, on a fresh copy of the data? If yes: **you've done it.** A year ago you were starting at \"how do I freeze panes\". Now you can defend a PM interval with a Weibull analysis and build an anomaly detector from raw vibration data.\n\nWell done, Josh. 🎉","readMins":2},{"id":"short-on-time","title":"Short on time?","kind":"short","md":"- **Must do (about 90 min at a PC):** This is the Phase 7 and course done-when, so it isn't skippable: the Session 1 non-specialist test (they must explain back points 2, 3 and 4) and the Session 2 portfolio polish checklist, ending with everything running from a fresh clone and the repo pinned.\n- **On your phone (10 min a day):** Write your 3-minute explanation with your own numbers on paper and practise it out loud. At work, have the weekend conversation with your manager about trying this on real data and agreeing the data rules, and pick your next cert, book and project.\n- **Safe to skip this week:** Only the extras can wait: reading through the industrial tools table and the \"Keep learning\" list in detail, and the Kaggle or Coursera certificates."},{"id":"tick-off","title":"Tick off","kind":"tick","md":"- [ ] **Non-specialist explanation passed.** Phase 7 done.\n- [ ] Portfolio: 8 projects, top-level README, pinned\n- [ ] Conversation with your manager about using it on real data (with permission)\n- [ ] Next-step plan: a cert, a book, a project\n- [ ] **Course complete** ✅"}],"quiz":[],"watch":[],"short":{"must":{"label":"Must do (about 90 min at a PC)","text":"This is the Phase 7 and course done-when, so it isn't skippable: the Session 1 non-specialist test (they must explain back points 2, 3 and 4) and the Session 2 portfolio polish checklist, ending with everything running from a fresh clone and the repo pinned."},"phone":{"label":"On your phone (10 min a day)","text":"Write your 3-minute explanation with your own numbers on paper and practise it out loud. At work, have the weekend conversation with your manager about trying this on real data and agreeing the data rules, and pick your next cert, book and project."},"skip":{"label":"Safe to skip this week","text":"Only the extras can wait: reading through the industrial tools table and the \"Keep learning\" list in detail, and the Kaggle or Coursera certificates."}}}]};
